<pre class="metadata">
title: Runtime Types
shortname: runtime-types
status: proposal
stage: 0
contributors: Sirisian
</pre>

<emu-intro id="sec-intro">
  <h1>Introduction</h1>
  <p>This proposal adds an optional static type system to ECMAScript. Its types are values at run time and are enforced there: a typed binding rejects a value outside its type, a conversion between numeric types is written explicitly rather than performed silently, and the type of a value is available through reflection. Annotations are optional and additive, so a program that does not use them keeps its current meaning.</p>

  <p>The system is deliberately close to the value-type discipline of systems languages. It adds sized integers, floats, and decimals; value-type classes with a defined in-memory layout; operator overloading; generics specialized by monomorphization; and types as first-class, interned values that ordinary code can compute with and reflect on. None of this is visible to a program that does not opt in.</p>

  <p>This document is the normative specification. It is written against the design, which, together with the rationale, the full set of extensions, and worked examples, is maintained in the <a href="https://github.com/sirisian/ecmascript-types">design repository</a>. Readers new to the proposal should start there; this document formalizes what that material describes.</p>

  <emu-note>
    <p>The specification is under active construction. The clauses below establish the shape of the document; normative text is added phase by phase, following the specification plan in the design repository. Until a section is filled in here, the corresponding behaviour is defined only by the design documents.</p>
  </emu-note>
</emu-intro>

<emu-clause id="sec-runtime-types">
  <h1>Runtime Types</h1>
  <p>This clause and its subclauses define the runtime type system as a set of additions to and revisions of the ECMAScript specification. The work is organized into the phases described in the design repository's specification plan: the static type-checking framework; the grammar of type expressions; the type universe; conversions; runtime enforcement; typed declarations, operators, functions, and classes; the type-object and reflection API; and the numeric library. Extensions (SIMD, generics, decorators, primitive metadata, and the rest) are specified as independent additions layered on this core.</p>

  <p>Each addition is expressed as an insertion into or revision of the edition of ECMAScript that <emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref> pins, so that this document reads as a patch against that specification rather than a restatement of it. Clauses appear in the order of the underlying ECMAScript specification, cross-referenced to the sections they modify, and the extension clauses follow the core, each of them named in <emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>.</p>

  <emu-clause id="sec-conventions">
    <h1>Conventions</h1>
    <p>This document is a diff. It is written against ECMA-262, 16th edition (ES2025), which is the <dfn id="pinned-edition">pinned edition</dfn>, and it should be re-pinned and re-diffed at each subsequent edition rather than left to drift. Within a grammar block, a production shown with <ins>inserted text</ins> is that edition's production with the marked additions; a production shown entirely as an insertion is new to this proposal. A cross-reference whose target is not defined here is a reference to the pinned edition and resolves against it. Where a later clause extends a production an earlier clause has already patched, the later block restates the production in full, marking only its own additions as inserted; the blocks are one production, and the latest restatement is its complete form.</p>

    <p>The <dfn id="the-design">design</dfn> is the collection of documents in the <a href="https://github.com/sirisian/ecmascript-types">ecmascript-types</a> repository: a README specifying the core and a set of extension documents specifying SIMD, generics, decorators, primitive metadata, memory layout, type programming, and the rest. This specification is that design rendered as a patch against the pinned edition. A note that cites the design is pointing at the source of a requirement, at a case the design works through, or at a question this specification decided and the design should record; as the proposal advances, each such citation should become a link to an issue in that repository, so that a reader can follow a decision to the discussion that settled it.</p>

    <p>Three conventions govern the semantics. The relations of the type system, SameType, IsSubtype, IsAssignable, and the operations over Type Records, are abstract operations rather than syntax-directed operations, because they relate types to types and not Parse Nodes to values; the syntax-directed part of the type system is the Static Type of an expression (<emu-xref href="#sec-static-type-of-an-expression"></emu-xref>), which each clause that adds an expression form extends. The static semantics that attach to a production are Early Errors, and this document states one with the phrase &ldquo;it is a type error if&rdquo;, which <emu-xref href="#sec-type-errors"></emu-xref> defines as an Early Error rather than a thrown error; they are stated at the rule they belong to rather than gathered into one block, because a type error is a condition on types and reads where the types are. A thrown *TypeError* or *RangeError* is reserved for a check that could not be resolved statically.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The editorial state of this document is worth stating plainly. Its structure, cross-references, and grammar are complete and it builds clean, and every obligation the design records is either specified here or named in <emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref> as belonging to an extension. What it has not had is the multi-editor review that turns a complete specification into an approved one, and the decisions it records, marked as such in the notes, are this specification's answers rather than the design's, offered so that a reader can disagree with a stated position instead of guessing at an unstated one.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-type-system-foundations">
    <h1>Type System Foundations</h1>
    <p>This clause defines the machinery the rest of the specification builds on: the representation of a type, the structural identity that interns types into unique values, the subtyping and assignability relations, the static type of an expression, compile-time evaluation of a type expression, and the reporting of type errors. It introduces no user-facing syntax; the productions and library that use this machinery are added by later clauses.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>ECMAScript defines runtime behaviour through abstract operations and a limited set of static semantics, chiefly Early Errors, but it has no framework for assigning a type to an expression or relating two types. This clause establishes that framework. Because a type in this proposal is also a value, a single notion, the Type Object, serves both as the entity the type checker reasons about and as the value a program observes.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <emu-clause id="sec-types-and-type-objects">
      <h1>Types and Type Objects</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="type-record">Type Record</dfn> is a Record used by this specification to describe an ECMAScript type. Each Type Record has a [[Kind]] field and the additional fields listed for that kind in <emu-xref href="#table-type-record-kinds"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-type-record-kinds" caption="Type Record Kinds">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>[[Kind]]</th><th>Additional Fields</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>~any~</td><td>(none)</td><td>The dynamic type. Any value is assignable to it, and a value of the ~any~ type is assignable to any type subject to a run-time check (<emu-xref href="#sec-requiretype"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~void~</td><td>(none)</td><td>The type of the absence of a value.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~primitive~</td><td>[[Name]] (a String), [[Arguments]] (a List of Type Records and other values)</td><td>A built-in value type such as `uint8` or `float64`, together with any type or value arguments, for example the width of `int.&lt;N&gt;`. The primitive types are enumerated in the type universe clause.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~literal~</td><td>[[Value]] (a String, Number, Boolean, BigInt, or Symbol), [[Base]] (a Type Record)</td><td>A literal type: the type whose one value is [[Value]], a subtype of its [[Base]] (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~parameterized~</td><td>[[Base]] (a Type Record), [[Metadata]] (an ECMAScript language value)</td><td>A metadata parameterization of any base type, such as `float32.&lt;{ m: 1 }&gt;` or a brand: a refinement of its [[Base]] (<emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~nominal~</td><td>[[Declaration]] (a Parse Node), [[Arguments]] (a List of Type Records and other values)</td><td>A class, interface, or enum type, identified by the declaration that introduced it, together with the arguments of a generic instantiation. [[Arguments]] is empty where the declaration is not generic.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~union~</td><td>[[Members]] (a List of Type Records)</td><td>A union type. A value belongs to it if it belongs to any member.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~intersection~</td><td>[[Members]] (a List of Type Records)</td><td>An intersection type. A value belongs to it if it belongs to every member.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~tuple~</td><td>[[Elements]] (a List of Tuple Element Records)</td><td>A tuple type of position-wise element types, each element carrying its spread flag and its default.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~array~</td><td>[[Element]] (a Type Record), [[Extent]] (a non-negative integer or ~dynamic~)</td><td>An array type. [[Extent]] is the fixed length of a `[N].&lt;T&gt;`, or ~dynamic~ for a `[].&lt;T&gt;`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~object~</td><td>[[Properties]] (a List of Property Type Records), [[IndexSignatures]] (a List of Index Signature Records)</td><td>A structural object type or interface type.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~function~</td><td>[[Signatures]] (a List of Signature Records)</td><td>A function type. It has more than one signature when the function is overloaded.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~reference~</td><td>[[Target]] (a Type Record)</td><td>A reference to a storage location holding a value of the target type, as introduced by `ref`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~shared~</td><td>[[Target]] (a Type Record)</td><td>A value type whose storage is shared between the threads of one heap, as introduced by `shared` (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-shared-modifier"></emu-xref>). A value of it is a value of its [[Target]]; what the modifier decides is where the storage lives and what a checker may assume of it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~parameter~</td><td>[[Declaration]] (a TypeParameter Parse Node), [[Arity]] (a non-negative integer)</td><td>A generic parameter standing for the type or value an application will bind, within the body and signatures of its declaration (<emu-xref href="#sec-generics"></emu-xref>). An [[Arity]] of 0 is such a parameter; an [[Arity]] of _n_ &gt; 0 is a higher-kinded parameter, which stands for a generic declaration of _n_ parameters rather than for a type, and is not itself a type (<emu-xref href="#sec-higher-kinded-parameters"></emu-xref>). Frozen: outside its declaration a fully specialized type contains none.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>~application~</td><td>[[Builder]] (a function), [[Arguments]] (a List of Type Records and ECMAScript language values, a ~parameter~ where an argument reads an unbound parameter)</td><td>A deferred compile-time application: a call of a compile-time-evaluable function at least one of whose arguments involves an unbound generic parameter, carried as a type until specialization evaluates it (<emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The structured fields referenced above, a Property Type Record, an Index Signature Record, a Signature Record, and a Tuple Element Record, are defined by the clauses that introduce object types, function types, and arrays and tuples. This clause depends only on Type Records being comparable and canonicalizable, which the operations below provide for every kind.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A <dfn id="type-object">Type Object</dfn> is an ECMAScript object that denotes a type. Every Type Object has a [[TypeRecord]] internal slot whose value is a canonical Type Record (<emu-xref href="#sec-canonicalizetype"></emu-xref>). A type name or type expression in expression position evaluates to a Type Object, and reflection returns one.</p>

      <p>Type Objects are interned. <emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref> maps each canonical Type Record to a unique Type Object, so two Type Objects denote the same type if and only if they are the same value. Consequently comparing two Type Objects with IsStrictlyEqual returns *true* exactly when they denote the same type, and a Type Object may be used as a property key or a key in a Map or Set to key on a type.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The observable behaviour of a Type Object, including how it exposes its own structure to reflection, is defined by <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection"></emu-xref>. The remainder of the type system uses only its [[TypeRecord]] and its identity.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
    <emu-clause id="sec-structural-identity">
      <h1>Structural Identity</h1>
      <p>Two types are the same when they have the same structure, with one exception: a ~nominal~ type is identified by its declaration, so two classes with identical members are still different types. This clause defines that notion and the canonical form that makes it decidable and internable. A pair of Type Records is carried through the recursive comparisons as a List so that comparison of a recursive type terminates; each such pair is represented as a Record with a [[First]] field and a [[Second]] field.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-sametype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SameType (
            _s_: a Type Record,
            _t_: a Type Record,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether _s_ and _t_ denote the same type.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. <del>Return SameTypeWithAssumptions(_s_, _t_, &laquo; &raquo;).</del>
          1. <ins>If IsSubtype(_s_, _t_, &laquo; &raquo;) is *false*, return *false*.</ins>
          1. <ins>Return IsSubtype(_t_, _s_, &laquo; &raquo;).</ins>
        </emu-alg>
        <p><ins>SameType is MUTUAL SUBTYPING. It is therefore reflexive, symmetric, and transitive wherever IsSubtype is reflexive and transitive, which is to say it is an equivalence relation - and callers that de-duplicate, intern, or otherwise treat it as equality may rely on that.</ins></p>
        <emu-note>
          <p><ins>It was previously defined as SameTypeWithAssumptions, whose algorithm carries three steps keyed on _s_ alone - for an enum, a literal, and a parameterized type - with no mirror for _t_. That made a relation named for equality ASYMMETRIC: SameType(`"a"`, `string`) answered *true* where SameType(`string`, `"a"`) answered *false*. Callers treating it as equality inherited an order dependence; de-duplicating a union by it discarded `string` from `"a" | string` while leaving `string | "a"` intact, so the same type written two ways admitted different values.</ins></p>
          <p><ins>The refinement those steps provided is not lost. It is what IsSubtype computes, and a caller asking "does _s_ refine to _t_" should call IsSubtype rather than a relation named for sameness.</ins></p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-sametypewithassumptions" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SameTypeWithAssumptions (
            _s_: a Type Record,
            _t_: a Type Record,
            _assumptions_: a List of Records that have [[First]] and [[Second]] fields,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether _s_ and _t_ denote the same type, treating each pair in _assumptions_ as already established so that comparison of recursive types terminates.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _s_ and _t_ are the same Record, return *true*.
          1. If _assumptions_ contains a Record _p_ such that _p_.[[First]] is _s_ and _p_.[[Second]] is _t_, return *true*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~nominal~ and _s_.[[Declaration]] is an enum declaration, then
            1. If IsSubtype(the underlying type of _s_.[[Declaration]], _t_, _assumptions_) is *true*, return *true*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~literal~, return IsSubtype(_s_.[[Base]], _t_, _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~parameterized~, then
            1. If IsSubtype(_s_.[[Base]], _t_, _assumptions_) is *true*, return *true*.
            1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~parameterized~ and SameTypeWithAssumptions(_s_.[[Base]], _t_.[[Base]], _assumptions_) is *true* and the metadata subtype judgment of <emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref> holds of _s_.[[Metadata]] and _t_.[[Metadata]], return *true*.
            1. Return *false*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is not _t_.[[Kind]], return *false*.
          1. Set _assumptions_ to the list-concatenation of _assumptions_ and &laquo; the Record { [[First]]: _s_, [[Second]]: _t_ } &raquo;.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~any~ or _s_.[[Kind]] is ~void~, return *true*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~primitive~, then
            1. If _s_.[[Name]] is not _t_.[[Name]], return *false*.
            1. Return SameArgumentList(_s_.[[Arguments]], _t_.[[Arguments]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~literal~, then
            1. If SameValue(_s_.[[Value]], _t_.[[Value]]) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Return SameTypeWithAssumptions(_s_.[[Base]], _t_.[[Base]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~parameterized~, then
            1. If _s_.[[Metadata]] and _t_.[[Metadata]] are not structurally equivalent (<emu-xref href="#sec-deferred-applications"></emu-xref>), return *false*.
            1. Return SameTypeWithAssumptions(_s_.[[Base]], _t_.[[Base]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~application~, then
            1. If _s_.[[Builder]] and _t_.[[Builder]] are not the same function, return *false*.
            1. Return SameArgumentList(_s_.[[Arguments]], _t_.[[Arguments]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~nominal~, then
            1. If _s_.[[Declaration]] and _t_.[[Declaration]] are not the same Parse Node, return *false*.
            1. Return SameArgumentList(_s_.[[Arguments]], _t_.[[Arguments]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~union~ or _s_.[[Kind]] is ~intersection~, return SameTypeList(_s_.[[Members]], _t_.[[Members]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~tuple~, then
            1. If _s_.[[Elements]] and _t_.[[Elements]] do not have the same number of elements, return *false*.
            1. For each integer _i_ such that 0 &le; _i_ and _i_ &lt; the number of elements of _s_.[[Elements]], do
              1. Let _p_ be _s_.[[Elements]][_i_].
              1. Let _q_ be _t_.[[Elements]][_i_].
              1. If _p_.[[Rest]] is not _q_.[[Rest]], return *false*.
              1. If _p_.[[Initial]] is ~none~ or _q_.[[Initial]] is ~none~, then
                1. If _p_.[[Initial]] is not ~none~ or _q_.[[Initial]] is not ~none~, return *false*.
              1. Else if SameValue(_p_.[[Initial]], _q_.[[Initial]]) is *false*, then
                1. Return *false*.
              1. If SameTypeWithAssumptions(_p_.[[Type]], _q_.[[Type]], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Return *true*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~array~, then
            1. If _s_.[[Extent]] is not _t_.[[Extent]], return *false*.
            1. Return SameTypeWithAssumptions(_s_.[[Element]], _t_.[[Element]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~reference~ or _s_.[[Kind]] is ~shared~, return SameTypeWithAssumptions(_s_.[[Target]], _t_.[[Target]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~function~, return SameFunctionType(_s_, _t_, _assumptions_).
          1. Assert: _s_.[[Kind]] is ~object~.
          1. Return SameObjectType(_s_, _t_, _assumptions_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>SameTypeList holds when two Lists of Type Records have the same length and are position-wise the same under _assumptions_. SameArgumentList is SameTypeList extended to compare non-type arguments, such as an array extent, with SameValue. SameObjectType (<emu-xref href="#sec-sameobjecttype"></emu-xref>) and SameFunctionType (<emu-xref href="#sec-samefunctiontype"></emu-xref>) compare the members of an ~object~ or ~function~ type. Because a canonical ~union~ or ~intersection~ has its members in a fixed order (<emu-xref href="#sec-canonicalizetype"></emu-xref>), position-wise comparison of members is sufficient.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-sametypestructural" type="abstract operation">
      <h1><ins>
        SameTypeStructural (
          _s_: a Type Record,
          _t_: a Type Record,
        ): a Boolean
      </ins></h1>
      <dl class="header">
        <dt>description</dt>
        <dd><ins>It determines whether _s_ and _t_ are the same type CONSTRUCTION, rather than whether they denote the same values.</ins></dd>
      </dl>
      <emu-alg>
        1. <ins>Return SameTypeWithAssumptions(_s_, _t_, &laquo; &raquo;).</ins>
      </emu-alg>
      <p><ins>This is a STRICTER relation than SameType. Two Type Records may denote the same values and still differ in construction - most importantly in the metadata they claim - so an implementation that interns Type Objects must use this relation and not SameType. Interning by SameType merges records whose claims differ, and the claim of whichever record is discarded is lost with it.</ins></p>
      <emu-note>
        <p><ins>It is the relation SameType was defined as before that operation became mutual subtyping, kept here under a name that says what it computes. The two are separated because they answer different questions: SameType asks whether a value of one is acceptable where the other is required, and SameTypeStructural asks whether the two were built the same way.</ins></p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-sametypelist" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SameTypeList (
            _a_: a List of Type Records,
            _b_: a List of Type Records,
            _assumptions_: a List of Records that have [[First]] and [[Second]] fields,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether _a_ and _b_ have the same length and are position-wise the same type under _assumptions_.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _a_ and _b_ do not have the same number of elements, return *false*.
          1. For each integer _i_ such that 0 &le; _i_ and _i_ &lt; the number of elements of _a_, do
            1. If SameTypeWithAssumptions(_a_[_i_], _b_[_i_], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-sameargumentlist" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SameArgumentList (
            _a_: a List,
            _b_: a List,
            _assumptions_: a List of Records that have [[First]] and [[Second]] fields,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether _a_ and _b_ have the same length and equal elements, comparing Type Record elements as types under _assumptions_, Parse Node elements by being the same Parse Node, compound compile-time constants by structural equivalence (<emu-xref href="#sec-deferred-applications"></emu-xref>), and other elements with SameValue.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _a_ and _b_ do not have the same number of elements, return *false*.
          1. For each integer _i_ such that 0 &le; _i_ and _i_ &lt; the number of elements of _a_, do
            1. Let _x_ be _a_[_i_].
            1. Let _y_ be _b_[_i_].
            1. If _x_ is a Type Record and _y_ is a Type Record, then
              1. If SameTypeWithAssumptions(_x_, _y_, _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Else if _x_ is a Type Record or _y_ is a Type Record, then
              1. Return *false*.
            1. Else if _x_ is a Parse Node and _y_ is a Parse Node, then
              1. If _x_ and _y_ are not the same Parse Node, return *false*.
            1. Else if _x_ is a Parse Node or _y_ is a Parse Node, then
              1. Return *false*.
            1. Else if _x_ and _y_ are compound compile-time constants, then
              1. If _x_ and _y_ are not structurally equivalent (<emu-xref href="#sec-deferred-applications"></emu-xref>), return *false*.
            1. Else if SameValue(_x_, _y_) is *false*, then
              1. Return *false*.
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-canonicalizetype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          CanonicalizeType (
            _t_: a Type Record,
          ): a Type Record
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the canonical form of _t_, reading no [[Origin]] (<emu-xref href="#sec-provenance"></emu-xref>) in deciding it. Canonical Type Records that denote the same type are the same Record, which is what lets <emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref> intern them.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~union~ or _t_.[[Kind]] is ~intersection~, then
            1. Let _members_ be a new empty List.
            1. For each element _m_ of _t_.[[Members]], do
              1. Let _c_ be CanonicalizeType(_m_).
              1. If _c_.[[Kind]] is _t_.[[Kind]], then
                1. For each element _n_ of _c_.[[Members]], append _n_ to _members_.
              1. Else,
                1. Append _c_ to _members_.
            1. Remove from _members_ each element that is the same type (SameType) as an element occurring earlier in _members_.
            1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~intersection~ and _members_ contains an element whose [[Kind]] is ~union~ and whose [[Members]] is empty, return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~union~, [[Members]]: &laquo; &raquo; } (the `never` type; see <emu-xref href="#sec-never-type"></emu-xref>).
            1. If _members_ has exactly one element, return that element.
            1. Order the elements of _members_ according to the total order of <emu-xref href="#sec-canonical-total-order"></emu-xref>.
            1. Return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: _t_.[[Kind]], [[Members]]: _members_ }.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~tuple~, then
            1. Let _elements_ be a new empty List.
            1. For each element _e_ of _t_.[[Elements]], do
              1. Append the Tuple Element Record { [[Type]]: CanonicalizeType(_e_.[[Type]]), [[Rest]]: _e_.[[Rest]], [[Initial]]: _e_.[[Initial]] } to _elements_.
            1. Return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~tuple~, [[Elements]]: _elements_ }.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~array~, return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~array~, [[Element]]: CanonicalizeType(_t_.[[Element]]), [[Extent]]: _t_.[[Extent]] }.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~reference~, return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~reference~, [[Target]]: CanonicalizeType(_t_.[[Target]]) }.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~shared~, return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~shared~, [[Target]]: CanonicalizeType(_t_.[[Target]]) }.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~literal~, return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~literal~, [[Value]]: _t_.[[Value]], [[Base]]: CanonicalizeType(_t_.[[Base]]) }.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~parameterized~, return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~parameterized~, [[Base]]: CanonicalizeType(_t_.[[Base]]), [[Metadata]]: _t_.[[Metadata]] }.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~application~, then
            1. Let _args_ be a new empty List.
            1. For each element _a_ of _t_.[[Arguments]], do
              1. If _a_ is a Type Record, append CanonicalizeType(_a_) to _args_.
              1. Else, append _a_ to _args_.
            1. Return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~application~, [[Builder]]: _t_.[[Builder]], [[Arguments]]: _args_ }.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~object~ or _t_.[[Kind]] is ~function~, return the canonical form of _t_ defined alongside that kind, with each contained Type Record canonicalized.
          1. Return _t_.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Flattening merges a nested union into its parent, and likewise for intersection; de-duplication and the fixed order make member order insignificant, so `A | B` and `B | A` canonicalize to one Record and, after interning, one Type Object. A ~nominal~, ~primitive~, ~any~, or ~void~ Type Record is already canonical apart from canonicalizing the Type Records in its [[Arguments]], which the recursion reaches when they occur. Recursion terminates because a cycle in a type passes through a ~nominal~ position, which is canonical without descending into its declaration. Flattening is also what removes a `never` member from a union: the member is an empty union, so it contributes nothing, and `T | never` canonicalizes to `T` with no rule naming the case. The intersection step above is the one algebraic fact about `never` that does not fall out this way (<emu-xref href="#sec-never-type"></emu-xref>). Canonicalization performs no subtype absorption: `uint8 | number` keeps both members, because folding by IsSubtype would make the canonical form depend on a relation that reads canonical forms, and the unabsorbed union costs nothing, since membership and assignability answer identically either way.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-gettypeobject" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          GetTypeObject (
            _t_: a Type Record,
          ): a Type Object
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the unique Type Object that denotes the type described by _t_.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _canonical_ be CanonicalizeType(_t_).
          1. If the type-object intern table contains a Type Object _existing_ such that SameType(_existing_.[[TypeRecord]], _canonical_) is *true*, return _existing_.
          1. Let _obj_ be a new Type Object whose [[TypeRecord]] internal slot is _canonical_.
          1. Add _obj_ to the type-object intern table.
          1. Return _obj_.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The intern table is a specification device; an implementation may realize it in any way that yields the same identities. It is a property of a heap: Type Objects, like other objects, are shared by the agents that share one and are not shared with an agent that does not (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-across-agents"></emu-xref>).</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-subtyping-and-assignability">
      <h1>Subtyping and Assignability</h1>
      <p><dfn id="subtyping">Subtyping</dfn> relates a more specific type to a more general one. <dfn id="assignability">Assignability</dfn> is the relation the type checker applies at an assignment, an argument, or a return: it is subtyping, widened at the boundary with the ~any~ type. Because this proposal performs no implicit numeric widening, a ~primitive~ type is a subtype of no other numeric type. It is a subtype of itself, of the ~any~ type, and of the same primitive bearing fewer metadata arguments, since a metadata parameterization refines the type it parameterizes rather than being a different type: `Meter` is a `float32.&lt;{ m: 1 }&gt;` and is a subtype of `float32`, which is what makes `m instanceof float32` *true*.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-issubtype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          IsSubtype (
            _s_: a Type Record,
            _t_: a Type Record,
            _assumptions_: a List of Records that have [[First]] and [[Second]] fields,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether every value of the type _s_ is a value of the type _t_, treating each pair in _assumptions_ as already established so that comparison of recursive types terminates. The initial call passes an empty List for _assumptions_.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If SameTypeWithAssumptions(_s_, _t_, _assumptions_) is *true*, return *true*.
          1. If _assumptions_ contains a Record _p_ such that _p_.[[First]] is _s_ and _p_.[[Second]] is _t_, return *true*.
          1. Set _assumptions_ to the list-concatenation of _assumptions_ and &laquo; the Record { [[First]]: _s_, [[Second]]: _t_ } &raquo;.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~any~, return *true*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~union~, then
            1. For each element _m_ of _s_.[[Members]], do
              1. If IsSubtype(_m_, _t_, _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Return *true*.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~union~, then
            1. For each element _m_ of _t_.[[Members]], do
              1. If IsSubtype(_s_, _m_, _assumptions_) is *true*, return *true*.
            1. Return *false*.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~intersection~, then
            1. For each element _m_ of _t_.[[Members]], do
              1. If IsSubtype(_s_, _m_, _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Return *true*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~intersection~, then
            1. For each element _m_ of _s_.[[Members]], do
              1. If IsSubtype(_m_, _t_, _assumptions_) is *true*, return *true*.
            1. Return *false*.
          1. If _s_ is a composite type (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-types"></emu-xref>), then
            1. If _t_ is the top composite type, return *true*.
            1. If _s_ is not the top composite type, then
              1. Let _sShape_ be the shape of _s_.
              1. If _t_ is a composite type that is not the top composite type, return IsSubtype(_sShape_, the shape of _t_, _assumptions_).
              1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~object~ and _sShape_.[[Kind]] is ~object~, return IsObjectSubtype(_sShape_, _t_, _assumptions_).
              1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~tuple~ and _sShape_.[[Kind]] is ~tuple~, return IsSubtype(_sShape_, _t_, _assumptions_).
              1. If _t_ is an interface type with a structural form, return IsSubtype(_s_, that form, _assumptions_).
            1. Return *false*.
          1. If _t_ is an interface type and _s_ does not refine _t_, then
            1. Return IsSubtype(_s_, the structural form of _t_, _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~nominal~ and _t_.[[Kind]] is ~object~, then
            1. If _s_ is an interface type, return IsSubtype(the structural form of _s_, _t_, _assumptions_).
            1. Return *false*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~shared~ and _t_.[[Kind]] is not ~shared~, return IsSubtype(_s_.[[Target]], _t_, _assumptions_).
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~shared~ and _s_.[[Kind]] is not ~shared~, return IsSubtype(_s_, _t_.[[Target]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is not _t_.[[Kind]], return *false*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~nominal~, then
            1. If _t_.[[Declaration]] is _s_.[[Declaration]], return SameArgumentList(_s_.[[Arguments]], _t_.[[Arguments]], _assumptions_).
            1. If _t_.[[Declaration]] is an ancestor of _s_.[[Declaration]] in the class and interface hierarchy, then
              1. Return the result of comparing _t_.[[Arguments]] against the arguments _s_ supplies to _t_.[[Declaration]] through the hierarchy, with SameArgumentList.
            1. Return *false*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~array~, then
            1. If _t_.[[Extent]] is not ~dynamic~ and _t_.[[Extent]] is not _s_.[[Extent]], return *false*.
            1. Return SameTypeWithAssumptions(_s_.[[Element]], _t_.[[Element]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~tuple~, then
            1. If the minimum length of _s_ is less than the minimum length of _t_, return *false*, where a tuple's minimum length counts the elements whose [[Rest]] is *false* and [[Initial]] is ~none~.
            1. If the maximum length of _s_ exceeds the maximum length of _t_, return *false*, where a tuple's maximum length is unbounded when any element's [[Rest]] is *true* and its element count otherwise.
            1. If _t_ has more than one element whose [[Rest]] is *true*, then
              1. If the element counts of _s_.[[Elements]] and _t_.[[Elements]] differ, return *false*.
              1. For each integer _i_ such that 0 &le; _i_ and _i_ &lt; the element count of _t_.[[Elements]], do
                1. If _s_.[[Elements]][_i_].[[Rest]] is not _t_.[[Elements]][_i_].[[Rest]], return *false*.
                1. If IsSubtype(_s_.[[Elements]][_i_].[[Type]], _t_.[[Elements]][_i_].[[Type]], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
              1. Return *true*.
            1. For each integer _i_ such that 0 &le; _i_ and _i_ &lt; the greater of the element counts of _s_.[[Elements]] and _t_.[[Elements]], do
              1. If _s_ cannot supply position _i_, continue, where a tuple can supply each position below its maximum length.
              1. If IsSubtype(the type at position _i_ of _s_, the type at position _i_ of _t_, _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*, where the type at a position of _t_ is the [[Type]] of the element that receives it and the type at a position of _s_ is the union of the [[Type]] fields of every element of _s_ that could receive it, an element receiving a position when some assignment of that many values to _s_.[[Elements]] gives it that one (<emu-xref href="#sec-sequenceassignment"></emu-xref>).
            1. Return *true*.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~reference~ or _s_.[[Kind]] is ~shared~, return SameTypeWithAssumptions(_s_.[[Target]], _t_.[[Target]], _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~function~, return IsFunctionSubtype(_s_, _t_, _assumptions_).
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~object~, return IsObjectSubtype(_s_, _t_, _assumptions_).
          1. Return *false*.
        </emu-alg>
        <p>Three of the steps above are one relation. A type <dfn id="refines">refines</dfn> another when it is a ~parameterized~ type whose [[Base]] is that type (<emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>), an enum whose underlying type is that type (<emu-xref href="#sec-enums"></emu-xref>), or a nominal type whose declaration extends or implements that type's declaration. A type is a subtype of every type it refines and of everything that type is a subtype of; the converse never holds without a crossing, a construction boundary for a parameterization, a validated call for an enum, and nothing at all for a class. The three steps are that rule's cases.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>A ~literal~ type is a subtype of its base and of everything its base is a subtype of, which is what makes `'on'` usable where a `string` is required while `string` is not usable where `'on'` is. A tuple's defaults take part in identity but not in subtyping, and tuples relate by the arity window: _s_ is a subtype of _t_ when every length _s_ admits, _t_ admits, and every position _s_ can supply is a subtype of the position of _t_ that receives it. Where _s_ has several rest elements a position may be supplied by more than one of them, so the type _s_ can put at that position is their union, and requiring each to be within _t_'s position is what makes the rule sound.</p>
          <p>Where _t_ has more than one rest element, its positions are not determined by their index and the exact relation is inclusion between two regular languages, which a product construction decides and which a subtyping check cannot afford to run at every use. The rule above is conservative there: it requires the two element lists to correspond one for one, which is sound and is exact whenever they do. A program wanting a relation the conservative rule refuses writes the two tuple types in corresponding shapes, and the case is narrow, since the target of a subtyping check is a declared type and a declared type with two rests is already an unusual thing to write.</p>
          <p>A deferred ~application~ is a subtype only of itself and of the `any` type. Before specialization nothing finer than identity is known about its result, so nothing finer is assumed: two mentions of one deferred call are one type by interning, and two different calls are unrelated until they evaluate. No step above names the kind because none needs to; an ~application~ source reaches the final step for every target but itself and `any`.</p>
          <p>A generic class is invariant in its arguments, so `Map.&lt;string, uint8&gt;` is a subtype of no other instantiation of `Map`. This is the conservative choice and the only one that is sound for a mutable container, since a `Map.&lt;string, uint8&gt;` used as a `Map.&lt;string, number&gt;` would accept a Number into storage typed `uint8`. A parameter may be *declared* covariant or contravariant, as it may in C# and Kotlin, and <emu-xref href="#sec-generic-variance"></emu-xref> states what such a declaration means; the step above is what it relaxes. Invariance is what a parameter carrying no such declaration has.</p>
          <emu-note id="sec-the-array-family-top">
            <p>`[` `]` `.` `&lt;` `any` `&gt;` is the top of the array family: an array of *some* element type, which every ~array~ and every ~tuple~ type is a subtype of. `any` is already the type of which every value is a value, and this is that reading carried to the array types; the extent rules are unchanged, so a fixed-length target still fixes the length and admits any element type within it.</p>
            <p>Element invariance is untouched for every other element type, for the reason given above: a `[` `]` `.` `&lt;` `uint8` `&gt;` used as a `[` `]` `.` `&lt;` `number` `&gt;` would accept a Number into storage typed `uint8`. What makes `any` admissible where a general covariance is not is that a store to an element is checked against the array's own element type at run time (<emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>), so a write through the wider view is refused whatever the static type permitted. A language whose containers are invariant and whose elements are unchecked supplies this as a wildcard or a star projection and forbids writing through it; the check here is what takes the place of that prohibition.</p>
            <p>Without this the bound a generic writes over the array family, `T extends [` `]`, is satisfied by an array whose element type is written `any` and by nothing else, since no array is declared that way.</p>
          </emu-note>

          <p>The kind-specific steps record the variance chosen by this proposal: a ~nominal~ type is a subtype along its declared inheritance; an ~array~ is invariant in its element, and a fixed-length array is a subtype of the ~dynamic~-length array of the same element, with the array whose element is `any` a supertype of every ~array~ and ~tuple~ type (<emu-xref href="#sec-the-array-family-top"></emu-xref>); a ~tuple~ is covariant position-wise; a ~reference~ is invariant in its target, as is a ~shared~ (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-shared-modifier"></emu-xref>); a composite type is covariant in its shape, which the frozenness of every composite makes sound (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-types"></emu-xref>). IsObjectSubtype (<emu-xref href="#sec-isobjectsubtype"></emu-xref>) applies width subtyping to properties and depth subtyping through a `readonly` one; IsFunctionSubtype (<emu-xref href="#sec-isfunctionsubtype"></emu-xref>) applies contravariant-parameter, covariant-return subtyping to signatures. An enum type is additionally a subtype of its underlying type (<emu-xref href="#sec-enums"></emu-xref>). The justification for each choice is given by the clause that introduces the corresponding feature.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-isassignable" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          IsAssignable (
            _s_: a Type Record,
            _t_: a Type Record,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether a value of the type _s_ may be assigned where the type _t_ is expected. This is the relation checked at bindings, arguments, and returns.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~any~ or _t_.[[Kind]] is ~any~, return *true*.
          1. Return IsSubtype(_s_, _t_, &laquo; &raquo;).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>When _s_ is the ~any~ type and _t_ is not, assignability holds but the value's membership in _t_ is not known statically; a check is inserted at the boundary (<emu-xref href="#sec-requiretype"></emu-xref>) that throws a *TypeError* if the value is not of the type _t_. When _t_ is the ~any~ type, no check is required. This two-part treatment, static where the types are known and a run-time check at the ~any~ boundary, is the gradual character of the system: it is why an untyped program keeps its behaviour while a typed one is checked.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-static-type-of-an-expression">
      <h1>The Static Type of an Expression</h1>
      <p>The <dfn id="static-type">Static Type</dfn> of an expression is the Type Record the type checker assigns to it. It is a syntax-directed operation defined by cases over the expression grammar. Each later clause that adds or modifies an expression form gives the Static Type for that form; the cases established by this clause are those that do not depend on a feature introduced later, listed in <emu-xref href="#table-static-type-base"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-static-type-base" caption="Static Type of Base Expression Forms">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Expression form</th><th>Static Type</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A numeric literal</td><td>The ~primitive~ type named `"number"`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A string literal</td><td>The ~primitive~ type named `"string"`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*true* or *false*</td><td>The ~primitive~ type named `"boolean"`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*null*</td><td>The type named `"null"`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A reference to a binding</td><td>The declared type of that binding, or the ~any~ type if the binding has none.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A type name or type expression in expression position</td><td>The ~primitive~ type named `"type"`.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The Static Type of a numeric literal given here is its type in the absence of a contextual type. Literal propagation, by which a literal takes the type its context requires, so that `0` in a `uint8` position is a `uint8`, refines this and is defined by the conversions clause. The Static Types of the remaining expression forms, including operators, calls, array and object literals, and `this`, are given by the clauses that specify those forms; until a form is specified, its Static Type is the ~any~ type. An unannotated binding, `let` and `const` alike, likewise has the ~any~ Static Type whatever its initializer: this proposal infers no binding types, so the widening question an erased system answers with `as const` does not arise here, and `Reflect.typeOf` reads the value where a program wants the runtime fact. Literal propagation nonetheless reaches a USE of an unannotated `const` whose initializer is a compile-time numeric constant expression, which produces the value the initializer would have produced had it been written at that position: `const K = 3.14` yields a Number where nothing requires a type, a `float64` in a `float64` position, and a `float32` in a `float32` one. This infers no type for the binding, and adds none: it decides which VALUE a use produces, which is the question propagation already answers one position earlier. A `let` is excluded, because a binding that may be reassigned must have a type its assignments are checked against; an annotation on a `const` excludes it for the same reason. The constants of the Math object named by <emu-xref href="#sec-value-properties-of-the-math-object"></emu-xref> participate as such a `const` does, since none of them can be written as a literal that denotes it.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-compile-time-evaluability">
      <h1>Compile-Time Evaluation</h1>
      <p>Because a type is a value, a type annotation may be an expression that computes one (<emu-xref href="#sec-computed-types"></emu-xref>); an expression whose computation can be carried out during type checking is <dfn id="compile-time-evaluable">compile-time evaluable</dfn>. This clause defines when such an expression may be evaluated, when it is, what its evaluation may do, and what carries its result before it can run: the evaluability discipline, the deferred application that stands for a call whose generic arguments are not yet bound, the evaluation of a call with memoization and a fixpoint for recursion, and the budget that bounds the whole.</p>

      <p>Evaluation is confined to checking and reads declarations rather than run-time bindings. A top-level type-position expression evaluates during the compilation of its module, with the other static semantics and before the evaluation of any module in the graph begins. A type-position expression inside a generic declaration evaluates at each specialization, once every generic parameter it reads is bound. The callee of a call resolves through imports to a function declaration, whose body these semantics interpret, so no module's run-time evaluation is required or performed by evaluating a type.</p>

      <p>No operation of this specification evaluates such an expression before every generic parameter it reads is bound. The invariant this serves is stated exactly: <dfn id="phase-boundary">the inference fixpoint is builder-free, and every checker-visible effect of user type code is declarative data, a forward-verified proposal, or a per-instantiation-checked contract</dfn>. Those three are the whole of what user code may contribute to checking, and each is closed: the declarative data are a signature's [[ThisType]] and [[Narrows]] (<emu-xref href="#sec-declarative-checker-facts"></emu-xref>) and the hooks of a meta type (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-metadata"></emu-xref>), which the checker reads and calls at named seams; a forward-verified proposal is a trial candidate or a declared inverse (<emu-xref href="#sec-inference-through-results"></emu-xref>), which binds nothing until forward evaluation and assignability confirm it; and a contract is checked at every evaluation before it is believed at any (<emu-xref href="#sec-checked-contracts"></emu-xref>). The computed constraints of <emu-xref href="#sec-computed-constraints"></emu-xref> are the measure of the boundary rather than an exception to it, since a constraint over `T` runs after `T` is bound and before the parameter it constrains is, which is after everything it reads.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The blunt form of this invariant, that a builder never runs during inference at all, is what a reader expects and is not quite what holds, so it is worth being precise about which part is load-bearing. A trial evaluates a builder while a parameter is being inferred; what it does not do is let the builder into the fixpoint. Each evaluation is of a builder whose arguments are already bound, to a candidate rather than to an inference variable, and its result is checked rather than solved against. Nothing runs backwards, nothing is unified, and no builder observes a partially-solved state. That is the difference between a proposer the checker verifies and a participant the checker trusts, and this proposal admits only the first. It is stated so that later ergonomic pressure has something to push against, and the sentence to push against is the one this note follows, not the slogan.</p>
        </emu-note>

      <emu-clause id="sec-iscompiletimeevaluable" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          IsCompileTimeEvaluable (
            _expr_: a Parse Node,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether _expr_ can be evaluated during type checking, reading only values known at that time.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _expr_ is a literal, return *true*.
          1. If _expr_ is a reference to a binding, then
            1. If the binding is a parameter of the function under evaluation, or a local binding of that function, return *true*.
            1. If the binding is immutable and its initializer is compile-time evaluable, return *true*.
            1. If the binding is a value generic parameter or a type generic parameter, return *true*.
            1. Return *false*.
          1. If _expr_ is a function call, then
            1. Let _callee_ be the expression that _expr_ calls.
            1. Let _args_ be the List of argument expressions of _expr_.
            1. If IsCompileTimeEvaluable(_callee_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. If _callee_ does not denote a compile-time-evaluable function, return *false*.
            1. For each element _arg_ of _args_, do
              1. If IsCompileTimeEvaluable(_arg_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Return *true*.
          1. If every operand of _expr_ is compile-time evaluable and the result of _expr_'s operator is determined by its operands, return *true*.
          1. Return *false*.
        </emu-alg>

        <p>A <dfn id="compile-time-evaluable-function">compile-time-evaluable function</dfn> is one whose body reads only its parameters, its own local bindings, immutable bindings whose initializers are compile-time evaluable, and other compile-time-evaluable functions. It may create local state and mutate it. Its body contains no `await`, no `yield`, no direct or indirect `eval`, no use of the Function constructor, and no construction of a Proxy; regular expressions are within the fragment. It performs no input or output and reads no ambient mutable state, none of which it can name under the reading rule above, which is why the discipline is a property of what the code can name rather than a wall around what it does.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>This is the same notion the `where` clauses and metadata annotations of the extensions require, and it is what lets a `switch` over a generic parameter be evaluated to a type during checking. A function that is not compile-time evaluable remains an ordinary function in expression position; only its use in type position is restricted. Local mutation is admitted because an accumulator array or a visited `Set` is how a walk over a type is written; shared module-level mutable state is not evaluable and is rejected statically, which also disposes of the hand-rolled memo cache, since the memoization of <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluatebuildercall"></emu-xref> is the supported mechanism and is observably equivalent. Fixing the fragment's grammar and semantics as a normative implementation target in its own right, with a conformance suite, is <emu-xref href="#annex-evaluable-fragment"></emu-xref>; the discipline above is the boundary it pins.</p>
        </emu-note>
            <emu-note>
        <p>A <emu-xref href="#sec-preprocessor-modules">preprocessor module</emu-xref> and every export of it used as a replacement decorator are required to be evaluable, for the reason a type builder is: both are user code an implementation runs while compiling, and the discipline is what makes the run deterministic. There is one notion here, not two.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-deferred-applications">
        <h1>Deferred Applications</h1>
        <p>A call whose arguments involve an unbound generic parameter cannot evaluate yet, and is carried as a type: the ~application~ Type Record of <emu-xref href="#table-type-record-kinds"></emu-xref>. The <dfn id="deferred-application-record">deferred application Type Record</dfn> of a |ComputedType| has [[Builder]] the function its callee resolves to and, for each argument in order, an element of [[Arguments]] that is: the TypeParameter Parse Node where the argument is a reference to an unbound generic parameter; the deferred application Type Record where the argument is itself a |ComputedType| reading one; and otherwise the argument's compile-time value, a Type Object contributing its [[TypeRecord]].</p>

        <p>Identity is by that key. SameTypeWithAssumptions compares two ~application~ Records by builder identity and SameArgumentList, and Type Objects are interned, so two mentions of `partial(T)` in one generic body are one Type Object before anything is known about `T`. At specialization, each TypeParameter Parse Node among the [[Arguments]] is replaced by the parameter's binding, and the application evaluates by <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluatebuildercall"></emu-xref>.</p>

        <p>Two compound compile-time constants, arrays or plain objects of compile-time constants, are <dfn id="structurally-equivalent">structurally equivalent</dfn> when they have the same shape and their leaves are pairwise SameValue, a Type Object comparing by identity and a RegExp by its `source` and `flags`. A compound constant argument contributes to an application's identity by structural equivalence rather than by reference, so `pick(User, ['id', 'name'])` written twice is one application although the array is a fresh object at each occurrence.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>An unbound parameter is represented by its declaring Parse Node rather than by a Type Record kind of its own, because a parameter is never a type by itself anywhere this specification currently reaches; what a bare parameter annotation denotes inside a generic body belongs to the generics work, and the hook for it is recorded in <emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>. Structural equivalence for compound constants is the value semantics the Composites proposal gives tuples and records; it is stated directly here so that the rule does not depend on that proposal, and <emu-xref href="#sec-composites"></emu-xref>, which specifies composites for this design, coincides with it by construction.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-evaluatebuildercall" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          EvaluateBuilderCall (
            _f_: a compile-time-evaluable function,
            _args_: a List,
          ): either a normal completion containing a Type Object or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It evaluates one type-level call, with memoization and a fixpoint for recursion, and checks the contracts of <emu-xref href="#sec-checked-contracts"></emu-xref> against its result.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _key_ be CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~application~, [[Builder]]: _f_, [[Arguments]]: _args_ }).
          1. If the evaluation memo contains an entry for _key_, return that entry's Type Object.
          1. If the in-flight List contains _key_, then
            1. If no placeholder exists for _key_, let the placeholder for _key_ be a fresh Type Object, created without interning and distinguished from every other value.
            1. Return the placeholder for _key_.
          1. Append _key_ to the in-flight List.
          1. Let _result_ be Completion(the evaluation of the body of _f_ with the elements of _args_ as its arguments under the semantics of this clause).
          1. Remove _key_ from the in-flight List.
          1. Let _value_ be ? _result_.
          1. If _value_ is not a Type Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If a placeholder exists for _key_, then
            1. If _value_ is the placeholder for _key_, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Let _final_ be the Type Record obtained from _value_.[[TypeRecord]] by replacing each position that holds the placeholder with a reference back to the type being defined, forming a cyclic Type Record.
            1. If no replaced position is a reference position, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Set _value_ to GetTypeObject(_final_).
          1. Record _value_ in the evaluation memo for _key_.
          1. Return _value_.
        </emu-alg>

        <p>The <dfn id="evaluation-memo">evaluation memo</dfn> is a specification-level map from canonical ~application~ Type Records to Type Objects, and the in-flight List tracks the keys under evaluation. Each distinct key evaluates once per program; every later occurrence, in any module, yields the same interned Type Object. An implementation may realize both devices in any way that yields these results.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The fixpoint is what makes a builder work on a recursive type with no API surface: a builder mapping over a type that references itself re-enters itself with the same key, receives the placeholder, and the placeholder resolves at return into the same kind of named-back-edge cyclic type that a recursive alias produces from syntax, validated by the same cycle rule (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-alias-declarations"></emu-xref>). The two throws distinguish the two failures: a call whose entire result is its own placeholder learnt nothing and is unproductive recursion, and a cycle resolved at a position that embeds the layout inline would demand an infinite layout. A recursion that never repeats a key, a genuinely infinite family, is not caught here; it runs into the budget of <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref>. This mechanism is the computational twin of the coinductive assumption Lists that SameTypeWithAssumptions and IsSubtype already carry.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-evaluation-budget">
        <h1>The Evaluation Budget</h1>
        <p>Evaluation under this clause is metered by two host-defined limits: a count of evaluation steps and a count of constructed Type Records, each per top-level type-position evaluation. Every operation that evaluates a type is metered, the instantiation of a generic type alias among them, since an unmetered one is a way to recurse without spending the budget. For a given source text, two hosts either produce, position for position, the same interned types, or at least one reports a type error: the limits decide whether compilation fails, never what it produces.</p>

        <p>The budget bounds a computation, which either completes or is abandoned and reported. A rule requiring a SEARCH — over the types an argument could take, rather than over a fixed evaluation — is not metered by it and is refused where it would otherwise arise (<emu-xref href="#sec-higher-kinded-parameters"></emu-xref>), because truncating a search under a budget yields a wrong answer where truncating a computation yields none.</p>

        <p>A limit must be one the implementation can actually reach. An implementation whose own resources — a native stack, a heap — are exhausted before the limit is has not met this clause, whatever the limit's value: the evaluation ends in a way no program can observe and no diagnostic names, which is the outcome this clause exists to prevent. Where an implementation cannot raise its own capacity, it lowers the limit instead. A limit above what an implementation can reach is not a limit.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>This bears on any floor a host is asked to meet. A step count in the millions bounds a computation that widens as it runs, and bounds nothing about one that RECURSES: a self-referential type alias reaches an implementation's native stack in thousands of steps, not millions, so an implementation meeting a high floor and reporting nothing has satisfied the number and failed the requirement. The requirement is that exhaustion be reported, and a host chooses limits low enough that it is.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <p>Exhaustion is not an abrupt completion the evaluated code can observe. No `try` statement within the evaluation handles it; the evaluation is abandoned, and EvaluateToTypeObject of the containing type-position expression is ~empty~, with a diagnostic naming the outermost call and the deepest frames.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>"Not an abrupt completion the evaluated code can observe" is a requirement on how exhaustion is REPRESENTED, not only on what it means. An implementation that reports exhaustion by throwing gives a `where` predicate or a builder a `try` that catches it, and the evaluation then continues on a budget it has already overrun. Recording exhaustion as a state of the evaluation, so that every metered operation after it does nothing and the containing evaluation fails whatever the evaluated code does with what it caught, has the behaviour this clause describes. The diagnostic belongs at the containing evaluation for the same reason: the operation that spends the last step is wherever the budget happened to run out, which is not the outermost call this clause asks to name.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Making exhaustion catchable inside evaluation would let a program's types depend on the host's limits: the same source would elaborate differently under a large budget and a small one, and the memo would cache whichever answer came first, which is nondeterminism through the error channel. Graceful degradation therefore has to be deterministic and structural: a builder takes an explicit cap as an argument and computes the identical type on every host, rather than falling back when fuel runs out. Bounding a deliberately Turing-complete type language by limits has precedent; the magnitudes are fixed here as floors a host meets or exceeds: ten million evaluation steps and one million constructed Type Records per top-level evaluation, ten thousand automaton states for the pattern decision procedure, and sixty-four specializations for a trial, calibrated against the erased-system limits the design cites. A host exceeding a floor can only turn a failure into a result, never a result into a different one, so the invariant above is undisturbed.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-evaluatetotypeobject" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          EvaluateToTypeObject (
            _expr_: a Parse Node,
          ): either a Type Object or ~empty~
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It evaluates _expr_, which appears in type position, to the Type Object it denotes, or defers it, or returns ~empty~ if it does not evaluate to one.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If IsCompileTimeEvaluable(_expr_) is *false*, return ~empty~.
          1. If _expr_ is a |ComputedType| and _expr_ reads a generic parameter that is not bound, return GetTypeObject(the deferred application Type Record of _expr_ (<emu-xref href="#sec-deferred-applications"></emu-xref>)).
          1. Let _result_ be Completion(the evaluation of _expr_ during type checking in which each call of a compile-time-evaluable function is performed by EvaluateBuilderCall).
          1. If _result_ is an abrupt completion, return ~empty~.
          1. Let _value_ be _result_.[[Value]].
          1. If _value_ is not a Type Object, return ~empty~.
          1. Return _value_.
        </emu-alg>
        <p>It is a type error if an expression appears in type position and EvaluateToTypeObject of it is ~empty~ (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-errors"></emu-xref>). When the ~empty~ arises from an abrupt completion whose value is an Error, the type error's diagnostic carries that Error's message, prefixed by the source text of the type-position expression; when it arises from the budget, the diagnostic is the one <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref> describes. Diagnostic content is not observable to the evaluated program.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Evaluation during type checking uses the ordinary evaluation semantics of the operators and calls involved, restricted to the compile-time-evaluable fragment, so no separate evaluator is defined. Because enum and sealed-class exhaustiveness is checked, a `switch` used to map a value to a type covers every case its argument can take and therefore yields a Type Object rather than *undefined*. A thrown exception becoming the diagnostic is the design's authored-error story: a builder states its own misuse message, which an erased type language cannot.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-type-errors">
      <h1>Type Errors</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="type-error">type error</dfn> is a violation of the type system detected by the type checker: an assignment whose source is not assignable to its target, a call whose arguments match no signature, a type annotation that is not compile-time evaluable, and the like. The clauses that follow state such a violation with the phrase &ldquo;it is a type error if&rdquo;.</p>

      <p>A type error that is determinable before a source text runs is an Early Error: a source text that contains one is rejected rather than evaluated. Detection is the work of the <dfn id="checking-pass">checking pass</dfn>, one pass per Script or Module over the same Parse Nodes the parser produced, performed after the source text is parsed and before it is evaluated. The purely syntactic type rules are applied together with the other Early Errors at parse; the rules that evaluate &mdash; a computed type, a builder call, a hook of the metadata protocol (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-metadata"></emu-xref>) &mdash; are applied by the checking pass, under the discipline and budget of <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>. For a Script the pass runs when evaluation of the script would begin, before the first step of its body; for a Module it runs per module, after the modules it depends on have been evaluated and before the module's own body, the point at which evaluation order reaches it. Before applying any judgment that consults them, the pass processes the source text's own type declarations &mdash; its type aliases, its interfaces, its `meta` declarations, and the implicit cast operators of its `primitive` blocks (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-operator-blocks"></emu-xref>) &mdash; in source order, which is what makes a meta type declared in a source text govern a judgment in the same source text; a declaration the pass has processed is not processed again when evaluation reaches its position.</p>

      <p>A check that cannot be resolved statically, because a value reaches a typed position only as the ~any~ type, is deferred to run time. <emu-xref href="#sec-runtime-enforcement"></emu-xref> specifies where these deferred checks are inserted and what they throw: a *TypeError* when a value is not of the required type, and a *RangeError* when a numeric value is outside the range of its type.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The design documents sometimes describe a statically-determinable violation, such as assigning a `uint32` to a `uint8` binding, as a &ldquo;*TypeError*&rdquo;. This specification realizes such a violation as an Early Error, and reserves a thrown *TypeError* for the ~any~ boundary and other genuinely dynamic checks. The distinction is observable: an Early Error rejects the source text before any of it runs, whereas a thrown *TypeError* occurs during evaluation. Treating a statically-determinable type violation as a whole-source rejection matches a strict, check-before-run type system; whether some violations should instead be deferred to a thrown error at the offending operation was a decision left open when this clause was first written and is now settled, in this direction, for the reason that follows: the compile-time evaluation phase (<emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>) already commits every implementation to the per-module checking pass this clause now names, so the cost that once made deferral attractive is sunk, and a diagnostic at the declaration is strictly better than the same diagnostic at first execution. For a module the rejection is observable after the module's dependencies have evaluated, which is when the pass runs; that two-phase timing is not new, being exactly how an error thrown by a module's own evaluation already behaves.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-type-grammar">
    <h1>Grammar</h1>
    <p>This clause adds the syntax for writing a type. It is grammar only: it defines what may be written and where, while the meaning of each form is given by the clauses that follow. Every production here is additive, and every construct it introduces is a syntax error in the current language, so no source text changes meaning.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The type grammar is a separate grammar from the expression grammar, reached from expression contexts only through the annotation and operator productions of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-annotations"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-types-in-expression-position"></emu-xref>. Keeping the two apart is what lets a type use `|`, `&amp;`, `{`, and `(` with meanings unrelated to their expression counterparts.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <emu-clause id="sec-lexical-grammar-for-types">
      <h1>Lexical Grammar</h1>

      <emu-clause id="sec-type-punctuators">
        <h1>Punctuators</h1>
        <p>Two punctuators are added. Neither token sequence occurs in any currently valid source text, which is what allows them to be added without changing the meaning of an existing program.</p>

        <emu-grammar type="definition">
          Punctuator ::
            OptionalChainingPunctuator
            <ins>TypePunctuator</ins>
            OtherPunctuator

          <ins>TypePunctuator :: one of
            `.&lt;` `:=`</ins>
        </emu-grammar>

        <emu-note>
          <p>`.&lt;` introduces a type argument list (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-references"></emu-xref>). Today a `.` must be followed by an identifier name, a private name, or a numeric part of a literal, so `.&lt;` is never produced by valid source text. The leading `.` is what distinguishes generic application from a comparison: `a.&lt;b&gt;(c)` cannot be read as `a &lt; b &gt; (c)`, which is the ambiguity that prevents the bare angle-bracket form. Because a numeric literal is lexed by longest match, `1.&lt;2` still lexes as the literal `1.` followed by `&lt;`, and so continues to mean a comparison.</p>
          <p>`:=` introduces a typed initializer or a conversion (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-in-expression-position"></emu-xref>). A `:` immediately followed by `=` does not occur in valid source text.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-input-element-type">
        <h1>The InputElementType Goal Symbol</h1>
        <p>A type argument list is delimited by `&lt;` and `&gt;`, and type arguments nest, so a source text may end one list immediately inside another. Under the ordinary lexical goals the two closing punctuators would be lexed as a single `&gt;&gt;` token. A goal symbol is therefore added in which `&gt;` is always a single punctuator.</p>

        <emu-grammar type="definition">
          <ins>InputElementType ::
            WhiteSpace
            LineTerminator
            Comment
            CommonToken
            RightBracePunctuator</ins>
        </emu-grammar>

        <p>The |InputElementType| goal symbol is used in all syntactic grammar contexts where a |TypeArguments| or a |TypeParameters| is being parsed, between its opening `.&lt;` or `&lt;` and its closing `&gt;`. When this goal symbol is used, the punctuators `&gt;&gt;`, `&gt;&gt;&gt;`, `&gt;=`, `&gt;&gt;=`, and `&gt;&gt;&gt;=` are not recognized; a `&gt;` is lexed as the single-character punctuator `&gt;`.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>This is the same device the specification already uses to lex `/` differently where a regular expression may appear. It resolves the nesting problem lexically rather than by a parser rule, so `Map.&lt;string, [].&lt;uint8&gt;&gt;` closes both lists. A shift operator inside a type argument list must be parenthesized, which is only relevant to a value argument, since a shift cannot otherwise appear in a type.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-imaginary-literals">
        <h1>Imaginary Literals</h1>
        <p>An imaginary literal is a numeric literal with the suffix `i`, denoting a value on the imaginary axis. The suffix occupies the slot a numeric literal followed immediately by an identifier start occupies today, which is a syntax error, and is the slot the `n` suffix of a BigInt literal already uses.</p>

        <emu-grammar type="definition">
          NumericLiteral ::
            DecimalLiteral
            DecimalBigIntegerLiteral
            <ins>DecimalImaginaryLiteral</ins>
            NonDecimalIntegerLiteral[+Sep]
            NonDecimalIntegerLiteral[+Sep] BigIntLiteralSuffix
            LegacyOctalIntegerLiteral

          <ins>DecimalImaginaryLiteral ::
            DecimalLiteral ImaginaryLiteralSuffix</ins>

          <ins>ImaginaryLiteralSuffix :: `i`</ins>
        </emu-grammar>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Unlike the BigInt suffix, which attaches only to an integer literal, the imaginary suffix attaches to any |DecimalLiteral|, so `4i`, `2.5i`, and `1e3i` are all imaginary literals. The rule that the source character following a numeric literal must not be an identifier start continues to apply, so `4if` remains a syntax error. The type of an imaginary literal is defined by the complex numbers extension.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-type-expressions">
      <h1>Type Expressions</h1>
      <p>A |Type| is the syntax of a type. Union and intersection compose types, and the remaining forms are primary.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        <ins>Type[Yield, Await] :
          UnionType[?Yield, ?Await]
          FunctionType[?Yield, ?Await]

        UnionType[Yield, Await] :
          `|`? IntersectionType[?Yield, ?Await]
          UnionType[?Yield, ?Await] `|` IntersectionType[?Yield, ?Await]

        IntersectionType[Yield, Await] :
          `&amp;`? PrimaryType[?Yield, ?Await]
          IntersectionType[?Yield, ?Await] `&amp;` PrimaryType[?Yield, ?Await]

        PrimaryType[Yield, Await] :
          `shared` PrimaryType[?Yield, ?Await]
          PredefinedType
          LiteralType
          TypeReference[?Yield, ?Await]
          ComputedType[?Yield, ?Await]
          KeyOfType[?Yield, ?Await]
          ArrayOrTupleType[?Yield, ?Await]
          ObjectType[?Yield, ?Await]
          ReferenceType[?Yield, ?Await]
          CoverParenthesizedTypeAndFunctionTypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]

        PredefinedType : one of
          `void` `null` `undefined`

        LiteralType :
          NumericLiteral
          `-` NumericLiteral
          `Infinity`
          `-` `Infinity`
          `NaN`
          `-` `NaN`
          StringLiteral
          `true`
          `false`

        ReferenceType[Yield, Await] :
          `ref` PrimaryType[?Yield, ?Await]

        KeyOfType[Yield, Await] :
          `keyof` PrimaryType[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <emu-note>
        <p><ins>The optional `|` and `&amp;` above are a leading SEPARATOR and carry no meaning: `| _T_` is the type _T_, whether or not _T_ is itself a union, and likewise for `&amp;`. They exist so that a type written across several lines can align its members, as the design writes `type Shape =` followed by a `|` on each line. Being on the base case of each production rather than a production of their own, they may appear at most once and only before the first member, so `_A_ | | _B_` has no parse. A leading token with no type after it has none either, the token being a separator that happens to come first rather than a prefix operator.</ins></p>
        <p>|FunctionType| is an alternative of |Type| rather than of |UnionType|, because a function type's return extends as far to the right as it can: `(string) =&gt; uint32 | null` returns `uint32 | null`. A function type is therefore not by itself a member of a union or an intersection, and putting one in a union requires parenthesizing it. That is true on either side, so the order of a union's members never matters.</p>
        <p>`void` and `null` are reserved words and so cannot be reached through |TypeReference|, which is why they are given their own production. `undefined` is not a reserved word but a global binding, an ordinary identifier; it is given its own production here as well, so that `undefined` in type position always names the undefined type rather than depending on the global binding being in scope, matching the treatment of `null`. The other built-in type names, `uint8` and the rest, are ordinary identifiers and are reached through |TypeReference|; they are shadowable, so a program that declares its own binding named `uint8` is unaffected.</p>
        <p>|ReferenceType| takes a |PrimaryType| operand, so `ref uint8 | null` is a union of `ref uint8` and `null`. `ref` is a contextual keyword: it is a valid identifier today, so `ref` followed by a token that cannot begin a type is an identifier.</p>
        <p>`keyof` is likewise a contextual keyword, and a |KeyOfType| binds tighter than union and intersection, so `keyof A | B` is the union of `keyof A` and `B`. Its result is the union of the operand's key types, one literal type per named member. The full definition over each kind is given by <emu-xref href="#sec-keyof"></emu-xref>, producing a union of the literal types of <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref>.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-clause id="sec-type-references">
        <h1>Type References and Type Arguments</h1>
        <p>A type reference names a type, optionally applying type arguments to it. Generic application always uses `.&lt;`, in a type and in an expression alike.</p>

        <emu-grammar type="definition">
          <ins>TypeReference[Yield, Await] :
            TypeName[?Yield, ?Await] TypeArguments[?Yield, ?Await]?

          TypeName[Yield, Await] :
            IdentifierReference[?Yield, ?Await]
            TypeName[?Yield, ?Await] `.` IdentifierName

          TypeArguments[Yield, Await] :
            `.&lt;` TypeArgumentList[?Yield, ?Await] `,`? `&gt;`

          TypeArgumentList[Yield, Await] :
            TypeArgument[?Yield, ?Await]
            TypeArgumentList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` TypeArgument[?Yield, ?Await]

          TypeArgument[Yield, Await] :
            Type[?Yield, ?Await]
            <ins>BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] `:` Type[?Yield, ?Await]</ins></ins>
        </emu-grammar>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Because `.&lt;` is a single punctuator, a qualified name and an application are told apart lexically: in `Temporal.Unit` the `.` is followed by an identifier, while in `Map.&lt;string, uint8&gt;` the `.&lt;` opens an argument list, so a |TypeName| cannot extend across it.</p>
          <p>A type argument is written as a |Type| even when it is a value, as the width in `int.&lt;8&gt;` and the lane count in `vector.&lt;float32, 4&gt;` are. A numeric or string literal argument is a |LiteralType|, and a name that resolves to a value generic parameter is a |TypeReference|, so the syntax need not distinguish the two; the clause on generics decides which a given parameter expects. A value argument that is a computed expression, such as an arithmetic combination of value generic parameters, is written through a |ComputedType| (<emu-xref href="#sec-computed-types"></emu-xref>): the builder call is the spelling for a computed argument, evaluated once its parameters are bound, and the argument position takes no further computation syntax of its own. A `where` clause (<emu-xref href="#sec-generic-where"></emu-xref>) constrains the values a variadic or value parameter admits.</p>
          <p><ins>A |TypeArgument| may NAME the parameter it supplies, `Box.&lt;Element: uint8&gt;`, which lets an application skip a parameter that has a default rather than repeat it: given `Grid&lt;T = float64, Rows = 4, Cols = 4&gt;`, `Grid.&lt;Cols: 8&gt;` says what differs where `Grid.&lt;float64, 4, 8&gt;` repeats what does not. The separator is `:` rather than `=` to match the named ARGUMENT form of a call, and because `=` already means "the default if none is supplied" in the declaration - the opposite sense at the same position.</ins></p>
          <p><ins>Each parameter takes, in order: its positional argument where one was supplied, otherwise the named argument bearing its name, otherwise its |TypeParameterDefault|. A parameter left by all three is an error, as it is for a positional application.</ins></p>
          <p><ins>It is a Syntax Error for a |TypeArgumentList| to contain a |TypeArgument| of the second form whose |BindingIdentifier| does not name a |TypeParameter| of the type or function being applied. This reaches the forms that take |TypeArguments| WITHOUT declaring parameters - an |ArrayOrTupleType| such as `[4].&lt;uint8&gt;`, whose extent and element the grammar does not name - so a named argument there names nothing and is refused. A name that matched nothing would otherwise be discarded silently and the parameter would take its default, which makes a misspelling change what a program means.</ins></p>
          <p><ins>It is a Syntax Error for two |TypeArgument|s of the second form in one |TypeArgumentList| to have the same |BindingIdentifier|, or for one to name a |TypeParameter| already supplied positionally. It is a Syntax Error for a |TypeArgument| of the first form to follow one of the second, so the positional arguments are exactly the leading ones and a positional argument's meaning does not depend on the names used after it.</ins></p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-computed-types">
        <h1>Computed Types</h1>
        <p>A type position admits a call. The callee is written as a type reference and the arguments as ordinary expressions, so `partial(User)`, `keysOf(T)`, and `pick(User, ['id', 'name'])` are types, as is a call of a call where a function returns a function that computes one.</p>

        <emu-grammar type="definition">
          <ins>ComputedType[Yield, Await] :
            TypeReference[?Yield, ?Await] Arguments[?Yield, ?Await]
            ComputedType[?Yield, ?Await] Arguments[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
        </emu-grammar>

        <p>A |ComputedType| is a compile-time type expression and is governed by <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>: the call must be compile-time evaluable, and the type the |ComputedType| denotes is the Type Object that EvaluateToTypeObject returns for it. A call that is not evaluable, that completes abruptly, or that does not produce a Type Object is a type error by the rule stated there.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The form is additive twice over. Every position in which a |Type| may appear is new syntax, so no existing program contains one; and within the grammar of this specification a |TypeName| followed by `(` derived nothing before this production, so no program written against the earlier grammar changes meaning either. Disambiguation costs one token: after a type reference, `.&lt;` opens type arguments, `(` opens a call, and any other token ends the reference.</p>
          <p>Only a call is admitted, deliberately. The design's rule is that an expression evaluating to a Type Object during checking is a valid type, and the call is the one expression form the type grammar needs to reach all of it: a conditional over IsAssignable, a map over a union's members, or any richer computation is a function away, written inside the builder rather than inline in the annotation. Keeping the surface to one form also keeps the evaluability requirement attached to one production.</p>
          <p>The same call in expression position is an ordinary call and needs nothing from this clause: a builder is an ordinary function, and calling it at run time yields the same interned Type Object the type position denotes. This production makes the call writable where a type is required; it does not change what the call is.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-keyof">
        <h1>Static Semantics of keyof</h1>
        <p>A |KeyOfType| denotes GetTypeObject of the Type Record that KeyTypesOf returns for the Type Record its operand denotes. It is a type error if KeyTypesOf of that Record is ~empty~.</p>

        <emu-clause id="sec-keytypesof" type="abstract operation">
          <h1>
            KeyTypesOf (
              _t_: a Type Record,
            ): either a Type Record or ~empty~
          </h1>
          <dl class="header">
            <dt>description</dt>
            <dd>It returns the type of the keys of _t_, or ~empty~ where _t_ has no keys.</dd>
          </dl>
          <emu-alg>
            1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~object~, then
              1. Let _keys_ be a new empty List.
              1. For each element _p_ of _t_.[[Properties]], do
                1. If _p_.[[Key]] is a String, let _base_ be the Type Record of the String type.
                1. Else, let _base_ be the Type Record of the Symbol type.
                1. Append the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~literal~, [[Value]]: _p_.[[Key]], [[Base]]: _base_ } to _keys_.
              1. For each element _x_ of _t_.[[IndexSignatures]], append _x_.[[KeyType]] to _keys_.
              1. Return CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~union~, [[Members]]: _keys_ }).
            1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~intersection~, then
              1. Let _keys_ be a new empty List.
              1. For each element _m_ of _t_.[[Members]], do
                1. Let _k_ be KeyTypesOf(_m_).
                1. If _k_ is ~empty~, return ~empty~.
                1. Append _k_ to _keys_.
              1. Return CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~union~, [[Members]]: _keys_ }).
            1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~union~, then
              1. If _t_.[[Members]] is empty, return _t_.
              1. Let _first_ be KeyTypesOf(_t_.[[Members]][0]).
              1. If _first_ is ~empty~, return ~empty~.
              1. Let _kept_ be a List of the union members of _first_: the elements of _first_.[[Members]] where _first_.[[Kind]] is ~union~, and &laquo; _first_ &raquo; otherwise.
              1. For each integer _i_ such that 1 &le; _i_ and _i_ &lt; the number of elements of _t_.[[Members]], do
                1. Let _k_ be KeyTypesOf(_t_.[[Members]][_i_]).
                1. If _k_ is ~empty~, return ~empty~.
                1. Remove from _kept_ each element that is not assignable (IsAssignable) to _k_.
              1. Return CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~union~, [[Members]]: _kept_ }).
            1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~parameterized~, return KeyTypesOf(_t_.[[Base]]).
            1. Return ~empty~.
          </emu-alg>
          <emu-note>
            <p>These are the rules of the design's `keysOf`, which computes the same function over a Type Object held in a variable, so the operator and the kit function cannot drift apart. A union's keys are the common keys, computed by keeping the first arm's keys that remain assignable to each further arm's, which is what lets the literal `'x'` survive against an arm whose keys come from a `string`-keyed index signature. An intersection's keys are the union of its members' keys. A Symbol key contributes its own literal type (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref>), so the operation is total over the keys an object type can have. A refinement has its base's keys, so `keyof` sees through a brand and through the `where` of a dependent record type (<emu-xref href="#sec-dependent-record-types"></emu-xref>) to the properties underneath, which is the only reading on which `keyof` and the property mappers agree about a refined object. A kind with no keys is ~empty~ and a type error, matching the kit's authored throw. `keyof never` is distinct from that case: `never` is the empty ~union~, so the union step above returns it unchanged and `keyof never` is `never`, not ~empty~ and not an error. The kit's `keysOf` fold must include the empty-union base case to agree, since its arm-intersection fold does not otherwise reach it; a `keysOf` written as a non-empty reduction over the arms should special-case the empty union to `never`.</p>
          </emu-note>
        </emu-clause>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-array-and-tuple-types">
        <h1>Array and Tuple Types</h1>
        <p>An array type is a fixed or dynamic count of elements of one type, and a tuple type is a fixed count of individually typed positions. Both are written in brackets, and are told apart by whether a type argument list follows the closing bracket.</p>

        <emu-grammar type="definition">
          <ins>ArrayOrTupleType[Yield, Await] :
            `[` `]`
            `[` `]` TypeArguments[?Yield, ?Await]
            `[` ArrayExtent[?Yield, ?Await] `]` TypeArguments[?Yield, ?Await]
            `[` TupleElementList[?Yield, ?Await] `,`? `]`

          ArrayExtent[Yield, Await] :
            AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]

          TupleElementList[Yield, Await] :
            TupleElement[?Yield, ?Await]
            TupleElementList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` TupleElement[?Yield, ?Await]

          TupleElement[Yield, Await] :
            Type[?Yield, ?Await] Initializer[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]?
            `...` Type[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
        </emu-grammar>

        <emu-note>
          <p>`[]` alone is the array of `any`, which is the bound of the array and tuple family, so a type parameter constrained by it is satisfied by any array or tuple. `[].&lt;T&gt;` is a dynamic array, `[N].&lt;T&gt;` a fixed one, and `[T1, T2]` a tuple. What the brackets carry is the EXTENT &mdash; whether it is stated, and what it is. Whether the value owns its storage is a different question and is not asked here: `Span.&lt;T&gt;` (<emu-xref href="#sec-span-type"></emu-xref>) is the type of a value that does not, and every form above is assignable to it. A tuple's trailing position may carry a default through |Initializer|, and a tuple may spread another tuple or an array type at either end.</p>
          <p>The first three alternatives and the fourth are distinguished only after the closing `]`, by whether a |TypeArguments| follows, and their bracketed contents belong to different grammars: an extent is an expression and a tuple element list is a list of types. A single-element case such as `[uint8]` therefore cannot be resolved by bounded lookahead. This specification resolves it with a cover grammar, as it does for parenthesized expressions and arrow parameters: the bracketed text is parsed under a cover production and refined to an extent expression or a tuple element list once the token after the closing `]` is known, an extent when a |TypeArguments| follows and a tuple otherwise. The committee may still prefer to distinguish the two forms by their opening bracket, which would remove the cover grammar rather than change the meaning of any program it already accepts; this is a decision worth taking before the syntax is final.</p>
        </emu-note>
        <p>It is a type error if a |TupleElement| with no |Initializer| follows one that has an |Initializer|, and it is a type error if a |TupleElement| with an |Initializer| follows a `...` element. A tuple is positional, so the only way to leave a position unsupplied is to stop short of it, which can omit a suffix and nothing else: a default anywhere but the tail could never be taken, since supplying the position after it supplies it too. The minimum length of <emu-xref href="#sec-array-membership"></emu-xref> therefore counts a prefix, and every length a tuple admits leaves unsupplied only positions that carry a default. A shape wanting to omit a member in the middle names its members, which is an object type.</p>

        <p>A tuple element's |Initializer| is its position's default, and [[Initial]] is that default's value. The |Initializer| must be compile-time evaluable (<emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>) and it is a type error otherwise: a tuple type is interned, so one Type Object serves every use of the type and the value in it is shared by all of them. A compile-time evaluable default is a value type, a string, or an enumerator, each of which is copied where it is used, so the sharing is unobservable; a default that allocated would hand every value of the type the same object.</p>

        <p>An array in a tuple position is converted position-wise, as one in an array position is converted element-wise: each element is converted to the type of the position it occupies, and an element past the declared positions is converted to the element type of the rest that collects it. A position the array does not supply takes its default, converted to that position's type as a supplied value would be, so the length may fall anywhere from the number of positions carrying neither a rest nor a default up to the number of positions, and beyond it only where a rest collects the surplus. Conversion rather than a bare check is what every boundary of this specification performs, and a tuple literal builds a new Array, so no storage another binding holds is rewritten by it.</p>

        <p>A fixed extent is part of the type and does not change. An operation that would give an array of such a type a length other than its extent is a *TypeError* exception, whether it is a store to an index at or past the extent, an assignment to `length`, or a method that appends or removes. A request for <em>capacity</em> past the extent is refused for the same reason although it changes no length: the storage of a fixed extent holds exactly that many elements for the life of the array, so `reserve` past the extent is a *TypeError* exception, `reserve` within it is a no-op, and the capacity of such an array is its extent rather than any allocation's. Stating the rule in terms of length alone would miss this, `reserve` being exactly the operation a length comparison cannot see (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-liveness"></emu-xref>). The extent is a compile-time constant: the layout computes a `byteLength` from it and an array view's `byteElementLength` defaults from it, so an extent that an ordinary store could change would not be constant for either. A growable `[` `]` `.` `&lt;` _T_ `&gt;` has no extent to hold and grows as an Array does.</p>

        <p>One type describes every count an array reports or accepts: its `length`, its `capacity` (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-liveness"></emu-xref>), an index used to read or write an element, and the length of a view over a buffer. That type is the <dfn id="index-type">index type</dfn>, and it is `uint64`. Its full range is ALLOCATABLE AND USABLE: an array type is not bounded by the maximum length of an ordinary Array, since a count type wider than what the container can hold would describe lengths no program could reach. An instance is an Array in its methods and its element behaviour and not in its extent limit. The width is `uint64` rather than `uint32` because a view's length comes from its buffer and is not bounded by the maximum length of an Array (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-views"></emu-xref>); an owned array is an Array and is bounded by it, so the range above 2<sup>32</sup> - 1 is unreachable for one and necessary for the other, and one type describes both. `%TypedArray%` is unaffected: its counts are Numbers and remain so (<emu-xref href="#sec-relationship-to-typed-arrays"></emu-xref>). It is a property of the language and not of an array: an array type takes ONE type argument, its element, and there is no per-array spelling that varies the counts' type. Naming it once rather than repeating `uint32` at each site is what lets the width be stated in one place, and what keeps `capacity` comparable with `length` without a conversion &mdash; the invariant that a capacity is at least a length is unstateable if the two are not one type.</p>

        <p>An array type may be written where an expression is expected, and there denotes the type's constructor: `new [100].`&lt;`uint8`&gt;`()` constructs an array of that type, and `class G extends [16].`&lt;`uint8`&gt;` { }` derives a class from it. A construction of a fixed extent has that many elements, each the default value of the element type (<emu-xref href="#sec-default-values"></emu-xref>); a construction of a dynamic one has none and grows. An instance is an Array carrying the element type, so a store to an element is checked against it and the methods of `Array.prototype` apply, and a class deriving from an array type has instances that are Arrays in the same way. Its [[Prototype]] is not `%Array.prototype%` itself but an object that INHERITS from it and adds the capacity operations (<emu-xref href="#sec-capacity-operations"></emu-xref>), so every method of `Array.prototype` reaches an instance unchanged while `capacity`, `reserve`, and `shrinkToFit` reach only an array that has an element type. Placing them on `Array.prototype` would put three members on every array in the language that exist to refuse the receiver they will usually have: `'capacity' in []` would be *true* for an ordinary array and reading it would throw. Two mentions of one array type denote the same constructor, since the types are interned and the constructor is the type's.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The bracketed form is an array literal where an expression is expected, so the two readings meet at `[100].`&lt;`uint8`&gt;`: an array literal cannot be followed by a type argument list, which is what tells them apart. The reading as a type is taken before the brackets are evaluated as a literal, since evaluating them is the reading being replaced.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <p>`shared` before a type marks a type whose storage is shared between the threads of one heap, as `let a: shared [4].&lt;uint8&gt;`, a fixed-length array so placed. It is a type error if the operand of `shared` is itself `shared`. Which types admit the modifier, what it places where, and what a checker may assume of a slot that carries it are given by <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-shared-modifier"></emu-xref>; the grammar is stated here because it is the type grammar, and the grammar is the core's.</p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-object-types">
        <h1>Object Types</h1>
        <p>An object type describes a value by its members. It is the inline form of an interface.</p>
        <p>The relationship runs both ways, and the reverse direction needs a name. The <dfn>structural form</dfn> of an interface type is the ~object~ Type Record whose [[Members]] are the members the interface declares, taken together with those it inherits, and with the interface's type arguments substituted. Every interface has one. A class has none: a class states a construction and an identity as well as a shape, and it is the identity that its type is for.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>This is what lets a value satisfy an interface by having its members, which <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref> reads. Without it the rules would refuse `f({ a: 'a' })` for `interface IExample { a: string; }`, since an interface is a ~nominal~ type (<emu-xref href="#table-type-record-kinds"></emu-xref>) and an object literal's type is ~object~, and the step separating the kinds would answer before any member was inspected. The composite types have relied on the notion since they were specified, naming it in their own subtype rule; this clause is where it is defined rather than assumed.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <emu-grammar type="definition">
          <ins>ObjectType[Yield, Await] :
            `{` `}`
            `{` TypeMemberList[?Yield, ?Await] TypeMemberSeparator? `}`

          TypeMemberList[Yield, Await] :
            TypeMember[?Yield, ?Await]
            TypeMemberList[?Yield, ?Await] TypeMemberSeparator TypeMember[?Yield, ?Await]

          TypeMemberSeparator : one of
            `,` `;`

          TypeMember[Yield, Await] :
            PropertyName[?Yield, ?Await] `?`? TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await] Initializer[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]?
            PropertyName[?Yield, ?Await] `?`? MethodSignature[?Yield, ?Await]
            IndexSignature[?Yield, ?Await]

          MethodSignature[Yield, Await] :
            TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]? `(` FunctionTypeParameterList[?Yield, ?Await]? `,`? `)` TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?

          IndexSignature[Yield, Await] :
            `[` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await] `]` TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
        </emu-grammar>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Members are separated by either `,` or `;`, and a trailing separator is permitted, so both `{ x: float32, y: float32 }` and `{ x: float32; y: float32; }` are written. A `?` marks an optional member.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <p>A member may be declared `readonly`, which sets the [[Readonly]] field of the Property Type Record it contributes. A write to a `readonly` member is a type error, at compile time where the type of the base is known and at run time otherwise, and every assignment form is a write: an assignment operator of any kind, and an increment or decrement. `readonly` is shallow here as it is for a field (<emu-xref href="#sec-typed-classes"></emu-xref>): it fixes the member, not the object the member refers to.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>This is what makes the depth covariance of <emu-xref href="#sec-isobjectsubtype"></emu-xref> sound. That clause admits a subtype through a `readonly` member "since a value read from it and never written through it need only be of the required type", and the second half of that sentence is a rule rather than an observation.</p>
          <p>The guarantee is weaker than a field's, and the difference is where each belongs. A class's `readonly` is a property of the OBJECT: the declaring class says so, the instance carries it, and a write is refused however the instance was reached. An object type's `readonly` is a property of the REFERENCE, since one object may be viewed through both a `readonly` and a writable type and the two views are the same object. The refusal is therefore what the type of the base expression supports, and a write through a base of type `any` is not refused.</p>
        </emu-note>
        <p>An |IndexSignature| constrains the properties an object type does not declare by name: `{ [key: string]: uint32 }` is the type whose remaining string-keyed properties, beyond those declared, are `uint32`. It is a type error if a |TypeMember|'s |Initializer| appears without `?`, since a default belongs to an optional member, the design's `c?: any = []`. A default is a construction feature: it is written where a value of the type is being built, an object literal taking the type, a typed parse (<emu-xref href="#sec-coercejsonvalue"></emu-xref>), and a typed composite creation (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-typeobject-call"></emu-xref>), and never by a check of a value that already exists. A check reads; an object lacking an optional member satisfies the type with the member absent, whether or not the member declares a default, and no check writes the default onto its subject. It is a type error if the key type of an |IndexSignature| is not `string`, `symbol`, `uint32`, or a union of these, and a type error if a member declared in the same |ObjectType| is not assignable to the signature's value type. Each signature contributes an Index Signature Record to the [[IndexSignatures]] field of the ~object~ Type Record the type constructs (<emu-xref href="#sec-object-types-semantics"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-function-types">
        <h1>Function Types</h1>
        <p>A function type is a parameter list and a return type. Its parameters may be named or unnamed, and may carry the `ref` and rest modifiers a declaration's parameters carry.</p>

        <emu-grammar type="definition">
          <ins>FunctionType[Yield, Await] :
            FunctionTypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await] `=&gt;` Type[?Yield, ?Await]

          FunctionTypeParameters[Yield, Await] :
            CoverParenthesizedTypeAndFunctionTypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]

          CoverParenthesizedTypeAndFunctionTypeParameters[Yield, Await] :
            `(` `)`
            `(` FunctionTypeParameterList[?Yield, ?Await] `,`? `)`

          FunctionTypeParameterList[Yield, Await] :
            FunctionTypeParameter[?Yield, ?Await]
            FunctionTypeParameterList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` FunctionTypeParameter[?Yield, ?Await]

          FunctionTypeParameter[Yield, Await] :
            `ref`? Type[?Yield, ?Await]
            `ref`? BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] `?`? TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]
            `...` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]
            `...` Type[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
        </emu-grammar>

        <p>When the production <emu-grammar>PrimaryType : CoverParenthesizedTypeAndFunctionTypeParameters</emu-grammar> is recognized, the interpretation of |CoverParenthesizedTypeAndFunctionTypeParameters| is refined using <emu-grammar>ParenthesizedType : `(` Type `)`</emu-grammar>; it is a type error if the cover does not match that refinement.</p>

        <emu-grammar type="definition">
          <ins>ParenthesizedType[Yield, Await] :
            `(` Type[?Yield, ?Await] `)`</ins>
        </emu-grammar>

        <emu-note>
          <p>`(uint8)` is a parenthesized type and `(uint8) =&gt; uint8` is a function type, and the two are told apart by the token after the closing parenthesis, exactly as a parenthesized expression is told from arrow parameters. The cover grammar is the same device the specification already uses there.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-type-parameters">
      <h1>Type Parameters</h1>
      <p>A generic declaration introduces its parameters in angle brackets. Declaration uses `&lt;`, while application uses `.&lt;`, so the ambiguity that application avoids does not arise at a declaration, where no expression may appear.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        <ins>TypeParameters[Yield, Await] :
          `&lt;` TypeParameterList[?Yield, ?Await] `,`? `&gt;`

        TypeParameterList[Yield, Await] :
          TypeParameter[?Yield, ?Await]
          TypeParameterList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` TypeParameter[?Yield, ?Await]

        TypeParameter[Yield, Await] :
          VarianceModifier? BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypeParameterConstraint[?Yield, ?Await]? TypeParameterDefault[?Yield, ?Await]?

        VarianceModifier : one of
          `in` `out`

        TypeParameterConstraint[Yield, Await] :
          `:` Type[?Yield, ?Await]
          `extends` Type[?Yield, ?Await]

        TypeParameterDefault[Yield, Await] :
          `=` Type[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A |VarianceModifier| declares the parameter's variance, whose meaning is given by <emu-xref href="#sec-generic-variance"></emu-xref>: `out` declares it covariant and `in` contravariant, and a parameter carrying neither is invariant. The words name the positions the parameter may occupy, `out` for the output positions a covariant parameter is restricted to and `in` for the input positions of a contravariant one, which is the spelling of C# and Kotlin whose variance this is.</p>
        <p>`in` is a reserved word, so it can only be a modifier here. `out` is not: it remains an ordinary identifier everywhere, INCLUDING as a type parameter's own name, and is a |VarianceModifier| only where a |BindingIdentifier| follows it immediately. So `&lt;out&gt;`, `&lt;out: T&gt;` and `&lt;out = T&gt;` each declare a parameter NAMED `out`, while `&lt;out T&gt;` declares a covariant `T`, and `&lt;out out&gt;` declares a covariant parameter named `out`. One token of lookahead separates the two readings, and no program that does not write a modifier changes meaning.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A parameter written with `:` is a value generic parameter, whose argument is a value of the named type, as `W` and `H` are in `class GridArray&lt;W: uint32, H: uint32&gt;`. A parameter written with `extends` is a type parameter bounded by a type, as `T` is in `T extends []`. A parameter with neither is an unbounded type parameter. The `where` clause that further constrains a parameter is defined by the generics extension.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-clause id="sec-computed-constraints">
        <h1>Computed Constraints and Literal Inference</h1>
        <p>The |Type| of a |TypeParameterConstraint| or a |TypeParameterDefault| may be a |ComputedType| (<emu-xref href="#sec-computed-types"></emu-xref>) that reads parameters declared earlier in the same |TypeParameterList|. It is a type error for it to read the parameter it belongs to or one declared later in the list.</p>

        <p>Parameters bind left to right. At a use of the generic declaration, whether through explicit arguments or through inference from a call, the first parameter is bound, then the next parameter's constraint is evaluated by <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref> over the bindings so far, then that parameter is bound and checked against its evaluated constraint, and so on down the list. Within the declaration itself, before any parameter is bound, such a constraint is carried as the deferred application of <emu-xref href="#sec-deferred-applications"></emu-xref>.</p>

        <p>Where a parameter's evaluated constraint is a literal type or a union of literal types (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref>), the binding inferred for that parameter from a call argument is the literal type of the argument's value, not the widened base. The binding is then checked against the constraint as any binding is.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>This is the one placement of a builder call that needs a sentence of new specification, and the one inference rule builders ask for. `function pluck&lt;T, K: keysOf(T)&gt;(o: T, key: K): indexed(T, K)` reads in order: infer `T` from `o`, evaluate `keysOf(T)` over that binding, and infer `K` from the second argument literally, so `pluck(user, 'name')` binds `K` to the literal type `'name'` and the return type evaluates to that property's type. The constraint does double duty, checking the argument and cueing the literal inference, which is the cue `K extends keyof T` gives an erased system. It is a rule about constraints rather than a hook: no user code runs inside the inference of the parameters the constraint reads, and a mistaken argument fails the ordinary constraint check, with the evaluated union available to the diagnostic.</p>
          <p>A constraint that is a value type binds a value, as the note above says of `W: uint32`; a constraint that is a union of literal types binds a literal type, whose value is recoverable from it. The design's kit reads that value back out constantly, which suggests these are one mechanism seen from two sides, a binding to the literal type with the value as its view, and the decision is that the literal-type binding is primitive with the value as its view, executed by the generics clause.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-type-annotations">
      <h1>Type Annotations</h1>
      <p>A type annotation attaches a type to a binding, a parameter, a return, or a class member. This clause adds the annotation and the positions that accept it; the meaning of an annotation in each position is given by the declarations, functions, and classes clauses.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        <ins>TypeAnnotation[Yield, Await] :
          `:` Type[?Yield, ?Await]

        TypedInitializer[In, Yield, Await] :
          `:=` AssignmentExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>A binding may carry an annotation, or a typed initializer that infers the binding's type from its initializer. The binding of a `for`-`of` or `for`-`in` head takes the same annotation, as `for (const a: int32 of f())`, and a |BindingPattern| in any of these positions annotates its elements through |SingleNameBinding|.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        LexicalBinding[In, Yield, Await] :
          BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> Initializer[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]?
          <ins>BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypedInitializer[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          BindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await] Initializer[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]

        VariableDeclaration[In, Yield, Await] :
          BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> Initializer[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]?
          <ins>BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypedInitializer[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          BindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await] Initializer[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]

        ForBinding[Yield, Await] :
          BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins>
          BindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>A parameter may carry an annotation, an optional marker, and a `ref` modifier.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        FormalParameter[Yield, Await] :
          <ins>`ref`?</ins> BindingElement[?Yield, ?Await]

        SingleNameBinding[Yield, Await] :
          BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>`?`?</ins> <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> Initializer[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]?

        BindingRestElement[Yield, Await] :
          `...` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins>
          `...` BindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>A rest element's annotation is the type of what it collects, an ~array~ or ~tuple~ type rather than an element type, so `...args: [].&lt;uint32&gt;` collects `uint32` values into a `[].&lt;uint32&gt;`. Its Parameter Record's [[Type]] holds that type, and the <dfn>element type</dfn> of a rest is the [[Element]] of an ~array~ type and the union of the [[Type]] fields of a ~tuple~ type's elements; an operation comparing a single argument against a rest compares against its element type. The production is restated because a rest reaches its binding through |BindingRestElement| rather than through |SingleNameBinding|, so without this the design's `function f(a: string, ...args: [].&lt;uint32&gt;) {}` and `let [a: uint8, ...b: [].&lt;uint8&gt;] = [1, 2]` have no parse.</p>

      <p>A parameter list may hold more than one rest parameter, and a rest may appear anywhere in it rather than only last, which the pinned edition's |FormalParameters| does not admit. The productions are restated so that a rest is an ordinary element of the list.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        FormalParameters[Yield, Await] :
          [empty]
          <del>FunctionRestParameter[?Yield, ?Await]</del>
          <del>FormalParameterList[?Yield, ?Await]</del>
          <del>FormalParameterList[?Yield, ?Await] `,`</del>
          <del>FormalParameterList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` FunctionRestParameter[?Yield, ?Await]</del>
          <ins>FormalParameterList[?Yield, ?Await] `,`?</ins>

        FormalParameterList[Yield, Await] :
          <del>FormalParameter[?Yield, ?Await]</del>
          <del>FormalParameterList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` FormalParameter[?Yield, ?Await]</del>
          <ins>FormalParameterElement[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>FormalParameterList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` FormalParameterElement[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>

        <ins>FormalParameterElement[Yield, Await] :
          FormalParameter[?Yield, ?Await]
          FunctionRestParameter[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>|UniqueFormalParameters| and the arrow forms are defined over |FormalParameters| and so admit the wider list without restatement; the duplicate-name early errors of the pinned edition apply to it unchanged. It is a Syntax Error for a |FunctionRestParameter| to carry an |Initializer|, as it is today. It is a type error if a parameter list contains two rest parameters with no parameter of a declared type between them: two rests that admit the same values have no boundary, and no assignment of arguments between them is more right than another (<emu-xref href="#sec-bindarguments"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Every language admitting a rest away from the end pairs it with something that marks the boundary. Swift allows several variadic parameters and requires the first parameter after one to carry an argument label; Ruby allows `def f(a, *b, c)` and binds the fixed parameters from both ends before the splat takes the middle; TypeScript reaches a non-final rest only through a tuple type with a middle rest element. This design has two such markers already, the declared types of the parameters and the named arguments of <emu-xref href="#sec-named-arguments"></emu-xref>, and the early error above is what keeps at least one of them present.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A function may carry type parameters and a return annotation.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        FunctionDeclaration[Yield, Await, Default] :
          `function` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `(` FormalParameters[~Yield, ~Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` FunctionBody[~Yield, ~Await] `}`
          [+Default] `function` <ins>TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `(` FormalParameters[~Yield, ~Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` FunctionBody[~Yield, ~Await] `}`
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>A class field may carry an annotation.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        FieldDefinition[Yield, Await] :
          ClassElementName[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> Initializer[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]?
      </emu-grammar>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The positions above are representative rather than exhaustive: the remaining function forms, including methods, arrow functions, generators, and class constructors, take the same annotations and are patched by the clauses that specify them (<emu-xref href="#sec-annotations-on-the-remaining-function-forms"></emu-xref>). An arrow function's parameter list and return annotation interact with the arrow cover grammar and are given by <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-arrow-functions"></emu-xref>.</p>
        <p>`ref` before a parameter is a contextual keyword. A parameter named `ref` remains valid, since an identifier that is not followed by a token that can begin a binding element is a parameter name; `function f(ref)` therefore continues to declare a parameter named `ref`, while `function f(ref a: int32)` declares a reference parameter named `a`. The claim rule this shares with every other borrowing form, and the Early Errors of the `ref` parameter &mdash; no |BindingPattern| and no |Initializer| &mdash; are stated with the rest of the borrowing syntax in <emu-xref href="#sec-reference-syntax"></emu-xref>.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-types-in-expression-position">
      <h1>Types in Expression Position</h1>
      <p>A type name is already an expression, since a type is a value, so `uint8` and `Map.&lt;string, uint8&gt;` may be written where a value is expected. The type forms whose syntax collides with the expression grammar cannot: a function type reads as an arrow function, an object type as a block, a tuple type as an array literal, and a union as a bitwise or. The `type` operator resolves the collision by parsing its operand as a type.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        UnaryExpression[Yield, Await] :
          UpdateExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          `delete` UnaryExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          `void` UnaryExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          `typeof` UnaryExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          `+` UnaryExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          `-` UnaryExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          `~` UnaryExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          `!` UnaryExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          [+Await] AwaitExpression[?Yield]
          <ins>TypeOperatorExpression[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>

        <ins>TypeOperatorExpression[Yield, Await] :
          `type` [no LineTerminator here] Type[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>A conversion converts the value of its left operand to a type, and is written after the value.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        RelationalExpression[In, Yield, Await] :
          ShiftExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          RelationalExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await] `&lt;` ShiftExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          RelationalExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await] `&gt;` ShiftExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          RelationalExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await] `&lt;=` ShiftExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          RelationalExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await] `&gt;=` ShiftExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          RelationalExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await] `instanceof` ShiftExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          [+In] RelationalExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `in` ShiftExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          [+In] PrivateIdentifier `in` ShiftExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          <ins>RelationalExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await] `:=` Type[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>RelationalExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await] [no LineTerminator here] `is` MatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Placing a conversion at |RelationalExpression| gives it the precedence of `instanceof`, so `a + b := uint8` converts the sum, and the right operand being a |Type| means `x := A | B` converts to the union rather than taking the bitwise or of a conversion.</p>
        <p>`type` is a contextual keyword, since a program may already bind the name `type`, and this leaves an ambiguity: `type (uint8) =&gt; uint8` is a type operator applied to a function type, while `type (x)` is a call of a function named `type`, and the two agree until the token after the closing parenthesis. This specification resolves it with a cover grammar, as the grammar already does for arrow parameters and parenthesized expressions: the parenthesized text is parsed under a cover production and refined to a function-type operand or a call argument list once the token after the closing parenthesis is known. The `type` declaration form raises no ambiguity, since its `type` is followed by a binding identifier, and the computed types of <emu-xref href="#sec-computed-types"></emu-xref> do not widen it: `type f(x)` was a syntax error and is now the operator applied to a computed type, so the only covered case is an operand that begins with `(`. The committee may still prefer to avoid the cover grammar by restricting the operand or by requiring the operator only where a type cannot otherwise be written; that would remove the covered case rather than change the meaning of any program the cover grammar already accepts, and is a decision worth taking before the syntax is final. It does not reach the two operands below, which stay ambiguous however the operand is restricted.</p>
        <p>Two operand tokens are ambiguous in a way no cover production can resolve. `type [0]` is the operator applied to the tuple type `[0]`, and a member access on a binding named `type`; `type -1` is the operator applied to the literal type `-1` (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref>), and a subtraction. Unlike the parenthesized case the two readings do not diverge at a later token: each is complete and ends where the other does, so a refinement has nothing to look at and the ambiguity must be decided rather than covered. It is decided here in the operator's favour: `type [0]` is the tuple type, `type -1` is the literal type, and a program that means the value writes `(type)[0]` or `(type) - 1`. This is the treatment ECMAScript already gives `let [` at the start of a statement, which is a destructuring declaration rather than a member access on a binding named `let`. A property access such as `o.type[0]` is unaffected, since only a bare `type` reaches the operator, and the [no LineTerminator here] restriction means a line break before `- 1` leaves it a subtraction. Deciding the two tokens differently is the hazard worth naming: `-` given to the operator and `[` left to member access would admit `type -1` while refusing `type [uint8]`, and a tuple type would then have no spelling in expression position at all. If the change of meaning is judged too high a price, the alternative that keeps a tuple operand writable is to make it an early error for such an operand to appear where the name `type` is also bound, so that the program is refused rather than reinterpreted.</p>
        <p>The operand of `type` is a full |Type| and so extends as far as one reaches: `type A | B` is the union of `A` and `B`, not the bitwise or of `type A` and `B`. A bare name is a type expression, so `type uint8` is a redundant spelling of `uint8`. The extensions that introduce type-position operators use the |Type| production defined here, sharing this precedence; `is` takes the wider |MatchPattern| (<emu-xref href="#sec-pattern-matching"></emu-xref>), of which a |Type| is one form, so `x is A | B` tests the union exactly as before.</p>
        <p>`is` is a contextual keyword rather than a reserved word, and the [no LineTerminator here] restriction is what that costs. `a is b` on one line is a Syntax Error today, so the new production takes syntax no program can be using; but `a`, `is`, and `b` on three lines is three statements today, by automatic semicolon insertion, and must stay three. The restriction keeps it so, at the price that a program cannot break the line before `is`. This is the treatment `async` already receives before an arrow's parameters, and the alternative, reserving the word, is not available.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
      <emu-clause id="sec-type-arguments-and-placement-new-in-expression-position">
        <h1>Type Arguments and Placement `new` in Expression Position</h1>
        <p>A specialization such as `f.&lt;uint8&gt;(x)` and a placement allocation such as `new(buffer, byteOffset) Type(0)` are member-expression forms. The productions below restate |MemberExpression| and |CallExpression| with the added alternatives (<emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref>).</p>

        <emu-grammar type="definition">
        MemberExpression[Yield,Await] :
          PrimaryExpression[?Yield, ?Await]
          MemberExpression[?Yield, ?Await] `[` Expression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `]`
          MemberExpression[?Yield, ?Await] `.` IdentifierName
          MemberExpression[?Yield, ?Await] TemplateLiteral[?Yield, ?Await, +Tagged]
          SuperProperty[?Yield, ?Await]
          MetaProperty
          `new` MemberExpression[?Yield, ?Await] Arguments[?Yield, ?Await]
          MemberExpression[?Yield, ?Await] `.` PrivateIdentifier
          <ins>MemberExpression[?Yield, ?Await] TypeArguments[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>`new` `(` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `)` MemberExpression[?Yield, ?Await] Arguments[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>`new` `(` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `,` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `)` MemberExpression[?Yield, ?Await] Arguments[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>`new` `(` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `,` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `,` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `)` MemberExpression[?Yield, ?Await] Arguments[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>`new` `.` Arguments[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>

        CallExpression[Yield,Await] :
          CoverCallExpressionAndAsyncArrowHead[?Yield, ?Await] #callcover
          SuperCall[?Yield, ?Await]
          ImportCall[?Yield, ?Await]
          CallExpression[?Yield, ?Await] Arguments[?Yield, ?Await]
          CallExpression[?Yield, ?Await] `[` Expression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `]`
          CallExpression[?Yield, ?Await] `.` IdentifierName
          CallExpression[?Yield, ?Await] TemplateLiteral[?Yield, ?Await, +Tagged]
          CallExpression[?Yield, ?Await] `.` PrivateIdentifier
          <ins>CallExpression[?Yield, ?Await] TypeArguments[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
        </emu-grammar>

        <p>A |MemberExpression| or |CallExpression| followed by |TypeArguments| evaluates the expression and specializes the result by the listed types. The value must be generic: a generic function, a generic class, or a parameterized type; the selection and substitution are those of <emu-xref href="#sec-generic-specialization"></emu-xref>, of which this production is the explicit written form. Where the expression's Static Type shows a value that is not generic it is a type error, and otherwise a specialization of a non-generic value throws a *TypeError* exception. The |OptionalChain| productions are not extended; a specialization does not follow `?.`.</p>

        <p>The placement forms allocate into an existing buffer rather than into fresh storage. The first argument is the buffer, the second, when present, is the byte offset and otherwise 0, and the third, when present, is the byte length reserved for each element and otherwise the constructed type's `byteLength`. Construction stores each field of each instance at its laid-out position (<emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>, memory layout); a third argument larger than `byteLength` leaves padding between elements, a smaller one makes elements overlap, and bytes the layout does not assign keep the buffer's contents, since reusing storage is what the form is for and the zero-fill rule of fresh allocation does not apply. It is a *RangeError* exception when the extent so computed exceeds the buffer's length. Over a resizable buffer the extent is recorded: shrinking the buffer below a live allocation's extent detaches those instances, touching a detached instance throws a *TypeError* exception, and growing never invalidates.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>`new` `(` begins both a placement form and an ordinary `new` whose constructor is parenthesized. Source that continues with a |MemberExpression| after the closing parenthesis is the placement form; under the base grammar such source has no parse as a single expression, so the added reading changes no existing program. The array form of the design, `new(buffer, byteOffset, byteElementLength) [10].&lt;Type&gt;(0)`, needs no further production: `[10].&lt;Type&gt;` is an |ArrayLiteral| specialized by |TypeArguments|, which the alternative above already admits as the target |MemberExpression|.</p>
          <p><ins>`new` `.` |Arguments| is TARGET-TYPED CONSTRUCTION: it constructs the type its position requires, so the type is not named. It is told apart from `new.target` by the token after the dot, an |Arguments| against an |IdentifierName|, and from both forms above by the dot itself; under the base grammar `new` `.` `(` has no parse, so the added reading changes no existing program.</ins></p>
          <p><ins>The type comes from the position rather than from the expression, so a position requiring no type gives nothing to construct: it is a Syntax Error for this form to appear where the contextual type is absent. A Syntax Error rather than an inference, because inferring the type would be the binding-type inference this proposal does not perform. It is a type error where the contextual type is not constructible, as in `let n: uint8 = new.(1)`. Among a constructible type's signatures the ordinary ranking chooses, with no rule of its own, and nesting follows without further rule, an inner form taking its type from the outer constructor's parameter.</ins></p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-type-universe">
    <h1>The Type Universe</h1>
    <p>This clause adds the value types of this proposal to the ECMAScript language types of <emu-xref href="#sec-ecmascript-language-types"></emu-xref>. For each type it fixes the set of values the type contains and the Type Record (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-and-type-objects"></emu-xref>) that describes it. The operations over these values, the conversions between them, and the literals that produce them are specified by later clauses. This clause settles only what the values are and what the types are called.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-value-types">
      <h1>Value Types</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="value-type">value type</dfn> is a type whose values are ECMAScript language values that are not Objects. A value of a value type has no identity: two values of the same value type that are the same value are indistinguishable, and nothing observable distinguishes one occurrence of such a value from another. The existing primitive types are value types in this sense, and every type this clause adds is one.</p>

      <p>The values of distinct value types are distinct. Where two types are described below as containing the same mathematical values, each contains its own values representing them: the `uint8` representing 0 and the `uint16` representing 0 are different values of different types, as the Number 1 and the BigInt 1 are today. Every value therefore belongs to exactly one type, which is what lets <emu-xref href="#sec-runtimetypeof"></emu-xref> report it.</p>

      <p>Every type added here is described by a Type Record whose [[Kind]] is ~primitive~, whose [[Name]] is the String naming the type, and whose [[Arguments]] is the List of its type and value arguments. `uint8` is described by the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~primitive~, [[Name]]: *"uint"*, [[Arguments]]: &laquo; 8 &raquo; }, and `vector.&lt;float32, 4&gt;` by { [[Kind]]: ~primitive~, [[Name]]: *"vector"*, [[Arguments]]: &laquo; the Type Record for `float32`, 4 &raquo; }.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Because Type Records are canonicalized and interned (<emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref>), a type written two ways is one Type Object: `int8` and `int.&lt;8&gt;` name the same Type Record and therefore compare strictly equal. The shorthand names of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-names"></emu-xref> are consequently aliases rather than separate types.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-integer-types">
      <h1>Integer Types</h1>
      <p>For each positive integer _N_, `int.&lt;N&gt;` is a value type whose values are the integers from -2<sup>_N_-1</sup> through 2<sup>_N_-1</sup> - 1 inclusive, and `uint.&lt;N&gt;` is a value type whose values are the integers from 0 through 2<sup>_N_</sup> - 1 inclusive. Each has exactly 2<sup>_N_</sup> values. Neither has a negative zero, an infinity, or a NaN.</p>

      <p>_N_ is a value argument, so `int.&lt;8&gt;` and `int.&lt;16&gt;` are distinct types, and the width of a type is recoverable from its Type Record.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>_N_ is bounded: it lies in the inclusive interval from 1 to 2<sup>16</sup>, and a width outside it is a type error. The bound is a specified constant like the budgets, generous enough for any hashing or cryptographic width and small enough that a computed type cannot demand an unbounded layout for one Type Record of cost. The design gives shorthands only through 128 and uses `uint.&lt;1&gt;` as the lane type of a boolean vector, but nothing above restricts _N_, and an unbounded _N_ obliges every implementation to provide arithmetic at arbitrary width, which is a substantially larger commitment than the named widths imply. Fixing _N_ to a bounded set, to the powers of two, or to a range up to 128 are all defensible; the choice belongs with the committee, and it interacts with the bit-field widths of the memory layout extension, which are the reason a width such as 3 is meaningful at all.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-binary-floating-point-types">
      <h1>Binary Floating-Point Types</h1>
      <p>`float16`, `float32`, `float64`, and `float128` are value types whose values are those of the corresponding IEEE 754-2019 binary interchange formats, with the same treatment of NaN the Number type receives: the many NaN encodings of the format are represented as a single NaN value. Each therefore has 2<sup>_w_</sup> - 2<sup>_p_</sup> + 3 values, where _w_ is the width of the format in bits and _p_ its precision in bits, as given in <emu-xref href="#table-binary-float-types"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-binary-float-types" caption="Binary Floating-Point Types">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>IEEE 754 format</th><th>Width _w_</th><th>Precision _p_</th><th>Values</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`float16`</td><td>binary16</td><td>16</td><td>11</td><td>63491</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`float32`</td><td>binary32</td><td>32</td><td>24</td><td>4278190083</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`float64`</td><td>binary64</td><td>64</td><td>53</td><td>18437736874454810627</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`float128`</td><td>binary128</td><td>128</td><td>113</td><td>2<sup>128</sup> - 2<sup>113</sup> + 3</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>Each of these types has a positive zero and a negative zero, a positive infinity and a negative infinity, and a single NaN, as the Number type does.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`float64` and the Number type have the same set of values but are distinct types, so neither is assignable to the other without a conversion. This is the rule stated for every pair of value types and is not a special case: Number is the type an untyped program computes with, and `float64` is the type a program asks for when it wants that format by name.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-decimal-floating-point-types">
      <h1>Decimal Floating-Point Types</h1>
      <p>`decimal32`, `decimal64`, and `decimal128` are value types whose values are those of the corresponding IEEE 754-2019 decimal interchange formats. A finite value of a decimal type is a triple of a sign, an integer coefficient, and an exponent, drawn from the ranges in <emu-xref href="#table-decimal-types"></emu-xref>. Each type additionally has a positive infinity, a negative infinity, and a single NaN.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-decimal-types" caption="Decimal Floating-Point Types">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>IEEE 754 format</th><th>Width</th><th>Coefficient digits</th><th>Exponent range</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`decimal32`</td><td>decimal32</td><td>32 bits</td><td>7</td><td>-101 through 90</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`decimal64`</td><td>decimal64</td><td>64 bits</td><td>16</td><td>-398 through 369</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`decimal128`</td><td>decimal128</td><td>128 bits</td><td>34</td><td>-6176 through 6111</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>Two finite values of a decimal type that have the same numerical value but different exponents are <em>distinct values</em> of the type. The values written `1.0`, `1.00`, and `1.000` are three values of `decimal128`, one for each exponent, and a decimal type therefore preserves the significance of the quantity it was given. IEEE 754 calls such a set of values a cohort.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Preserving trailing zeros is the point of the decimal types: a price given to two places prints to two places, which is what the money types of the primitive metadata extension depend on. The consequence is that the numerical order and the value identity of a decimal type come apart, and the equality operations must choose between them. The design settles two of the three: `==` compares numerical value, so `1.0 == 1.00` is *true*, and SameValueZero compares numerical value, so `1.0` and `1.00` are one key in a Map or a Set rather than two. Identity follows the representation: SameValue distinguishes the cohort members `1.0` and `1.00`, so they are distinct map keys and distinct literal types, while `===` and `==` compare numerically and find them equal, the same split Number already has for `+0` and `-0`.</p>
        <p>This specification settles SameValue the way the paragraph above already uses it: SameValue distinguishes cohort members, so `Object.is(1.0, 1.00)` is *false* for two `decimal128` values of different exponents, while SameValueZero and `==` compare numerical value and find them equal. This is the split the design records for `===`/`==` versus identity, and it is the parallel with negative zero, where SameValue distinguishes `+0` from `-0` and SameValueZero does not. The alternative, SameValue comparing numerical value, was rejected because it would make `Object.is` unable to distinguish values that print differently, which no other type permits; its cost, that SameValue and SameValueZero then differ on a case unrelated to zero, is the same cost negative zero already imposes and is judged the lesser one. This remains a decision the committee owns, recorded here because the type universe is where the cohort becomes observable.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-rational-types">
      <h1>Rational Types</h1>
      <p>For each positive integer _N_, `rational.&lt;N&gt;` is a value type whose values are the exact rational numbers representable as a quotient of two values of `int.&lt;N&gt;`. A value of `rational.&lt;N&gt;` is a pair of a numerator and a denominator, both values of `int.&lt;N&gt;`, such that the denominator is greater than 0 and the greatest common divisor of the numerator and the denominator is 1. Every value of the type is therefore in lowest terms, and each rational number has at most one representation in the type.</p>

      <p>A rational type has no negative zero, no infinity, and no NaN. An operation whose exact result is not representable, including one whose normalized numerator or denominator falls outside `int.&lt;N&gt;`, throws a *RangeError* rather than rounding.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Normalizing on construction is what makes the type a value type in the ordinary sense: without it, one half would have many representations and equality would have to normalize before comparing. It is also what makes the *RangeError* unavoidable, since a sum of two representable rationals can have an unrepresentable normalized denominator. Refusing to round is the distinction between this type and a float; a rational that cannot be represented exactly is an error, not an approximation.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-complex-types">
      <h1>Complex Types</h1>
      <p>For a value type _T_ that is `number` or one of the binary floating-point types, `complex.&lt;T&gt;` is a value type whose values are the ordered pairs of a real part and an imaginary part, each a value of _T_. The number of values of `complex.&lt;T&gt;` is the square of the number of values of _T_.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The width-named shorthands of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-names"></emu-xref> count total bits rather than component bits, following the convention of NumPy and Go, so `complex64` is a pair of `float32` and not a pair of `float64`.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-vector-types">
      <h1>Vector Types</h1>
      <p>For a value type _T_ that is an integer, binary floating-point, or vector type, and a positive integer _N_, `vector.&lt;T, N&gt;` is a value type whose values are the sequences of exactly _N_ values of _T_. _T_ is the lane type and _N_ the lane count. The number of values of `vector.&lt;T, N&gt;` is the number of values of _T_ raised to the power _N_.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A vector's lane type may itself be a vector, which is how the boolean vectors of the design are built: `boolean8` is `vector.&lt;boolean1, 8&gt;`, where `boolean1` is the design's name for `uint.&lt;1&gt;`, eight one-bit lanes, and `boolean8x16` is `vector.&lt;boolean8, 16&gt;`. The lane counts an implementation can hold in a register are a matter for the SIMD extension; the type exists for every positive _N_.</p>

        <p>It is a type error to form `vector.&lt;T, N&gt;` where _T_ is not a lane type or _N_ is not a positive integer, since neither denotes a vector type. Where the lane type is itself a vector, this applies to it in turn.</p>

      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-vector-lanes">
      <h1>Vector Lanes</h1>
      <p>A vector's lanes are reached by two pairs of operations, and the pairs differ in when the lane is chosen. Where the lane is a compile-time constant the choice is checked, and where it is an expression it is not; a design uses the first wherever it can (design, simd.md).</p>

      <p>For a vector type `vector.&lt;T, N&gt;`:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>`lane.&lt;`_I_`&gt;()` has the Static Type _T_. It is a type error if _I_ is not a non-negative integer less than _N_.</li>
        <li>`withLane.&lt;`_I_`&gt;(`_value_`)` has the Static Type `vector.&lt;T, N&gt;`, and takes a _value_ of type _T_. It is a type error if _I_ is not a non-negative integer less than _N_. Its value is the receiver's, with lane _I_ replaced by _value_; the receiver is unchanged, since a vector is a value type (<emu-xref href="#sec-vector-types"></emu-xref>).</li>
        <li>A member access with a computed key of type `uint32` has the Static Type _T_, and an assignment to one takes a _value_ of type _T_. The key is an expression, so no static rule bounds it: reading or writing a lane whose index is not less than _N_ throws a *RangeError*.</li>
      </ul>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The asymmetry is the reason both forms are given. `lane.&lt;`_I_`&gt;()` is refused at check time for an index out of range and `v[i]` is not, because an index that is an expression is not known until the access happens. The design's rule is to write the constant form wherever the index is a constant, and the specification's part is only that the two differ in when the bound is enforced.</p>
        <p>`withLane.&lt;`_I_`&gt;(`_value_`)` returning a new vector rather than modifying the receiver is not a rule of this clause; it follows from a vector being a value type, and is stated because a reader arriving from an array is likely to expect otherwise.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The assignment is asked about because `withLane` expresses the same intent and returns a value, where `v[i] = `_value_` mutates one in place, which is what a value type is meant not to do (simd.md, Open Questions). It is kept, and the reason is not the consistency one: an array does already permit a value type's element to be assigned, but the decisive point is that `withLane`'s index is a COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT. The two operations therefore do not overlap where it matters &mdash; refusing the assignment would leave a lane whose index is computed with no way to be written at all &mdash; so the redundancy the question supposes does not exist.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>An operation taking a computed index and returning a new vector would restore the redundancy and reopen the question honestly. Nothing here proposes one; a revision that added it could then refuse the assignment without leaving the computed case unwritable, which is the only ground on which refusing it would be complete.</p>
      </emu-note>
      </emu-note>

      <p>`vector.&lt;T, N&gt;` declares a cast operator from _T_, so a value of the lane type converts to a vector by filling every lane with it, and the conversion is one of the user-defined casts of <emu-xref href="#sec-conversions"></emu-xref> rather than a rule of its own. A value whose type is not the lane type does not convert: for a `float32` _s_, `float32x4` accepts _s_ and `float64x2` does not, and a design that wants the second converts _s_ to `float64` first.</p>

      <p>Where the lane type is `uint.&lt;1&gt;`, a lane and a bit coincide: lane _i_ of a `vector.&lt;uint.&lt;1&gt;, N&gt;` is bit _i_ of an _N_-bit integer, counting from the least significant. The conversion between such a vector and an integer type is that correspondence read in each direction. An integer value converted to the vector type gives the vector whose lane _i_ is bit _i_ of the value, and a value of the vector type converted to an integer type gives the integer whose bit _i_ is lane _i_.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is what makes a bit vector a bitfield rather than a name for a byte. A `boolean8` initialized from `0b00000010` has lane 1 set and the rest clear, so `a[1]` is 1 and `a[0]` is 0; assigning 1 to lane 3 and reading the value back as an integer gives `0b00001010`.</p>
        <p>The numbering is the one <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-comparisons"></emu-xref> states for the masks a comparison produces, and it is stated here as a rule because that clause states it as a remark about hardware. A bit vector converts whether or not a comparison produced it.</p>
        <p>A bit vector is not a bit-field. The sub-byte integer members a class may declare, with their own bit order and offsets, are <emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>'s and are a different feature: they pack members of differing widths into a layout, where a bit vector is _N_ lanes of one width that permute, compare, and select like any other vector. The two are stated in different clauses because they answer different questions, and a reader meeting `boolean8` here is not meeting the other.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>`sum()` has the Static Type _T_ and is the sum of the receiver's lanes. Its result is that of adding the lanes in an implementation-defined order, which for a binary floating-point lane type is observable: addition is not associative there, so two implementations may return different values for one receiver.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The order is left to the implementation because fixing it would forbid the instruction that makes the operation worth having: a pairwise horizontal add is what the hardware provides, and it associates differently from a left fold. A design needing a fixed order writes the fold it wants over `lane.&lt;`_I_`&gt;()`.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-vector-permutation">
      <h1>Vector Permutation</h1>
      <p>A permutation names a lane of a source for each lane of its result, so it reorders, repeats, narrows, and widens in one operation. Two forms draw from one source and from two (design, simd.md).</p>

      <p>For a vector type `vector.&lt;T, N&gt;` and indices _I_&#8321; through _I_<sub>_L_</sub>, each a compile-time constant:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>`swizzle.&lt;`_I_&#8321;`, &hellip;, `_I_<sub>_L_</sub>`&gt;()` has the Static Type `vector.&lt;T, L&gt;`. Lane _j_ of the result is lane _I_<sub>_j_</sub> of the receiver. It is a type error if any _I_<sub>_j_</sub> is not a non-negative integer less than _N_, or if _L_ is not a positive integer.</li>
        <li>`shuffle.&lt;`_I_&#8321;`, &hellip;, `_I_<sub>_L_</sub>`&gt;(`_other_`)` has the Static Type `vector.&lt;T, L&gt;` and takes an _other_ of type `vector.&lt;T, N&gt;`. An index below _N_ selects that lane of the receiver, and an index from _N_ to 2_N_-1 selects lane _I_<sub>_j_</sub>-_N_ of _other_. It is a type error if any _I_<sub>_j_</sub> is not a non-negative integer less than 2_N_, or if _L_ is not a positive integer.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>_L_ need not equal _N_. A permutation of fewer indices than the receiver has lanes narrows, and one of more widens: `swizzle.&lt;0, 1&gt;()` on a four-lane vector has the Static Type `vector.&lt;T, 2&gt;`.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Each index is a compile-time constant because that is what the instruction this form compiles to takes: the lane selector of `shufps` and its kind is an immediate operand. Other instructions take a selector in a register, `pshufb`, `tbl`, and `vrgather` among them, so a permutation whose selection is data is possible and is a DIFFERENT operation. It would be named `swizzleDyn` here, and it is an open question of the design (simd.md) rather than an omission from this clause.</p>
        <p>What is open about it is one rule: an index that is data may be out of range, where an index that is a constant is refused before the program runs, and there is no answer to defer to. Rust's `swizzle_dyn` and WebAssembly's `i8x16.swizzle` yield zero for such an index; x86's `pshufb` wraps; ARM's `tbl` and RISC-V's `vrgather` zero; and Java's `VectorShuffle` does neither, wrapping an invalid index into a negative band that a later operation may use to steer a second source. WebAssembly specified zero and then added a relaxed form whose result is implementation-defined, because upholding zero on x86 costs instructions. Each answer has been chosen by a real design and the hardware disagrees with itself, so whichever this specification chose would be extra work on some target &mdash; which is why the operation waits on a decision rather than on a clause.</p>
        <p>The two-source numbering is the one place a reader is likely to err. For a four-lane receiver, indices 0 through 3 name the receiver's lanes and 4 through 7 name _other_'s, so `a.shuffle.&lt;0, 1, 4, 5&gt;(b)` takes the first half of each.</p>
        <p>`swizzle` is how the eight- and sixteen-lane vectors permute, since the component accessors of <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-component-accessors"></emu-xref> name only a vector's first four lanes.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The design writes the index list as a variadic value generic parameter, `swizzle&lt;...I: [].&lt;uint32&gt;&gt;()`, whose length gives the result's lane count. Value generics and their variadic form are the generics extension's (<emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>); the rules above are what that spelling means, stated so that this clause does not wait on it.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-vector-component-accessors">
      <h1>Vector Component Accessors</h1>
      <p>A vector of four or fewer lanes has names for its lanes, drawn from two sets, and a sequence of those names is a permutation written as a property (design, simd.md). `xyzw` and `rgba` name the same lanes in that order, so `v.x` and `v.r` are one lane and `v.wzyx` and `v.abgr` are one permutation.</p>

      <p>A property key is a component accessor of a receiver of type `vector.&lt;T, N&gt;` when all of the following hold:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>_N_ is at most 4.</li>
        <li>The key is a sequence of 1 to 4 characters, each drawn from `xyzw` or each drawn from `rgba`. A key mixing the sets is not a component accessor.</li>
        <li>Each character's index within its set is less than _N_.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Such an accessor has the Static Type _T_ for a key of one character, and `vector.&lt;T, L&gt;` for a key of _L_ characters. _L_ is independent of _N_: a two-lane vector's `xyxy` has the Static Type `vector.&lt;T, 4&gt;`.</p>

      <p>An accessor whose key names no lane twice is assignable, and takes a value of the accessor's own Static Type. An accessor whose key names a lane twice is not assignable, since an assignment to it would give one lane two values; it is a type error to assign to one.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A vector of more than four lanes has no component accessors, because the two sets have four characters each. Such a vector permutes with <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-permutation"></emu-xref>, which is why that clause takes indices rather than names.</p>
        <p>That limit is a decision rather than a gap awaiting names. Every shading language that has these accessors stops at four, and no language with wider vectors has invented a set for them: Rust and Java give wide vectors indices and permutations and no names at all. Extending a set would collide, since `a` and `b` already name lanes in `rgba` and would name different lanes at a different width; adding a second pair of sets for lanes 4 through 7 does not reach sixteen; and numeric names in the manner of OpenCL's `s0` through `s7` would spell what `lane.&lt;`_I_`&gt;()` and `swizzle` already spell. The four names exist because geometry has conventional names for its axes, not because permutation wants sugar, so a vector with no such convention has no names and loses nothing.</p>
        <p>The design writes the third set GLSL provides, `stpq`, as deliberately absent: HLSL and WGSL dropped it, and two sets already name every lane.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A component accessor is a property, not a syntactic form, and the distinction is observable. The names are non-enumerable properties of the vector's prototype, shared by every value of that shape, so:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>`v['xyz']` reaches the same value the dotted access does, since the key is looked up rather than parsed.</li>
        <li>Reflection over the object a vector converts to sees the accessors: `Object.getOwnPropertyNames(`_v_`)` includes every accessor name of that vector's lane count.</li>
        <li>`Object.keys(v)` is empty, since the accessors are not own properties.</li>
        <li>`Object.getOwnPropertyNames(`_C_`.prototype)` for a four-lane vector type _C_ includes every accessor name, of which there are 680 counting both sets and every result length from 1 to 4.</li>
      </ul>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`in` and `Reflect.get` are not among these, and the reason is the language's rather than this clause's: both require an Object, and a vector is a value type. A String behaves the same way &mdash; `'0' in 'abc'` is a *TypeError* though `'abc'[0]` reads &mdash; so an accessor being a property does not make these operations reach it. An earlier draft of this clause listed them, which was an error about JavaScript rather than about vectors.</p>
        <p>A form keyed on the receiver's static type would satisfy neither: a computed access could not reach it, and reflection could not see it. Making the accessors properties is what keeps a vector an ordinary object, at the cost of several hundred prototype names that `Object.getOwnPropertyNames` reports. None of them collides with a method of the vector prototype, since every accessor name is drawn from `xyzw` and `rgba` alone.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A component accessor is equivalent to the permutation of the same lanes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A read through a key of one character is `lane.&lt;`_I_`&gt;()` for that character's index _I_ (<emu-xref href="#sec-vector-lanes"></emu-xref>).</li>
        <li>A read through a key of _L_ characters is `swizzle.&lt;`_I_&#8321;`, &hellip;, `_I_<sub>_L_</sub>`&gt;()` for their indices (<emu-xref href="#sec-vector-permutation"></emu-xref>).</li>
        <li>An assignment through an assignable key replaces the named lanes with the corresponding lanes of the assigned value, leaving the receiver's other lanes unchanged.</li>
      </ul>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The equivalence is why this clause adds no operation. `v.xzzw` is `v.swizzle.&lt;0, 2, 2, 3&gt;()` and `v.x` is `v.lane.&lt;0&gt;()`; the names are how a program reads, and the permutation is what an implementation compiles. A compound assignment such as `v.xy += `_w_ is the read, the operation, and the assignment in the order any compound assignment takes them, and needs no rule here.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-vector-lane-wise-math">
      <h1>Lane-wise Math</h1>
      <p>The function properties of the Math object apply lane-wise to a vector argument, returning a vector of the argument's shape. Where a function takes more than one argument, the arguments are vectors of one shape and the function applies at each lane; a scalar argument beside a vector one broadcasts (<emu-xref href="#sec-vector-lanes"></emu-xref>).</p>
      <p>The set divides by whether the scalar function is exactly specified. `min`, `max`, `abs`, `sqrt`, `floor`, `ceil`, `round`, and `trunc` are exact, and lane _j_ of the result is the scalar result for lane _j_ of the argument. The remaining functions - the trigonometric, exponential, logarithmic, and hyperbolic families and `pow`, `cbrt`, and `hypot` - are implementation-approximated as their scalar forms are, and <b>the vector form is approximated independently of the scalar form</b>: lane _j_ of the vector result is not required to equal the scalar function applied to lane _j_.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The independence is deliberate. A vector math library computes a transcendental to a different accuracy than the scalar library does, so requiring the two to agree would forbid an implementation from using the vector instruction at all, which is to forbid the reason the operation is offered here. A program requiring bit-identical results between the two spellings applies the scalar function lane by lane, which is what it is asking for.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-vector-masks">
      <h1>Mask Operations</h1>
      <p><emu-xref href="#sec-vector-comparisons"></emu-xref> states what a comparison between vectors produces. This clause states what consumes one. A <dfn>mask</dfn> is a vector whose lane type is `uint.&lt;1&gt;`, which is what the design names `boolean`_N_ (<emu-xref href="#sec-vector-lanes"></emu-xref>), and a vector whose lane type is itself such a vector is a mask too: that is the wide form a comparison yields, so a `float32x4` comparison's `boolean32x4` result is a mask as much as a `boolean4` is. The operations below are declared on a mask in either form and on no other vector. A lane of the compact form is <dfn>set</dfn> when its bit is set, and a lane of the wide form when every bit of it is, which is what a comparison writes into one: all-set or all-clear.</p>

      <p>For a mask of _N_ lanes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>`all()` has the Static Type `boolean`, and is *true* when every lane is set.</li>
        <li>`any()` has the Static Type `boolean`, and is *true* when at least one lane is set.</li>
        <li>`select(`_whenSet_`, `_whenClear_`)` takes two vectors of one type `vector.&lt;U, N&gt;` and has that Static Type. Lane _j_ of the result is lane _j_ of _whenSet_ where lane _j_ of the receiver is set, and lane _j_ of _whenClear_ otherwise.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>_U_ is not the receiver's lane type. A mask selects between vectors of any lane type, so long as they share its lane count.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`select` over an arithmetic result is how a program computes some lanes and leaves the others, which a target with predicated instructions performs in one instruction rather than two. `m.select(_a_ + _b_, _src_)` is the write-masked form, whose untaken lanes come from _src_, and `m.select(_a_ + _b_, _zero_)` is the zero-masked form. An implementation may lower either to such an instruction, computing only the lanes the mask takes, when the arm whose lanes it elides is an expression whose evaluation has no effect an ECMAScript program can observe; arithmetic over vectors is such an expression.</p>
        <p>That condition, and not the shape of the call, is what bounds the permission. `select` is an ordinary method, so its arguments are evaluated before it is entered: an argument that calls a function, reads an accessor, or otherwise has an effect is evaluated whatever the mask holds, and only lanes of an arm an implementation can show to be pure may go uncomputed. No syntax is added for the masked forms, because this composition already denotes them, and a per-operation mask parameter would give every operator a second spelling for what it already says.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>`select` is an ordinary call, so **both arguments are evaluated** before it is made, whichever lanes of the receiver are set. It is not a conditional and does not have a conditional's evaluation.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>That is the trade `select` exists to make. A branch evaluates one side and costs a misprediction where the condition varies per lane; `select` evaluates both and costs neither, which is why branchless code is written with it. A design that must not evaluate one side writes a branch.</p>
        <p>A mask is a vector and nothing about these operations makes it a separate kind of value: a lane of it is read by index, it permutes, and it carries component accessors like any other vector of its shape, so `m.x` is a lane of it and `m.xyxy` is a four-lane mask.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-vector-widths">
      <h1>Vector Widths</h1>
      <p>`vector.&lt;T, N&gt;` admits any positive lane count, which is what lets one kernel be written against a width. The width a target executes in one instruction is a property of the target, and `float32.preferredLanes` is that width for the lane type `float32`, and every lane type carries the same property.</p>

      <p>Its result is a compile-time constant, so a type may be formed from it and a generic declaration may specialize on it. Its value is host-defined.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The operation is on the LANE type rather than on `vector`, and the reason is that a bare parameterized primitive is not a value: `uint`, `int`, `rational`, and `vector` denote types only when applied, so `const x: uint = 5` is refused for the same reason `const x: vector = ...` is. A `vector.preferredLanes(`_T_`)` would need `vector` to be a value, which would single it out among the four. `complex` is not among them: <emu-xref href="#sec-complex-numbers"></emu-xref> gives it a default argument, so the bare name IS an application - `complex` is `complex.&lt;number&gt;` - which is what lets an imaginary literal have the type `complex` and what makes `complex` and `complex128` distinct types. A lane type is already a Type Object and already carries statics &mdash; `byteLength` and the parsing operations among them &mdash; so `float32.preferredLanes` is one more property on a surface that exists, and it reads as what the operation asks: how many lanes of this type fill a register.</p>

        <p>This is the one operation of this specification whose result may differ between hosts, so it is the one place a program's TYPES may differ between hosts: a `vector.&lt;float32, vector.preferredLanes(float32)&gt;` is a different type on a target with four lanes and on one with eight. That is the point of the operation rather than a defect in it — a program written against it is portable in the sense that matters, which is that it compiles to the target's own width rather than to a width chosen for another machine.</p>
        <p>It is also why a design that needs one type across hosts writes the lane count rather than asking for it. The determinism this specification fixes elsewhere is determinism for a given host (<emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref>), and this operation does not weaken it: two evaluations on one host agree.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A vector whose lane count exceeds the target's width is formed and used as any other, and an implementation executes it in as many instructions as it needs.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Gather, scatter, and masked load and store are not specified. The design defers them (simd.md), and they are named here so that their absence reads as a boundary rather than an omission: they are the operations that vectorize an indirect access, and they need an addressing model this specification does not have.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-vector-wrapping">
      <h1>Wrapping a Vector</h1>
      <p>A type alias of a vector type is that vector type, so a value of it has the component accessors of <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-component-accessors"></emu-xref> and the operations of the clauses above. A class holding a vector as a member is a distinct nominal type, and does not have them: a class does not acquire the members of its own field's type.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This follows from the rules for aliases and classes rather than adding one, and is stated because it decides a design question the design document poses. A `Vector4` written as an alias swizzles and a `Vector4` written as a class does not, unless it declares the accessors it wants. The trade is nominal distinction: two aliases of `vector.&lt;float32, 4&gt;` are one type, so a `Quaternion` and a `Vector4` written that way are mutually assignable, where two classes are not.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-any-type">
      <h1>The any Type</h1>
      <p>`any` is the type of every value. Every ECMAScript language value is a value of the `any` type, and a binding whose type is `any` accepts any value. It is described by the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~any~ }, and it is the type of a binding that carries no annotation, which is what makes an untyped program a program whose bindings are all of the `any` type.</p>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-void-type">
      <h1>The void Type</h1>
      <p>`void` is the type with no values. It is described by the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~void~ }, and it is written as the return type of a function that returns no value. No value is a value of the `void` type, so no binding may have it.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A call of a function whose return type is `void` evaluates to *undefined*, as a call of a function with no `return` statement does today. The `void` type is the statement that a program must not depend on that result, not a claim that no result exists.</p>
        <p>It follows that a `void` return type constrains the CONSUMER of the result and not the value leaving the function: the check it installs is the one that refuses a binding, and no check is performed on the returned value itself. `return;` and falling off the end are the same function written two ways, so testing *undefined* against a type with no values would refuse one and admit the other. `undefined` is the type to write for a function that returns the value *undefined* and whose caller may hold it (<emu-xref href="#sec-null-and-undefined-types"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-null-and-undefined-types">
      <h1>The null and undefined Types</h1>
      <p>`null` is the type whose one value is *null*. It is described by the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~primitive~, [[Name]]: *"null"*, [[Arguments]]: &laquo; &raquo; }, and it is written with the |PredefinedType| `null`.</p>
      <p>`undefined` is the type whose one value is *undefined*. It is described by the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~primitive~, [[Name]]: *"undefined"*, [[Arguments]]: &laquo; &raquo; }, and it is written with the |PredefinedType| `undefined`. It is distinct from the `void` type: `void` is the type with no values, written to say that a result must not be depended on, whereas `undefined` has the one value *undefined* and a binding may hold it.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>These are the types <emu-xref href="#sec-runtimetypeof"></emu-xref> reports for *null* and *undefined*. The reject type of a typed promise is `undefined` by convention when the promise never rejects, which is one place the `undefined` type is written directly; another is a union such as `uint32 | undefined` for a value that may be absent, the position an erased system reaches with an optional. `null` and `undefined` are ordinary members of the type universe and may appear in a union, an intersection, or any other type position.</p>
        <p>Resolving the `undefined` type name to `void` looks like it keeps the name and RuntimeTypeOf in agreement, and an implementation of this specification did so. They agree on a type with no values, so *undefined* is not a value of the type its own RuntimeTypeOf reports, and with it every `uint32 | undefined` becomes a union no value can inhabit. The agreement to keep is at the `undefined` type: RuntimeTypeOf(*undefined*) is `undefined`, the name denotes that same type, and `void` remains a return type that no binding may hold.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-never-type">
      <h1>The never Type</h1>
      <p>`never` is the type with no values: the union of no members, described by the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~union~, [[Members]]: &laquo; &raquo; }. It is not a new kind of type; it is the empty case of a kind the type system already has, admitted as a type in its own right because computation reaches it regardless. A union filtered of its every member is this type, whatever this clause says.</p>

      <p>Its algebra follows from the operations already defined, with no rule specific to it except the one noted last.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>`never` is a subtype of every type. In IsSubtype (<emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>) a source of the ~union~ kind is a subtype when every member is, and a quantification over no members holds.</li>
        <li>No type other than `never` is a subtype of `never`. A target of the ~union~ kind requires some member to accept the source, and there is none.</li>
        <li>`never` is the identity of union. Canonicalization (<emu-xref href="#sec-canonicalizetype"></emu-xref>) flattens a member of the same kind into its parent, and flattening an empty union contributes no members, so `T | never` canonicalizes to `T` with no rule naming the case.</li>
        <li>`never` annihilates intersection: an intersection with a `never` member is `never`. This is the one fact that does not fall out, and CanonicalizeType carries the step for it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>No value is a value of the `never` type: IsOfType (<emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref>) over a union with no members is *false* for every value, and DefaultValueOf (<emu-xref href="#sec-defaultvalueof"></emu-xref>) of it is ~none~, so a binding of the type can be neither defaulted nor, since no expression's value can satisfy it, initialized. A value of the `any` type reaching a position of the `never` type fails its boundary check (<emu-xref href="#sec-requiretype"></emu-xref>) for every value. That vacuity is the point: `never` exists to be computed with, as the result of removing every member from a union, not to be inhabited.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Because the empty union canonicalizes to itself and Type Objects are interned (<emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref>), every computation that produces the empty union produces one Type Object, so `never` has the same identity story as every other type. The name is admitted through <emu-xref href="#sec-type-names"></emu-xref> on the same terms as every other built-in type name, so `let x: never;` is writable, and the name is shadowable exactly as `uint8` is. Admitting it does not extend `switch` exhaustiveness, which remains reserved to enums and sealed hierarchies (<emu-xref href="#sec-sealed-classes"></emu-xref>). It is also distinct from the ~empty~ sentinel of the narrowing operations (<emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref>), which reports a test that cannot succeed as a mistake rather than narrowing to the type with no values.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-literal-types">
      <h1>Literal Types</h1>
      <p>A literal type has exactly one value. It is described by the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~literal~, [[Value]]: _v_, [[Base]]: _b_ }, where _v_ is a String, Number, Boolean, BigInt, or Symbol and _b_ is the Type Record of the existing language type of which _v_ is a value. A |LiteralType| (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-expressions"></emu-xref>) denotes the literal type of the value it writes: a |StringLiteral| with base `string`, a |NumericLiteral| with base `number` or `bigint` as the literal is written, and `true` or `false` with base `boolean`. `Infinity` and `NaN` are Numbers that are written with a name rather than a numeral, and a |LiteralType| may write either, negated or not: <emu-xref href="#table-metadata-values"></emu-xref> admits any Number as a metadata value and says that a NaN is equivalent to a NaN, so a meta type whose constraint is a bound could otherwise not state its own `default` in a parameterization. A Symbol has no literal syntax, so no |LiteralType| writes a symbol literal type: one arises through construction (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-maketype"></emu-xref>) or through `keyof` over a symbol-keyed object type (<emu-xref href="#sec-keyof"></emu-xref>), and a program names it with an ordinary binding.</p>

      <p>The one value of a literal type is a value of its base, so the type is a subtype of its base (<emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>), `'on'` is usable where a `string` is required, and a value is of a literal type exactly when it is that value. A union of literal types is the closed set the design's discriminated unions select on, and `keyof` produces one (<emu-xref href="#sec-keyof"></emu-xref>). `boolean` and `true | false` have the same values and are distinct types: `true | false` is a subtype of `boolean` through the literal rule, and `boolean` is deliberately not a subtype of the pair, because completing the relation over value-set equality is the road to deciding language equivalence, which the pattern judgment already declines.</p>

      <p>A literal expression whose contextual type (<emu-xref href="#sec-contextual-types"></emu-xref>) is a literal type, or a union or intersection with a literal type among its members, takes the literal type of its own value where that literal type is assignable to the contextual type, so `let a: 'on' | 'off' = 'on';` gives `a` the literal type `'on'`. A contextual type of any other shape leaves the literal to the rules already given for untyped literals, so `let s: string = 'on';` still gives `s` a `string` and `let x: uint8 = 5;` is unchanged.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The base of a numeric literal is `number` however the literal will be used, matching the Static Type a literal takes with no contextual type; a literal type over another numeric type, the `uint8` 3 as a type, is expressible through construction but not through syntax, and its interaction with literal propagation is settled where it was needed: a `:` constraint naming a value type gives a literal argument the literal type over that base, the contextual rule above extended to the constraint's context, so `W: uint32` binds the literal type of 4 over `uint32` with 4 as its value view, and propagation elsewhere keeps base `number`. A Symbol is a literal type's value like any other, compared by SameValue, which for a Symbol is identity, so a symbol's identity is already its type's identity and the `unique symbol` of an erased system has nothing left to do. Any Symbol qualifies, well-known, registered, or freshly created, and no declaration form distinguishes them because none is needed. A symbol literal type is reproducible outside its Agent exactly when its Symbol is: one from `Symbol.for` names itself by its key, while one from `Symbol` is Agent-local, so a type carrying it cannot be shared, and the extension that shares types across Agents reports that rather than inventing an identity (<emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-parameterized-types">
      <h1>Parameterized Types</h1>
      <p>A metadata parameterization refines a base type with a metadata value: the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~parameterized~, [[Base]]: _b_, [[Metadata]]: _m_ }. Over a primitive base it is written `float32.&lt;{ m: 1 }&gt;`; over any base it is reachable through construction (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-maketype"></emu-xref>), so an object, class, or function type may carry a brand or a pattern. A width or lane argument such as the _N_ of `int.&lt;N&gt;` is not a parameterization: structural arguments live in a ~primitive~ Record's [[Arguments]], and metadata rides this wrapper.</p>

      <p>A parameterized type has exactly the representation of its [[Base]]. Metadata never changes layout, alignment, or size.</p>

      <p>The default relation is the branding rule. A parameterized type is a subtype of its base, so the brand is shed freely on the way up; the base is not a subtype of the parameterization, so the way down is a crossing: calling the Type Object, as `UserId(7)`, is the construction boundary, and the metadata's validation judgment runs there where one is defined. Two parameterizations of one base with structurally equivalent metadata are one type by interning; two with different metadata are related only as the <dfn id="metadata-subtype-judgment">metadata subtype judgment</dfn> admits. A metadata value's fields are governed by the meta types that claim them, which supply that judgment and the validation judgment below, and which are required to be pure, compile-time evaluable, and deterministic; <emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-metadata"></emu-xref> defines them all.</p>

      <p>A value is of a parameterized type when its runtime type is that type or a subtype of it, or when it is a value of the [[Base]] and the <dfn id="metadata-validation-judgment">validation judgment</dfn> holds of the value and the metadata (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-metadata"></emu-xref>). A brand, whose meta type defines no validation, therefore admits no bare value of its base except through the construction boundary, which is the point of a brand.</p>

      <p>The `StringPattern` meta type carries a pattern as metadata, a source and flags rather than a RegExp object, so that one pattern written in two modules is one type; a hook that reads it is handed a RegExp built from those. Its validation judgment holds of a String exactly when the entire String matches the pattern, the whole-string discipline <emu-xref href="#sec-parsing"></emu-xref> already uses; its subtype judgment holds only of patterns whose `source` and `flags` are identical, which structural equivalence already makes one type, so the floor is reflexivity and the conservatism is intentional. No template-literal type kind exists or is planned: a finite string family is ordinary code over a union of literal types, and an infinite one is a pattern.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Interning is what makes a brand a real newtype: `brand(uint32, 'UserId')` written in two modules is one type with no registry, because the metadata compares structurally and GetTypeObject does the rest. The brand is not a lie told to the checker, as an intersection with a phantom symbol is in an erased system: the parameterization is visible to reflection, participates in validation, and can carry conversion semantics when the brand is a unit rather than a tag. Builders still cannot mint a nominal type, a class, an enum, or a sealed hierarchy, and this clause is why they do not need to: the thing nominal minting is wanted for is a brand.</p>
        <p>The sanctioned sharpening of the pattern subtype judgment is the design's decision procedure over the regular fragment: pattern pairs free of backreferences and lookaround, within a fixed automaton size, get the exact language-inclusion answer, and pairs beyond the bound get the syntactic one. The tier is decided by syntactic size and never by remaining fuel, so the judgment is identical on every host, the same invariant <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref> enforces; the size constant is among the design's open budget numbers. A parameterized field with no initializer takes the base's default where validation admits it or none is defined, and has none otherwise; whether a brand should instead always demand an initializer is a policy its meta type states by defining validation.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-the-type-type">
      <h1>The type Type</h1>
      <p>`type` is the type whose values are the Type Objects (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-and-type-objects"></emu-xref>). It is described by the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~primitive~, [[Name]]: *"type"*, [[Arguments]]: &laquo; &raquo; }, and it is the Static Type of a type name or type expression in expression position (<emu-xref href="#sec-static-type-of-an-expression"></emu-xref>). A function that computes a type annotates its return with it.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`type` is not a value type: its values are Objects, and they have identity. That identity is exactly what interning fixes, so two occurrences of a type expression that denote the same type are the same Type Object rather than merely equal ones. `type` is nonetheless a primitive type in the sense of the Type Record kinds, since it is a named type of the language rather than a structural description of an object's members.</p>
        <p>Because `type` is itself a type, `type` is a value of `type`, and a type argument may be constrained to it, which is what a generic parameter written `T: type` asks for. <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection"></emu-xref> defines what a Type Object exposes.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-existing-language-types">
      <h1>The Existing Language Types</h1>
      <p>The names `number`, `boolean`, `string`, `symbol`, `object`, and `bigint` denote the existing ECMAScript language types of <emu-xref href="#sec-ecmascript-language-types"></emu-xref>: the Number type, the Boolean type, the String type, the Symbol type, the Object type, and the BigInt type respectively. This proposal adds no values to any of them.</p>

      <p>Each is described by a Type Record whose [[Kind]] is ~primitive~ and whose [[Name]] is the name above, except `object`, which is described by { [[Kind]]: ~object~, [[Properties]]: &laquo; &raquo; }, the object type with no required properties, to which every Object is assignable.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`number` is a value type like the rest, so no other numeric type is assignable to it and it is assignable to no other numeric type. It is nonetheless the type an untyped program computes with, and the type a numeric literal takes in the absence of a contextual type (<emu-xref href="#sec-static-type-of-an-expression"></emu-xref>), which is why an unannotated program continues to mean what it means today.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-numeric-types-of-this-proposal">
      <h1>Numeric Types</h1>
      <p>The specification already treats a numeric type as a set of values together with a fixed table of abstract operations over them, and defines Number and BigInt that way in <emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-types"></emu-xref>. Each integer, binary floating-point, decimal floating-point, rational, complex, and vector type is a numeric type in that sense, and provides the operations of that table for which it is defined.</p>

      <p>For a numeric type _T_, the operations are those named in <emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-types"></emu-xref>: _T_::unaryMinus, _T_::bitwiseNOT, _T_::exponentiate, _T_::multiply, _T_::divide, _T_::remainder, _T_::add, _T_::subtract, _T_::leftShift, _T_::signedRightShift, _T_::unsignedRightShift, _T_::lessThan, _T_::equal, _T_::sameValue, _T_::sameValueZero, _T_::bitwiseAND, _T_::bitwiseXOR, _T_::bitwiseOR, and _T_::toString. A numeric type need not define every operation; one it does not define is not applicable to its values, and an operator that would use it is a type error. The definitions are given by <emu-xref href="#sec-operators"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-library"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Reusing this table rather than introducing a parallel one is what lets the existing operator semantics carry over unchanged. The specification generalized these operations from Number to two types when BigInt was added, and the shape it arrived at, an operator dispatching on the numeric type of its operands with no implicit conversion between types, is the shape this proposal needs. What this clause adds is more numeric types, not a new mechanism.</p>
        <p>The table was written for types that are closed under their operations and totally ordered, and not every type here is either. A complex type has no `lessThan`, since the complex numbers are not ordered. A vector type's comparisons return a vector of lane results rather than a Boolean, which is why the SIMD extension gives them their own return type rather than reusing `lessThan`. A rational type's operations can fail where a float's would round. These are the cases <emu-xref href="#sec-which-operations-each-family-defines"></emu-xref> settles, and they are the reason the table above is a list of what may be defined rather than what must be.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-type-names">
      <h1>Type Names</h1>
      <p>Each type of this clause is denoted by a name. The generic names `int`, `uint`, `rational`, `complex`, and `vector` denote a type when applied to arguments. The remaining names in <emu-xref href="#table-type-name-shorthands"></emu-xref> are shorthands, each denoting the same Type Record as its expansion and therefore, after interning, the same Type Object.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-type-name-shorthands" caption="Type Name Shorthands">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Shorthand</th><th>Expansion</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`int8`, `int16`, `int32`, `int64`, `int128`</td><td>`int.&lt;8&gt;`, `int.&lt;16&gt;`, `int.&lt;32&gt;`, `int.&lt;64&gt;`, `int.&lt;128&gt;`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`uint8`, `uint16`, `uint32`, `uint64`, `uint128`</td><td>`uint.&lt;8&gt;`, `uint.&lt;16&gt;`, `uint.&lt;32&gt;`, `uint.&lt;64&gt;`, `uint.&lt;128&gt;`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`boolean1`</td><td>`uint.&lt;1&gt;`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`boolean8`, `boolean16`, `boolean32`, `boolean64`</td><td>`vector.&lt;boolean1, 8&gt;` and so on for 16, 32, and 64 lanes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`rational`</td><td>`rational.&lt;64&gt;`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`complex64`, `complex128`, `complex`</td><td>`complex.&lt;float32&gt;`, `complex.&lt;float64&gt;`, `complex.&lt;number&gt;`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`never`</td><td>the union of no members, the `never` type (<emu-xref href="#sec-never-type"></emu-xref>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>_T_`x`_N_, as in `int8x16`, `uint32x4`, `float32x4`, `boolean32x8`</td><td>`vector.&lt;`_T_`, `_N_`&gt;` for the lane type _T_ and lane count _N_ named</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The shorthands are aliases, not distinct types, so `int8 === int.&lt;8&gt;` is *true* and a value of one is a value of the other. `complex` expands through `number` rather than `float64`, so `complex` and `complex128` are distinct types, as `number` and `float64` are.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>How these names are bound is not settled by the design and is not settled here. Every other part of this proposal is gated by syntax that is an error today, which is the argument that no existing program changes meaning. Type names are not: they are ordinary identifiers, and `number`, `string`, `object`, `boolean`, `symbol`, `any`, and `never` are among the most common identifiers in existing code. The available answers each cost something, and this specification decides for the one that costs existing programs nothing: the built-in type names resolve only in type positions, every one of which is new syntax, through the ordinary scope chain first and through the built-in table only where no user binding of the name exists. Expression-position access is the `type` operator, `const T = type uint8;`, which exists for exactly this. Nothing binds in expression scope, so no existing use, assignment, shadowing, or `typeof` probe of these names changes meaning, and there is nothing to reassign because there is no binding to assign to; a scope that shadows a name in type position loses the short spelling there and writes the operator form. The committee can trade convenience back in later; compatibility cannot be traded back.</p>
        <p>Binding them as properties of the global object is what the language does for every constructor it has added, and it keeps `var string = "x"` working, since a `var` at global scope reuses an existing property. But the design also asks that these names cannot be reassigned, and a non-writable global property would make that same assignment throw in strict mode and fail silently outside it, so the two requirements cannot both hold. Binding them as immutable lexical declarations of the global scope satisfies the design but makes `var string` at the top level of an existing script a *SyntaxError*, which is not acceptable. Requiring them to be imported, which earlier drafts of the design assumed, has no compatibility cost at all and gives up the ambient spelling the rest of the document uses. Resolving them in type position only, as a language with separate type and value namespaces would, is compatible but contradicts types being values, since the same name must denote the type in expression position for reflection to work.</p>
        <p>This is the one place where the proposal's compatibility argument does not reach, and it deserves a decision and a web-compatibility analysis before Stage 2.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-conversions">
    <h1>Conversions</h1>
    <p>This clause defines when a value of one type becomes a value of another. The governing rule is that it does not: a value of one value type never implicitly becomes a value of another, and every conversion between two distinct value types is written in the source text. This clause states that rule, defines the four things that remain implicit and are not conversions between two typed values, and specifies the explicit conversion that a program writes when it wants one.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The rule exists because no widening is lossless for every pair. `int64` to `float64` loses precision above 2**53, and a language that admits the easy cases has to enumerate the hard ones anyway. Refusing all of them is the rule Rust, Swift, and Go use. It is livable here for the same reason it is livable there: the initializers and arguments that would want a conversion are usually literals, and a literal never needs one (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref>).</p>
    </emu-note>

    <emu-clause id="sec-the-conversion-rule">
      <h1>The Conversion Rule</h1>
      <p>At an assignment, an argument, or a return, the source value's type must be assignable to the target type (<emu-xref href="#sec-isassignable"></emu-xref>). Since a ~primitive~ type is a subtype only of itself and of the `any` type, no numeric value type is assignable to another, and a program that requires one to become another states the conversion.</p>

      <p>Four things remain implicit. None is a conversion between two typed values.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-implicit-conversions" caption="What Remains Implicit">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A literal in a typed position</td><td>The literal has no type of its own and takes the type its position requires (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref>). This is not a conversion; nothing is converted from anything.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A value of the `any` type in a typed position</td><td>The value is checked at the boundary and, if it is a numeric value the target represents exactly, converted; a value the target cannot represent raises a *RangeError*, and one of the wrong type a *TypeError* (<emu-xref href="#sec-requiretype"></emu-xref>). An untyped program has no typed boundaries and so behaves as it does today.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Two parameterizations of one base that differ only in their metadata</td><td>They convert by ConvertParameterization (<emu-xref href="#sec-metadata-conversion"></emu-xref>), whose hooks apply at the same boundaries. `Kilometer` and `Meter` are both `float32`, so this is a conversion within a type rather than between two.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A type that declares a conversion</td><td>The conversion applies at the boundary, because the declaring type opted in (<emu-xref href="#sec-user-defined-conversions"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-inferred-result-type">
      <h1>The Inferred Result Type of a Function Literal</h1>
      <p>Where a function literal has no declared return type, its result type is INFERRED from its body, and the inference is the same whichever way the body is written. For a concise arrow body the result type is the Static Type of the body expression. For a block body it is the join of the contributions below, and a literal with a declared return type contributes that type instead of any of this.</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Each `return` within the literal contributes the Static Type of its expression, or *undefined* where it has none.</li>
        <li>Where the body can complete without returning, *undefined* is contributed as well, since falling off the end answers *undefined*.</li>
        <li>A `return` within a NESTED function literal contributes nothing, belonging to that literal.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The join of a set of contributions is the single type where they are all that type, and the union of the distinct ones otherwise. Where any contribution is the ~any~ type the result type is ~any~: a union with an unknown member is unknown, and answering the remaining members would state more than the body supports. An implementation is permitted to be less precise about whether a body can complete - to treat a body as able to complete where it cannot - because contributing *undefined* to the join can only widen the result.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The rule exists so that the two spellings of one function agree. Without it `a.map(_x_ =&gt; _x_)` carries its element type through and `a.map(_x_ =&gt; { return _x_; })` does not, and a program is punished for the braces. Inference of a result type is not inference of a PARAMETER type: a parameter takes its type from the position the literal is written in (<emu-xref href="#table-contextual-types"></emu-xref>) or from its own annotation, and those types are what the body is checked under while this operation reads it.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-contextual-types">
      <h1>Contextual Types</h1>
      <p>The <dfn id="contextual-type">contextual type</dfn> of an expression is the type its position requires, or ~none~ where its position requires nothing. It is determined by the position rather than by the expression, and it is what a literal consults.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-contextual-types" caption="Contextual Types by Position">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Contextual type</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>The |Initializer| of a binding with a |TypeAnnotation|</td><td>The annotated type.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An argument of a call whose callee has a single applicable signature</td><td>The declared type of the corresponding parameter.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An argument whose corresponding parameter's declared type is a generic parameter</td><td>That parameter's evaluated constraint (<emu-xref href="#sec-computed-constraints"></emu-xref>), or ~none~ where it has none.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The operand of a `return` in a function with a return annotation</td><td>The annotated return type.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`this` within the body of a non-arrow function literal whose contextual type is a ~function~ type</td><td>The [[ThisType]] of that type's applicable signature, where it is not ~none~ (<emu-xref href="#sec-this-adoption"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The right operand of an assignment to a target of a known type</td><td>The type of the target.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An operand of a binary operator whose other operand has a known value type</td><td>The type of the other operand.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An element of an array or tuple literal in a position of a known array or tuple type</td><td>The element type of that position.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The operand of an explicit conversion to _T_</td><td>~none~. A conversion converts a value, so its operand is typed on its own.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Any other position</td><td>~none~.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>An argument of an overloaded call has no single contextual type until an overload is chosen, and the choice depends on the argument. The literal ranking of <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-overload-ranking"></emu-xref> is what resolves that circularity for a literal argument. <emu-xref href="#sec-overload-resolution"></emu-xref> specifies the rest of overload resolution.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-literal-propagation">
      <h1>Literal Propagation</h1>
      <p>A numeric literal has no type of its own. In a position whose contextual type is a numeric value type, the literal takes that type, and a literal whose value that type cannot represent is a type error rather than a silent truncation. This refines the Static Type given for a numeric literal in <emu-xref href="#table-static-type-base"></emu-xref>, which is the type of a literal whose contextual type is ~none~.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-literalvalueintype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          LiteralValueInType (
            _literal_: a Parse Node,
            _t_: a Type Record,
          ): either a value of the type _t_ or ~unrepresentable~
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the value of the type _t_ that _literal_ denotes, or ~unrepresentable~ if _t_ cannot represent it.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _mv_ be the mathematical value denoted by _literal_, as defined by the numeric literal grammar, before any rounding.
          1. If _t_ is an integer type, then
            1. If _mv_ is not an integer, return ~unrepresentable~.
            1. If _t_ has a value representing _mv_ (<emu-xref href="#sec-integer-types"></emu-xref>), return that value.
            1. Return ~unrepresentable~.
          1. If _t_ is a binary floating-point type or the Number type, then
            1. Let _v_ be the result of rounding _mv_ to the nearest value of _t_, with ties to even.
            1. If _v_ is an infinity and _mv_ is finite, return ~unrepresentable~.
            1. Return _v_.
          1. If _t_ is a decimal floating-point type, then
            1. Let _q_ be the number of digits _literal_ writes after its decimal point, negated, adjusted by any exponent _literal_ writes.
            1. Let _c_ be the integer such that _mv_ = _c_ &times; 10**_q_.
            1. If _c_ is outside the coefficient range of _t_, return ~unrepresentable~.
            1. If _q_ is outside the exponent range of _t_, return ~unrepresentable~.
            1. Return the value of _t_ with coefficient _c_ and exponent _q_.
          1. If _t_ is `bigint`, then
            1. If _mv_ is not an integer, return ~unrepresentable~.
            1. Return the BigInt value whose mathematical value is _mv_.
          1. If _t_ is a rational type, then
            1. Let _n_ be the numerator of _mv_ expressed in lowest terms with a positive denominator.
            1. Let _d_ be the denominator of _mv_ expressed in lowest terms with a positive denominator.
            1. Let _ct_ be the integer type of the components of _t_.
            1. If _n_ is a value of _ct_ and _d_ is a value of _ct_, return the value of _t_ with numerator _n_ and denominator _d_.
            1. Return ~unrepresentable~.
          1. If _t_ is a complex type, then
            1. Let _ct_ be the type of the components of _t_.
            1. Let _r_ be LiteralValueInType(_literal_, _ct_).
            1. If _r_ is ~unrepresentable~, return ~unrepresentable~.
            1. Return the value of _t_ whose real component is _r_ and whose imaginary component is the zero of _ct_.
          1. Return ~unrepresentable~.
        </emu-alg>

        <emu-note>
          <p>"The mathematical value denoted by _literal_ ... before any rounding" is a requirement on the IMPLEMENTATION as much as on this operation, and it is easy to violate without noticing. A lexer that produces a Number for every numeric literal has already rounded by the time any contextual type is known: `9007199254740993` has become 9007199254740992, and an implementation reading its own scanned value would answer that, which is a value the source did not write. Retaining the literal's source text, or its exact value, is what lets this operation be implemented as specified. The rounding step for a binary floating-point _t_ is where rounding is permitted, and it is written as a step precisely because it must not have happened earlier.</p>
        </emu-note>
        <emu-note>
          <p>`bigint` takes a plain integer literal like any other numeric type, so `let x: bigint = 65` needs no `n` suffix. The suffix exists because BigInt was added before there was a type in the position to take the literal's type FROM; where a type is written it carries no information the annotation does not, and requiring it there would make `bigint` the one numeric type a literal could not reach. It remains the only spelling in an UNTYPED position, where there is nothing to take a type from, and it remains necessary for a value beyond 2<sup>53</sup> in any implementation that reads a literal through a Number before its type is known.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The mathematical value of a literal is exact. `0.1` denotes one tenth, not the Number nearest to one tenth, so `0.1` in a `decimal64` position is the decimal one tenth and in a `rational` position is 1/10, while in a `float32` position it rounds. This falls out of the specification already defining a literal's mathematical value and rounding it only when producing a Number.</p>
          <p>A decimal type reads its cohort member from the source text rather than from the mathematical value, since `1.0` and `1.00` have the same mathematical value and are different values of the type (<emu-xref href="#sec-decimal-floating-point-types"></emu-xref>). A literal therefore carries its significance into the type, which is what makes a price written to two places a value with two places.</p>
          <p>Representability is exact for an integer, a decimal, and a rational, and is range membership for a float, which rounds. A float is the one family where a literal that is not representable exactly is still accepted, because requiring exactness would reject `0.1` in a `float32` position and no useful program could be written.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>The Static Type of a numeric literal whose contextual type is a numeric value type _t_ is _t_. It is a type error if LiteralValueInType(_literal_, _t_) is ~unrepresentable~.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The error is an Early Error (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-errors"></emu-xref>), so `f(256)` where `f` takes a `uint8` does not compile, and neither does `let a: uint8 = 200; a + 300;`, where the literal takes the type of the other operand. This is the difference between a literal and a cast: a cast is an instruction to discard information and truncates, while a literal that does not fit its type is a mistake.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>One case in the design does not follow this rule. A store to an element of a typed array, as in `array[0] = 300` where `array` is a `[].&lt;uint8&gt;`, is specified to wrap to 44 rather than to be rejected, on the grounds that it is unchanged from what a `Uint8Array` does today. But `[].&lt;uint8&gt;` is new syntax, so no existing program writes that store, and the compatibility argument does not apply to it; meanwhile the literal rule says the same literal in a `uint8` binding does not compile. The two cannot both be right for a literal element, and <emu-xref href="#sec-array-defaults-and-stores"></emu-xref> decides for the literal rule: the store of an out-of-range literal is rejected like every other typed position, and the wrap of <emu-xref href="#table-out-of-range-treatment"></emu-xref> continues to govern a stored value that is not a literal.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-explicit-conversion">
      <h1>Explicit Conversion</h1>
      <p>An explicit conversion is written as a call on the type, `uint8(v)`, or with the conversion operator after the value, `v := uint8` (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-in-expression-position"></emu-xref>). The two are the same operation. A conversion is an instruction to discard information: it truncates, wraps, or rounds as the target requires, and does not fail merely because information is lost.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-convertvalue" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          ConvertValue (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _from_: a Type Record,
            _to_: a Type Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing an ECMAScript language value or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It converts _value_, of the type _from_, to the type _to_.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If SameType(_from_, _to_) is *true*, return _value_.
          1. If _from_.[[Kind]] is ~any~, return ? RequireType(_value_, _to_).
          1. If _to_.[[Kind]] is ~any~, return _value_.
          1. If _from_ is a numeric type and _to_ is a numeric type, return ? NumericConvert(_value_, _from_, _to_).
          1. If _to_ is `string` or `boolean`, return ? PrimitiveConvert(_value_, _to_).
          1. Let _conversion_ be the user-defined conversion from _from_ to _to_, if one is declared (<emu-xref href="#sec-user-defined-conversions"></emu-xref>).
          1. If _conversion_ is not ~empty~, return ? the result of applying _conversion_ to _value_.
          1. If IsAssignable(_from_, _to_) is *true*, return _value_.
          1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The final step is reached only where the operand types are related by no rule above. Where those types are known statically the conversion is rejected as a type error before evaluation (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-errors"></emu-xref>), so the exception is thrown only where a type reaches the conversion dynamically, as when a Type Object computed at run time is called as a function.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-numericconvert" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          NumericConvert (
            _value_: a value of a numeric type,
            _from_: a numeric Type Record,
            _to_: a numeric Type Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing a value of the type _to_ or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It converts _value_ between two numeric types according to <emu-xref href="#table-numeric-conversions"></emu-xref>.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If <emu-xref href="#table-numeric-conversions"></emu-xref> specifies a *RangeError* for _value_ converted from _from_ to _to_, throw a *RangeError* exception.
          1. Let _result_ be the value given by <emu-xref href="#table-numeric-conversions"></emu-xref> for the families of _from_ and _to_.
          1. Assert: _result_ is a value of the type _to_.
          1. Return _result_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-primitiveconvert" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          PrimitiveConvert (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _to_: a Type Record for `string` or `boolean`,
          ): either a normal completion containing a value of the type _to_ or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It converts _value_ to one of the ordinary primitive types, where a conversion for its source is defined.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _to_ is `boolean`, return ToBoolean(_value_).
          1. Assert: _to_ is `string`.
          1. If <emu-xref href="#table-string-conversion-sources"></emu-xref> gives no conversion for _value_, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return ? ToString(_value_).
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-table id="table-string-conversion-sources" caption="Conversion Sources for the String Type">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Conversion</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A String</td><td>The value.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A value of a numeric type, including `bigint`</td><td>ToString of the value.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A Boolean</td><td>ToString of the value.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*undefined*, *null*, an object, or a Symbol</td><td>None. A *TypeError*.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The rule for the `string` type divides its sources by whether the source has a canonical text or only a diagnostic one, and not by whether it is a primitive. A number, a BigInt, and a Boolean each have exactly one text that denotes them, ToString of them is total, and nothing is lost: `5` is `'5'` and reads back as `5`. That is worth having without ceremony, and it is why this rule is not the mirror image of the numeric one. The asymmetry is principled rather than convenient: ToString of a number is total and lossless, while ToNumber of a string is partial and lossy, so the direction that cannot fail is admitted and the direction that can is written as a parse (<emu-xref href="#sec-parsing"></emu-xref>).</p>
        <p>*undefined*, *null*, an object, and a Symbol are refused because their text is a diagnostic rather than a denotation. The values they would produce are the language's best-known silent failures: a `'undefined'` reaching an interface that was meant to show a name, a `'[object Object]'` where a field was meant, and an array quietly becoming its comma-joined elements. A program that wants those writes `String(_v_)` and has then said so. A Symbol is refused for the reason it always was, which is that ToString of one throws.</p>
        <p>The `boolean` type takes every value, and that is deliberate rather than an omission. ToBoolean is total, every value has a defined truthiness, and `if (_v_)` is the language's own idiom for asking. There is no lost value to warn about and no second answer a program might have meant.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-table id="table-numeric-conversions" caption="Numeric Conversions">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>integer</td><td>integer of width _M_</td><td>The mathematical value of the source modulo 2**_M_, interpreted as a value of the target. Signed targets wrap in two's complement.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>integer</td><td>binary float</td><td>The source rounded to the nearest value of the target, with ties to even.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>binary float</td><td>integer of width _M_</td><td>If the source is NaN or an infinity, 0. Otherwise the source truncated toward zero, modulo 2**_M_, interpreted as a value of the target.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>binary float</td><td>binary float</td><td>The source rounded to the nearest value of the target, with ties to even. A finite source outside the target's range becomes an infinity of the same sign.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>the Number type</td><td>`float64`, and the reverse</td><td>The source unchanged. The two types have the same values (<emu-xref href="#sec-binary-floating-point-types"></emu-xref>), so the conversion is exact and total.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>rational</td><td>binary float or the Number type</td><td>The exact quotient rounded to the nearest value of the target, with ties to even.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>binary float or the Number type</td><td>rational</td><td>The source's exact value, which is a dyadic rational, in lowest terms. A *RangeError* if its numerator or denominator is not representable, or if the source is NaN or an infinity.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>any numeric type</td><td>decimal</td><td>The source's exact value if it is representable, otherwise rounded to the target's precision. A *RangeError* if the source's exponent is outside the target's range, since a decimal's range is a property of the type rather than of the format.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>decimal</td><td>binary float or integer</td><td>Open. See the note below.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>complex or vector</td><td>any other family, and the reverse</td><td>Not defined by this clause. The complex numbers and SIMD extensions define the conversions of their types.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The design leaves the conversions between the decimal types and the binary types open, noting only that they would need to be clear. They are the hard ones, and the difficulty is not arithmetic but which cohort member results. A `float64` converted to a `decimal128` has an exact decimal expansion, since every binary float is a terminating decimal, but that expansion for 0.1 is a 55-digit value that no `decimal128` can hold, so the result must be rounded and its exponent chosen. Choosing the exponent that preserves the most significance, choosing the shortest expansion that round-trips, and choosing a fixed quantum are all defensible and produce different values. This clause specifies the direction that is exact and leaves the other open deliberately.</p>
        <p>Integer wrapping is the same operation the language already performs when storing to a `Uint8Array`, and the float to integer rule is the shape of the existing ToInt32 and ToUint8 operations: truncate, then reduce modulo the width, with a non-finite source becoming zero. Reusing that shape means a typed array store and an explicit cast agree.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-user-defined-conversions">
      <h1>User-Defined Conversions</h1>
      <p>A type may declare a conversion to or from another type, and a declared conversion applies at an assignment, an argument, and a return, because the type that declares it has opted in. Three forms declare one.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-user-defined-conversions" caption="Declared Conversions">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Declared on</th><th>Converts</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A constructor taking one parameter of type _S_</td><td>The target class _T_</td><td>_S_ to _T_. A converting constructor, so `let t: MyType = 1;` is legal when `MyType`'s constructor takes a `float32`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`operator` _T_`()`, a parameterless member</td><td>The source class _S_</td><td>_S_ to _T_, by converting the receiver.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`operator` _T_`(value: ` _S_`)`, a member taking one parameter</td><td>The target _T_</td><td>_S_ to _T_, running with no receiver. This is the form a type declares when its constructor is already spoken for, as `Temporal.Instant`'s is by epoch nanoseconds.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>When both a converting constructor and a declared conversion could apply to the same pair, the constructor is preferred. It is a type error if two conversions of the same form apply and neither is more specific.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A conversion to a tuple type is what makes a class destructurable: a class declaring `operator [number, number, string]()` may be destructured into three bindings. The operator overloading extension specifies the declaration syntax; this clause specifies only when a declared conversion is consulted.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <p><ins>Array destructuring is defined over the ITERATION protocol rather than over a contextual type - `const [_x_, _y_, _z_] = _a_` performs GetIterator(_a_) - so a declared tuple conversion is consulted where that iteration would otherwise fail. Where the value of an ArrayBindingPattern or an ArrayAssignmentPattern is not iterable and its class declares a conversion to a tuple type, the conversion is applied and its result is destructured in place of the value.</ins></p>
      <p><ins>Iteration is attempted FIRST, so a class that both declares `[Symbol.iterator]` and a tuple conversion is destructured by its iterator. A declared conversion says how the value converts, not that the value is a sequence: it does not make `_a_` spreadable, nor usable in a `for`-`of` head, both of which continue to require an iterator.</ins></p>
      <emu-note>
        <p><ins>An earlier draft of the note above attributed this to the destructuring position carrying a tuple contextual type. It does not carry one - a destructuring pattern cannot be annotated at all, `const [_x_]: [number] = _a_` being a Syntax Error - and reading the rule that way leads an implementation to look for a contextual type that is never there.</ins></p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-literal-overload-ranking">
      <h1>Literal Overload Ranking</h1>
      <p>A literal argument may satisfy more than one signature of an overloaded function, since it has no type of its own and several parameter types could represent it. The signatures are ranked by the parameter type in the order of <emu-xref href="#table-literal-ranking"></emu-xref>, and the first that can represent the literal is chosen. This ranks literals. An argument that is a typed value is not ranked; it selects the signature its type is assignable to.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-literal-ranking" caption="Literal Ranking of Numeric Types">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Types, in order</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1</td><td>`float64`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2</td><td>`float128`, `float32`, `float16`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3</td><td>`decimal128`, `decimal64`, `decimal32`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4</td><td>`uint128`, `uint64`, `uint32`, `uint16`, `uint8`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5</td><td>`int128`, `int64`, `int32`, `int16`, `int8`</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The ranking is the only place the order of the numeric types matters, and it matters only for a literal. Given `f(a: float32)` and `f(a: uint32)`, the call `f(1)` selects the `float32` signature by the ranking, while `f(uint32(1))` selects the `uint32` one because its argument is typed.</p>
        <p>The ranking as the design gives it does not include `number`, and it should. `number` is the type a numeric literal takes with no contextual type, the type an untyped program computes with, and the type the design's own justification for ranking `float64` first appeals to. Given `f(a: number)` and `f(a: float32)`, the call `f(1)` has no answer above. The natural repair is to rank `number` first, ahead of `float64`, on the same reasoning that ranks `float64` ahead of the rest. It is recorded here rather than assumed because it changes which function a call selects.</p>
        <p>The ranking also omits the rational types and the parameterized widths `int.&lt;N&gt;` and `uint.&lt;N&gt;` for an _N_ that is not a named shorthand, both of which can represent a literal. <emu-xref href="#sec-completing-the-literal-ranking"></emu-xref> says where they fall.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-existing-abstract-conversion-operations">
      <h1>The Existing Abstract Conversion Operations</h1>
      <p>The abstract operations of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-conversion"></emu-xref>, including ToNumber, ToString, ToPrimitive, and ToBoolean, continue to apply to the values of the existing language types unchanged. A value of a type added by this proposal is not an Object, so ToPrimitive returns it unchanged, and ToBoolean of a numeric value is *false* exactly when the value is a zero or a NaN of its type.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>ToNumber is where the conversion rule and the existing semantics meet, and the meeting needs care. ToNumber of a `uint8` would produce a Number, which is a conversion between two value types that the rule forbids, so an operator specified in terms of ToNumber cannot simply be applied to a typed operand. The resolution is that an operator on typed operands dispatches through the numeric type protocol (<emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-types-of-this-proposal"></emu-xref>) rather than through ToNumber, so `+a` on a `uint8` is a `uint8` and never a Number. ToNumber retains its meaning for the `any` type, where the operand's type is not known statically and the existing dynamic semantics apply. <emu-xref href="#sec-operator-dispatch"></emu-xref> specifies the dispatch; this note records why ToNumber is not the mechanism.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-runtime-enforcement">
    <h1>Runtime Enforcement</h1>
    <p>A type violation is reported where it is found: at compile time where the types are known, and at run time otherwise. This clause specifies the second half. It defines the runtime type of a value, the test that decides whether a value belongs to a type, the check that enforces a type at a boundary, the boundaries at which that check is inserted, and the circumstances under which it is not needed at all.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The checks exist because of the `any` type. A value reaches a typed position with its type unknown only when it passes through `any`, which is the type of every binding an untyped program declares. Everything else is settled statically. This is what makes the system gradual rather than merely optional: the guarantee a typed program has is not that its author annotated carefully, but that the boundary with untyped code is checked.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <emu-clause id="sec-runtime-type-of-a-value">
      <h1>The Runtime Type of a Value</h1>

      <emu-clause id="sec-runtimetypeof" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          RuntimeTypeOf (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
          ): a Type Record
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the Type Record describing the type of which _value_ is a value.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _value_ is a value of one of the types added by <emu-xref href="#sec-type-universe"></emu-xref>, return that type's Type Record.
          1. If _value_ is *undefined*, return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~primitive~, [[Name]]: *"undefined"*, [[Arguments]]: &laquo; &raquo; }.
          1. If _value_ is *null*, return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~primitive~, [[Name]]: *"null"*, [[Arguments]]: &laquo; &raquo; }.
          1. If _value_ is a Boolean, a String, a Symbol, a Number, or a BigInt, return the Type Record of the corresponding type named in <emu-xref href="#sec-existing-language-types"></emu-xref>.
          1. If _value_ is a Type Object, return the Type Record for `type`.
          1. Assert: _value_ is an Object.
          1. If _value_ has a [[RuntimeType]] internal slot, return _value_.[[RuntimeType]].
          1. If _value_ is an instance of a class, return the ~nominal~ Type Record of that class.
          1. If _value_ is an Array, return the ~array~ Type Record whose [[Element]] is the element type _value_ carries where it has one, and otherwise the union of the runtime types of its elements, or the `any` type where it has none; and whose [[Extent]] is _value_'s extent where it carries one and ~dynamic~ otherwise.
          1. If _value_ is callable and has declared signatures, return the ~function~ Type Record whose [[Signatures]] are those signatures.
          1. Return the ~object~ Type Record whose [[Properties]] describes the own properties of _value_ and their declared types, where a property with no declared type has the `any` type.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>A callable value that declares NO signature reaches the last step and reports an ~object~ Type Record, as it did before this step existed. That is the same rule an unannotated member follows: an unannotated declaration stays unannotated, and reporting a signature of all-`any` parameters for a function that declared none would be inference the program did not ask for.</p>

        <p>The step is placed where it is for the reason the Array step above it exists: an operation that RANKS types rather than walking values sees only what this returns, so a callable reporting an ~object~ Type Record could not be ranked against a ~function~ type. `f` `is` `F` and passing `f` where an `F` is declared already answer from the signatures; without this step `RuntimeTypeOf` answered differently about the same value.</p>

        <p>Every value has exactly one runtime type, because the values of distinct value types are distinct (<emu-xref href="#sec-value-types"></emu-xref>). The `uint8` representing 0 and the `uint16` representing 0 are different values, so `a instanceof uint16` is *false* for a `uint8` binding `a` even though `typeof a` reports *"number"* for both. `typeof` is unchanged by this proposal: it reports the underlying language type, and RuntimeTypeOf is what reports the type of this proposal. <emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-typeof"></emu-xref> exposes the latter as `Reflect.typeOf`.</p>
          <p>An Array reports an ~array~ type rather than the ~object~ type describing its indices, so that the type of a value agrees with the types it is a member of: membership for an ~array~ type walks a value's length and elements (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-membership"></emu-xref>), and the union of the elements' types is the tightest [[Element]] for which that walk succeeds. Without the agreement an operation that RANKS types rather than walking values - overload resolution over <emu-xref href="#table-argument-match-ranks"></emu-xref>, and the inference of a type parameter from an argument - compared an ~object~ type against an ~array~ type and found no relation, so no argument could select a parameter of an array type.</p>

          <p>The [[RuntimeType]] slot is what a Proxy constructed with a type argument carries, so that it reports the type its trap checks enforce rather than the shape of its target. A Proxy constructed without one has no slot and is an ordinary Object.</p>
          <p>The types named *"undefined"* and *"null"* are the `undefined` and `null` types of <emu-xref href="#sec-null-and-undefined-types"></emu-xref>, which the type universe names and which |PredefinedType| (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-expressions"></emu-xref>) admits in type position. The design writes `undefined` as a type, since the reject type of a typed promise is `undefined` by convention when the promise never rejects, and a value that may be absent has a type such as `uint32 | undefined`.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-type-membership">
      <h1>Type Membership</h1>

      <emu-clause id="sec-isoftype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          IsOfType (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _t_: a Type Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing a Boolean or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether _value_ is a value of the type _t_. It may call user code, since deciding a structural type reads the value's properties. A structural check reads each member of the type once and decides on what it read.</dd>

        </dl>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Reading each member once is a discipline rather than an optimization. Where the value is a Proxy or carries accessors, a trap or getter runs at most once per member per check, so it cannot answer one way to the step that admits the value and another to a later one. This is the rule the composite types already follow when reading a source's properties in a single pass (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-types"></emu-xref>), generalized to every structural check now that <emu-xref href="#sec-object-types"></emu-xref> lets any interface be satisfied structurally.</p>
        </emu-note>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~any~, return *true*.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~void~, return *false*.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~union~, then
            1. For each element _m_ of _t_.[[Members]], do
              1. If ? IsOfType(_value_, _m_) is *true*, return *true*.
            1. Return *false*.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~intersection~, then
            1. For each element _m_ of _t_.[[Members]], do
              1. If ? IsOfType(_value_, _m_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Return *true*.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~parameterized~, then
            1. If IsSubtype(RuntimeTypeOf(_value_), _t_, &laquo; &raquo;) is *true*, return *true*.
            1. If ? IsOfType(_value_, _t_.[[Base]]) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. If the <emu-xref href="#metadata-validation-judgment">validation judgment</emu-xref> holds of _value_ and _t_.[[Metadata]], return *true*.
            1. Return *false*.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~literal~, return SameValue(_value_, _t_.[[Value]]).
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~object~, then
            1. If _value_ is not an Object, return *false*.
            1. For each Property Type Record _p_ of _t_.[[Properties]], do
              1. Let _present_ be ? HasProperty(_value_, _p_'s key).
              1. If _present_ is *false*, then
                1. If _p_ is not optional, return *false*.
              1. Else,
                1. Let _pv_ be ? Get(_value_, _p_'s key).
                1. If ? IsOfType(_pv_, _p_'s type) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Return *true*.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~array~ or _t_.[[Kind]] is ~tuple~, then
            1. If _value_ is not an Object, return *false*.
            1. Return ? the result of checking _value_'s length and elements against _t_ (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-membership"></emu-xref>).
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~function~, then
            1. If IsCallable(_value_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Return IsAssignable(RuntimeTypeOf(_value_), _t_).
          1. Return IsSubtype(RuntimeTypeOf(_value_), _t_, &laquo; &raquo;).
        </emu-alg>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Deciding a structural type reads the value's properties, so the check calls user code: a property backed by a getter runs it, which may throw, may have side effects, and may return a different value each time it is read. A check is therefore not a passive observation, and this is why the operation returns a completion. A getter that returns a value of the required type once and a different value afterwards defeats the check, which is the same aliasing exposure noted below. Whether a boundary check should read through accessors at all, or inspect only own data properties, is settled as reading them: a property's type is a claim about what the property yields, and an interface implemented by accessors, which is how interfaces are routinely implemented, would otherwise be unverifiable at exactly the boundary that exists to verify it.</p>
          <p>The final step is the general case: the value's runtime type is compared against the target with the subtype relation of <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>, so the runtime test and the static one agree by construction. The earlier steps exist because a structural type cannot be decided by comparing two Type Records: an ordinary Object's runtime type is a description of the properties it happens to have, and the question is whether it has the ones the target requires.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The ~function~ case is checked shallowly and is unsound, and this is the one place where the specification knowingly permits a type violation to go unreported. A function's signature cannot be verified without calling it, so the check confirms only that the value is callable and that the signature it declares is assignable to the target, which admits `any`. An untyped function reaching a `(uint8) =&gt; uint8` position therefore passes, and if it returns a String the caller receives one where its static type promised a `uint8`.</p>
          <p>This is the higher-order problem every gradual type system meets, and the design does not address it. The available answers are the known ones. The value can be wrapped so that each call checks its arguments and its result, which is sound and is what a contract system does, at the cost of an allocation per boundary crossing, a change to object identity, and a blame mechanism to say which side broke the contract. The check can stay shallow and the unsoundness be accepted, as an optional type system does, at the cost of the guarantee. Or a check can be inserted at each call through a binding whose runtime signature has an `any` where its static type does not, which is a wrapper by another name but pays only where the imprecision is. The choice bears on the proposal's central claim, since a type the engine cannot rely on is a type the engine cannot optimize against. This specification accepts the third: the boundary checks the shape, and the hole through a higher-order crossing is documented rather than papered over. Wrapping breaks the identity interning makes load-bearing, `f === f` across a boundary, and taxes every crossing to police a rare one; blame is machinery without precedent in the language. A program that needs the guarantee declares its functions, and nothing this proposal promises elsewhere is withdrawn, since the layout premise never extended inside a function value.</p>
          <p>The ~object~ case is checked at the boundary but not afterwards, and has the same shape of problem. A plain Object's properties are untyped unless declared with the `{ (a: uint8): 1 }` form of the object typing section, so an aliased write through an untyped reference can invalidate a check that has already passed. <emu-xref href="#sec-object-types-semantics"></emu-xref> settles whether a boundary imposes the target's property types on the object it accepts: it does not.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-the-boundary-check">
      <h1>The Boundary Check</h1>
      <p>At a boundary where a value of the `any` type reaches a position of a known type, the value is checked and, if it is a numeric value that the target can represent exactly, converted. A value the target cannot represent raises an error.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-requiretype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          RequireType (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _t_: a Type Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing an ECMAScript language value or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It enforces the type _t_ on _value_ at a boundary, returning the value of the type _t_ to be used.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If ? IsOfType(_value_, _t_) is *true*, return _value_.
          1. If _t_ is a ~union~ type, return ? SelectUnionMember(_value_, _t_).
          1. If _t_ is a numeric type and RuntimeTypeOf(_value_) is a numeric type, then
            1. Let _r_ be the result of converting _value_ to _t_ by <emu-xref href="#table-numeric-conversions"></emu-xref>, except that a conversion that would wrap, truncate toward zero, or round a finite value to an infinity instead yields ~unrepresentable~.
            1. If _r_ is ~unrepresentable~, throw a *RangeError* exception.
            1. Return _r_.
          1. If _t_ is `string` or `boolean`, return ? PrimitiveConvert(_value_, _t_).
          1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The two failures are different and are reported differently. A numeric value at a numeric type has a conversion available and the only question is whether this value survives it, which is a question of range: hence the *RangeError*. A value of any other type has no numeric conversion to attempt, which is a question of type: hence the *TypeError*, and it is raised before any coercion is tried. Coercing first and then range-checking the result would make an annotation a coercion rather than a check: it would accept a string, a Boolean, *null*, and any object with a `valueOf`, and at a float type, where every value is in range, it could not fail at all, so a missing field would become a NaN that surfaces far from the annotation that admitted it.</p>
          <p>This is why a string is not a source here even when it reads as a number. `'5'` at a `uint8` is refused and the program writes `uint8.parse('5')` or `uint8.tryParse('5')`, which is the same position <emu-xref href="#sec-parsing"></emu-xref> states, and which distinguishes a string that is not a literal of the type from one that is a literal out of range.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-union-boundary-selection">
        <h1>Selecting a Union Member at a Boundary</h1>
        <p>Where the target of a boundary is a union and the value is not already of it, some member receives the value, and which one is decided by what the VALUE is rather than by where a member was written. A union is canonical (<emu-xref href="#sec-canonicalizetype"></emu-xref>): its members are held in a fixed order and Type Objects are interned on that record, so `uint8 | uint32` and `uint32 | uint8` are one type, and a boundary may meet it as a Type Object that no source text spelled. A rule that read the order of the members would therefore give one type two behaviours.</p>

        <emu-clause id="sec-selectunionmember" type="abstract operation">
          <h1>
            SelectUnionMember (
              _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
              _t_: a Type Record whose [[Kind]] is ~union~,
            ): either a normal completion containing an ECMAScript language value or a throw completion
          </h1>
          <dl class="header">
            <dt>description</dt>
            <dd>It returns the value of some member of _t_ that _value_ crosses into, or throws where no member admits it.</dd>
          </dl>
          <emu-alg>
            1. For each member _m_ of _t_.[[Members]], do
              1. If ? IsOfType(_value_, _m_) is *true*, return _value_.
            1. If _value_ is a Number, a BigInt, or a value of a numeric type, then
              1. Let _exact_ be a List of the members of _t_.[[Members]] that are numeric types whose values include the mathematical value of _value_ exactly.
              1. Order _exact_ by width, narrowest first; where two members have the same width, an unsigned integer type precedes a signed one; and where _value_ is an integer, every integer type precedes every floating-point and decimal type.
              1. For each member _m_ of _exact_, do
                1. Let _r_ be Completion(RequireType(_value_, _m_)).
                1. If _r_ is a normal completion, return _r_.
            1. For each member _m_ of _t_.[[Members]], do
              1. Let _r_ be Completion(RequireType(_value_, _m_)).
              1. If _r_ is a normal completion, return _r_.
            1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
          </emu-alg>
        </emu-clause>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The rungs answer, in order: is the value already of a member, does a member represent it exactly as a number, and does any member admit it at all. The last is where the canonical-text rung of the `string` rule lands, and putting it last is the substance of this clause: at `string | uint32` a Number would otherwise have crossed into `string` and the boundary would have stored `"42"`, silently textifying a number that a member of the same union represents exactly. That is the failure <emu-xref href="#sec-primitiveconvert"></emu-xref> refuses `undefined` and an object to prevent, and it should not be reachable through the order two members were written in.</p>
          <p>The narrowest representing member is preferred because a union of numeric types is written to say which representations a value may have, and the tightest one that holds the value loses nothing; an integer source prefers an integer member for the same reason. A member's choice is observable, since it decides what `Reflect.typeOf` reports and which arithmetic the value then participates in, so a program that means one member in particular writes the conversion rather than relying on this operation.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>The three ways a numeric value may fail to fit a type are distinguished, and each is treated according to what the program said.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-out-of-range-treatment" caption="Treatment of a Value That Does Not Fit">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>The program writes</th><th>Example</th><th>Treatment</th><th>Because</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A literal in a typed position</td><td>`let u: uint8 = 300;`</td><td>An Early Error. The source text does not compile.</td><td>A literal that does not fit its type is a mistake, and it is visible in the source text.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An explicit conversion</td><td>`uint8(300)`</td><td>The value wraps, to 44.</td><td>A cast is an instruction to discard information. The program asked.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A value of the `any` type at a typed boundary</td><td>`let u: uint8 = anyValue;`</td><td>A *RangeError* at run time.</td><td>Nothing in the source text says to discard information, and the value is not known until it arrives.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The boundary converts rather than merely checking, which is what makes untyped data usable: a Number parsed from JSON reaching a `uint8` field is a Number, not a `uint8`, and a check that demanded it already be a `uint8` would reject every value that ever crossed the boundary. Converting only where the target can represent the value exactly, and raising a *RangeError* otherwise, keeps the loss from being silent. This is the same distinction the checked arithmetic of `Math.addChecked` draws against the wrapping of `+`.</p>
        <p>The rule above permits a float target to round, since it treats only wrapping, truncation, and overflow to an infinity as unrepresentable, so a Number 0.1 reaching a `float32` boundary becomes the nearest `float32`. The alternative, demanding exactness, would reject almost every float that crossed a boundary and is not usable. This matches the treatment of a literal, which also rounds into a float and errors only outside the range, and it is recorded here because it is a choice rather than a consequence.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-check-insertion">
      <h1>Where Checks Are Inserted</h1>
      <p>A check is inserted where a value whose static type is `any` reaches a position whose type is not, and nowhere else. <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref> lists the positions.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-check-sites" caption="Boundaries">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>The initializer or assigned value of a binding with a declared type _t_</td><td>RequireType(_value_, _t_)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An argument passed to a parameter of declared type _t_</td><td>RequireType(_value_, _t_)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The operand of a `return` in a function with a declared return type _t_</td><td>RequireType(_value_, _t_)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A value stored to a property or field of declared type _t_</td><td>RequireType(_value_, _t_)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A value stored to an element of an array of element type _t_</td><td>RequireType(_value_, _t_)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A value read from a position of the `any` type into an operator whose operands are typed</td><td>RequireType(_value_, _t_) for the operand type _t_ the operator requires</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A value crossing into a typed position through reflection, including `Reflect.set` and `Reflect.defineProperty`</td><td>RequireType(_value_, _t_)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The list is the same list of boundaries at which a user-defined conversion is consulted (<emu-xref href="#sec-user-defined-conversions"></emu-xref>) and at which the metadata protocol of the primitive metadata extension applies, and at which a `where` clause that cannot be decided statically is evaluated. This is not a coincidence: a boundary is where a value acquires a type it did not have, and every mechanism that has something to say about that acquisition says it there.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>RequireType at an object type CONVERTS the members the type declares, and does not merely test them. A plain object reaching `{ x: `uint8` }` has a Number where a `uint8` is required, and testing would refuse every object literal a program writes; the array element positions have converted since the propagation rule was written, and an object's members are the same boundary one level in. The conversion is applied to the object rather than to a copy of it, because an object may carry properties the type does not declare and keeps them under width subtyping, which a copy assembled from the declared members alone would discard. Converting the members does not decide membership on its own: a type may state more than its members do - a `where` predicate, an index signature - so the conversion precedes the membership test rather than replacing it.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <emu-note>
          <p>"A value of the wrong type" is decided by the target, and for the `number` type it means any value that is not numeric. Reading such a value through ToNumber instead - a String as its parsed value or a NaN, *null* as zero, *undefined* as a NaN, an object as whatever its primitive is - would produce at a checked boundary exactly the silent failures the `string` target's rule is written to prevent, and would do it at the type a program is most likely to annotate. The explicit conversion remains available and is what a program writes when it wants that reading: `_v_ := `number`` is a cast, and a cast is not a boundary.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-check-elision">
      <h1>Elision of Checks</h1>
      <p>A check is required only where the static types do not already establish the result. It is not inserted, and an implementation need not perform one, in the following circumstances.</p>

      <ul>
        <li>The static type of the value is not `any`, is not a literal type, is assignable to the target, and is STABLE (<emu-xref href="#sec-elision-stability"></emu-xref>). The result is established statically, and any violation was an Early Error. The literal exclusion is not a detail: a numeric literal is assignable to `uint8` by <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref> and the boundary is still what CONSTRUCTS the value of that type, so `let _x_: `uint8` = 5` must perform its conversion and produce a `uint8` rather than the Number 5. What licenses eliding a check is that the value is ALREADY of the target type, so the boundary would have returned it unchanged; assignability alone is a weaker statement than that.</li>
        <li>The target is the `any` type. Every value is a value of it.</li>
        <li>The value's static type and the target are the same type.</li>
        <li>At a RETURN, where every `return` of the function satisfies the conditions above and the function cannot complete without one. A return annotation is shared by every `return` in its function, so the elision is a property of the function rather than of a statement, and a function that can fall off its end returns *undefined*, which the annotation must admit for the elision to hold.</li>
        <li>The index of a read or write of a fixed-length `[N].&lt;T&gt;` is known to be below _N_, because _N_ is a compile-time constant and the index is a value generic that a `where` clause constrains to be less than _N_, or the counter of a `for` over a range all of whose members are less than _N_. The bound is proven statically and no check is performed.</li>
      </ul>

      <emu-clause id="sec-elision-stability">
        <h1>Stability of a Static Type</h1>
        <p>The static type of an expression is <dfn id="stable-static-type">stable</dfn> when the value the expression produces at run time is of that type for the reason the type checker gave. It is stable unless its derivation reads a signature from a binding that the source text may replace: a call whose callee is a name that is not immutably bound, that the source text assigns to, or that a direct `eval` in the source text could assign to, has an unstable static type, and so does any expression containing one.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Without this condition the first elision above is unsound in fully annotated code, and no inference is needed to reach it:</p>
          <pre><code class="javascript">
function f(): uint32 { return 5; }
function g(): uint32 { return f(); }
f = function () { return 'now-a-string'; };
const n: uint32 = g();
          </code></pre>
          <p>Both checks are licensed by the first bullet &mdash; `g`'s return because `f()` is a `uint32`, and the binding because `g()` is &mdash; and with both elided the string reaches `n` and nothing reports it. A function declaration creates a mutable binding, so the assignment on the third line is admitted by the shallow ~function~ check of <emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref>, which is the one place this specification knowingly permits a violation to go unreported. That admission is bounded: it lets a wrong function be stored. What the elision would add is that every later boundary trusts the signature anyway, which turns a bounded admission into an unbounded one.</p>
          <p>The condition is on the source text rather than on the form of the declaration, because a name that nothing assigns cannot be replaced, whatever declared it. An ordinary program therefore keeps every elision it had; only one that writes to the name pays for the possibility, and it pays at the boundaries that read a signature from it. An engine reaches the same conclusion dynamically, by guarding an assumed callee and discarding the assumption when the binding is written; stating it statically is what lets the check be absent rather than merely predicted.</p>
          <p>A call whose callee is a property rather than a name is not covered here. The same reasoning applies to it &mdash; a property may also be replaced &mdash; but a property is a wider question, since a frozen intrinsic, a `readonly` field, and an ordinary property are not alike, and the case this clause is written for is the replaced function binding above.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A range's bounds are part of what its MEMBERS are, so the four shapes of one span do not prove the same thing: `0..&lt;3` proves an index into a `[3]` and `0&lt;..=3` does not, its members being 1 through 3 and its ceiling therefore 4. An open start raises the floor and a closed end raises the ceiling, independently of each other, which is why the rule above is stated over members rather than over endpoints &mdash; the members are already what <emu-xref href="#sec-ranges"></emu-xref> fixes, and restating it here would be a second place for the two to disagree.</p>
        <p>A value generic alone does not carry the proof, which is why the rule above names a `where` clause rather than the parameter. Inside a generic body the parameter's VALUE is not yet bound, so `a[I]` in a function taking `[N].&lt;T&gt;` proves nothing about the relation of _I_ to _N_ and may index 9 into a `[4]`. What supplies the relation is the `where` clause, checked at each specialization once its parameters are bound (<emu-xref href="#sec-where-clauses"></emu-xref>), so a body may assume it without being re-checked per application.</p>
        <p>Stating it over members also makes the proof independent of SPELLING. `0..&lt;8` and `0..=7` have the same members, so a rule keyed on the written end would prove one and refuse the other, which is a distinction about notation rather than about values.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A check at a PARAMETER is not elided on the evidence of the calls a checker can see. A parameter's annotation is shared by every call site, and an implementation compiles a source text rather than a program: a function is reachable through `Function.prototype.apply`, as an argument to a built-in that calls it, through a direct `eval`, and from another module or another source text entirely. An implementation may elide a parameter check where it establishes that every call is proven, which is a whole-program property and is outside what this clause requires. The same holds of a store to a property, which is reached through `Reflect.set` and through every other route to <emu-xref href="#sec-ordinaryset"></emu-xref> as well as through an assignment.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>In a fully typed program every check above is elided, and what remains at the boundaries of a partly typed one reduces to a tag test on a sealed typed class. This is the property the proposal's premise rests on: a check the engine can discharge statically is not a cost, and the type is available to the engine as a fact rather than a hint. A program that is entirely untyped inserts no checks either, since it has no typed positions. The cost falls where the boundary is, in proportion to how mixed the program is.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-typed-storage">
      <h1>Typed Storage</h1>
      <p>A class in which at least one public or private field is typed is automatically sealed, as if PreventExtensions had been performed on each of its instances, and its prototype is frozen. A field may be written, since a field's type is what constrains it, but a property may not be added or removed, and the prototype may not be modified.</p>

      <p>A store to a typed field or a typed element checks the value against the field's or element's declared type, by <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>. Deleting a typed field, a typed element, or a member required by an implemented interface throws a *TypeError* exception.</p>

      <p>A class whose fields are typed may opt out with the `dynamic` modifier, which leaves it unsealed. Such a class cannot be used for sequential data and cannot be a value type in a typed array.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Sealing is what makes a typed field's type a fact about the layout rather than a claim about the initializer. Without it a typed class would be a class whose fields happen to have been assigned values of the right type so far, which is not something an engine can lay out contiguously or an offset can be computed from. The memory layout extension depends on this, and it is the reason sealing is automatic rather than requested: a field's type and a reallocatable object are not compatible, so the type implies the seal.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-instanceof-for-type-objects">
      <h1>The instanceof Operator</h1>
      <p>A Type Object has a %Symbol.hasInstance% method. When called with argument _v_, it returns ? IsOfType(_v_, the Type Object's [[TypeRecord]]).</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`instanceof` therefore extends to every type of this proposal through the protocol the language already has, and needs no new operator. `a instanceof uint8` is *true* for a `uint8` binding and `a instanceof uint16` is *false*, a fixed-length array is NOT an instance of the corresponding dynamic-length array type &mdash; the extents must agree (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-and-tuple-types"></emu-xref>), and `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` is the type that accepts either &mdash; and a class is unaffected, since a class's type object is its constructor and the prototype chain semantics apply as they do today. A successful check narrows the static type in the branch it guards, which <emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref> specifies.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
    <emu-clause id="sec-typed-catch">
      <h1>Typed `catch` Clauses</h1>
      <p>A `catch` clause may name the type it handles, and a `try` may then carry more than one. The productions below restate their ECMAScript definitions (<emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        TryStatement[Yield,Await,Return] :
          <del>`try` Block[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return] Catch[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]</del>
          <ins>`try` Block[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return] CatchClauses[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]</ins>
          `try` Block[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return] Finally[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]
          <del>`try` Block[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return] Catch[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return] Finally[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]</del>
          <ins>`try` Block[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return] CatchClauses[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return] Finally[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]</ins>

        <ins>CatchClauses[Yield, Await, Return] :
          Catch[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]
          CatchClauses[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return] Catch[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]</ins>

        Catch[Yield,Await,Return] :
          `catch` `(` CatchParameter[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `)` Block[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]
          `catch` Block[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>It is a type error if a |Catch| other than the last of its |CatchClauses| has no |TypeAnnotation|. When a value _v_ is thrown, the clauses are tried in order, and the first runs whose declared type _v_ satisfies, in the sense in which RequireType (<emu-xref href="#sec-requiretype"></emu-xref>) would accept _v_ at that type; a |Catch| with no annotation satisfies every value. Within a clause the binding's Static Type is the declared type, the narrowing of <emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref> applied to the catch binding. A value no clause accepts propagates as if the `try` had no `catch`: typed clauses filter, they do not swallow, so the completion is rethrown after |Finally|, when present, runs as it does today.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is the design's error handling document made normative: ordered dispatch, first match, an untyped tail for the rest. A rejected promise reaches a typed clause through `await` unchanged, since `await` rethrows the rejection reason and the dispatch above neither knows nor cares that the throw crossed an async boundary. The errors this proposal itself raises, *RangeError* at a checked numeric conversion and at an out-of-bounds access, *TypeError* at a dynamic boundary, are ordinary values here, so `catch (e: RangeError)` handles every range failure in one clause (design, errorhandling.md).</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-declarations">
    <h1>Declarations</h1>
    <p>This clause gives meaning to the annotations the grammar attached to bindings in <emu-xref href="#sec-type-annotations"></emu-xref>. It specifies what a typed binding holds before it is assigned, what a typed initializer infers, what an assignment to a typed binding does, the declaration that names a type, and the narrowing by which a test refines the type of a binding in the branch it guards.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-type-alias-declarations">
      <h1>Type Alias Declarations</h1>
      <p>A type alias declaration names a type. It is added to the declaration forms.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        Declaration[Yield, Await] :
          HoistableDeclaration[?Yield, ?Await, ~Default]
          ClassDeclaration[?Yield, ?Await, ~Default]
          LexicalDeclaration[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]
          <ins>TypeAliasDeclaration[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>

        <ins>TypeAliasDeclaration[Yield, Await] :
          `type` [no LineTerminator here] BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]? `=` Type[?Yield, ?Await] WhereClauses[?Yield, ?Await]? `;`</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>A type alias declaration with no |TypeParameters| creates an immutable binding for its |BindingIdentifier|, initialized to GetTypeObject of the Type Record its |Type| denotes. The name is therefore usable in expression position, where it evaluates to that Type Object, and in type position, where it denotes the type. A declaration with |TypeParameters| creates no binding and is usable only in type position, since a generic alias denotes a type for each list of arguments rather than one type.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`type Point = { x: float32, y: float32 };` is therefore close to a `const` whose value is a type object, and the two spellings agree: `Point === type { x: float32, y: float32 }` is *true*, because Type Objects are interned (<emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref>). What the declaration adds over the `const` is that the name is also usable where a type is expected. An alias is transparent: it introduces no new type, so `Point` and its expansion are the same type and are mutually assignable, and a nominal type is created only by a class, an interface, or an enum.</p>

      <p>An alias may refer to itself, directly or through other aliases, provided every cycle passes through a position that holds a reference rather than an inline layout: a member written `T | null`, the element of a dynamic array, or a field of a sealed class. It is a type error if a cycle never does, since the type would demand an infinite inline layout, which is the same rule <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-classes"></emu-xref> applies to a value type class containing itself. The fixpoint of <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluatebuildercall"></emu-xref> validates a computed cycle by this rule.</p>
        <p>The `[no LineTerminator here]` restriction is what keeps `type` a contextual keyword. `type` followed by a line terminator and then an identifier is two statements today, an expression statement naming `type` and another naming the identifier, and it continues to be. This is the same device the design relies on throughout, and it is why `type X = 1;` on one line is unambiguous: it is a syntax error today.</p>
        <p>Whether a generic alias may be exported, and how `export type Point = ...` interacts with the module's binding namespace, is left to the modules clause. A generic alias creating no binding is a decision worth revisiting: it means `Grown` in `type Grown&lt;T&gt; = [...Row, T];` cannot be reflected on, while a non-generic alias can be.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-typed-bindings">
      <h1>Typed Bindings</h1>
      <p>`var`, `let`, and `const` accept the same annotations. A binding declared with a |TypeAnnotation| has the annotated type; a binding declared without one has the `any` type. The type of a binding is fixed at its declaration and does not change with what is assigned to it.</p>

      <p>A binding declared with a type and an |Initializer| is initialized to the value of the initializer, checked against the declared type by the rules of <emu-xref href="#sec-conversions"></emu-xref>: the check is static where the initializer's type is known, and is RequireType where the initializer has the `any` type. A binding declared with a type and no |Initializer| is initialized to the default value of that type (<emu-xref href="#sec-default-values"></emu-xref>) rather than to *undefined*.</p>

      <p>A `const` declaration without an |Initializer| remains a Syntax Error, whether or not the binding carries a |TypeAnnotation|.</p>

      <p>Assignment to a binding of declared type _t_ checks the assigned value against _t_ by the same rules. The temporal dead zone is unchanged: a `let` or `const` binding is uninitialized until its declaration is evaluated, and accessing it before then is a *ReferenceError*. The default value applies at the declaration, not before it.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>That a typed binding takes its type's default rather than *undefined* is the point at which a type stops being a claim about a program and becomes a fact about storage. `let a: uint32;` holds a `uint32`, so every read of it yields a `uint32` and the engine may lay it out as four bytes; were it *undefined* until assigned, its type would be `uint32 | undefined` in all but name, and the layout would have to carry a bit saying which. This is the same reason a typed field takes its type's default, and the two rules are one rule.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-default-values">
      <h1>Default Values</h1>

      <emu-clause id="sec-defaultvalueof" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          DefaultValueOf (
            _t_: a Type Record,
          ): either a value of the type _t_ or ~none~
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the value a binding or field of the type _t_ holds before it is assigned, or ~none~ if _t_ has no default.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~any~, return *undefined*.
          1. If _t_ is a numeric type, return the value of _t_ representing 0.
          1. If _t_ is the String type, return the empty String.
          1. If _t_ is the Boolean type, return *false*.
          1. If _t_ is the type named *"undefined"*, return *undefined*.
          1. If _t_ is the type named *"null"*, return *null*.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~union~, then
            1. If _t_.[[Members]] contains the type named *"null"*, return *null*.
            1. If _t_.[[Members]] contains the type named *"undefined"*, return *undefined*.
            1. Return ~none~.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~array~, then
            1. If _t_.[[Extent]] is ~dynamic~, return a new empty array of the type _t_.
            1. Let _d_ be DefaultValueOf(_t_.[[Element]]).
            1. If _d_ is ~none~, return ~none~.
            1. Return a new array of the type _t_ whose _t_.[[Extent]] elements are each _d_.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~tuple~, then
            1. For each element _e_ of _t_.[[Elements]], do
              1. If _e_.[[Rest]] is *false* and _e_.[[Initial]] is ~none~ and DefaultValueOf(_e_.[[Type]]) is ~none~, return ~none~.
            1. Return a new tuple of the type _t_ whose elements are, for each element of _t_.[[Elements]] whose [[Rest]] is *false* and in order, its [[Initial]] where that is not ~none~ and the default value of its [[Type]] otherwise.
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~parameterized~, then
            1. Let _d_ be DefaultValueOf(_t_.[[Base]]).
            1. If _d_ is ~none~, return ~none~.
            1. Let _e_ be a new empty Object, which is the metadata of a value of _t_.[[Base]] that carries none.
            1. Let _crossed_ be Completion(ConvertParameterization(_d_, _e_, _t_.[[Metadata]])).
            1. If _crossed_ is an abrupt completion, return ~none~.
            1. Return _crossed_.[[Value]].
          1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~nominal~ and _t_ denotes a value type class, return the instance of _t_ each of whose fields holds the default of the field's type, or ~none~ if any field's type has no default.
          1. Return ~none~.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>It is a type error to declare a binding or a field with a type _t_ and no initializer when DefaultValueOf(_t_) is ~none~.</p>

      <p>A parameterization's default is its base's zero having crossed into it, by the same conversion an initializer of the base's type crosses (<emu-xref href="#sec-metadata-conversion"></emu-xref>), so that `let _x_: _T_;` and `let _x_: _T_ = 0;` succeed together and fail together. Three consequences follow from the crossing rather than from a rule of its own. A meta type whose `validate` refuses the base's zero leaves the parameterization with no default, since the crossing throws and the step reports ~none~: `float64.&lt;{ min: 1 }&gt;` has no zero because 0 is not one of its values. A brand for which no cast is declared has no default either, being "reachable only by construction" and a declaration without an initializer not being one. And a parameterization whose base declares a cast has the crossed zero, which is what keeps a unit type zero-fillable: `Velocity` is a `float64` whose dimensions meta type defines no `validate`, so the cast admits the zero and `let _v_: Velocity;` holds 0 m/s.</p>

      <p>The meta types and casts declared in the surrounding realm are those declared as of the operation's application, as they are for the <emu-xref href="#metadata-subtype-judgment">metadata subtype judgment</emu-xref> and for the same reason: a parameterization may be written above the declarations that govern it, so the checking pass processes a source text's `meta` declarations and its `primitive` blocks' implicit cast operators before applying this operation (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-errors"></emu-xref>), and whether a declaration needs an initializer is answered against those. Two call sites fix the realm rather than taking the current one, because in each the value is produced for a program that is not the one asking. An unwritten ThreadLocal read (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading"></emu-xref>) takes the default in the realm in which the ThreadLocal was created, so that two agents reading one storage read one value. `Reflect.defineProperty` with a `type` and no `value` (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-and-declared-types"></emu-xref>) takes it in the realm of the Type Object it was given, which is the only call site at which a program chooses the moment.</p>

      <p>Every default above is a zero: the numeric zero, the empty String, *false*, *null*, and an array or an aggregate whose storage is zero-filled. Zero-filling is part of the semantics and not an optimization a program may opt out of, and no form of this proposal allocates without it. What is filled for an aggregate is its layout (<emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>), padding included, so two instances of one value type class that are `===` field by field are also equal byte for byte.</p>

      <p>An aggregate has a default exactly where its parts do: a value type class where every field's type does, a fixed-extent array where its element's type does, and a tuple where every position it fills does. A parameterized field is the case to watch, since the step above gives a parameterization a default only where its base's zero can cross into it. A `Vector3` of three `Meter` fields is zero-fillable exactly where `float64` declares the cast into the dimensions meta type, and `let _d_: [10].&lt;Vector3&gt;;` is a type error where it does not &mdash; not because a unit has no zero, but because nothing in that program says how a bare zero becomes one. Writing that cast is what gives a design's unit types their zeros, and with it the aggregates holding them.</p>

      <p>Read the other way, the same rule is how a type says that no zero of it is meaningful. A class that needs its constructor to run states that by holding a field whose type has no default (<emu-xref href="#sec-typed-classes"></emu-xref>), and a brand into which no cast is declared is such a type: its values arrive only by construction, through the operators its base declares (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-operator-blocks"></emu-xref>), so an aggregate holding one has no zero-filled form and says so at the declaration rather than at the first use.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The reason is security rather than convenience. An allocation that exposed the bytes of a previously freed one would leak whatever had been there: another script's data, a former secret, or a heap pointer that defeats address space layout randomization. A fresh page from the operating system is already zero, so the fill costs nothing on a large new allocation, and reused memory must be cleared regardless, so there is nothing to gain by exposing it. The one pattern an uninitialized form would serve, allocate and then fill from input, is served by placement `new` over an existing buffer and by array views.</p>
        <p>A type with no default is one for which no zero is meaningful, and the operation reports that rather than inventing one. A union of two non-nullable types is the ordinary case: `let x: uint8 | string;` has two candidate zeros and no reason to prefer either, so it requires an initializer. `symbol`, `object`, a function type, and a class whose construction must run are the others.</p>
        <p>The design states the defaults for the numeric, SIMD, String, Boolean, nullable, and array types and does not reach the rest, so the boundaries above are supplied here and need review. Three deserve attention. A `bigint` is given the zero `0n` on the same reasoning as the other numeric types. A tuple defaults element-wise, which follows from a tuple of value types being laid out contiguously, but a tuple with a defaulted trailing position already has an initializer for that position, which <emu-xref href="#sec-array-defaults-and-stores"></emu-xref> reconciles. A value type class defaults field-wise, which is what makes `let d: [10].&lt;A&gt;;` hold ten zero-filled instances as the memory layout extension requires, but it means a class can be brought into existence without its constructor running, which <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-classes"></emu-xref> endorses.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-typed-initializers-semantics">
      <h1>Typed Initializers</h1>
      <p>A binding declared with a |TypedInitializer| takes the type of its initializer rather than the `any` type. Its declared type is the Static Type of the |AssignmentExpression|, and it is initialized to that expression's value.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`let a := new MyType();` declares `a` of type `MyType`, where `let a = new MyType();` would declare it of the `any` type. The two spellings differ in what may be assigned afterwards, not in what is stored: the first rejects `a = 5`, the second permits it. `:=` therefore removes the redundancy of `let a: MyType = new MyType();` without introducing inference anywhere the program did not ask for it, which is why it is a distinct operator rather than a rule that an initialized binding infers its type. An unannotated binding remaining `any` is what keeps an untyped program untyped.</p>
        <p>It is a type error if the Static Type of the initializer is the `any` type, since `let a := f();` where `f` is untyped would declare a binding of the `any` type through a syntax that asks for a type.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-narrowing">
      <h1>Narrowing</h1>
      <p>A test that establishes something about a value's type refines the Static Type of that value in the branch the test guards. The Static Type of a reference to a binding at a given point is its declared type refined by the narrowing facts that hold there.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-narrowing-forms" caption="Narrowing Forms">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Where true</th><th>Where false</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`v instanceof T`</td><td>NarrowTo(_s_, _T_)</td><td>NarrowFrom(_s_, _T_)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`#f in v`, a brand check</td><td>NarrowTo(_s_, the class declaring `#f`)</td><td>NarrowFrom(_s_, the class declaring `#f`)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`%`, the pipeline topic (<emu-xref href="#sec-pipeline-operator"></emu-xref>)</td><td>Every row of this table, in the positions the test governs. The topic is a binding, so a test on it narrows it as a test on any reference does — which is a second reason it is a binding and not a substitution, since a substitution has no identity to narrow.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`v is T` (<emu-xref href="#sec-is-operator"></emu-xref>), and generally `v is P` for a pattern _P_ (<emu-xref href="#sec-is-pattern"></emu-xref>)</td><td>NarrowTo(_s_, PatternType(_P_))</td><td>NarrowFrom(_s_, PatternType(_P_))</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`v === e` or `v !== e`, where the Static Type of _e_ is a literal type _L_</td><td>NarrowTo(_s_, _L_)</td><td>NarrowFrom(_s_, _L_)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`v.p === e` or `v.p !== e` under the same condition, where _s_ is a ~union~</td><td>NarrowTo(_s_, the union of the members of _s_ whose type at `p` admits _L_)</td><td>NarrowFrom(_s_, that union)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`v` _op_ `e`, where _op_ is a comparison, the Static Type of `v` is a ~parameterized~ type, and `e` is a compile-time constant _c_</td><td>NarrowTo(_s_, _s_ with its metadata replaced by NarrowMetadata(_s_'s metadata, _op_, _c_))</td><td>NarrowTo(_s_, _s_ with its metadata replaced by NarrowMetadata(_s_'s metadata, the negation of _op_, _c_))</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A call of a signature whose [[Narrows]] holds a Narrowing Record for the position `v` is passed in, where the signature's [[Return]] is `boolean`</td><td>NarrowTo(_s_, that Record's [[Type]])</td><td>NarrowFrom(_s_, that Record's [[Type]])</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The same, where the signature's [[Return]] is ~void~</td><td colspan="2">NarrowTo(_s_, that Record's [[Type]]) in every position the call dominates, rather than in a branch (<emu-xref href="#sec-declared-narrowing"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`typeof v === ` a String literal _k_</td><td>NarrowTo(_s_, the union of the types whose `typeof` is _k_)</td><td>NarrowFrom(_s_, that union)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`v == null` or `v != null`, and the nullish operators `??`, `??=`, and `?.`</td><td>The nullish branch: NarrowTo(_s_, `null | undefined`)</td><td>The present branch: NarrowFrom(_s_, `null | undefined`)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A `switch` on a value of an enum type or a sealed class hierarchy</td><td>In each case, NarrowTo(_s_, the case's type)</td><td>In the default, _s_ with every case's type removed</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A `match` clause `when P` (<emu-xref href="#sec-pattern-matching"></emu-xref>)</td><td>In the arm, NarrowTo(_s_, PatternType(_P_))</td><td>In later clauses, NarrowFrom(_s_, PatternType(_P_)) where the clause is unguarded</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>`v` above is a reference to a binding or a chain of property accesses over one, a <dfn id="narrowing-path">path</dfn>, and _s_ is its Static Type where the test occurs. A narrowing fact holds in any position the test governs, not only in the body of an `if`: the two branches of a conditional expression are narrowed by its condition, the right operand of `&amp;&amp;` is narrowed by the truth of its left operand, and the right operand of `||` by the falsity of its left. A fact about a path holds until the path or a prefix of it is assigned a value whose type is not within the narrowed type. `==` and `!=` narrow as `===` and `!==` do wherever the Static Types of both operands make the two agree, which a union of literal types over one base always does, so the design's `this.country == 'US'` narrows and a comparison that could coerce does not.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Facts reach paths because the types this proposal narrows are about fields as much as bindings, and `if (addr.country == 'US')` is the design's own example. Assignment to a prefix discards a fact about everything under it, which is the design's account of a `mode` reassigned after a `name` was narrowed by it. What does not discard a fact is an intervening call that could reach the path, and that is the same shallow reading of a function value that <emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref> takes and the same hole, admitted in the same place: the boundary check is the backstop, since a value that has drifted out of its type is caught where it next crosses (<emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-clause id="sec-canonical-total-order">
        <h1>The Canonical Total Order</h1>
        <p>The total order over canonical Type Records that CanonicalizeType invokes is fixed as follows, so that the member order a program observes through reflection is the same on every host. Records of different [[Kind]] order by the row order of <emu-xref href="#table-type-record-kinds"></emu-xref>. Records of one kind order by their fields in that table's order, compared as: a Type Record recursively by this order; a List element-wise and then shorter first; a String by code units; a Number by numeric value with *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub> before *+0*<sub>𝔽</sub> and *NaN* greatest; a BigInt by value; a Boolean with *false* before *true*; a Symbol by the order in which each is first interned into a Type Record within the surrounding Agent; a function, as an ~application~'s [[Builder]], by the source position of its declaration, module before offset; and a metadata value by the structural comparison of <emu-xref href="#sec-deferred-applications"></emu-xref> with its leaves ordered by these same rules. Where one field holds values of two different ECMAScript language types, as a ~literal~'s [[Value]] does, they order by type, in the order String, Number, BigInt, Boolean, Symbol.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The order is arbitrary and load-bearing at once: nothing about it matters except that every host computes the same one, because canonical order is observable, a union's arms reflect in it and a builder that turns a union into a tuple inherits it, and the determinism invariant of <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref> therefore reaches it. A builder wanting a different order sorts. The order among Symbols is Agent-local, because a Symbol is, and it is stable within a program because the same source interns the same symbols in the same sequence; a Symbol never reaches a comparison in another Agent, since a type carrying one cannot be shared.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-narrowto" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          NarrowTo (
            _s_: a Type Record,
            _t_: a Type Record,
          ): either a Type Record or ~empty~
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the type of a value known to be of the type _s_ and also of the type _t_, or ~empty~ if no value can be of both.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~any~, return _t_.
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~union~, then
            1. Let _kept_ be a new empty List.
            1. For each element _m_ of _s_.[[Members]], do
              1. If IsSubtype(_m_, _t_, &laquo; &raquo;) is *true*, append _m_ to _kept_.
              1. Else if IsSubtype(_t_, _m_, &laquo; &raquo;) is *true*, append _t_ to _kept_.
            1. If _kept_ is empty, return ~empty~.
            1. Return CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~union~, [[Members]]: _kept_ }).
          1. If IsSubtype(_s_, _t_, &laquo; &raquo;) is *true*, return _s_.
          1. If IsSubtype(_t_, _s_, &laquo; &raquo;) is *true*, return _t_.
          1. If _s_ and _t_ have no common values, return ~empty~.
          1. Return CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~intersection~, [[Members]]: &laquo; _s_, _t_ &raquo; }).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Two distinct ~primitive~ types have no common values, since each value belongs to exactly one of them (<emu-xref href="#sec-value-types"></emu-xref>), and neither does a ~primitive~ type share values with an ~object~ or ~function~ type. Two ~object~ types, or two ~nominal~ types that are interfaces, may have common values, since a class may satisfy both, so an intersection of those is inhabited and is returned. This is the step that yields ~empty~ for `d ?? 5` where `d` is a `uint8`: the narrowed type would be the intersection of `uint8` and `null | undefined`, which has no values.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-narrowfrom" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          NarrowFrom (
            _s_: a Type Record,
            _t_: a Type Record,
          ): either a Type Record or ~empty~
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the type of a value known to be of the type _s_ and not of the type _t_, or ~empty~ if there is no such value.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _s_.[[Kind]] is ~union~, then
            1. Let _kept_ be a new empty List.
            1. For each element _m_ of _s_.[[Members]], do
              1. If IsSubtype(_m_, _t_, &laquo; &raquo;) is *false*, append _m_ to _kept_.
            1. If _kept_ is empty, return ~empty~.
            1. Return CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~union~, [[Members]]: _kept_ }).
          1. If IsSubtype(_s_, _t_, &laquo; &raquo;) is *true*, return ~empty~.
          1. Return _s_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>It is a type error to apply a narrowing form where the test can never succeed or can never fail, since the branch it guards is then dead code the program did not intend. These are the branches for which NarrowTo or NarrowFrom returns ~empty~. The sentinel is deliberately not the `never` type (<emu-xref href="#sec-never-type"></emu-xref>): a test that cannot succeed is reported as a mistake rather than narrowed to the type with no values, because a branch the program wrote and can never take is dead code and not a computation.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is why `d ?? 5` is rejected where `d` is a `uint8`: the left operand can never be nullish, so NarrowTo of `uint8` and `null | undefined` keeps no member and the test is a mistake rather than silent dead code. The same rule rejects `a instanceof string` where `a` is a `uint8`.</p>
        <p>Narrowing on `typeof` is weaker here than in a type system layered over the language, and deliberately so, because `typeof` is unchanged: it reports *"number"* for every numeric type, so `typeof v === "number"` narrows `uint8 | string` to `uint8` but cannot separate `uint8` from `uint16`, and reports *"object"* for the SIMD, rational, and complex types. `Reflect.typeOf` and `instanceof` are what distinguish those, and `instanceof` is the form that narrows. A program discriminating a union of numeric types therefore writes `instanceof`, not `typeof`.</p>
        <p>Narrowing a binding rather than an expression is sound here only because a narrowed binding is invalidated by an assignment that leaves the narrowed type, which the last sentence above states. The design's `a ??= 5;` narrowing `a` to `uint8` "until a nullable reassignment" is that rule. It is worth noting what this clause does not give: a narrowing of a property or an element, `if (o.f instanceof T)`, is not stated by the design and is not specified here, and it cannot be sound without knowing that `o.f` is not reassigned in between, which for a sealed typed class of a `readonly` field is knowable and otherwise is not. <emu-xref href="#sec-object-types-semantics"></emu-xref> settles it.</p>
        <p>The `switch` row covers the exhaustiveness the design reserves to enums and sealed classes. A union of literal types deliberately does not drive exhaustiveness, so it narrows in each case but its `default` is not proven unreachable.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-operators">
    <h1>Operators</h1>
    <p>This clause specifies what an operator does when its operands are typed. Little new machinery is required, because the specification already has the mechanism: when BigInt was introduced, the operators were generalized from one numeric type to two, and an operator now reduces its operands to numeric values, rejects a pair whose types differ, and dispatches to an abstract operation belonging to the type. This proposal adds numeric types to that mechanism. It does not add a second one.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The principle the design states is that typed code changes what a program checks and what its values can represent, and does not change what an operator means. Every rule below follows from it. Where an operator's result is forced by the type rather than chosen, as an integer quotient is, the type decides; everywhere else the operator means what it means today.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <emu-clause id="sec-operator-dispatch">
      <h1>Dispatch</h1>
      <p>ToNumeric is revised so that a value of a numeric type of this proposal reaches an operator as itself rather than as a Number.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-tonumeric-revised" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          ToNumeric (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
          ): either a normal completion containing either a Number, a BigInt, or a value of a numeric type of this proposal, or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns _value_ converted to a numeric value, preserving the numeric type it already has.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _primValue_ be ? ToPrimitive(_value_, ~number~).
          1. <ins>If _primValue_ is a value of a numeric type of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-universe"></emu-xref>, return _primValue_.</ins>
          1. If _primValue_ is a BigInt, return _primValue_.
          1. Return ? ToNumber(_primValue_).
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>The remaining machinery is unchanged and generalizes as written. In <emu-xref href="#sec-applystringornumericbinaryoperator"></emu-xref>, the step that throws a *TypeError* when the two operands are not of the same numeric type applies to every numeric type, and the table associating an operator with an abstract operation is read as associating it with _T_::op for the numeric type _T_ of the operands. The operations are those of <emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-types-of-this-proposal"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is the whole of the dispatch. `1n + 1` already throws a *TypeError* today because the two operands are of different numeric types and neither converts to the other, and `uint8(1) + uint16(1)` throws for exactly the same reason, by the same step, with no rule specific to it. The shape the committee arrived at for BigInt, an operator that dispatches on the numeric type of its operands and refuses to mix them, is the shape this proposal needs, which is why adding sixteen numeric types costs one inserted step.</p>
        <p>ToNumber is not reached for a typed operand and so is never the vehicle for an implicit conversion, which is what <emu-xref href="#sec-existing-abstract-conversion-operations"></emu-xref> anticipated. ToNumber keeps its meaning for a value of the `any` type, whose numeric type is not known statically: the operand arrives as whatever it is, and the same steps apply to it dynamically.</p>
        <p>The string behaviour of `+` is unchanged and applies before any of this: if either operand is a String after ToPrimitive, the result is the concatenation. `uint8(1) + "x"` is therefore *"1x"*. This is not an implicit conversion between value types in the sense <emu-xref href="#sec-the-conversion-rule"></emu-xref> forbids, since no `uint8` becomes a value of another numeric type; it is the meaning `+` has always had, and changing it for typed operands would violate the principle above.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-arithmetic-never-promotes">
      <h1>Arithmetic Never Promotes</h1>
      <p>Two operands of the same value type produce a result of that type. Two operands of different value types are a type error, since neither is assignable to the other. Where one operand is a literal it takes the type of the other (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref>), so a literal never forces a conversion.</p>

      <p>The LITERAL rule of the preceding paragraph also reaches `==`, `!=`, `===`, `!==`, and a `case` label, though the type error of the preceding paragraph does not. A comparison answers rather than computing, so two operands of different numeric types compare unequal instead of being an error; but a literal in one of those positions still takes the other operand's type, so `uint8(5) === 5` is *true*. A binding adopts nothing, so `uint8(5) === n` for an `n` of the `number` type is *false*, and the values of distinct value types remain distinct. A `bigint` literal takes no Number-family type, being of a numeric type of its own. A literal the type cannot represent is not equal to any value of it, so the comparison is *false* rather than an error - unlike an arithmetic position, where the same literal is an Early Error, because there the literal must become a value and here it need not.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Reaching equality is what makes a typed value usable with the comparisons programs actually write, including inside a callback: `a.find(x =&gt; x === 65)` over a `[].&lt;uint16&gt;` finds its element, where without the rule it could find nothing. The alternative considered was to leave `===` strictly identity-based, which is the treatment BigInt receives - but a BigInt has its own literal syntax, `5n`, and the types of this proposal have none, so a literal is the ONLY way to write one of their values in a comparison. Declining would have made every such comparison require an explicit conversion.</p>
      </emu-note></p>

      <p>It is a type error if the Static Types of the operands of an arithmetic, bitwise, shift, or relational operator are numeric types that are not the same type. Where either operand has the `any` type the check is deferred, and the *TypeError* of <emu-xref href="#sec-operator-dispatch"></emu-xref> is thrown at run time if the types then differ.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`float32` arithmetic is `float32` arithmetic: `x * x` for a `float32` `x` is computed in `float32` and rounds once, rather than widening to `float64` and rounding twice. C promotes `float` to `double` in many contexts, so the same source can produce different results depending on where a value is used, and the reproducibility this proposal offers across engines depends on refusing that.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-which-operations-each-family-defines">
      <h1>The Operations Each Family Defines</h1>
      <p>A numeric type defines the operations of <emu-xref href="#table-family-operations"></emu-xref>. An operator whose operation the operand's type does not define is a type error.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-family-operations" caption="Operations by Family">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Defines</th><th>Does not define</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>integer</td><td>Every operation of <emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-types-of-this-proposal"></emu-xref>.</td><td>None.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>binary floating-point, and the Number type</td><td>unaryMinus, exponentiate, multiply, divide, remainder, add, subtract, lessThan, equal, sameValue, sameValueZero, toString.</td><td>For a binary floating-point type of this proposal: bitwiseNOT, the shifts, and the bitwise operations, since each would require converting the operand to an integer type.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>decimal floating-point</td><td>unaryMinus, exponentiate, multiply, divide, remainder, add, subtract, lessThan, equal, sameValue, sameValueZero, toString.</td><td>bitwiseNOT, the shifts, and the bitwise operations.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>rational</td><td>unaryMinus, multiply, divide, add, subtract, lessThan, equal, sameValue, sameValueZero, toString.</td><td>bitwiseNOT, the shifts, the bitwise operations, and remainder. exponentiate is defined only for an integer exponent.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>complex</td><td>unaryMinus, exponentiate, multiply, divide, add, subtract, equal, sameValue, sameValueZero, toString.</td><td>lessThan, since the complex numbers are not ordered; remainder; and the bitwise and shift operations.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>vector</td><td>unaryMinus, multiply, divide, add, subtract, equal, sameValue, sameValueZero, toString, applied lane-wise; and the bitwise and shift operations where the lane type is an integer type.</td><td>lessThan, whose result for a vector is a vector of lane results rather than a Boolean. The SIMD extension declares the comparison operators on the vector types instead, overloaded on their return type, so they dispatch by <emu-xref href="#sec-user-defined-operators"></emu-xref> rather than through the protocol.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The Number type continues to define the bitwise and shift operations, which convert their operand with ToInt32 as they do today, and no existing program changes. A `float32` does not, because the same conversion applied to it would be an implicit conversion between two value types: `~x` for a `float32` `x` is a type error rather than a silent trip through `int32`. This is the one place where an operator that works on a Number does not work on the corresponding typed value, and it follows from the conversion rule rather than from a decision about bitwise operators.</p>
        <p>The table is the answer to the three cases <emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-types-of-this-proposal"></emu-xref> left open. Two of them are absences, complex having no order and a rational having no remainder. The third, a vector comparison returning a vector, is not an absence but a signature the protocol cannot express, since lessThan is required to return a Boolean. Rather than widening the protocol for one family, the vector comparisons are declared operators, which is a path the specification already provides and which admits the return-type overloading they need (<emu-xref href="#sec-overloading-on-return-type"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-operator-results">
      <h1>The Result of an Operator</h1>
      <p><emu-xref href="#sec-which-operations-each-family-defines"></emu-xref> states which operations a family has. This clause states what an application of one yields, which <emu-xref href="#table-static-type-base"></emu-xref> does not reach: that table gives the Static Type of a literal, a binding, and a type name, and an operator application is none of those.</p>

      <p>The Static Type of an operator application is determined as follows.</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. If the operator is `&&`, `||`, or `??`, the Static Type is the union of the short-circuiting part of the left operand's Static Type and the right operand's Static Type, where the short-circuiting part is the falsy part (<emu-xref href="#sec-falsy-and-truthy-parts"></emu-xref>) for `&&`, the truthy part for `||`, and, for `??`, the result of NarrowFrom (<emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref>) applied to that Static Type and `null | undefined`; where that part has no members, the Static Type is the right operand's Static Type alone, and where the complementary part has no members, so that the left operand always short-circuits and the right is never evaluated, the Static Type is the short-circuiting part alone.
        1. Else if the operator is an equality or relational operator other than one whose operands are vectors, the Static Type is the ~primitive~ type named *"boolean"*.
        1. Else if either operand's Static Type is a ~literal~ type and the other's is not, the literal adopts the other operand's type (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref>) and this rule is applied again.
        1. Else if the operands' Static Types are not the same type, it is a type error: an operator does not combine two numeric types (<emu-xref href="#sec-arithmetic-never-promotes"></emu-xref>).
        1. Else if the operands' Static Type is a ~vector~ type, the Static Type is that vector type, the operator having been applied at each lane; except that a relational or equality operator between vectors is overloaded on its result type (<emu-xref href="#sec-vector-comparisons"></emu-xref>).
        1. Else if the family of the operands' Static Type does not define the operation, it is a type error.
        1. Else, the Static Type is the operands' Static Type.
      </emu-alg>

      <p>The two exceptions in that step are duals, and between them they keep the Static Type to the values the expression can produce: `obj &amp;&amp; f()` is the type of `f()`, since no object value is falsy and the left cannot be produced, while `undefined &amp;&amp; f()` is `undefined`, since the right is never evaluated at all.</p>

      <p>The first step is where the short-circuit operators part company with the rest: they produce one of their OPERANDS rather than a value of a common type, so the step that refuses two unlike types does not reach them and `a && a &lt; 10` is a `uint32` or a *boolean* rather than an error. A contextual type applies to the operands rather than to the application, since it is an operand that is produced: `let c: uint32 = x || 10` puts the `10` at `uint32` by literal propagation exactly as `let c: uint32 = 10` does. A literal that is a member of a union reaches its target only this way, since assignability compares the union as a whole.</p>

      <p>The last step is the substance of it: an operator on operands of one type yields that type, and never a wider one. What follows from that, and is not implied by a type's name, is collected here.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-operator-results" caption="Result of an Operator by Family">
        <table>
          <tr><th>Family</th><th>Arithmetic `+` `-` `*` `/` `%` `**`</th><th>Shifts and bitwise</th><th>Relational and equality</th><th>Unary `-`</th></tr>
          <tr><td>integer</td><td>the operand type, wrapping (<emu-xref href="#sec-integer-operations"></emu-xref>); `/` truncates toward zero</td><td>the operand type</td><td>*Boolean*</td><td>the operand type</td></tr>
          <tr><td>binary floating-point</td><td>the operand type</td><td>not defined</td><td>*Boolean*</td><td>the operand type</td></tr>
          <tr><td>decimal floating-point</td><td>the operand type</td><td>not defined</td><td>*Boolean*</td><td>the operand type</td></tr>
          <tr><td>rational</td><td>the operand type; `%` not defined, `**` for an integer exponent only</td><td>not defined</td><td>*Boolean*</td><td>the operand type</td></tr>
          <tr><td>complex</td><td>the operand type; `%` not defined</td><td>not defined</td><td>equality only; the complex numbers are not ordered</td><td>the operand type</td></tr>
          <tr><td>vector</td><td>the operand type, at each lane</td><td>the operand type where the lane type is an integer type</td><td>the three forms of <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-comparisons"></emu-xref></td><td>the operand type</td></tr>
          <tr><td>`bigint`</td><td>`bigint`; does not combine with a Number</td><td>`bigint`</td><td>*Boolean*</td><td>`bigint`</td></tr>
          <tr><td>`string`</td><td>`+` concatenates; the others are the existing Number coercion</td><td>the existing Number coercion</td><td>*Boolean*</td><td>the existing Number coercion</td></tr>
          <tr><td>enum</td><td>the underlying type of the enum</td><td>the underlying type</td><td>*Boolean*</td><td>the underlying type</td></tr>
          <tr><td>~nominal~</td><td>the return type of the declared operator, or the existing rules where none is declared</td><td>as declared</td><td>*Boolean* unless declared otherwise</td><td>as declared</td></tr>
          <tr><td>~reference~</td><td>as its referent's type: an operand is read through the reference</td><td>as its referent's type</td><td>as its referent's type</td><td>as its referent's type</td></tr>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Three results in the table are worth stating in words, being the ones a type's name does not suggest.</p>
        <p>An integer type divides by truncating toward zero and wraps on overflow, so `7 / 2` at `uint8` is 3 and `1 - 3` at `uint8` is 254. <emu-xref href="#sec-checked-and-saturating-arithmetic"></emu-xref> gives the forms that trap or clamp instead.</p>
        <p>A one-bit integer is an integer, so `1 + 1` at `boolean1` is 0. The name suggests a Boolean, but the family is integer and the arithmetic wraps at one bit as it wraps at eight.</p>
        <p>A relational or equality operator yields a *Boolean* and not a value of a sized type, because it answers a question rather than computing a quantity and so has no result type to preserve. A comparison between vectors is the exception the fourth step names, and is overloaded on its result type because a lane-wise comparison has more than one useful shape.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-falsy-and-truthy-parts">
      <h1>The Falsy and Truthy Parts of a Type</h1>
      <p>The <dfn id="falsy-part">falsy part</dfn> of a type is the union of the members of that type that have a falsy value, and the <dfn id="truthy-part">truthy part</dfn> is the union of the members that have a truthy value. A member contributes to a part as follows, and where a member contributes to neither the part is a union of no members, the `never` type.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-falsy-and-truthy-parts" caption="Contribution of a Member to Each Part">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Member</th><th>Falsy part</th><th>Truthy part</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A ~literal~ type</td><td>The member, where its value is falsy.</td><td>The member, where its value is truthy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`undefined`, `null`</td><td>The member.</td><td>Nothing.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`boolean`</td><td>The literal type of *false*.</td><td>The literal type of *true*.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A numeric type, `string`, or `bigint`</td><td>The member.</td><td>The member.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`symbol`, `type`, an ~object~, ~array~, ~tuple~, ~function~, or ~nominal~ type</td><td>Nothing.</td><td>The member.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`void`</td><td>Nothing.</td><td>Nothing.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A numeric type, `string`, and `bigint` contribute the whole member to both parts, because each has both falsy and truthy values and the falsy one has no type of its own to name: a `uint32` zero is a value of `uint32`, and there is no narrower type this specification can write for it. Naming the member is the widening direction, and a wider Static Type is sound where a narrower one would be a claim the type system cannot support. An implementation that can name the value more precisely, as it can for `boolean`, may do so.</p>
        <p>These parts are what make the common idioms type as written. `s || "anon"` at a `string` is a `string`, since the truthy part of `string` is `string` and both operands are then of one type. `x ?? 10` where `x` is `uint32 | undefined` is a `uint32`, since NarrowFrom removes the absent member. `obj &amp;&amp; f()` is the type of `f()`, since no object value is falsy and the left contributes nothing. And `a &amp;&amp; a &lt; 10` where `a` is a `uint32` is `uint32 | boolean` rather than `boolean`, which is the honest statement: the expression evaluates to `a` where `a` is zero. A program that wants the Boolean writes `!!(a &amp;&amp; a &lt; 10)`.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-integer-operations">
      <h1>Integer Operations</h1>
      <p>For an integer type _T_ of width _N_, each operation computes the exact mathematical result and then reduces it to a value of _T_ as <emu-xref href="#table-numeric-conversions"></emu-xref> specifies for an integer target: the result is taken modulo 2**_N_ and interpreted as a value of _T_. Overflow therefore wraps, in two's complement for a signed type, and discards the bits that do not fit.</p>

      <p>_T_::divide returns the exact quotient truncated toward zero. _T_::remainder returns the remainder whose sign follows the dividend, unchanged in meaning from the Number type. If the divisor is zero, both throw a *RangeError* exception.</p>

      <p>It is a type error if the divisor of `/` or `%` is a literal zero and the operands are of an integer type of this proposal.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Truncating toward zero and taking the remainder's sign from the dividend are one decision rather than two, because the identity `(a / b) * b + a % b = a` holds only when the quotient and the remainder round the same way. Truncated division pairs with a dividend-signed remainder and floored division with a divisor-signed one; mixing them produces an arithmetic that does not add back up whenever the dividend is negative and the remainder is not zero, which is two of the four sign combinations. The floored pair is available under its own name rather than by redefining the operators, as `Math.divFloor` and `Math.mod` in <emu-xref href="#sec-floored-division"></emu-xref>.</p>
        <p>That an integer quotient truncates is not a choice. An `int32` has nowhere to put 1.5, so the result type decides the answer, and this is the one operator whose meaning the type changes. A floored `%` would be a choice, since both answers fit the type, which is why the operator keeps the meaning it has for a Number.</p>
        <p>Division by zero throws because an integer type has no infinity and no NaN, so there is nothing to return. A float is unaffected and keeps the answer it has today: `float32(1) / 0` is an infinity and `float32(1) % 0` is a NaN, because a float has somewhere to put them.</p>
        <p>The literal-zero rule matches the treatment of a literal that does not fit its type (<emu-xref href="#table-out-of-range-treatment"></emu-xref>): it is a mistake visible in the source text, so it does not compile, while a divisor whose value is not known until run time raises a *RangeError* there. It deliberately does not extend to `bigint`, where `1n / 0n` continues to throw a *RangeError* exception at run time: that expression is legal today, and rejecting it earlier would break an existing program. The asymmetry is a compatibility constraint rather than a design view, and it is worth stating in the design document, which does not currently note that the rule stops at the new types.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-unary-operators-for-typed-values">
      <h1>Unary Operators</h1>
      <p>Unary `-` applies _T_::unaryMinus for the numeric type _T_ of its operand. For an unsigned integer type of width _N_, the result is 2**_N_ minus the operand, modulo 2**_N_, so unary minus on an unsigned value wraps rather than raising an error.</p>

      <p>Unary `+` returns its operand unchanged when the operand is a value of a numeric type of this proposal. It continues to throw a *TypeError* for a BigInt, and continues to apply ToNumber otherwise.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Unary minus on an unsigned value is defined rather than rejected because the alternative is worse: an unsigned type is a bit pattern as much as a count, and `-x` on it is the two's complement negation the hardware computes. Casting to the signed type first is usually clearer, and a program that wants the error uses the checked form.</p>
        <p>Unary `+` is a decision, and it is recorded here because it splits from BigInt. `+x` throws for a BigInt because `+x` is the idiom for coercing to a Number, and returning a BigInt from it would silently defeat that idiom. The same argument applies to a `uint8`, and would say `+x` should throw for every type this proposal adds. Against it: `operator+()` is an overloadable unary operator on a class in the design, so unary `+` already means more than ToNumber, and an earlier clause of this specification states that `+a` on a `uint8` is a `uint8`. This clause follows the design. If the committee prefers consistency with BigInt, the change is to this step alone, and it would make `+x` a *TypeError* for every numeric type of this proposal.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-equality-and-comparison">
      <h1>Equality and Comparison</h1>
      <p>`===` and `!==` are not overloadable and retain their identity semantics. What identity means follows from the type. The identity of a reference type is the reference, so two distinct objects are never `===`. The identity of a value type is its value, as it already is for a `uint8` and a String, so two instances of a value type class are `===` exactly when their fields are, compared field by field.</p>

      <p>`==` between two values of the same numeric type applies _T_::equal. Between two values of different numeric types it is *false* rather than a type error, as it is today between a Number and a BigInt of unequal value, and IsLooselyEqual continues to compare their mathematical values where both are numeric. A decimal compares by numerical value, so a value with trailing zeros equals one without them (<emu-xref href="#sec-decimal-floating-point-types"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <p>The relational operators apply _T_::lessThan through IsLessThan, unchanged.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>That `===` compares a value type class field by field rather than by reference is what makes a value type a value: two `Vector2` instances holding the same coordinates are the same value, and a `Map` keyed on them has one entry. This is the existing rule for a `uint8` and a String applied to an aggregate, not a new one.</p>
        <p>`==` returning *false* across numeric types rather than throwing is the existing treatment of a Number against a BigInt, and it is deliberately not aligned with the arithmetic operators, which do throw. This specification states the rule the design left open: `==` and `!=` between two typed values compare by the loose-equality algorithm they already use, so `uint8(1) == uint16(1)` is *true* by comparison of mathematical values, while `uint8(1) + uint16(1)` remains a compile-time type error. The two are deliberately not aligned: an arithmetic operator produces a value and so must fix the type it produces, which is why a cross-type use is rejected rather than silently widened; equality asks a question and produces a Boolean whatever its operands' types, so it has no result type to fix and no information to lose by answering. This is a stated position rather than an inherited one; a committee that prefers equality to throw across typed values, for symmetry with arithmetic, has the alternative recorded here.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-user-defined-operators">
      <h1>User-Defined Operators</h1>
      <p>A class may declare an operator, in which case the receiver is the left operand and the declaration's parameter is the right. Where an operator's operands are of a type that declares it, the declaration is applied in place of the steps above. The overloadable operators are the arithmetic, bitwise, shift, relational, and logical operators, their compound assignment forms, the increment and decrement operators in prefix and postfix form, the unary operators, the index accessors, and the conversion operator of <emu-xref href="#sec-user-defined-conversions"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <p>`===` and `!==` are not overloadable. An operator that the operand's type already defines through <emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-types-of-this-proposal"></emu-xref> may not be redeclared for that type, so a program cannot redefine `uint8` addition. An operator a built-in type does not define may be declared for it, which is how the SIMD extension gives the vector types their comparisons and how the primitive metadata extension declares operators on a parameterized primitive.</p>
      <p><ins>A relational operator that the class does NOT declare is derived from `&lt;` where `&lt;` is declared: `_a_ &gt; _b_` is `_b_ &lt; _a_`, `_a_ &lt;= _b_` is the negation of `_b_ &lt; _a_`, and `_a_ &gt;= _b_` is the negation of `_a_ &lt; _b_`. The result of the declared operator is coerced with ToBoolean before being negated, since it is a user function and may return any value. A DECLARED operator always takes precedence over the derivation, so a class that declares both `&lt;` and `&gt;` uses each as written.</ins></p>
      <p><ins>Without this an undeclared relational operator reached the abstract relational comparison, which coerces both operands to `"[object Object]"` and compares that String with itself - making `&gt;` always *false* and `&lt;=` and `&gt;=` always *true* for any class declaring only `&lt;`. Half of those answers are correct by coincidence, which is why only the reversed operand order reveals it: `_M_(2) &gt; _M_(1)` answered *false*.</ins></p>
      <emu-note>
        <p><ins>Deriving `&lt;=` as the negation of `_b_ &lt; _a_` assumes a TOTAL order. Where neither `_a_ &lt; _b_` nor `_b_ &lt; _a_` holds - as for a *NaN*-like value under a partial order - the derived `&lt;=` answers *true* where *false* is meant. A type whose order is partial should declare `&lt;=` itself, which the precedence rule above permits. Partiality cannot be detected from `&lt;` alone, so this is a caution to the author of such a type rather than a rule the implementation can enforce.</ins></p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>An index accessor is applied by a computed member access rather than by an operator between two operands. A computed access may supply more than one index, `grid[x, y]`, which is how the design writes a multidimensional or jagged index. Where the base of a computed access is an instance of a class that declares an index accessor, and every index supplied is a value of a numeric type, the access applies the accessor whose parameter count matches the number of indices, with those indices as its argument list, in place of the ordinary property read or write: the read direction for a value use and the write direction for the target of an assignment, the write direction taking the indices followed by the assigned value. An index that is not of a numeric type makes the access an ordinary property access, so an index accessor does not shadow the string-keyed and symbol-keyed properties, including the methods, of an instance it applies to. Resolution among several index accessors of one class is overload resolution on the number of indices and then on their types (<emu-xref href="#sec-overload-resolution"></emu-xref>), as for any overloaded operator, so a class may declare `[i]` and `[x, y]` and each is reached by the access that matches it.</p>

      <p>Where a class declares a read direction whose Static Type is a reference type and declares no write direction for that number of indices, an assignment whose target is such an access stores through the reference the read direction returns. This is the assignment-target rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-location-consuming-contexts"></emu-xref> applied to an index access: the accessor is a call whose result is a reference, and a reference denotes the place a write goes, which is why the design writes such an accessor without a setter. A read direction whose result is not a reference has no location for a write to reach, and an assignment to it is a *TypeError* exception. It is a type error for a class to declare both a reference-returning read direction and a write direction for the same number of indices, since the write would have two meanings.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A read-modify-write applies the accessor once per direction, as it does for a declared read and write pair: the read yields the value at the location and the write asks for the location again.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>Where no accessor applies, a computed access supplying several indices has the meaning it has in the base language: the brackets hold one expression, the indices are its comma-separated operands, and the key is the value of the last. Every operand is evaluated, in order, whether or not an accessor applies, so which reading a given access takes is observable only in the value it produces and never in the effects it has. A parenthesized `m[(a, b)]` supplies one index, as it reads.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Resolution among several declarations of one operator is overload resolution on the operand types, specified in <emu-xref href="#sec-overload-resolution"></emu-xref>. The operator overloading extension works the rules through a case where the receiver is not the left operand, a scalar multiplying a vector, and states what an engine must guarantee for the result to compile as well as the equivalent C++ would.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-functions">
    <h1>Functions and Overloading</h1>
    <p>This clause defines the structure of a function type, the identity and subtyping of one, and the resolution of a call among several signatures declared for one name.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-signature-records">
      <h1>Signature Records</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="signature-record">Signature Record</dfn> describes one callable signature. It has the fields of <emu-xref href="#table-signature-record"></emu-xref>. A ~function~ Type Record's [[Signatures]] is a List of them, with more than one element when the function is overloaded.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-signature-record" caption="Signature Record Fields">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>[[Parameters]]</td><td>a List of Parameter Records</td><td>The parameters, in order.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[Return]]</td><td>a Type Record</td><td>The declared return type, or the `any` type where none is declared.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[ThisType]]</td><td>a Type Record or ~none~</td><td>The declared `this` type, or ~none~ where none is declared.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[Narrows]]</td><td>a List of Narrowing Records</td><td>The narrowings a call of this signature declares (<emu-xref href="#sec-declared-narrowing"></emu-xref>). Empty where none is declared.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[TypeParameters]]</td><td>a List of Parse Nodes</td><td>The type parameters the signature declares, empty when it is not generic.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[Untyped]]</td><td>a Boolean</td><td>*true* when the signature declares no parameter type and no return type, in which case it is a catch-all.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>A <dfn id="parameter-record">Parameter Record</dfn> has a [[Name]] (a String), a [[Type]] (a Type Record), an [[Optional]] field (a Boolean, *true* when the parameter is marked `?` or has a default), a [[Rest]] field (a Boolean), an [[Initial]] field (an ECMAScript language value or ~none~, the declared default), and a [[Reference]] field (a Boolean). [[Reference]] is *true* exactly when [[Type]].[[Kind]] is ~reference~: the flag restates the type for convenience, and reflection reads the fact from the type.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A signature is identified by its parameter types and its return type, so two declarations of one name may differ in the return type alone (<emu-xref href="#sec-overloading-on-return-type"></emu-xref>). A parameter's name is carried because the design's named arguments select by it, and is not part of the signature's identity. A parameter's [[Initial]] and a signature's [[ThisType]] are part of the identity, compared by SameValue and as a type; the name is carried from the first occurrence interned, so two structurally identical declarations reflect the first's names. That is an order-of-loading fact, deterministic within a program and tolerated because a parameter has one name and there is nothing else to do with two. It is also the measure of what may be added: no field may be admitted whose value depends on the order modules were reached in, which is a stronger rule than counting fields and is the one <emu-xref href="#sec-provenance"></emu-xref> satisfies by unioning where a name wins. A decorator's metadata is the case that tests this and does not become an exception to it: two functions of one signature are one type however differently their parameters are decorated, so metadata cannot ride a Signature Record, and <emu-xref href="#sec-decorators"></emu-xref> reads it from the declaration instead. [[ThisType]] and [[Narrows]] are the design's declarative checker facts: data the checker reads and never code it runs. Both are part of a signature's identity, and both are consumed by <emu-xref href="#sec-declarative-checker-facts"></emu-xref>.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-function-type-identity-and-subtyping">
      <h1>Identity and Subtyping of Function Types</h1>
      <p>These operations complete the cases <emu-xref href="#sec-sametypewithassumptions"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref> defer for the ~function~ kind.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-samefunctiontype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SameFunctionType (
            _s_: a ~function~ Type Record,
            _t_: a ~function~ Type Record,
            _assumptions_: a List of Records that have [[First]] and [[Second]] fields,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether _s_ and _t_ are the same function type.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _s_.[[Signatures]] and _t_.[[Signatures]] do not have the same number of elements, return *false*.
          1. For each integer _i_ such that 0 &le; _i_ and _i_ &lt; the number of elements of _s_.[[Signatures]], do
            1. Let _a_ be _s_.[[Signatures]][_i_].
            1. Let _b_ be _t_.[[Signatures]][_i_].
            1. If _a_.[[Parameters]] and _b_.[[Parameters]] do not have the same number of elements, return *false*.
            1. For each integer _j_ such that 0 &le; _j_ and _j_ &lt; the number of elements of _a_.[[Parameters]], do
              1. Let _p_ be _a_.[[Parameters]][_j_].
              1. Let _q_ be _b_.[[Parameters]][_j_].
              1. If _p_.[[Optional]] is not _q_.[[Optional]], return *false*.
              1. If _p_.[[Rest]] is not _q_.[[Rest]], return *false*.
              1. If _p_.[[Reference]] is not _q_.[[Reference]], return *false*.
              1. If _p_.[[Initial]] is ~none~ or _q_.[[Initial]] is ~none~, then
                1. If _p_.[[Initial]] is not ~none~ or _q_.[[Initial]] is not ~none~, return *false*.
              1. Else if SameValue(_p_.[[Initial]], _q_.[[Initial]]) is *false*, then
                1. Return *false*.
              1. If SameTypeWithAssumptions(_p_.[[Type]], _q_.[[Type]], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. If _a_.[[ThisType]] is ~none~ or _b_.[[ThisType]] is ~none~, then
              1. If _a_.[[ThisType]] is not ~none~ or _b_.[[ThisType]] is not ~none~, return *false*.
            1. Else if SameTypeWithAssumptions(_a_.[[ThisType]], _b_.[[ThisType]], _assumptions_) is *false*, then
              1. Return *false*.
            1. If SameTypeWithAssumptions(_a_.[[Return]], _b_.[[Return]], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Signatures are compared position-wise, so the order in which a name's overloads are declared is part of the function type. Canonicalization does not reorder them, because there is no order over signatures that is both total and meaningful, and because the declaration order is already observable through the reflected `signatures` list.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-issignaturesubtype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          IsSignatureSubtype (
            _a_: a Signature Record,
            _b_: a Signature Record,
            _assumptions_: a List of Records that have [[First]] and [[Second]] fields,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether a function with the signature _a_ may be used where the signature _b_ is required.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _a_.[[Untyped]] is *true*, return *true*.
          1. If _a_ requires more arguments than _b_ may supply, return *false*.
          1. For each integer _j_ such that 0 &le; _j_ and _j_ &lt; the number of elements of _b_.[[Parameters]], do
            1. Let _q_ be _b_.[[Parameters]][_j_].
            1. If _a_.[[Parameters]] has no element at index _j_ and no element of _a_.[[Parameters]] has [[Rest]] *true*, return *false*.
            1. Let _p_ be the element of _a_.[[Parameters]] that receives the argument at index _j_, or, where more than one could receive it, the union of the types of those that could (<emu-xref href="#sec-sequenceassignment"></emu-xref>).
            1. If _p_.[[Reference]] is not _q_.[[Reference]], return *false*.
            1. Let _pType_ be _p_.[[Type]], or the element type of _p_.[[Type]] where _p_.[[Rest]] is *true*.
            1. Let _qType_ be _q_.[[Type]], or the element type of _q_.[[Type]] where _q_.[[Rest]] is *true*.
            1. If IsSubtype(_qType_, _pType_, _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
          1. If _a_.[[ThisType]] is not ~none~, then
            1. If _b_.[[ThisType]] is ~none~, return *false*.
            1. If IsSubtype(_b_.[[ThisType]], _a_.[[ThisType]], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
          1. For each Narrowing Record _n_ of _b_.[[Narrows]], do
            1. If _a_.[[Narrows]] contains no Narrowing Record whose [[Target]] is _n_.[[Target]] and whose [[Type]] is a subtype (IsSubtype) of _n_.[[Type]] under _assumptions_, return *false*.
          1. If _b_.[[Return]].[[Kind]] is ~void~, return *true*.
          1. Return IsSubtype(_a_.[[Return]], _b_.[[Return]], _assumptions_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Parameters are contravariant and the return is covariant, which is the standard rule and the only one that is sound: a function used where `(uint8) =&gt; uint8` is required will be handed a `uint8`, so it must accept at least every `uint8`, and its result will be used as a `uint8`, so it must produce only `uint8` values. A `ref` parameter is invariant because it is a location that is both read and written, so a mismatch in either direction is unsound; this is the parameter-level counterpart of the ~reference~ rule in <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>.</p>
          <p>A function that accepts fewer arguments than the target supplies is admitted, since ECMAScript already ignores extra arguments and rejecting it would make every callback that omits an unused parameter unusable. A `void` return is required of nothing, since a caller that has declared it will not use the result.</p>
          <p>This admission does not agree with the arity rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-resolveoverload"></emu-xref>, and the disagreement is worth stating. A call of `f` declared as `function f(a: uint8) {}` with two arguments selects no signature and is an error, because a signature is viable only for an argument list its arity accepts. But `f` is assignable to `(uint8, string) =&gt; void` by the rule above, and a call through a binding of that type reaches `f` with the second argument present and is not an error. The same call is therefore rejected when written directly and permitted through a function type. Either extra arguments are acceptable, in which case overload resolution should admit them and prefer the signature that uses the most, or they are not, in which case a function that ignores a parameter should not satisfy a type that supplies it and every such callback needs its parameter list widened. The design states the first rule and not the second, and this specification supplies the second in the shape ECMAScript's existing tolerance of extra arguments suggests. This specification settles it as the two rules answering two questions. A direct call is checked against what the author declared, where an extra argument is a mistake worth catching at the site that made it; assignability answers what a value can safely receive, where an extra argument is ignored by the language itself. Both rules stand as written, and a committee preferring one question's answer for both has each rule's consequences recorded above.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-isfunctionsubtype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          IsFunctionSubtype (
            _s_: a ~function~ Type Record,
            _t_: a ~function~ Type Record,
            _assumptions_: a List of Records that have [[First]] and [[Second]] fields,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether every value of the function type _s_ is a value of the function type _t_.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. For each element _b_ of _t_.[[Signatures]], do
            1. If there is no element _a_ of _s_.[[Signatures]] such that IsSignatureSubtype(_a_, _b_, _assumptions_) is *true*, return *false*.
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>An overloaded function type is the conjunction of its signatures, so a function satisfies it by satisfying each one, possibly with a different signature for each. This is why the quantifiers run this way round: every signature the target promises must be met by some signature the source provides, and a source may provide more.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-overload-resolution">
      <h1>Overload Resolution</h1>
      <p>An overloaded name evaluates to a single function object that resolves at the call site. Its `name` is the shared name, its `length` is the smallest parameter count among its signatures, and `call`, `apply`, and `bind` resolve by the same rules. Individual signatures are not addressable as properties; they are exposed by the function reflection of the decorators extension.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-resolveoverload" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          ResolveOverload (
            _signatures_: a List of Signature Records,
            _args_: a List of Parse Nodes,
            _contextualType_: a Type Record or ~none~,
          ): either a Signature Record, ~none~, or ~ambiguous~
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It selects the signature a call invokes.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _viable_ be a new empty List.
          1. For each element _sig_ of _signatures_, do
            1. If BindArguments(_sig_, _args_) is ~unmatched~ (<emu-xref href="#sec-bindarguments"></emu-xref>), then
              1. Continue.
            1. If some element of _args_ is not assignable to the type of the parameter that receives it, then
              1. Continue.
            1. If _contextualType_ is not ~none~ and IsAssignable(_sig_.[[Return]], _contextualType_) is *false*, then
              1. Continue.
            1. Append _sig_ to _viable_.
          1. If _viable_ is empty, return ~none~.
          1. For each element _sig_ of _viable_, do
            1. Let _rank_ be the greatest rank in <emu-xref href="#table-argument-match-ranks"></emu-xref> required by any element of _args_ to match its parameter in _sig_.
            1. Let _sig_'s worst rank be _rank_.
          1. Let _best_ be the elements of _viable_ whose worst rank is least.
          1. If _best_ has exactly one element, return that element.
          1. Return ~ambiguous~.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-table id="table-argument-match-ranks" caption="Argument Match Ranks">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>The argument matches its parameter by</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1</td><td>Being of exactly the parameter's type.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2</td><td>Being an untyped literal that takes the parameter's type, ordered among competing parameter types by <emu-xref href="#table-literal-ranking"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3</td><td>A user-defined conversion (<emu-xref href="#sec-user-defined-conversions"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4</td><td>Binding to a signature whose [[Untyped]] is *true*.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>It is a type error if ResolveOverload returns ~none~, and it is a type error if it returns ~ambiguous~. Where an argument has the `any` type the resolution is performed at run time on the argument's runtime type, and the same errors are thrown as a *TypeError* there.</p>

      <p>Two signatures declared for one name must not be ambiguous for any argument list. It is a type error to declare a signature that is viable for the same argument list as an existing one at the same rank.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Ranking by the worst match rather than by a sum keeps the rule from trading a bad match on one argument against a good one on another, which is where the ranking rules of C++ become hard to predict. A typed argument never ranks below 1: a typed argument of the wrong type is not viable at all, so it is never a question of how well it matches.</p>
        <p>An untyped function is a catch-all because its signature has [[Untyped]] *true*, which ranks last. Given `function f() {}` and `function f(a: uint8) {}`, the call `f(1)` selects the typed signature, since the literal ranks 2 against the catch-all's 4, while `f(1, 2)` selects the untyped one, since the typed signature's arity does not accept two arguments and it is not viable. A function that takes no arguments and is nonetheless typed is written by declaring the return type, as `function f(): void {}`, which makes [[Untyped]] *false* and so makes `f(1)` an error.</p>
        <p>The ambiguity rule reaches further than duplicate parameter lists. `function f(a: uint8) {}` and `function f(a: uint8, b: string = 'b') {}` are ambiguous, because the default makes the second viable for a single `uint8` argument at the same rank; so are `function f(a: float32): void {}` and `function f(...a: [].&lt;float32&gt;): void {}`, because the rest parameter covers the one-argument call. A default or a rest parameter expands a signature into a family of arities, and it is the family that must not collide. Several rests multiply the family rather than changing the rule, and a signature with a rest of an untyped element collides with a great deal, which is a reason to type one.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-named-arguments">
      <h1>Named Arguments</h1>
      <p>An argument at a call site may be written as a name and a value, `name: AssignmentExpression`, which supplies the value for the parameter of that name rather than for the parameter at that position. A named argument is a compact way to supply a later parameter while omitting earlier ones that have a default. The parameter names a signature carries (<emu-xref href="#sec-signature-records"></emu-xref>) are what a named argument selects by.</p>
      <p>The |Arguments| grammar admits a named argument alongside the positional and spread forms. A named argument is an |IdentifierName| followed by `:` and an |AssignmentExpression|, at the top of an argument. This is new syntax: a call argument that is an identifier followed by `:` is a syntax error in the base language, so no existing call changes meaning, and the form is available only where the runtime types feature is enabled.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        ArgumentList[Yield, Await] :
          <del>AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]</del>
          <del>`...` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]</del>
          <del>ArgumentList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]</del>
          <del>ArgumentList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` `...` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]</del>
          <ins>Argument[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>ArgumentList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` Argument[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>

        <ins>Argument[Yield, Await] :
          AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]
          `...` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]
          NamedArgument[?Yield, ?Await]

        NamedArgument[Yield, Await] :
          IdentifierName `:` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>This is the first restatement of |ArgumentList|, factoring the pinned edition's four alternatives through an |Argument| element exactly as |FormalParameters| is factored above, so that a new argument form is one added alternative rather than two. The references extension adds one such alternative, the `ref` argument, by restating |Argument| (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-syntax"></emu-xref>); per <emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref> that later block is the production's complete form.</p>
      <p>A call is bound to the parameters of the selected signature by BindArguments (<emu-xref href="#sec-bindarguments"></emu-xref>). It is a type error if a named argument's name is not a parameter of the signature, and it is a type error if BindArguments returns ~unmatched~.</p>
      <p>A rest parameter is filled by name as well as by position. A named argument whose name is a rest parameter's supplies a value of that rest, and the positional arguments that follow it join the same rest in the order written, so `function f(a: uint8, b: string = '', ...args: [].&lt;string&gt;) {}` called `f(8, args: 'a', 'b')` binds `a` to 8, leaves `b` its default, and gives `args` the elements 'a' and 'b'. Where a parameter after that rest must still be filled, the rest gives back what the parameters after it require, which is the same rule BindArguments applies to a positional call.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-sequenceassignment" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SequenceAssignment (
            _slots_: a List of Records with a [[Rest]] field (a Boolean) and an [[Optional]] field (a Boolean),
            _n_: a non-negative integer,
            _admits_: an Abstract Closure taking a non-negative integer and a Record of _slots_ and returning a Boolean,
          ): either a List of non-negative integers or ~unmatched~
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It distributes _n_ items among _slots_ in order, returning how many items each slot takes, or ~unmatched~ where no distribution admits every item. A slot whose [[Rest]] is *true* takes zero or more items, one whose [[Optional]] is *true* takes one or none, and any other takes exactly one. _admits_(_i_, _slot_) reports whether the item at index _i_ may be taken by _slot_.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _slots_ is empty, then
            1. If _n_ is 0, return a new empty List.
            1. Return ~unmatched~.
          1. Let _slot_ be _slots_[0].
          1. Let _tail_ be a List of the elements of _slots_ from index 1.
          1. Let _limit_ be 0.
          1. Repeat, while _limit_ &lt; _n_ and _admits_(_limit_, _slot_) is *true* and (_slot_.[[Rest]] is *true* or _limit_ &lt; 1),
            1. Set _limit_ to _limit_ + 1.
          1. Let _min_ be 0.
          1. If _slot_.[[Rest]] is *false* and _slot_.[[Optional]] is *false*, set _min_ to 1.
          1. For each integer _m_ from _limit_ down to _min_, in descending order, do
            1. Let _shifted_ be a new Abstract Closure with parameters (_index_, _which_) that captures _admits_ and _m_ and performs the following steps when called:
              1. Return _admits_(_index_ + _m_, _which_).
            1. Let _rest_ be SequenceAssignment(_tail_, _n_ - _m_, _shifted_).
            1. If _rest_ is not ~unmatched~, return the list-concatenation of &laquo; _m_ &raquo; and _rest_.
          1. Return ~unmatched~.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>This is leftmost-greedy matching with backtracking, which is what the design means by taking arguments greedily and giving them back to satisfy the signature: a slot takes as many items as it can and yields one at a time until the slots after it can be satisfied. A parameter list, and a tuple's element list, is a regular pattern over a sequence — a fixed slot is one item, an optional slot is one or none, a rest is zero or more — and this operation is the match.</p>
          <p>The result is deterministic. Because the search takes the greatest count first at each slot in turn, the assignment returned is the greatest in the lexicographic order on the count lists, so it does not depend on how an implementation searches; an implementation may use any method that agrees with it. The recursion terminates because each call passes a shorter _slots_.</p>
          <p>The design's own example is the one to check the rule against. For `function f(...a: [].&lt;uint32&gt;, ...b: [].&lt;uint32&gt;, c: uint32)` called `f(0, 1, 2)`, the first slot takes all three, the tail cannot be satisfied, it yields to two, `b` then takes the one remaining and `c` cannot be satisfied, `b` yields to none, and the assignment settles at `a`: &laquo; 0, 1 &raquo;, `b`: &laquo; &raquo;, `c`: 2. Where the types differ the filter does the work with no further rule: in `function f(a: string, ...args: [].&lt;uint32&gt;, ...args2: [].&lt;string&gt;, callback: () =&gt; void)`, `args` stops at the first argument that is not a `uint32`.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-bindarguments" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          BindArguments (
            _sig_: a Signature Record,
            _args_: a List of arguments,
          ): either a List of bindings or ~unmatched~
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It binds a call's arguments to a signature's parameters, giving each parameter the value or values it receives.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. For each named argument _a_ of _args_, in order, do
            1. Let _p_ be the parameter of _sig_.[[Parameters]] whose [[Name]] is _a_'s name; if there is none, return ~unmatched~.
            1. Assign _a_'s value to _p_, and where _p_.[[Rest]] is *true*, assign to _p_ the positional arguments following _a_ up to the next named argument as well.
          1. Let _positional_ be the positional arguments of _args_ that precede the first named argument, in order.
          1. Let _slots_ be a List containing, for each parameter _p_ of _sig_.[[Parameters]] that no named argument assigned to, a Record { [[Rest]]: _p_.[[Rest]], [[Optional]]: _p_.[[Optional]] }, in parameter order.
          1. Let _admits_ be a new Abstract Closure with parameters (_index_, _which_) that captures _positional_ and performs the following steps when called:
            1. Let _p_ be the parameter _which_ stands for.
            1. Let _target_ be _p_.[[Type]], or the element type of _p_.[[Type]] where _p_.[[Rest]] is *true*.
            1. Return IsAssignable(the Static Type of _positional_[_index_], _target_).
          1. Let _counts_ be SequenceAssignment(_slots_, the number of elements of _positional_, _admits_).
          1. If _counts_ is ~unmatched~, return ~unmatched~.
          1. Assign to each such parameter, in order, the number of elements of _positional_ that _counts_ gives it, a rest parameter receiving them as an array of its type and any other receiving the one element.
          1. For each parameter _p_ of _sig_.[[Parameters]] that received nothing, do
            1. If _p_.[[Initial]] is not ~none~, assign _p_.[[Initial]] to _p_.
            1. Else if _p_.[[Rest]] is *true*, assign an empty array of _p_.[[Type]] to _p_.
            1. Else if _p_.[[Optional]] is *false*, return ~unmatched~.
          1. Return the bindings.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>A named argument pins its parameter before the positional match runs, so it prunes the search rather than widening it, and the positionals that follow a named rest join that rest as the paragraph above says. A rest that receives nothing is bound to an empty array rather than left unfilled, which is what makes `b` an empty array rather than an error in the design's example.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
      <p>A spread of an object, `...AssignmentExpression` where the value is an object with no `Symbol.iterator` method, binds each of the object's own enumerable String-keyed properties by parameter name, as though each were written as a named argument. A spread whose value is iterable fills positions in order, as in the base language. Because the two are distinguished by the presence of an iterator, an object used to supply arguments by name is one that is not iterable; an iterable, including an Array, spreads by position.</p>
      <p>Where the called value is overloaded (<emu-xref href="#sec-overload-resolution"></emu-xref>), the parameter names are those of the selected signature, and a named or object-spread call that no signature can bind is a type error by the same rule as a positional call that matches no signature.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Named arguments do not change a signature's identity: a parameter's name is carried but is not compared (<emu-xref href="#sec-signature-records"></emu-xref>), so the same function reached through two declarations with different parameter names is one type, and a named call selects by whichever names the interned signature carries.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-overloading-on-return-type">
      <h1>Overloading on Return Type</h1>
      <p>Because a signature is identified by its return type as well as its parameter types, two signatures may differ only in their return. The return type does not participate in ranking; it participates in viability, through the contextual type of the call (<emu-xref href="#sec-contextual-types"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <p>The contextual type of a call is the type its position requires. Where a call has no contextual type and more than one signature remains viable, the call is ~ambiguous~ and it is a type error.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Given `function f(): uint32` and `function f(): string`, the call `f()` alone is an error, `const a: string = f();` selects the second, and `g(f())` selects the first where `g` takes a `uint32`, because the parameter supplies the contextual type. `h(f())` is an error where `h` is itself overloaded on `uint8` and `string`, since an argument of a call with more than one applicable signature has no contextual type. The circularity is real and is resolved by rejecting the call rather than by guessing.</p>
        <p>Return-type overloading exists for the operators. A SIMD comparison returns either a vector of lane results or a compact mask, which Intel's intrinsics distinguish by name and which real types cannot: `operator&lt;(v: int32x4): int32x4` and `operator&lt;(v: int32x4): boolean8` are the same signature but for the return. `const a: int32x4 = x == y;` and `const b: boolean8 = x == y;` select between them, and the comparison written with no contextual type is an error rather than a default.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-completing-the-literal-ranking">
      <h1>The Literal Ranking Completed</h1>
      <p><emu-xref href="#table-literal-ranking"></emu-xref> orders the numeric types a literal may take. It is completed here for the types it omits, which are the ones a call may nonetheless select.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-literal-ranking-completion" caption="Literal Ranking of the Remaining Numeric Types">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Rank</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`number`</td><td>Ahead of `float64`, and so ahead of every other numeric type.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`int.&lt;N&gt;` and `uint.&lt;N&gt;` for an _N_ that is not a named shorthand</td><td>Among the widths of their family, in the same order: a narrower width after a wider one. `uint.&lt;24&gt;` ranks after `uint32` and before `uint16`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`rational.&lt;N&gt;`</td><td>After every integer type. A wider _N_ before a narrower one.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`complex.&lt;T&gt;` and `vector.&lt;T, N&gt;`</td><td>Not ranked. A literal does not take either type, so no signature is selected for a literal argument by them.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Ranking `number` first is the repair <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-overload-ranking"></emu-xref> records. The design's ranking begins at `float64` and justifies it on the ground that Number is a `float64` and the conversion is exact, which is the argument for ranking `number` itself first: it is the type a literal takes with no contextual type, and the type an untyped program computes with. Without it, `f(a: number)` against `f(a: float32)` has no answer for `f(1)`. This changes which function a call selects and so was a supplied repair when first recorded and is now load-bearing: <emu-xref href="#sec-overloading-of-the-standard-library"></emu-xref> shows every untyped call into the overloaded standard library keeps its meaning only because `number` ranks first, so the ranking stands as a back-compatibility condition rather than a preference.</p>
        <p>Placing the rationals after the integers keeps a literal that is an integer selecting an integer signature, which is what a program that declares both would expect. Within a family a wider width ranks first, following the design's own order, which prefers the type that can represent the most.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-typed-arrow-functions">
      <h1>Arrow Functions</h1>
      <p>An arrow function's parameters may carry annotations and its body may carry a return annotation. The annotations occupy the positions <emu-xref href="#sec-type-annotations"></emu-xref> gives them, within the cover grammar the arrow syntax already uses.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        ArrowFunction[In, Yield, Await] :
          ArrowParameters[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> [no LineTerminator here] `=>` ConciseBody[?In]
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>When the production <emu-grammar>ArrowParameters : CoverParenthesizedExpressionAndArrowParameterList</emu-grammar> is refined using |ArrowFormalParameters|, a parameter may carry the annotations of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-annotations"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`(x: uint8): uint8 =&gt; x * x` is therefore an arrow function of one `uint8` parameter returning a `uint8`. The return annotation sits between the parameters and the `=&gt;`, which is the only position available, and it is what distinguishes an annotated arrow from a function type: `(uint8) =&gt; uint8` in a type position is a type, while `(x: uint8): uint8 =&gt; x * x` in an expression position is a function. An arrow function is not overloadable, since it has no name to overload.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-annotations-on-the-remaining-function-forms">
      <h1>Annotations on the Remaining Function Forms</h1>
      <p>Every function-shaped production takes the return annotation the same way: a |TypeAnnotation| between the parameter list and the body. The productions below restate their ECMAScript definitions with that insertion (<emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref>); parameter annotations need no insertion here, since a parameter of any of these forms is a |FormalParameter| or |BindingElement| and carries its annotation there.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        MethodDefinition[Yield,Await] :
          ClassElementName[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `(` UniqueFormalParameters[~Yield, ~Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` FunctionBody[~Yield, ~Await] `}`
          GeneratorMethod[?Yield, ?Await]
          AsyncMethod[?Yield, ?Await]
          AsyncGeneratorMethod[?Yield, ?Await]
          `get` ClassElementName[?Yield, ?Await] `(` `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` FunctionBody[~Yield, ~Await] `}`
          `set` ClassElementName[?Yield, ?Await] `(` PropertySetParameterList `)` `{` FunctionBody[~Yield, ~Await] `}`

        GeneratorMethod[Yield,Await] :
          `*` ClassElementName[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `(` UniqueFormalParameters[+Yield, ~Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` GeneratorBody `}`

        AsyncMethod[Yield,Await] :
          `async` [no LineTerminator here] ClassElementName[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `(` UniqueFormalParameters[~Yield, +Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` AsyncFunctionBody `}`

        AsyncGeneratorMethod[Yield,Await] :
          `async` [no LineTerminator here] `*` ClassElementName[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `(` UniqueFormalParameters[+Yield, +Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` AsyncGeneratorBody `}`

        GeneratorDeclaration[Yield,Await,Default] :
          `function` `*` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] `(` FormalParameters[+Yield, ~Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` GeneratorBody `}`
          [+Default] `function` `*` `(` FormalParameters[+Yield, ~Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` GeneratorBody `}`

        AsyncFunctionDeclaration[Yield,Await,Default] :
          `async` [no LineTerminator here] `function` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] `(` FormalParameters[~Yield, +Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` AsyncFunctionBody `}`
          [+Default] `async` [no LineTerminator here] `function` `(` FormalParameters[~Yield, +Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` AsyncFunctionBody `}`

        AsyncGeneratorDeclaration[Yield,Await,Default] :
          `async` [no LineTerminator here] `function` `*` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] `(` FormalParameters[+Yield, +Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` AsyncGeneratorBody `}`
          [+Default] `async` [no LineTerminator here] `function` `*` `(` FormalParameters[+Yield, +Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` AsyncGeneratorBody `}`

        FunctionExpression :
          `function` BindingIdentifier[~Yield, ~Await]? `(` FormalParameters[~Yield, ~Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[~Yield, ~Await]?</ins> `{` FunctionBody[~Yield, ~Await] `}`

        GeneratorExpression :
          `function` `*` BindingIdentifier[+Yield, ~Await]? `(` FormalParameters[+Yield, ~Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[+Yield, ~Await]?</ins> `{` GeneratorBody `}`

        AsyncFunctionExpression :
          `async` [no LineTerminator here] `function` BindingIdentifier[~Yield, +Await]? `(` FormalParameters[~Yield, +Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[~Yield, +Await]?</ins> `{` AsyncFunctionBody `}`

        AsyncGeneratorExpression :
          `async` [no LineTerminator here] `function` `*` BindingIdentifier[+Yield, +Await]? `(` FormalParameters[+Yield, +Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[+Yield, +Await]?</ins> `{` AsyncGeneratorBody `}`

        AsyncArrowFunction[In,Yield,Await] :
          `async` [no LineTerminator here] AsyncArrowBindingIdentifier[?Yield] <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> [no LineTerminator here] `=>` AsyncConciseBody[?In]
          CoverCallExpressionAndAsyncArrowHead[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> [no LineTerminator here] `=>` AsyncConciseBody[?In] #callcover
      </emu-grammar>
        <p>A method may declare type parameters, as a function declaration may. They are in scope in the method's own signature and body, and a method of a generic class also reads that class's parameters there; where a method declares a parameter of the same name as one of the class's, the method's is the one its body reads. The arguments are supplied at the call, explicitly with `.` `&lt;` `&gt;` or inferred from the call's arguments, by the rules of <emu-xref href="#sec-generics"></emu-xref>.</p>
        <p>An accessor may not declare type parameters. A getter is never written as a call and takes no arguments, so a parameter it declared could be neither supplied nor inferred; a setter could infer one from the assigned value, but parameterizing one half of a property and not the other would leave the two halves of one construct with different rules. A method serves where a parameterized accessor would, and states which type it is applied to at the site rather than deducing it from a value. An accessor of a generic class reads that class's parameters as any other body of the declaration does; what it may not do is introduce parameters of its own.</p>

      <p>A `set` accessor declares no return type, so its production is restated unchanged; its single parameter is annotated through |PropertySetParameterList|. When |CoverCallExpressionAndAsyncArrowHead| is refined using |AsyncArrowHead|, a parameter may carry the annotations of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-annotations"></emu-xref>, exactly as the synchronous cover is refined in <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-arrow-functions"></emu-xref>. The static and runtime semantics of an annotation on any of these forms are those of <emu-xref href="#sec-functions"></emu-xref>: the annotation gives the declared return type, an `async` function's annotation types the promise the call returns, and a generator's annotation types the values the iterator yields.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The design writes `async function f(): Promise.&lt;uint8, Error&gt;` and typed generator methods throughout; this clause is where those parses come from. A class constructor is the one method that takes no return annotation, which <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-classes"></emu-xref> states.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-classes-interfaces-and-enums">
    <h1>Classes, Interfaces, and Enums</h1>
    <p>This clause defines the nominal and structural types a program declares: the object type and its identity and subtyping, which complete the last cases <emu-xref href="#sec-type-system-foundations"></emu-xref> defers; the rules that make a class a value type; interfaces; enums; and the sealed hierarchy that gives a closed union.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-object-types-semantics">
      <h1>Object Types</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="property-type-record">Property Type Record</dfn> describes one member of an ~object~ type. It has a [[Key]] (a property key), a [[Type]] (a Type Record), an [[Optional]] field (a Boolean), a [[Readonly]] field (a Boolean), and an [[Initial]] field (an ECMAScript language value or ~none~, the member's declared default).</p>

      <p>An <dfn id="index-signature-record">Index Signature Record</dfn> has a [[KeyType]] and a [[ValueType]], each a Type Record. No production of the type grammar writes one: an object type acquires index signatures through construction (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-maketype"></emu-xref>), they take part in identity below and in `keyof` (<emu-xref href="#sec-keyof"></emu-xref>), and their membership and subtype rules are parked with the objects work that will write the source syntax.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-sameobjecttype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SameObjectType (
            _s_: an ~object~ Type Record,
            _t_: an ~object~ Type Record,
            _assumptions_: a List of Records that have [[First]] and [[Second]] fields,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether _s_ and _t_ are the same object type.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _s_.[[Properties]] and _t_.[[Properties]] do not have the same number of elements, return *false*.
          1. For each element _p_ of _s_.[[Properties]], do
            1. Let _q_ be the element of _t_.[[Properties]] whose [[Key]] is _p_.[[Key]], or ~empty~ if there is none.
            1. If _q_ is ~empty~, return *false*.
            1. If _p_.[[Optional]] is not _q_.[[Optional]], return *false*.
            1. If _p_.[[Readonly]] is not _q_.[[Readonly]], return *false*.
            1. If _p_.[[Initial]] is ~none~ or _q_.[[Initial]] is ~none~, then
              1. If _p_.[[Initial]] is not ~none~ or _q_.[[Initial]] is not ~none~, return *false*.
            1. Else if SameValue(_p_.[[Initial]], _q_.[[Initial]]) is *false*, then
              1. Return *false*.
            1. If SameTypeWithAssumptions(_p_.[[Type]], _q_.[[Type]], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
          1. If _s_.[[IndexSignatures]] and _t_.[[IndexSignatures]] do not have the same number of elements, return *false*.
          1. For each integer _j_ such that 0 &le; _j_ and _j_ &lt; the number of elements of _s_.[[IndexSignatures]], do
            1. Let _a_ be _s_.[[IndexSignatures]][_j_].
            1. Let _b_ be _t_.[[IndexSignatures]][_j_].
            1. If SameTypeWithAssumptions(_a_.[[KeyType]], _b_.[[KeyType]], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. If SameTypeWithAssumptions(_a_.[[ValueType]], _b_.[[ValueType]], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Members are matched by key rather than by position, so the order in which an object type lists them is not part of its identity and `{ x: float32, y: float32 }` and `{ y: float32, x: float32 }` are one type. Canonicalization orders the [[Properties]] of an ~object~ Type Record by key, which is what allows the interning of <emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref> to give them one Type Object, and orders the [[IndexSignatures]] by the fixed total order on their [[KeyType]]s. That ordering serves identity; whether the source's own member order should additionally be preserved for display and reflection, carried outside the canonical form the way a parameter's name already is (<emu-xref href="#sec-signature-records"></emu-xref>), is settled against a second channel: interning merges every spelling of a type into one object, so a non-canonical order would be the first spelling's, an accident of module load order, and the determinism this specification enforces elsewhere forbids exactly that kind of accident. A reflected object type lists its properties in key order whatever order the source wrote.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-isobjectsubtype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          IsObjectSubtype (
            _s_: an ~object~ Type Record,
            _t_: an ~object~ Type Record,
            _assumptions_: a List of Records that have [[First]] and [[Second]] fields,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines whether every value of the object type _s_ is a value of the object type _t_.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. For each element _q_ of _t_.[[Properties]], do
            1. Let _p_ be the element of _s_.[[Properties]] whose [[Key]] is _q_.[[Key]], or ~empty~ if there is none.
            1. If _p_ is ~empty~, then
              1. If _q_.[[Optional]] is *false*, return *false*.
              1. Continue.
            1. If _p_.[[Optional]] is *true* and _q_.[[Optional]] is *false*, return *false*.
            1. If _q_.[[Readonly]] is *false* and _p_.[[Readonly]] is *true*, return *false*.
            1. If _q_.[[Readonly]] is *true*, then
              1. If IsSubtype(_p_.[[Type]], _q_.[[Type]], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Else,
              1. If SameTypeWithAssumptions(_p_.[[Type]], _q_.[[Type]], _assumptions_) is *false*, return *false*.
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>An object type is subtyped in width, so a type with more members is a subtype of one with fewer: an object that has everything `{ x: float32 }` requires is usable where that type is required, whatever else it has.</p>
          <p>It is subtyped in depth only through a `readonly` member. A `readonly` member is covariant, since a value read from it and never written through it need only be of the required type. A writable member is invariant, because covariance there is unsound: were `{ x: Meter }` a subtype of `{ x: float32 }`, a bare `float32` could be written through the supertype view into a slot the program believes holds a `Meter`. This is the same rule the ~reference~ and ~array~ kinds already follow in <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>, and it is the point at which this proposal parts company with an erasable type system, which can afford covariance there because nothing depends on the claim. Here the layout does.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>A boundary that accepts a value into a position of an ~object~ type does not impose that type's members on the value. The value's own properties keep the types they were declared with, and a property the value declared without a type keeps the `any` type.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This answers the question <emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref> raises and it answers it in the direction that admits the unsoundness, because the alternative is worse. Imposing the target's member types would mean that passing a plain object to a `{ x: uint8 }` position silently retypes the object for every other holder of it, which is an action at a distance no other boundary performs, and would make the same object have different property types depending on where it had been. The consequence is that a check on a structural type is a statement about the value at the moment it crossed, not a guarantee about it afterwards, and a later write through an untyped alias can invalidate it. A program that wants the guarantee declares the property's type with the `{ (x: uint8): 1 }` form of the object typing section, or uses a class, where the field's type is a property of the layout.</p>
        <p>A narrowing of a property, as in `if (o.f instanceof T)`, follows from the same fact and is admitted only where the property cannot change between the test and the use: a `readonly` field of a sealed typed class, whose write is a type error after construction. Elsewhere the narrowing does not hold, since `o.f` may be a getter or may be assigned in between, and <emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref> therefore narrows bindings rather than paths.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-typed-classes">
      <h1>Typed Classes</h1>
      <p>A class is <dfn id="typed-class">typed</dfn> when at least one of its public or private fields carries a type annotation. A typed class is automatically sealed and its prototype frozen, as <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-storage"></emu-xref> specifies. A class declared `dynamic` is not sealed and is not a value type.</p>

      <p>A typed class is a <dfn id="value-type-class">value type class</dfn> when every one of its fields has a type that is a value type. Instances of a value type class are values in the sense of <emu-xref href="#sec-value-types"></emu-xref>: they are laid out contiguously, they are stored inline in a field or an array element of that type, and assigning one copies it. It is a type error for a value type class to contain itself, directly or through a cycle of value type fields, since the layout would have no finite size (<emu-xref href="#sec-layout-finiteness"></emu-xref>). A field that closes such a cycle is written `T | null`, which is a reference.</p>

      <p>A constructor declares no return type, since the class is the type a construction yields. A field with no initializer holds the default value of its type (<emu-xref href="#sec-defaultvalueof"></emu-xref>), and it is a type error to declare a field whose type has no default and give it no initializer.</p>

      <p>`===` on two instances of a value type class compares them field by field, since the identity of a value type is its value. `===` on two instances of a class that is not a value type compares references.</p>

      <p>A field may be declared `readonly`, a modifier written before the field name and after `static` where both appear. A `readonly` field may be assigned only in its own initializer and in a body of a constructor of the declaring class. Every other assignment is a type error: at compile time where the type is known, and at run time otherwise, so an assignment from a method a constructor calls, from a constructor or method of a subclass, through a reference, or through reflection is a *TypeError*. The permission is the running constructor's, not the constructor's presence on the stack, so a method the constructor invokes cannot write the field even though a constructor is executing. `readonly` is shallow: it fixes the binding, not the object the field refers to, so a `readonly` field holding an object may have that object mutated.</p>

      <p>Performing SetIntegrityLevel with `frozen` on an instance of a typed class, as `Object.freeze` does, makes every field of the instance `readonly` at run time in addition to making its properties non-writable. A subsequent assignment to any field is therefore a *TypeError* in every mode, rather than the silent failure a non-writable property gives outside strict mode. This applies to an instance of a typed class, the same instances that are automatically sealed; freezing an ordinary object is unchanged.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>That a value type class defaults field by field means an instance can come into existence without its constructor running, which <emu-xref href="#sec-default-values"></emu-xref> flags. This clause endorses it, because the design already requires it in two places and the alternative would break both. `let d: [10].&lt;A&gt;;` must hold ten zero-filled instances for the memory layout extension's contiguous array to exist at all, and a conversion from a matching object literal, `{ ... } := Vector3`, is specified to fill the layout directly and run no constructor. A value type class is therefore a shape with a zero, not an object with an invariant its constructor establishes, and a class that needs its constructor to run states that by holding a field whose type has no default, which makes the zero-filled form a type error. A parameterized field is one way to hold one: a class of unit-typed fields has a zero exactly where the base declares the cast that admits it (<emu-xref href="#sec-default-values"></emu-xref>), so a design that wants its vectors zero-fillable writes that cast, and one whose brand is meant to be reached only through the operators that produce it need not. This is a real constraint on what a value type class can promise, and it should be stated in the design rather than left to follow from the array case.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-abstract-classes">
      <h1>Abstract Classes</h1>
      <p>A class may be declared `abstract`, `sealed`, or `dynamic`. The productions below restate their ECMAScript definitions with the modifier list (<emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref>); `sealed` is specified by <emu-xref href="#sec-sealed-classes"></emu-xref>, `dynamic` by <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-classes"></emu-xref>, and the abstract method form is an alternative of the |ClassElement| restatement in <emu-xref href="#sec-operator-declarations"></emu-xref>. It is a type error if a modifier is repeated, and a type error if `sealed` and `dynamic` are declared together.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        ClassDeclaration[Yield,Await,Default] :
          <ins>ClassModifiers?</ins> `class` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] ClassTail[?Yield, ?Await]
          [+Default] <ins>ClassModifiers?</ins> `class` ClassTail[?Yield, ?Await]

        ClassExpression[Yield,Await] :
          <ins>ClassModifiers?</ins> `class` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await]? ClassTail[?Yield, ?Await]

        <ins>ClassModifiers :
          ClassModifier
          ClassModifiers ClassModifier

        ClassModifier : one of
          `abstract` `sealed` `dynamic` `partial`</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>An abstract method is a signature with no body, and its annotation types the implementations: it is a type error if a subclass implements an inherited abstract method with a signature the abstract declaration does not accept. It is a type error if a |ClassElement| of the abstract form appears in a class not declared `abstract`, and a type error if a class not declared `abstract` leaves an inherited abstract method unimplemented. Where the Static Type of the constructor in `new C(&hellip;)` is an abstract class it is a type error; the [[Construct]] internal method of an abstract class's constructor throws a *TypeError* exception when NewTarget is that constructor itself, so a dynamic construction fails at run time while `super()` from a concrete subclass, arriving with a concrete NewTarget, runs the abstract constructor as a constructor body should.</p>

      <p>An abstract class is never a value type class (<emu-xref href="#value-type-class"></emu-xref>). Its purpose is polymorphic dispatch through the hierarchy, which is reference behaviour, so an abstract class and every subclass reached through an abstract-typed binding are reference types.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The design's `Shape` declares `abstract area(): number;` beside a concrete `describe()`, which is the division of labour: an interface is pure signature, while an abstract class carries the partial implementation a family of subclasses completes (design, README, Abstract Classes).</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-interfaces-semantics">
      <h1>Interfaces</h1>
      <p>An interface is declared with the productions below; |InterfaceDeclaration| is an alternative of the |Declaration| restatement of <emu-xref href="#sec-meta-declarations"></emu-xref>, and |ClassTail| is restated with the `implements` clause (<emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        <ins>InterfaceDeclaration[Yield, Await] :
          `interface` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]? `{` InterfaceBody[?Yield, ?Await]? `}`

        InterfaceBody[Yield, Await] :
          InterfaceMemberList[?Yield, ?Await] TypeMemberSeparator?

        InterfaceMemberList[Yield, Await] :
          InterfaceMember[?Yield, ?Await]
          InterfaceMemberList[?Yield, ?Await] TypeMemberSeparator InterfaceMember[?Yield, ?Await]

        InterfaceMember[Yield, Await] :
          TypeMember[?Yield, ?Await]
          OperatorDefinition[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>

        ClassTail[Yield,Await] :
          ClassHeritage[?Yield, ?Await]? <ins>ImplementsClause[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` ClassBody[?Yield, ?Await]? `}`

        <ins>ImplementsClause[Yield, Await] :
          `implements` ImplementsList[?Yield, ?Await]

        ImplementsList[Yield, Await] :
          TypeReference[?Yield, ?Await]
          ImplementsList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` TypeReference[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>An interface's members are the |TypeMember| forms of an object type, index signatures and optional members with defaults included, and additionally operator members, as `interface Ordered.&lt;T&gt; { operator&lt;(other: T): boolean; }` written with declaration-site `&lt;`&hellip;`&gt;`; a type satisfies an operator member by declaring the operator. It is a type error if a class whose |ClassTail| carries an |ImplementsClause| does not satisfy every member, operator members included, of every listed interface.</p>

      <p>An interface declares a contract. Its type is a ~nominal~ type whose [[Declaration]] is the interface declaration, and a class that implements it is a subtype of it by <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>, which follows the declared hierarchy. An interface may also type an object, an array, or a function structurally.</p>

      <p>A class that implements an interface cannot remove the contract: deleting a member the interface requires throws a *TypeError* exception, and this holds through a subclass, a reference, and reflection.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>An interface is nominal where a class declares that it implements one and structural where a value is checked against it, which are two questions rather than one contradiction: the first asks whether a declaration promised the contract, the second whether a value meets it. `IsOfType` decides the second by <emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref>, so an object that has the members satisfies an interface-typed position whether or not any class declared it.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-enums">
      <h1>Enums</h1>
      <p>An enum is declared with the productions below; |EnumDeclaration| is an alternative of the |Declaration| restatement of <emu-xref href="#sec-meta-declarations"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        <ins>EnumDeclaration[Yield, Await] :
          `enum` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? `{` EnumMemberList[?Yield, ?Await]? `,`? `}`

        EnumMemberList[Yield, Await] :
          EnumMember[?Yield, ?Await]
          EnumMemberList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` EnumMember[?Yield, ?Await]

        EnumMember[Yield, Await] :
          IdentifierName Initializer[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]?</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>It is a type error if two enumerators of one declaration have the same name. Every initializer is evaluated in the compile-time evaluable fragment (<emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>), since an enumerator is a constant of the program. An enumerator's initializer stands in a position whose contextual type is the underlying type, so a literal there takes it (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref>): `enum D: decimal64 { A = 1.00 }` holds the decimal `1.00` rather than a rounded double, and `enum B: bigint { A = 1 }` needs no `n` suffix. The contextual type is applied rather than required, since an initializer may also be a function of two parameters, which this clause calls rather than stores.</p>

      <p>It is a type error if an enumerator's value is already an enumerator of another enum of the same agent. Where the underlying type's values carry their own identity, that is an object, a function, or a symbol, an enumerator IS the value its initializer denotes, which is what makes it usable as that value without conversion. A value serving two enums would therefore be a value of two nominal types, and <emu-xref href="#sec-value-types"></emu-xref> admits none. Where the underlying type's values are compared by content, an enumerator is a distinct value of that content carrying its enum, so two enums may name the same content, and `enum A { X = 0 }` and `enum B { Y = 0 }` declare different values that this rule does not reach.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The rule above is stated over values rather than over the form of an initializer. An initializer that constructs its value cannot conflict, but one that calls a function to construct it is equally sound, and no syntactic test separates the two. A declaration may therefore claim a value the program obtained elsewhere, including a shared or built-in one, and the effect of doing so is that <emu-xref href="#sec-runtimetypeof"></emu-xref> reports the enum for that value.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>An enum declaration introduces a ~nominal~ type whose values are its enumerators. The type after `:` is the enum's <dfn id="underlying-type">underlying type</dfn>, and an enum declared without one has the underlying type `int32`. Any type may be an underlying type, including a function type and `symbol`.</p>

      <p>The first enumerator, when it has no initializer, takes 0, and it is a type error when the underlying type is not numeric, since a non-numeric enumeration must define its starting value. A later enumerator with no initializer takes the result of applying the underlying type's prefix increment operator `operator++` to the previous enumerator's value, with the previous enumerator itself unmodified; where the underlying type declares no prefix increment, it takes a value equal to the previous one. An enumerator initialized with a function of two parameters is given the result of calling that function with the enumerator's index and its name as a String, and a following enumerator with no initializer is given the result of calling the most recently given such function with its own index and name, until an initializer replaces it; an initializer that is not such a function sets its enumerator's value without disturbing the function for those after it. Such a function is not a closure.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The increment above is the underlying type's own, not an operation on numbers. A `bigint` enumeration counts by `1n`; an enumeration over a class whose declaration provides `operator++` counts by calling it, with the previous enumerator unmodified; and a type declaring no prefix increment repeats, which is what gives `B` the value of `A` in `enum S: string { A = "x", B }`.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>An enum type is a subtype of its underlying type, so a value of an enum type is usable wherever the underlying type is required and no conversion is written. The reverse is explicit: calling the enum type with a value of the underlying type returns the enumerator whose value it is, and throws a *TypeError* when it is not one of them.</p>

      <p>A `switch` whose discriminant is a value of an enum type is checked at compile time. Each case label must be an enumerator of that enum, and it is a type error for a label that is not one; and where the `switch` has no `default`, it is a type error unless it has a case for every enumerator. This is the enum row of the narrowing and exhaustiveness rules (<emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref>), the same closed-set reasoning a sealed hierarchy uses (<emu-xref href="#sec-sealed-classes"></emu-xref>): the enumerators are a fixed set the declaration owns, so covering them is a claim the checker can prove.</p>

      <p>An enum's Type Object has <dfn id="enum-prototype">%Enum.prototype%</dfn> as its prototype, which has %Type.prototype% as its own, so an enum carries everything a Type Object carries and an enumeration surface besides. That surface is: `toString`, which takes a VALUE and returns the key of the enumerator having it, or *undefined* where no enumerator has it; `keys`, `values`, and `entries`, each returning an Array Iterator over the enumerators in declaration order, of the keys, of the values, and of two-element key-and-value Arrays respectively; and `@@iterator`, which is `entries`. An enum's Type Object also has an own property for each enumerator's POSITION, so an enumeration is indexed by position beside being indexed by name.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`toString` takes the value as an argument rather than being a method on an enumerator, because an enumerator IS a value of the underlying type: the one-way subtype rule above is what makes `comp / 32` read directly, and it leaves an enumerator with no method of its own to override. String interpolation therefore sees the underlying value, and obtaining the key is what `toString` is for.</p>
        <p>`@@iterator` is `entries` rather than `values`, the choice Map makes and the opposite of the one Array and Set make, and for their reason: what an enumeration holds is named values, so yielding a bare value discards the name that distinguishes an enum from its underlying type. Indexing by position rather than by underlying value is the counterpart choice - a lookup BY VALUE is the reverse conversion and is already spelled `Count(_n_)`, so an index that repeated it would leave the enumeration with no way to be read in order. `forEach`, `filter`, and `map` are deliberately absent: `entries` composes with the Array methods to give all three, and this specification has declined composable surface before.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The one-way rule is the rule of C#, Rust, and Swift. It is what lets a bitmask index such as `comp / 32` read directly while `Component(id)` remains a deliberate and validated step, and it is why an enum can be used for arithmetic, indexing, and comparison without a cast.</p>
        <p>An enum's subtyping is a further case of the ~primitive~ and ~nominal~ rules in <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>, which follow a declared class hierarchy and a metadata parameterization but do not reach an enum's underlying type. It is stated here rather than there because it is the same shape of relation, a type that refines another and is subsumed into it, and the specification should ultimately give the three one rule rather than three.</p>
        <p>`Reflect.typeOf(Count.Zero)` reports `Count`, by the rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-typeof"></emu-xref> that a value's runtime type is the most specific type of which it is a value. This does not make the enumerator anything other than a value the underlying type also accepts: membership in `int32` follows from `Count` being a subtype of it, not from a second runtime type, so the uniqueness <emu-xref href="#sec-runtimetypeof"></emu-xref> requires is preserved and no conversion is performed. The case is exactly parallel to a metadata parameterization, where `Reflect.typeOf` of a `Meter` is `float32.&lt;{ m: 1 }&gt;` and not `float32`, while `m instanceof float32` is nonetheless *true*.</p>
        <p>What remains is that this specification now has three relations of one shape: a metadata parameterization refining its primitive, an enum refining its underlying type, and a class extending its base. Each is a case of the refinement relation that <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref> now names once, and the design would be better stating the enum case as subtyping rather than as an implicit conversion, which is what it is: describing it as a conversion is what makes it look like a fifth exception to <emu-xref href="#table-implicit-conversions"></emu-xref> when it is not an exception at all.</p>
        <p>That an enumerator reports its enum is a requirement on the VALUE and not only on the type: an implementation must be able to recover the enum from the value. Where the underlying type's values are compared by content, an enumerator may be a distinct value carrying its enum. Where they carry their own identity, the enumerator is the value itself and the enum is recovered from it by other means, which is what the at-most-one rule above makes possible.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-sealed-classes">
      <h1>Sealed Classes</h1>
      <p>A class declared `sealed` may not be extended outside the module that declares it, so its subclasses are a fixed and known set. A sealed class is a reference type rather than a value type, so a field of a sealed type holds an instance of any of its subclasses directly rather than through a nullable union.</p>

      <p>A `sealed abstract` class can be neither instantiated nor extended outside its module. Its concrete subclasses are therefore a closed set with no case among them for the base, and a `switch` over a value of its type is exhaustive when it has a case for each subclass.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is the algebraic data type, assembled from parts the proposal has for other reasons rather than from a new construct. The cases are the subclasses, the case labels are their type objects, which are values because types are values, and the match is the `switch` the control structures section already defines. What makes it work is that the set is closed: exhaustiveness is a claim about a hierarchy no other module can extend, which is why it is reserved to a sealed class and an enum and is deliberately not available for a union of literal types, whose members are not a declaration anyone owns.</p>
        <p>That a sealed class is a reference type is what lets a field hold a subclass instance polymorphically. A value type class cannot, since its field embeds the base slice of the layout and has no room for a subclass, which is why the two forms differ and why holding a value type class polymorphically is spelled `T | null`.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-partial-classes">
      <h1>Partial Classes</h1>
      <p>A class declared `partial` re-opens a class that is already declared, whether earlier in the same program, in another module, or as an intrinsic, and adds methods and operators to it. A `partial` declaration names an existing class; it declares no binding of its own, and its members are merged into the named class so that its instances and the class itself gain them. An instance method is added to the class's prototype, a static method to the constructor, and a named operator registers in the class's operator table, each as a fresh declaration of the member would place it.</p>
      <p>The `partial` keyword is required to extend a class this way. A class declaration whose name is already declared, and which is not `partial`, is an error, so a program cannot fork a class's behaviour by an accidental re-declaration. It is a type error if a `partial` declaration names a value that is not a class.</p>
      <p>A `partial` declaration adds behaviour and no cases: it introduces no subclass and no instance state, so it does not enlarge the closed set of a sealed hierarchy and does not change a class's layout. Extending a class with a `partial` declaration is therefore available from any module, including on a `sealed` class, which is the difference between it and a mixin: applying a mixin to a sealed class from outside its declaring module would create a subclass and is refused (<emu-xref href="#sec-sealed-classes"></emu-xref>), while a `partial` extension adds no subclass and is allowed. A `partial` declaration over a CLASS does not re-open the constructor or add fields; it is a channel for methods and operators. A `partial` declaration over an INTERFACE may add members, and adds no instance state by doing so, since an interface declares a shape and constructs nothing: the three reasons above - no subclass, no instance state, no change to a layout - all hold of it, which is why the restriction a class needs an interface does not. A member already declared on the interface is a *TypeError* rather than an override, so the meaning of an interface does not depend on the order its declarations load.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The metadata channel of the decorators and metadata extension is the principal use: a program adds typed Symbol-keyed members to an intrinsic metadata interface by declaring a `partial interface` over it, which is how a type's reflected metadata is extended without owning the declaration that carries it.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-reflection">
    <h1>Reflection</h1>
    <p>This clause defines what a program observes of a type at run time: the operation that reports a value's type, the opacity of a Type Object, the operation that exposes a type's structure, and the effect of a declared type on the existing reflective operations.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reflect-typeof">
      <h1>Reflect.typeOf ( _value_ )</h1>
      <p>This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Return GetTypeObject(RuntimeTypeOf(_value_)).
      </emu-alg>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`Reflect.typeOf` reports the most specific type of which the value is a value, not every type it belongs to. For a `uint8` binding it returns `uint8`; for a value of the metadata-parameterized `Meter` it returns `float32.&lt;{ m: 1 }&gt;`, the full parameterization, which is how reflection obtains constraint information at run time. Membership in a less specific type is not a second runtime type but a consequence of subtyping, which is why `m instanceof float32` is *true* while `Reflect.typeOf(m)` is not `float32`.</p>
        <p>It is the companion of `typeof`, which is unchanged and reports the underlying language type: `typeof a` is *"number"* for a `uint8` binding while `Reflect.typeOf(a)` is `uint8`. `typeof` on a Type Object is *"object"*, since a Type Object is an Object, and `typeof uint8 === "object"` therefore doubles as feature detection for this proposal.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-type-object-opacity">
      <h1>The Opacity of a Type Object</h1>
      <p>A Type Object exposes no property that reveals the structure of the type it denotes. Given the Type Object for `Player | null` no property yields its members, and given the one for `[].&lt;uint32&gt;` no property yields its element type or its extent. A Type Object's [[TypeRecord]] is an internal slot and is reachable only through <emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-getreflection"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <p>One exception is display: a Type Object has a `toString` method returning the canonical source form of the type it denotes, so calling `String` on the Type Object for `'a' | 'b'` yields the String *"'a' | 'b'"*. The printed form exists for diagnostics and authored error messages, not for inspection: its format follows the canonical form and is therefore stable, but parsing it is not the supported way to read a type's structure, which remains <emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-getreflection"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <p>A Type Object is callable: it has a [[Call]] internal method, so a type may be applied to an argument as `T(v)`. For a plain type the call is the explicit conversion of <emu-xref href="#sec-conversions"></emu-xref>, the same operation as `v := T`. For an enum type the call is the reverse conversion of <emu-xref href="#sec-enums"></emu-xref>, returning the enumerator whose underlying value the argument is. This does not make a Type Object a function to `typeof`, which reports *"object"* for it (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-typeof"></emu-xref>); the callability is the conversion surface, not a change of kind. Callability does not affect a Type Object's identity, so the interning of <emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref> is unchanged and two mentions of one type remain one callable object.</p>
      <p><ins>A Type Object is any object carrying [[TypeRecord]], and that is the whole of the test: an array type object carries one, so `[].&lt;uint8&gt;` reflects as any other type does. A CLASS is the exception, and deliberately so - the design says a class's type object is its constructor, and a constructor is a function whose `typeof` ECMA-262 fixes as *"function"*. Giving it [[TypeRecord]] would make it report *"object"* by the rule above, so a class denotes its type through an ASSOCIATION from the constructor to its Type Object, which the reflection operations resolve. A class is therefore the one denotation where the record is reached rather than carried.</ins></p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Opacity is what lets the interning of <emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref> be an implementation strategy rather than an obligation to materialize a graph. An engine that represents `[].&lt;uint32&gt;` as a tag rather than as an object with an `element` property is conforming, and a program cannot tell. It also keeps the type-object surface from becoming a second, weaker reflection API alongside the real one: there is one way to inspect a type, and it is named.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reflect-getreflection">
      <h1>Structural Reflection</h1>
      <p>`Reflect.getReflection.&lt;Reflect.Type&gt;(_t_)` returns a reflection node describing the structure of the type _t_ denotes. The node has a `kind` property whose value is a String naming the [[Kind]] of _t_'s Type Record, and the further properties of <emu-xref href="#table-reflection-nodes"></emu-xref>. Every property of a node that denotes a type holds a Type Object, so a walker recurses by reflecting it in turn.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-reflection-nodes" caption="Reflection Nodes by Kind">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>`kind`</th><th>Further properties</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>*"primitive"*</td><td>`type`, the Type Object itself; `generic`, either *undefined* or a record with `base` (a Type Object) and `arguments` (a List of Type Objects and values), the decomposition of a generic instantiation. This one node covers every named leaf: the built-in primitives, classes, interfaces, enums, and the `any`, `void`, and `type` types. It also carries `family`, the String the Type Object's own `family` reports (<emu-xref href="#table-type-families"></emu-xref>), so a walker holding a node need not return to the Type Object to ask which of those a leaf is.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*"literal"*</td><td>`value`, a String, Number, Boolean, BigInt, or Symbol; `base`, a Type Object.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*"parameterized"*</td><td>`base`, a Type Object; `metadata`, an object: the metadata half of a parameterization such as `float32.&lt;{ m: 1 }&gt;`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*"union"*</td><td>`arms`, a List of Type Objects, in the canonical order; empty for the `never` type.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*"intersection"*</td><td>`members`, a List of Type Objects, in the canonical order.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*"tuple"*</td><td>`elements`, a List of records with `type` (a Type Object), `rest`, and `initial`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*"array"*</td><td>`element`, a Type Object; `extent`, a non-negative integer or *undefined* for a dynamic length.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*"object"*</td><td>`properties`, a List of records with `name` (a String or Symbol), `type` (a Type Object), `optional`, `readonly`, `initial`, and `origin` (<emu-xref href="#sec-provenance"></emu-xref>); `indexSignatures`, a List of records with `key` and `value` (Type Objects).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*"function"*</td><td>`signatures`, in declaration order, each a record with `parameters` (records with `type`, `name`, `index`, `optional`, `rest`, `initial`, and `metadata`), `return` (a record with `type` and `metadata`), `thisType` (a Type Object or *undefined*), and `narrows` (a List of records with `target`, a String or the String *"this"*, and `type`, a Type Object). The `metadata` properties are always *undefined* here and are populated only where the same shape reflects a declaration (<emu-xref href="#sec-decorators"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*"reference"*</td><td>`target`, a Type Object: the `ref` borrow type of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-expressions"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>*"cycle"*</td><td>`name`, a String: the reader-facing back-edge at which a recursive type closes. It appears in reflection output only and is not accepted by construction.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The node kinds and the Type Record kinds of <emu-xref href="#table-type-record-kinds"></emu-xref> correspond, and the correspondence now round-trips: every kind is readable, and writable through <emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-maketype"></emu-xref>, with two deliberate wrinkles. The named leaves, ~primitive~ and ~nominal~ alike and with them `any`, `void`, and `type`, share the one *"primitive"* node, whose payload is the interned Type Object itself with a generic instantiation additionally decomposed, so a leaf is opaque to a walker exactly as <emu-xref href="#sec-type-object-opacity"></emu-xref> requires and a nominal type never exposes its declaration. And the ~application~ kind has no node at all, because specialization evaluates every deferred application before any code runs, so reflection cannot encounter one.</p>
        <p>The same `Reflect.getReflection` reflects a declaration's members, keyed on the declaration rather than on a type, which is how a class's fields, a function's parameters, and an enum's enumerators are exposed. That context and the rest of the reflection facility, including its metadata, are defined by <emu-xref href="#sec-decorators"></emu-xref>. This clause defines only the type-object context it operates on.</p>
        <p>A union's arms are reflected in the canonical order rather than in the order written, since canonicalization has already reordered them and the Type Object is shared with every other spelling of the same type. A function's signatures are reflected in declaration order, which <emu-xref href="#sec-samefunctiontype"></emu-xref> preserves for this reason.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reflect-maketype">
      <h1>Reflect.makeType ( _node_ )</h1>
      <p>The input is structural: any object matching a node's shape in <emu-xref href="#table-reflection-nodes"></emu-xref> describes that node, and a Type Object anywhere within it contributes its [[TypeRecord]]. This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. If _node_ is not an Object matching a node's shape in <emu-xref href="#table-reflection-nodes"></emu-xref>, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Let _record_ be the Type Record that _node_, read as a description, describes.
        1. If _record_ is not a valid type, throw a *TypeError* exception with the message the equivalent source declaration produces, or, where no declaration can write the type, a message naming the rule it violates.
        1. Return GetTypeObject(_record_).
      </emu-alg>
      <p>For every type _T_, `Reflect.makeType(Reflect.getReflection.&lt;Reflect.Type&gt;(`_T_`))` is _T_. Construction canonicalizes and interns by <emu-xref href="#sec-canonicalizetype"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref>, the same operations that give a source-written type its identity, which is what makes the round trip the identity function rather than an equivalence.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Canonicalization is where invalidity is caught: a node describing an infinite value-type layout, an intersection of distinct value types, or an index signature with an illegal key type fails exactly as the equivalent declaration fails, with the same message, so a builder's misuse reads as the misuse it is. Validity is not syntactic reachability: a symbol literal type is a valid type with no declaration form at all, which is why it is constructed rather than written; an index signature has the declaration form of <emu-xref href="#sec-object-types"></emu-xref>, and the declaration and a builder's construction reach the same Record. A *"cycle"* node is not accepted, since it is reflection output; a constructed cycle arises only from the fixpoint of <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluatebuildercall"></emu-xref>. The function is compile-time evaluable, and at run time it is an ordinary function returning the same interned objects, so `String(Reflect.makeType(&hellip;))` prints the canonical form of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-object-opacity"></emu-xref>.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reflect-isassignable">
      <h1>Reflect.isAssignable ( _source_, _target_ )</h1>
      <p>This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. If _source_ is not a Type Object or _target_ is not a Type Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Return IsAssignable(_source_.[[TypeRecord]], _target_.[[TypeRecord]]).
      </emu-alg>
      <emu-note>
        <p>This is the checker's own judgment (<emu-xref href="#sec-isassignable"></emu-xref>) exposed unchanged, so what a builder branches on and what the checker enforces cannot disagree; the conditional types of an erased system are written `Reflect.isAssignable(T, U) ? X : Y` inside a builder. It is deliberately the assignability relation rather than a new one: mutual assignability is composed from it, and identity is `===` by interning. The function is compile-time evaluable.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reflect-never">
      <h1>Reflect.never</h1>
      <p>The initial value of `Reflect.never` is the Type Object of the `never` type (<emu-xref href="#sec-never-type"></emu-xref>). This property has the attributes { [[Writable]]: *false*, [[Enumerable]]: *false*, [[Configurable]]: *false* }.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>A name for the empty union, so that code need not spell it as a construction; `Reflect.makeType({ kind: 'union', arms: [] })` returns the same object.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-and-declared-types">
      <h1>Reflection and Declared Types</h1>
      <p>The existing reflective operations respect a declared type. `Reflect.set` on a property with a declared type _t_ performs RequireType(_value_, _t_) and throws where the value does not satisfy it. `Reflect.get` returns the property's value, which is of its declared type. `Reflect.defineProperty` accepts a `type` key in the property descriptor, whose value is a Type Object, and a descriptor with a `type` and no `value` gives the property the default value of that type (<emu-xref href="#sec-defaultvalueof"></emu-xref>), taken in the realm of that Type Object rather than in the caller's, since a parameterization's default is a crossing and a crossing consults a realm's declarations. `Reflect.deleteProperty` on a property with a declared type, and on a member required by an implemented interface, throws a *TypeError* exception.</p>

      <p>A Proxy constructed with a type argument _T_ has a [[RuntimeType]] internal slot whose value is _T_'s Type Record, so `Reflect.typeOf` reports _T_ rather than the shape of its target, and its traps are checked against _T_. A Proxy constructed without one has no such slot and is of the `any` type. Each trap result for a property with a declared type, whether the type is declared by _T_ or carried by the target as a typed own property, is checked against that type at the trap boundary. Constructing a Proxy whose target is an instance of a typed class or a typed array throws a *TypeError* exception: such a value is backed by a layout rather than a property table, a field access does not consult a handler, and there is no point at which a trap could correctly run.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Reflection is a boundary in the sense of <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>, not an exception to it. A value entering a typed position through `Reflect.set` is checked exactly as one entering through an assignment, which is what keeps a typed field's type a fact about its storage: were reflection able to write past the check, an engine could not lay the field out on the strength of its type, and the proposal's premise would not hold. That a typed property cannot be deleted is the same guarantee in the other direction, since a layout with a hole in it is not the layout the type described.</p>
        <p>A Proxy reporting the type it was constructed with rather than its target's shape is what makes the trap checks meaningful: the contract a proxy enforces is the one it was given, and reporting anything else would describe a value the program never promised.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-weak-references-and-typed-objects">
      <h1>Weak References and Typed Objects</h1>
      <p>An instance of a typed class cannot be held weakly. Passing one to the WeakRef constructor, using one as a key of a WeakMap or a member of a WeakSet, or registering one as the target of a FinalizationRegistry throws a *TypeError* exception. This is CanBeHeldWeakly returning false for such a value: a value type has no identity (<emu-xref href="#sec-value-types"></emu-xref>), so there is no object whose liveness a weak reference could observe, and no cell a weak collection could key on. The value rejected here is the same one that cannot be the target of a Proxy (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-and-declared-types"></emu-xref>): the instance of a typed class that seals its instances, a value backed by a layout whose identity its type, not the reference, governs.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>A reference type, an ordinary object or a class that carries no typed field and is therefore not sealed, is held weakly as before, since it has the identity a weak reference is about. A class becomes ineligible exactly when it becomes a typed, sealed class, the same point at which it becomes ineligible to be proxied, so the two restrictions have one cause and one boundary. A composite object is refused in the same positions for the mirror-image reason, an identity that coincides with contents rather than none at all; <emu-xref href="#sec-composite-modifications"></emu-xref> states the exclusion and merges both into CanBeHeldWeakly.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-numeric-library">
    <h1>The Numeric Library</h1>
    <p>This clause defines the library the numeric types need: the overloading of the existing functions over those types, the named arithmetic forms that check or saturate rather than wrap, the floored division pair, the leading-zero count at the argument's own width, the parsing of a string into a numeric type, and the predicates that ask a numeric value about itself.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-overloading-of-the-standard-library">
      <h1>Overloading of the Existing Functions</h1>
      <p>The functions of the Math object, and the other library functions that TAKE a value of a numeric type, are overloaded for the numeric types by <emu-xref href="#sec-overload-resolution"></emu-xref>. A function that merely returns a number, with no parameter whose type could select a signature, is not overloaded and is unchanged. <emu-xref href="#table-numeric-library-signatures"></emu-xref> lists the signatures: which functions, over which families, with what return, so that a call selects a signature as any overloaded call does and no conversion is written where the arguments already have a signature's types.</p>

      <p>Separately from the listing, a library function that takes a Number as a QUANTITY - a length, a count, an index, a position, a width of indentation - accepts a value of any numeric type in that position and reads it as its value. Such a parameter is not overloaded: there is one behaviour and one result, and the type of the argument selects nothing. The rule is stated because the alternative is silent: an operation that decides a branch by asking whether its argument is a Number takes the OTHER branch for a value of a numeric type, so `Array(3)` allocates three elements while a `Array(_t_)` for a `uint32` _t_ of 3 would build a one-element array holding _t_. What a quantity parameter must not do is change what it answers for an existing call, so a Number continues to be read as it is today.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is the reading side, and it does not create a conversion. An operation that REFUSES an implicit conversion from a Number refuses one from a value of a numeric type for the same reason and with the same error: ToBigInt throws a *TypeError* for a Number, and throws one for a `uint32` as well, the more so because no conversion between a sized integer type and `bigint` exists to be performed implicitly (<emu-xref href="#sec-conversions"></emu-xref>). The `BigInt` constructor's own Number step is unaffected and continues to answer for a Number; a program that wants a BigInt from a typed integer writes the conversion, once one exists.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>The listing ranges over the sized integer types `int.&lt;N&gt;` and `uint.&lt;N&gt;` for every _N_ the type system admits, over `bigint`, over the binary float types of <emu-xref href="#sec-binary-floating-point-types"></emu-xref>, over the decimal types of <emu-xref href="#sec-decimal-floating-point-types"></emu-xref>, and over the rational types of <emu-xref href="#sec-rational-types"></emu-xref>. Coverage is not conditioned on which types an implementation has values for: the `float128` and decimal rows are as normative as the rest. `number` is not in the set, because the existing signatures over the Number type are unchanged and are what an untyped call selects. The vector types are not in the set, their arithmetic belonging to the SIMD extension's operators, and the complex numbers extension declares the rows of its own family, noted below the table.</p>

      <p>Every signature takes all of its numeric parameters at one type _T_. There are no mixed signatures and no resolution at a common supertype, because no numeric value type is assignable to another (<emu-xref href="#sec-isassignable"></emu-xref>): a call mixing two typed widths is viable at no signature, and it is a type error, thrown as a *TypeError* exception where resolution happens at run time. The program states the conversion. A literal argument beside a typed one is ranked rather than typed and takes the parameter's type where it can represent it, so `Math.max((1 := uint8), 3)` selects the `uint8` signature and is a `uint8`, while `Math.max((1 := uint8), 300)` is a type error, 300 being representable at no signature the `uint8` argument leaves viable.</p>

      <p>A signature's declared return type is a boundary, and the boundary applies its family's own treatment of a result that does not fit. For an integer type, sized or `bigint`, the exact mathematical result is converted at _T_ by the checked treatment of <emu-xref href="#sec-requiretype"></emu-xref>: a representable result is a value of _T_ and an unrepresentable one throws a *RangeError* exception, so `Math.pow((2 := uint8), (10 := uint8))` and `Math.abs((-128 := int8))` throw, their results having no `uint8` or `int8` to be. For a binary float type the result is converted by the float rule of <emu-xref href="#table-numeric-conversions"></emu-xref>, rounded to the nearest value of _T_ with a finite result beyond the type's range becoming an infinity of its sign, which is the treatment the type's own arithmetic applies, so `Math.exp((100 := float32))` is the `float32` positive infinity rather than an error. For a decimal type, precision rounding is silent and a result outside the exponent range throws a *RangeError* exception. For a rational type the result is exact, and a fixed-width result whose lowest-terms numerator or denominator does not fit its `int.&lt;N&gt;` throws a *RangeError* exception. Two domain errors are raised before any result exists: `Math.sqrt` of a negative value of an integer type throws a *RangeError* exception, and `Math.pow` over an integer type throws a *RangeError* exception when its exponent is negative, whatever its base.</p>

      <p>The semantics are stated on mathematical values. Where a row's result is exact, an implementation must produce it exactly at every width; an integer row computed through a binary64 intermediate that returns a wrong integer carrying the type does not conform. Where a row is implementation-approximated, the approximation is computed as if at the argument type's format or better and rounded once to that format. `Math.sqrt` is correctly rounded at every binary float and decimal width, as IEEE 754-2019 requires of the operation; the other approximated rows carry the latitude the Math functions have always had.</p>

      <p>NaN and the infinities are values of every binary float type and of every decimal type, and a NaN or infinite result at those types is a value of _T_, not an error: `Math.log((-1 := float32))` is the `float32` NaN. The integer and rational families have no such values, which is why their domain cases above throw instead. The existing conventions of the functions, among them the NaN and signed-zero handling of `min` and `max` and `Math.round` rounding half up, are unchanged and are evaluated at _T_.</p>

      <p>A family has a row for a function exactly where the operation has an answer in that family's own mathematics, with the return type leaving nothing to decide about the answer's representation. The integer roots are such answers: for an integer _T_, `Math.sqrt` and `Math.cbrt` return the exact root truncated toward zero as a value of _T_, the direction integer division truncates (<emu-xref href="#sec-integer-operations"></emu-xref>) and the semantics the BigInt Math proposal gives `BigInt.sqrt` and `BigInt.cbrt`. The transcendental functions have no integer counterpart, so they have no integer rows, and an integer-typed argument to one fails resolution; the program writes the conversion it means.</p>

      <p>The listing declares one signature per parameter list: no function of the numeric library is overloaded on its return type alone, so a bare call is never ambiguous (<emu-xref href="#sec-overloading-on-return-type"></emu-xref> remains available to operators and to user code, where a call without a contextual type is legitimately an error). A contextual type participates in resolution as it does anywhere. Where a call's numeric arguments are untyped literals, the contextual type selects a typed signature and the literals take its parameter type, so the call is a computation at _T_ rather than a conversion of a Number result: `const a: uint8 = Math.pow(2, 10);` resolves at `uint8`, computes the exact 1024, and throws a *RangeError* exception at the return, while `uint8(Math.pow(2, 10))` is the untyped call followed by a wrapping cast and is 0, the distinction of <emu-xref href="#table-out-of-range-treatment"></emu-xref> visible at one call site. Where an argument is typed and the contextual type is of another family, no signature is viable and it is a type error; the cast the program then writes selects the computation by its placement, `float64(Math.sqrt(10n))` being the integer root converted, exactly 3, and `Math.sqrt(float64(10n))` the real root of the converted argument. A cast constrains nothing here: a Type Object call accepts any numeric source, so it supplies no contextual type and a call within it resolves bare.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-numeric-library-signatures" caption="The Numeric Library Signatures">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Functions</th><th>Integer types</th><th>`bigint`</th><th>Binary float types</th><th>Decimal types</th><th>Rational types</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`abs`, `sign`</td><td>A value of _T_; `abs` of the most negative value of a signed type throws a *RangeError* exception</td><td>A value of the type</td><td>A value of _T_</td><td>A value of _T_</td><td>A value of the type; `abs` throws where the magnitude's numerator does not fit its `int.&lt;N&gt;`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`min`, `max`</td><td>A value of _T_</td><td>A value of the type</td><td>A value of _T_</td><td>A value of _T_</td><td>A value of the type</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`floor`, `ceil`, `round`, `trunc`</td><td>The argument</td><td>The argument</td><td>A value of _T_, exact</td><td>A value of _T_, evaluated exactly; the result's cohort member follows the roundToIntegral rule of IEEE 754-2019</td><td>The nearest `int.&lt;N&gt;` in the function's direction, checked at `int.&lt;N&gt;`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`sqrt`, `cbrt`</td><td>The integer root, truncated toward zero, a value of _T_; `sqrt` of a negative value throws a *RangeError* exception</td><td>The integer root, truncated toward zero; `sqrt` of a negative value throws a *RangeError* exception</td><td>A value of _T_; `sqrt` correctly rounded, `cbrt` implementation-approximated</td><td>A value of _T_; `sqrt` correctly rounded, `cbrt` implementation-approximated</td><td>No signature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`exp`, `expm1`, `log`, `log1p`, `log2`, `log10`, `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `asin`, `acos`, `atan`, `sinh`, `cosh`, `tanh`, `asinh`, `acosh`, `atanh`</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td><td>A value of _T_, implementation-approximated</td><td>A value of _T_, implementation-approximated</td><td>No signature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`atan2`, `hypot`</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td><td>A value of _T_, all arguments of one _T_, implementation-approximated</td><td>A value of _T_, all arguments of one _T_, implementation-approximated</td><td>No signature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`pow`</td><td>The exact power, checked at _T_; a negative exponent throws a *RangeError* exception</td><td>BigInt::exponentiate; a negative exponent throws a *RangeError* exception</td><td>A value of _T_, implementation-approximated</td><td>A value of _T_, implementation-approximated</td><td>No signature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`clz32`</td><td>For _N_ &le; 32: 32 less the bit length of the mathematical value taken modulo 2**32, checked at _T_</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`imul`</td><td>For _N_ &le; 32: the product's low 32 bits as an `int32`, whatever _T_ its arguments carry</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`fround`, `f16round`</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td><td>The value rounded through binary32 or binary16, a value of _T_</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`sumPrecise`</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td><td>For an iterable of values of one _T_: the exact sum, rounded once to _T_</td><td>No signature</td><td>No signature</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>The complex numbers extension declares the rows of its family beside its arithmetic: `Math.abs` of a `complex.&lt;T&gt;` is the real magnitude, a value of _T_; the transcendental functions the extension names return values of its types; and `Math.conj` and `Math.arg` are its additions. `Math.random` takes no numeric argument, and its typed form `Math.random.&lt;T&gt;()` belongs to the random extension. The value properties of the Math object, `Math.PI` among them, remain values of the Number type.</p>

      <p>The Static Type of a member access reading the `length` property of an array is the <emu-xref href="#index-type">index type</emu-xref> (<emu-xref href="#sec-static-type-of-an-expression"></emu-xref>), as is the Static Type of a member access reading `capacity`. The value READ is a value of that type and not a Number, so `a.length is `<emu-xref href="#index-type">index type</emu-xref> is *true* where the array has an element type, and a count therefore carries its width into whatever it is used in. An array with NO element type is untouched: its `length` is a Number as it has always been.</p>

      <p>The Static Type of a COMPUTED member access on an array &mdash; `a[`_i_`]` &mdash; is the array's element type. It had been the `any` type, which left indexing untyped in both directions of the check: a store to an element was checked against the element type and a read from one was not, so `let b: boolean = a[0]` was accepted on an array of `uint32`. Indexing is the operation an array exists for, and it is checked in the same way whichever side of an assignment it appears on.</p>

      <p>Where the array has a fixed extent and the index is a numeric literal, the index is decidable before the program runs: an index that is negative, not an integer, or not less than the extent is a type error rather than the *RangeError* it would raise at run time. An index that is not a literal keeps the run-time check, which remains the rule for every index; the static case is the one an implementation may elide, the extent being a compile-time constant (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-and-tuple-types"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The bound on which functions are overloaded is a compatibility rule, and it is the same one that leaves `parseInt` and `parseFloat` alone (<emu-xref href="#sec-parsing"></emu-xref>) and that decides the `bigint` predicate rows (<emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-predicates"></emu-xref>). An overload is selected by an argument's type, so a call written without one is unchanged; but a function with no numeric parameter has nothing to select on, and giving its result a value type would change what every existing call returns. `'A'.charCodeAt(0) === 65` is *true* today, and a `uint16` result would make it *false*, since the values of distinct value types are distinct (<emu-xref href="#sec-value-types"></emu-xref>). The same reasoning covers `indexOf` and `lastIndexOf`, whose `=== -1` is the universal idiom, the Date getters, and the byte lengths. Where a typed result is wanted from such a function, the program writes a conversion.</p>
        <p>This is what makes the conversion rule livable rather than merely strict. `Math.clz32` of a `uint32` is a `uint32`, `Math.min` of two `float32` values is a `float32`, and a `uint32` loop counter compares against `array.length` without a cast, because the library meets the program where it is. A library that returned a Number from every function would force a conversion at every call and would make typed code read worse than untyped code, which is the failure mode the design is avoiding.</p>
        <p>The existing signatures are unchanged, so `Math.min` of two Numbers is a Number and every existing program means what it meant. The overloads are additions, and a call with untyped literal arguments and no contextual type selects among them by <emu-xref href="#table-literal-ranking"></emu-xref>, which ranks `number` first and so keeps an untyped call on the signature it selects today.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The `bigint` rows are those of the BigInt Math proposal: `abs`, `sign`, `min`, `max`, `pow`, `sqrt`, and `cbrt`, the roots truncated toward zero, `sqrt` of a negative throwing, and `pow` refusing a negative exponent. That proposal places the operations on the BigInt constructor rather than on Math, because in the base language a `Math.sqrt` whose result type depends on a runtime argument is a hazard every caller inherits. The objection does not transfer: here a signature is selected statically and the untyped surface is untouched, so the listing stays on Math, agreeing with the proposal value for value, and if the proposal advances, `BigInt.sqrt(x)` and `Math.sqrt` of a `bigint` are one mathematics with two spellings.</p>
        <p>The integer roots follow the shape integer division set. The result type leaves nothing to decide, so the type decides, and toward zero is the direction this specification's integer arithmetic already rounds: `Math.sqrt((17 := uint8))` is `(4 := uint8)`, and `Math.cbrt` of a negative integer value is defined, the cube root of -9 truncating to -2. A program that wants the real root of an integer writes the conversion, one cast that states the meaning.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`Math.pow` and `**` deliberately part ways at an integer type. `(2 := uint8) ** (10 := uint8)` wraps to 0, because the operators wrap (<emu-xref href="#sec-integer-operations"></emu-xref>); `Math.pow((2 := uint8), (10 := uint8))` throws a *RangeError* exception, because a declared return is a checked boundary. This is the `a + 1` against `Math.addChecked(a, 1)` distinction of <emu-xref href="#sec-checked-and-saturating-arithmetic"></emu-xref> again, stated here so it reads as the design it is rather than as a disagreement: the operator is the cheap wrapping form and the function the checked one. What `**` itself does with a negative typed exponent is a question for <emu-xref href="#sec-integer-operations"></emu-xref>; this clause specifies the functions only.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-checked-and-saturating-arithmetic">
      <h1>Checked and Saturating Arithmetic</h1>
      <p>The operators wrap on overflow (<emu-xref href="#sec-integer-operations"></emu-xref>). Where wrapping is not wanted, the named forms of <emu-xref href="#table-checked-and-saturating"></emu-xref> are used. Each is overloaded for every integer type and returns a value of its operands' type.</p>

      <p>Each form performs the operation its corresponding operator performs, and differs from it only in what happens when the type cannot represent the answer. "The exact result" in <emu-xref href="#table-checked-and-saturating"></emu-xref> therefore means the exact result of THAT operation, not of an operation in the rationals: the division forms divide as `/` divides, truncating toward zero (<emu-xref href="#sec-integer-operations"></emu-xref>), so `Math.divChecked(_a_, 2)` on an `int32` holding 7 is 3 and not an error. A division is the only one of the four whose result can fail to be representable for a reason other than magnitude, and it does so in exactly one case, the most negative value of a signed type divided by -1.</p>

      <p>That the operators wrap while a CONVERSION does not is the one place these rules look as though they disagree, so it is worth stating why they do not. A conversion is a value crossing into a type that cannot hold it, which is a mistake about the value: a store past an element type's range, or a binding initialized out of range, yields ~unrepresentable~ (<emu-xref href="#table-numeric-conversions"></emu-xref>) and raises. An arithmetic overflow is not a mistake about a value but an operation the type performs modularly by definition, which is what a fixed-width integer IS and what every use of one for hashing, checksums, or bit manipulation depends on. The two therefore meet without contradicting: in `let z: uint8 = x + y` with `x` 200 and `y` 100 the addition wraps to 44 and the conversion then has nothing to refuse, because the wrap happened at the operator and the value reaching the binding is in range. Where that is not wanted, <emu-xref href="#table-checked-and-saturating"></emu-xref> names the forms that refuse or saturate instead.</p>

      <p>A zero divisor produces no result at all, so neither treatment applies to it: `Math.divChecked` and `Math.divSaturating` both throw a *RangeError* exception when the divisor is zero. Saturating is a rule about a result the type cannot hold, and there is no result here to be nearest to.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-checked-and-saturating" caption="Checked and Saturating Forms">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`Math.addChecked`, `Math.subChecked`, `Math.mulChecked`, `Math.divChecked`</td><td>The exact result where the type can represent it. Otherwise a *RangeError*. A zero divisor is a *RangeError*.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`Math.addSaturating`, `Math.subSaturating`, `Math.mulSaturating`, `Math.divSaturating`</td><td>The exact result where the type can represent it. Otherwise the nearest value of the type, which is its greatest value when the exact result exceeds it and its least when the exact result falls below it. A zero divisor is a *RangeError*.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Silent wrapping is the wrong answer when the value is a count rather than a bit pattern, and the right answer when it is a bit pattern rather than a count. Neither is right always, so the operator takes the case that has to be cheap and the named forms take the rest: `a + 1` on a `uint8` holding 255 is 0, `Math.addChecked(a, 1)` throws a *RangeError* exception, and `Math.addSaturating(a, 1)` is 255.</p>
        <p>These forms exist for the integer types because only there is wrapping the default. A float already saturates, to an infinity, and a decimal already raises a *RangeError*, since a decimal's range is a property of the type rather than of the format (<emu-xref href="#table-numeric-conversions"></emu-xref>). A rational already raises a *RangeError* on an unrepresentable result. Each family's overflow behaviour is therefore the one its values make available, and the named forms give the integer types the two the others have built in.</p>
        <p>That a saturating form can still raise is worth stating plainly, because the name suggests otherwise. It raises for one reason only, and it is not an overflow: a division by zero has no result, and saturating chooses among the results a type can hold rather than inventing one where none exists. Every case that IS an overflow saturates, including the signed division that overflows, so `Math.divSaturating` of the most negative `int8` by -1 is 127.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-floored-division">
      <h1>Floored Division</h1>
      <p>`Math.divFloor(_a_, _b_)` returns the exact quotient of _a_ and _b_ rounded toward negative infinity. `Math.mod(_a_, _b_)` returns the remainder whose sign follows the divisor. Both are overloaded for every integer type, and both also have a signature over the Number type.</p>

      <p>A zero divisor follows the family. At an integer type both throw a *RangeError* exception, since an integer type has no infinity and no NaN and so has nothing to return. Over the Number type both answer as the operators do: `Math.mod(_a_, 0)` is *NaN*, as `_a_ % 0` is, and `Math.divFloor(_a_, 0)` is an infinity, as `Math.floor(_a_ / 0)` is.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>These are the other half of the pair <emu-xref href="#sec-integer-operations"></emu-xref> describes: `Math.divFloor(_a_, _b_) * _b_ + Math.mod(_a_, _b_) = _a_` holds for the same reason the operators' identity holds, because the quotient and the remainder round the same way. They are named rather than assumed so that a program picks the pair it wants instead of receiving one by default.</p>
        <p>Unlike the checked and saturating forms, this pair is not restricted to the integer types. Those exist only there because only there is wrapping the default, and a checked form over the Number type would have nothing to check. This pair is not about overflow but about rounding direction, which is meaningful for any real, and its own motivating use is written in untyped code: `array[Math.mod(_i_, _array_.length)]`.</p>

        <p>`Math.mod` is the `%` of Python, the `mod` of Kotlin, and the `mod` of Haskell. For a positive divisor, which is every case in which an index is being wrapped, its result equals the Euclidean remainder and is never negative, so `array[Math.mod(i, array.length)]` is always in range. Where the divisor may be negative and a non-negative result is still wanted, `Math.mod(_a_, Math.abs(_b_))` gives it, which is why no separate Euclidean pair is provided.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-counting-leading-zeros">
      <h1>Counting Leading Zeros</h1>
      <p>`Math.clz` is overloaded for the sized integer types, and for them alone. For a value of an integer type _T_ of width _N_, it returns _N_ less the bit length of the argument's mathematical value taken modulo 2**_N_, as a value of _T_, checked at the return as <emu-xref href="#sec-overloading-of-the-standard-library"></emu-xref> checks every declared return. The operation is a question about an _N_-bit two's-complement encoding, so the families without one have no signatures: `bigint` has no width to count from the top of, and the float, decimal, and rational types have no integer encoding to count in. There is no untyped signature; `Math.clz32` is already the 32-bit count.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is `Math.clz32` freed from its width. The two agree at 32 bits, answering alike for every `int32` and `uint32`, and differ below it: `Math.clz((1 := uint8))` is 7 where `Math.clz32((1 := uint8))` is 31, because `clz32` counts in a 32-bit field whatever its argument's width and `clz` counts in the argument's own. Typed code that means the argument's width, which is most of it, writes `clz`; `clz32` remains for the 32-bit field itself and for untyped callers.</p>
        <p>The count of a value of _T_ always fits an unsigned _T_, and fits a signed _T_ at every width past `int.&lt;2&gt;`; at the two narrowest signed widths a zero's count exceeds the type's greatest value and the checked return throws a *RangeError* exception, the same edge the `clz32` row of <emu-xref href="#table-numeric-library-signatures"></emu-xref> notes. `bigint` is excluded because the operation is width-relative and a `bigint` has no width; the width-free counterpart is a bit length, which is a different function and is not added here.</p>
        <p>A call whose argument is an untyped literal resolves like any other, by <emu-xref href="#table-literal-ranking"></emu-xref>, which selects the widest width that can represent it. That is well defined and almost never meant, since the width is the semantics here, so a program writes the width it means, by a typed argument, by an annotation on the destination, or by a cast: `const n: uint8 = Math.clz(1);` is 7.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-parsing">
      <h1>Parsing</h1>
      <p>Each numeric type has a `parse` function. For an integer type and for `bigint` its signature is `parse(_string_, _radix_ = 10)`; for the binary floating-point, decimal, rational, and complex types it is `parse(_string_)`. It returns a value of the type the function belongs to.</p>

      <p>The accepted input is the grammar of a literal of that type, with optional leading and trailing white space and an optional sign. Numeric separators are accepted, and the form taking a radix accepts the prefix matching that radix. The entire string must be a literal of the type: no trailing text is consumed.</p>

      <p>`parse` throws a *SyntaxError* when the string is not a literal of the type, and a *RangeError* when it is a literal whose value the type cannot represent.</p>

      <p>Each type also has a `tryParse` function with the same parameters, returning a value of the type where `parse` would return one and *null* where `parse` would fail to parse its argument. Its return type is the union of the type and the null type. A call that is not a parse at all, because the receiver is not a numeric type, throws as `parse` does rather than answering *null*: that is a mistake in the program, and reporting it as a failed parse would name the wrong thing.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`parseInt` and `parseFloat` are unchanged and continue to consume a prefix and to return `NaN`. The new functions do neither, because neither is available to them: an integer type cannot represent `NaN` at all, so a failed parse has nothing to return and must throw. Doing the same for the float types is a choice rather than a necessity, and it is the right one, since a failed parse that becomes a `NaN` surfaces far from its cause. Refusing trailing text follows: a function that cannot signal failure with a value has no reason to be lenient about what it accepts.</p>
        <p>Distinguishing the two errors is what makes the failure legible. `uint8.parse('12abc')` throws a *SyntaxError* exception, since the string is not a `uint8` literal, while `uint8.parse('256')` throws a *RangeError* exception, since it is a literal and the value is out of range. This is the same distinction <emu-xref href="#table-out-of-range-treatment"></emu-xref> draws between a value of the wrong type and a value of the right type out of range.</p>
        <p>`tryParse` returning a nullable union rather than a sentinel is what lets the failure be handled by narrowing (<emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref>): the result is a `uint8` in the branch that tests it against *null* and needs no cast there. A `string` is deliberately not a conversion source for a numeric type, so `let c: uint8 = '1';` is a type error and the parse is always written; <emu-xref href="#sec-requiretype"></emu-xref> is where that is enforced, and it is enforced at the explicit conversion too, so `'1' := uint8` is a type error for the same reason. The pair is what makes the strictness livable: a program that knows its input writes `parse` and lets the failure throw, and one that does not writes `tryParse` and handles the *null*.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-numeric-predicates">
      <h1>The Numeric Predicates</h1>
      <p>The global functions `isFinite` and `isNaN`, and the functions `Number.isFinite`, `Number.isNaN`, `Number.isInteger`, and `Number.isSafeInteger`, are overloaded for the numeric types by <emu-xref href="#sec-overload-resolution"></emu-xref>. Each overload takes one value of a numeric type and returns a `boolean`, answering as <emu-xref href="#table-numeric-predicates"></emu-xref> gives for the value's family. The existing signatures are unchanged: an untyped call coerces, or declines to, exactly as it does today.</p>

      <p>The `bigint` family is the one exception to the table's uniformity, and it is a compatibility rule rather than a semantic one: an overload taking a `bigint` is declared only for a function that has no defined answer for a BigInt today. `isNaN` and `isFinite` perform ToNumber on their argument, which throws a *TypeError* exception for a BigInt, so each declares one. The four `Number` statics already answer *false* for a BigInt, because a BigInt is not a Number, so none of them declares one and a BigInt passed to a `Number` static reaches that static's existing signature and is answered exactly as it is today.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-numeric-predicates" caption="The Numeric Predicates">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Predicate</th><th>Integer types</th><th>`bigint`</th><th>Binary float types</th><th>Decimal types</th><th>Rational types</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`isNaN`, `Number.isNaN`</td><td>*false*</td><td>*false*, at `isNaN`; none declared for `Number.isNaN`, which answers *false* as it does today</td><td>*true* where the value is the NaN of _T_</td><td>*true* where the value is the NaN of _T_</td><td>*false*</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`isFinite`, `Number.isFinite`</td><td>*true*</td><td>*true*, at `isFinite`; none declared for `Number.isFinite`, which answers *false* as it does today</td><td>*true* where the value is finite</td><td>*true* where the value is finite</td><td>*true*</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`Number.isInteger`</td><td>*true*</td><td>none declared; `Number.isInteger` answers *false* as it does today</td><td>*true* where the mathematical value is integral</td><td>*true* where the numerical value is integral; `1.00` is integral</td><td>*true* where the denominator is 1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`Number.isSafeInteger`</td><td>*true* where the mathematical value's magnitude is at most 2**53 - 1; constant *true* for _N_ &le; 53</td><td>none declared; `Number.isSafeInteger` answers *false* as it does today</td><td>*true* where the mathematical value is integral with magnitude at most 2**53 - 1</td><td>*true* where the numerical value is integral with magnitude at most 2**53 - 1</td><td>*true* where the denominator is 1 and the magnitude is at most 2**53 - 1</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>The complex numbers extension declares its family's rows for all four predicates. The expected shape is the componentwise one: a complex value is NaN where either component is, and finite where both are.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The integer and rational columns are constants, and that is the point of them: a sized integer is a type whose `isFinite` has one answer, and the predicate saying so at the type is what lets a reader delete the check. The decimal columns are value tests even though the arithmetic of this specification never produces a decimal NaN or infinity, overflow raising a *RangeError* exception instead: the values exist in the type (<emu-xref href="#sec-decimal-floating-point-types"></emu-xref>), so the predicate tests the value rather than asserting a constant the type system does not guarantee.</p>
        <p>Every test is of the mathematical or format value, never of a representation. At a width past 2**53, `Number.isSafeInteger` of an `int64` holding 2**60 is *false* because the value is out of safe range, not because a payload lost precision.</p>
        <p>A plain BigInt value is a value of the `bigint` type, and it raises the one compatibility question in this table that the type system does not settle by itself. Every other row answers about a value that no existing program can write: a `float32` NaN and an `int32` need syntax this proposal adds, so giving them answers displaces nothing. A BigInt needs no new syntax at all. `Number.isInteger(5n)` is *false* in code running today, and making it *true* under the flag would change the meaning of a program that opted into nothing, which nothing else in this proposal does. The rows are therefore declared where they displace an error and declined where they displace an answer: `isNaN(1n)` and `isFinite(1n)` throw today, so the flag defines them, and the `Number` statics answer today, so the flag leaves them alone.</p>
        <p>The visible cost is that `isFinite(1n)` is *true* while `Number.isFinite(1n)` is *false*. The two functions differ in kind already, one coercing its argument and the other testing it, and this proposal closes only the case where the coercion threw; it does not make them agree, and it makes them disagree about no value that either answered before. `Number.isInteger` and `Number.isSafeInteger` have no global counterpart, so no surface takes a BigInt for those two questions at all. Their answers at the `bigint` type, were a surface for them to be declared on to exist, are *true* and *true* where the magnitude is at most 2**53 - 1; they are recorded here rather than in the table because a table of what the predicates answer should not carry an answer no call can obtain.</p>
        <p>The other rows repair a quieter hazard: without them, `Number.isNaN` of a `float32` NaN is *false* and `Number.isInteger` of an `int32` is *false*, each predicate silently testing representation where its name promises a value test.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-arrays">
    <h1>Arrays and Tuples</h1>
    <p>This clause completes the ~array~ and ~tuple~ kinds: the membership test <emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref> defers, the default value of a tuple, and the checking of an element store.</p>

    <p>A <dfn id="tuple-element-record">Tuple Element Record</dfn> describes one position of a ~tuple~ type. It has a [[Type]] (a Type Record), a [[Rest]] field (a Boolean, *true* for a `...` element), and an [[Initial]] field (an ECMAScript language value or ~none~, the position's declared default).</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-array-membership">
      <h1>Membership</h1>
      <p>For an ~array~ Type Record _t_, a value _v_ is of the type _t_ when _v_ is an Object, _v_'s length is _t_.[[Extent]] where that is not ~dynamic~, and every element of _v_ is of the type _t_.[[Element]].</p>

      <p>For a ~tuple~ Type Record _t_, a value _v_ is of the type _t_ when _v_ is an Object and SequenceAssignment (<emu-xref href="#sec-sequenceassignment"></emu-xref>) over _t_.[[Elements]], _v_'s length, and the closure reporting whether the value at an index is of an element's [[Type]] is not ~unmatched~, where an element's slot has the [[Rest]] of that element and is optional exactly when its [[Initial]] is not ~none~. Where _t_ has at most one element whose [[Rest]] is *true* this is the familiar rule: the length is at least the number of elements carrying neither a rest nor a default, at most the element count where no element is a rest and unbounded otherwise, and each value is of the type of the element that receives it.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A tuple's length is a range rather than a number because a trailing position may carry a default and a position may spread an array type. `[uint8, uint32 = 10]` accepts a one-element and a two-element array, which is what lets a shorter array satisfy a longer tuple return, and `[uint32, ...[].&lt;float32&gt;]` accepts any length from one upward.</p>
        <p>Checking every element is linear in the array's length, which is the cost the `any` boundary has for an array and does not have for a `uint8`. It falls only where an array of unknown type reaches a typed position; an array whose static type is already the target is not checked at all (<emu-xref href="#sec-check-elision"></emu-xref>), and a `[].&lt;uint8&gt;` passed to a `[].&lt;uint8&gt;` parameter costs nothing. Whether an implementation may instead check an element on each read, deferring the cost and spreading it, is the same question the ~object~ and ~function~ kinds raise, and it has the same answer here as there: the check as specified is eager, and a lazier one would need the wrapper the higher-order case would need.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-array-defaults-and-stores">
      <h1>Defaults and Stores</h1>
      <p>The default value of a ~tuple~ type is the array whose elements are, for each element of [[Elements]] whose [[Rest]] is *false*, its [[Initial]] where that is not ~none~ and the default value of its [[Type]] otherwise; a [[Rest]] element contributes no elements. It is ~none~ where an element has an [[Initial]] of ~none~ and a [[Type]] with no default.</p>

      <p>A store to an element of an array of element type _t_ checks the value against _t_ by <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>. `length` is of the <emu-xref href="#index-type">index type</emu-xref>. Deleting an element of a typed array throws a *TypeError* exception.</p>

      <p>A method of the array that takes or returns an ELEMENT takes or returns it at the element type. `includes`, `indexOf`, and `lastIndexOf` take their search element at _t_; `fill`, `push`, and `unshift` take the values they store at _t_; `at` returns _t_. A literal argument in one of those positions takes _t_ by <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref>, so `a.includes(65)` finds the element `65` of a `[].&lt;uint16&gt;` rather than comparing a `uint16` against a Number and failing. The same holds of a typed collection: the key and value positions of a `Map.&lt;K, V&gt;` and the element positions of a `Set.&lt;T&gt;` take their declared types, and so do those of a `WeakMap.&lt;K, V&gt;` and a `WeakSet.&lt;T&gt;`, whose key and element positions are constrained to the types a weak reference may hold and are checked against the declared type on top of that constraint. A method that STORES takes its value at the declared type however it is spelled, `getOrInsert` and `getOrInsertComputed` included, the latter checking the value its callback returns. A LOOKUP answers the value type or *undefined*, since a lookup that finds nothing answers *undefined*: the Static Type of `get` on a `Map.&lt;K, V&gt;` is _V_ | *undefined* rather than _V_, so a binding of type _V_ does not admit it without a test. An operation that RETURNS a collection carries an element type determined by where its elements can come from: `intersection` and `difference` on a `Set.&lt;T&gt;` draw only from the receiver and return a `Set.&lt;T&gt;` whatever the other operand holds, while `union` and `symmetricDifference` draw from both and return a `Set.&lt;T | U&gt;` for an other operand of `Set.&lt;U&gt;`. Where the other operand's element type is not known, the result's is not either, and the result carries none: a union with an unconstrained set is unconstrained, and answering `Set.&lt;T&gt;` would state more than the values support. `isSubsetOf`, `isSupersetOf`, and `isDisjointFrom` answer a Boolean.</p>

      <p>These are ordinary parameters of a declared type, so <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref> governs them and nothing further is needed: a literal that cannot take the element type is an Early Error, and a value of the `any` type is checked at the boundary. A search is not treated more leniently than a store. `a.includes(70000)` on a `[].&lt;uint16&gt;` is a type error rather than *false*, for the reason <emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref> gives for a narrowing whose test can never succeed: the answer is knowable from the types, so reporting it as an ordinary *false* would hide a mistake rather than answer a question. An UNTYPED array declares no element type and so constrains none of these positions.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This reconciles the two rules <emu-xref href="#sec-default-values"></emu-xref> leaves in tension. A tuple defaults element-wise, but an element that carries a default already has a value for its position, so the initializer is used where there is one and the type's default only where there is not. `[uint8, uint32 = 10]` therefore defaults to `[0, 10]` rather than being rejected because the second position was already spoken for.</p>
        <p>The literal element store remains the open case <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref> records: the design specifies `array[0] = 300` on a `[].&lt;uint8&gt;` as wrapping to 44 on the grounds of matching `Uint8Array`, while the literal rule would reject it, and the compatibility argument does not reach new syntax. This clause resolves it against the design's example: a literal element store follows the literal rule and is rejected, because the compatibility argument does not reach new syntax, `[].&lt;uint8&gt;` exists nowhere today and `Uint8Array` is untouched, and a literal that does not fit its destination is the mistake every other literal rule here treats it as. A typed value stores by <emu-xref href="#table-out-of-range-treatment"></emu-xref> as before, so the runtime wrap survives where it belongs.</p>
      </emu-note>

    <emu-clause id="sec-relationship-to-typed-arrays">
      <h1><ins>Relationship to %TypedArray%</ins></h1>
      <p>`%TypedArray%` and its constructors are UNTOUCHED by this proposal. A `Uint8Array` reports its `length` as a Number, wraps a store that does not fit, and behaves in every respect as it does today. Nothing here changes an object that already exists.</p>

      <p>The two are SEPARATE ARRAY SYSTEMS rather than one system with two spellings, and they do not mix directly: a `Uint8Array` is not a `[].&lt;`uint8`&gt;` and does not coerce to one, its elements being Numbers rather than values of a numeric value type. `%TypedArray%` is the system for byte-oriented work against existing APIs, and the array types are the system for typed storage; a program picks one per data structure rather than converting between them.</p>

      <p>Where they meet is a BUFFER, which both already understand. A `%TypedArray%` exposes its `buffer`, `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;(`_buffer_`)` (<emu-xref href="#sec-span-type"></emu-xref>) reinterprets those bytes at the element type _T_, and the result is a window &mdash; so writing through it writes the same bytes the `%TypedArray%` reads.</p>

      <p>An implementation SHOULD report the deprecation once per agent, on the first use of a `%TypedArray%` constructor or of `%TypedArray.prototype%`. Once per agent rather than per use: a diagnostic that fires inside a loop over binary data is noise, and the purpose is to tell an author that the family they are using has a successor, which needs saying one time. The report names the replacement rather than only the deprecation.</p>

      <p>Nothing about the report is observable to an ECMAScript program &mdash; no property, no altered result, no thrown completion &mdash; and an implementation that emits nothing is conforming. Deprecated says a program SHOULD move; untouched says no program BREAKS by not moving, and a deprecation that changed behaviour would be the compatibility event this clause exists to avoid.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The alternative was to give `%TypedArray%` the counts and the growth of an array type, so that the two families were interchangeable. It was measured and declined. A `length` at the index type is a value of a numeric value type, and an untyped operand meeting one is a type error &mdash; so `for (let i = 0; i &lt; u.length; i++)` and `total += u.length` would raise in existing programs, and `u.length === n` against an untyped binding would silently answer *false* rather than raise. The first two are the ordinary way binary data is processed, and the third reports nothing at all.</p>
        <p>Two systems that each behave well, with a stated boundary and a buffer between them, is a smaller cost than one system that behaves well in new code and changes the meaning of old code. It is also legible: a reader can see which system a value belongs to from how it was written.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-ordered-element-types">
      <h1><ins>Ordered Element Types</ins></h1>
      <p><ins>`Array.prototype.sort` and `Array.prototype.toSorted` order by the String representation of each element when no comparator is given. Where an array's ELEMENT TYPE is known and carries an order of its own, that order is used instead, so `[].&lt;uint8&gt;` sorts as `%Uint8Array%.prototype.sort` does rather than as text. An array with no element type - an unannotated Array - is unaffected, as is any call that supplies a comparator.</ins></p>
      <p><ins>A type is ORDERED when it is one of the following. Anything else, including `any`, an object type without a declared comparison, and a union of unlike types, is not, and an array of it keeps the String comparison.</ins></p>
      <emu-table>
        <table>
          <tr><th><ins>Type</ins></th><th><ins>Order</ins></th></tr>
          <tr><td><ins>A numeric value type</ins></td><td><ins>By numeric value. A `float` type places *NaN* last and `-0` before `+0`, as %TypedArray%.prototype.sort does, so that an array and the corresponding TypedArray agree.</ins></td></tr>
          <tr><td><ins>A 64-bit integral type</ins></td><td><ins>By value, at the type's OWN precision. `int64` and `uint64` hold magnitudes a Number cannot distinguish - 9007199254740993 and 9007199254740992 are one double - so an implementation that reads such a value as a Number before comparing leaves them in their original order, which is a wrong ANSWER rather than a lost digit.</ins></td></tr>
          <tr><td><ins>`bigint`</ins></td><td><ins>By value, compared AS a BigInt. Converting to a Number to compare would change the ORDER above 2<sup>53</sup>, not merely lose precision.</ins></td></tr>
          <tr><td><ins>`string`</ins></td><td><ins>Lexicographically, which is what the String comparison already gives.</ins></td></tr>
          <tr><td><ins>`boolean`</ins></td><td><ins>*false* before *true*.</ins></td></tr>
          <tr><td><ins>An enum</ins></td><td><ins>By the rule its underlying type already carries (<emu-xref href="#sec-enums"></emu-xref>): a numeric underlying type by value, a `string` underlying type by DECLARATION POSITION rather than alphabetically.</ins></td></tr>
          <tr><td><ins>A class declaring `operator &lt;`</ins></td><td><ins>By that operator.</ins></td></tr>
        </table>
      </emu-table>
      <emu-note>
        <p><ins>An implementation compares with a two-way predicate rather than deriving a three-way result: a comparison sort needs to know only whether one element precedes another, and deriving "less, equal, or greater" from `operator &lt;` would call it twice per comparison to no purpose.</ins></p>
        <p><ins>The order comes from the element TYPE, so it is fixed once for a whole sort rather than decided per pair.</ins></p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-extensions">
    <h1>Extensions</h1>
    <p>The design is organized as a core and a set of extensions, each of which adds types, operations, or syntax to the core rather than revising it. This clause states what an extension may do and records the hooks the preceding clauses have already named.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-extension-mechanism">
      <h1>The Extension Mechanism</h1>
      <p>An extension may add types to <emu-xref href="#sec-type-universe"></emu-xref>, provided each is described by a Type Record of one of the kinds of <emu-xref href="#table-type-record-kinds"></emu-xref>. It may define the operations of <emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-types-of-this-proposal"></emu-xref> for a type it adds, and may declare operators for a type that does not define them (<emu-xref href="#sec-user-defined-operators"></emu-xref>). It may add syntax, subject to the constraint the whole proposal is under: the syntax must be an error in the current language. It may add library functions, overloaded by <emu-xref href="#sec-overload-resolution"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <p>An extension may not revise the type-checking framework of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-system-foundations"></emu-xref>. In particular it may not add a kind to <emu-xref href="#table-type-record-kinds"></emu-xref>, may not change SameType or IsSubtype for a kind it did not introduce, and may not introduce an implicit conversion beyond those of <emu-xref href="#table-implicit-conversions"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The last of these is the constraint worth stating explicitly, because it is the one an extension is most likely to want to break. A units extension wants `Meter` to become `float32`, a decimal extension wants a Number to become a `decimal128`, and a SIMD extension wants a scalar to broadcast into a vector. Each of these is available without an implicit conversion: the first is a metadata parameterization, which is a subtype of its base and so is subsumed rather than converted; the second is written; and the third is an operator the extension declares. The framework holds because every extension so far has been expressible within it, which is evidence that the four implicit cases are the right four rather than merely the ones that were listed first.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-extension-hooks">
      <h1>The Hooks</h1>
      <p><emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref> lists the extensions the preceding clauses name and what each is relied on for. It is a map of the obligations this specification has incurred outward, not a specification of the extensions themselves.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-extension-hooks" caption="Extensions and the Hooks They Fill">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Extension</th><th>Relied on for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>primitive metadata</td><td>The meta types the design ships over the protocol of <emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-metadata"></emu-xref>, which is where they are declared rather than specified: the `Dimensions` of units of measure with its unit aliases and dimensional algebra, the `NumberBounds` of the JSON Schema constraints, and the `DecimalContext` that fixes a decimal's scale and rounding. The protocol itself, its decomposition, its conversion, its narrowing, and the operator and cast declaration forms are delivered by that clause.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>memory layout</td><td>The placement construction that lands an instance on existing bytes, and the descriptor generation the layout is read for. The layout properties, the placement rule, the explicit controls, the bit-fields, and the finiteness condition that <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-classes"></emu-xref> states are delivered by <emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SIMD</td><td>The lane-wise intrinsics an engine lowers to one instruction each, `swizzle`, `shuffle`, `lane`, `select`, and the reductions, together with the accessor vocabulary. The comparison operators and the lane counts are delivered by <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-comparisons"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>simd</td><td>Gather, scatter, and masked load and store, which vectorize an indirect access and need an addressing model this specification does not have. The design defers them too (simd.md), and every other operation of that document is stated in the clauses <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-types"></emu-xref> through <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-wrapping"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>generics</td><td>The design's full generics surface built on the model of <emu-xref href="#sec-generics"></emu-xref>: specialized overloads and their resolution; constraint families as specialization selectors (`uint&lt;N: uint32&gt;`); variadic packs and the derivation of a result type from a pack's length; argument-bound value generics and inference from an expected type; the constraint-family forms `int`, `uint`, and `enum.&lt;TValue&gt;`; and the generic parameters a decorator declares, which the design writes and this specification neither states nor defers elsewhere. The ~parameter~ kind, substitution and specialization, the `where` clause, and declaration-site variance are delivered by that clause.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>dependent record types</td><td>The design's JSON Schema correspondence, by which a `where` clause and a schema's `dependentRequired`, `dependentSchemas`, and `if`/`then`/`else` express one another, and the validator generation that correspondence is for. The `is` operator, the `where` clause, and their narrowing are delivered by <emu-xref href="#sec-dependent-record-types"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>decorators</td><td>Delivered by <emu-xref href="#sec-decorators"></emu-xref>, which now covers the shape of every reflection (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shapes"></emu-xref>), what each context admits as a replacement value (<emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-values"></emu-xref>), `addInitializer` (<emu-xref href="#sec-addinitializer"></emu-xref>), the retrieval forms (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-retrieval"></emu-xref>), the metadata channel, and the resolution and order of a decoration. What remains at this hook is the decorator SYNTAX itself, which rides the decorators proposal rather than this one.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>complex numbers</td><td>The operators and `Math` overloads of the complex types. The type of an imaginary literal and the conversions are delivered by <emu-xref href="#sec-complex-numbers"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>operator overloading</td><td>The math library the design works the feature through, and the four conditions it states an engine must meet to compile that library well. The declaration forms, the receiver rule, and the resolution are delivered by <emu-xref href="#sec-operator-declarations"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>threading</td><td>Specified by <emu-xref href="#sec-threading"></emu-xref>. What remains at this hook is what that clause leaves to a host: the sizing and management of the pool (<emu-xref href="#sec-thread-pool"></emu-xref>), the disposition of host-scheduled work still pending when a thread's function has settled (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-agent-cluster"></emu-xref>), the AbortSignal the cancellation rules refer to, and the concurrent Map and Set the design would like but does not require. Two things the clause records as possible future work rather than as gaps: an explicit opt-in to settle-side execution of a promise reaction, and an option pinning the parallel partition so that a result reproduces across implementations and not only within one.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ranges</td><td>The range literal's grammar and its lexical interaction with `.`, the members of `Range`, and the extensions that take a range as a bound. The case ranges and the bounded `for` counter that <emu-xref href="#sec-check-elision"></emu-xref> relies on are delivered by <emu-xref href="#sec-ranges"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>type programming</td><td>The tooling plan: an evaluator embedded in a language server, a computed type displayed as its application beside its expansion, and a builder stack on a budget diagnostic. Every other obligation recorded here is discharged: construction, relations, `never`, and evaluation by <emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-maketype"></emu-xref>, <emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-isassignable"></emu-xref>, <emu-xref href="#sec-never-type"></emu-xref>, and <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>, the kit and the evaluable fragment by <emu-xref href="#annex-standard-kit"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#annex-evaluable-fragment"></emu-xref>, symbol literal types by <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref>, the declarative checker facts by <emu-xref href="#sec-declarative-checker-facts"></emu-xref>, contracts, trials, and declared inverses by <emu-xref href="#sec-checked-contracts"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-inference-through-results"></emu-xref>, and structural matching, provenance, and distribution by <emu-xref href="#sec-structural-matching"></emu-xref>, <emu-xref href="#sec-provenance"></emu-xref>, and <emu-xref href="#sec-expansion-artifact"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The extensions not listed, including composites, pattern matching, do expressions, the pipeline operator, serialization, structure of arrays, the regular expression types, Temporal, and the standard library, are specified in terms of the core and are relied on by nothing here. That is a claim about direction and not about importance: serialization is where a typed program meets a network, and it is built from the layout, the metadata hooks, and the `where` clauses this specification already gives, adding no rule any of them must accommodate. The one place the direction reverses is threading, whose heap sharing decides what a Type Object's identity means (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-across-agents"></emu-xref>), which is why it alone among the outer extensions has a clause here.</p>
        <p>The dependency now runs in one direction without exception. The metadata parameterization that <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref> once forward-referenced is core (<emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>), as the refinement relation it shares with an enum's underlying type and a class's base always suggested it should be. What remains with the primitive metadata extension is the protocol's hook surface: the judgments beyond the branding default, and the conversion machinery.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-coverage-of-the-design-documents">
      <h1>Coverage of the Design Documents</h1>
      <p>Every document of the design has a disposition here: it is specified by clauses of this document, it is deferred to its own future clauses with a hook already named, or it is deferred outright, in which case whatever it adds must fit the mechanism above. The examples directory is a corpus this specification is checked against rather than a source of requirements.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-design-document-coverage" caption="Disposition of the design documents">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Disposition</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>README</td><td>The core; specified throughout this document.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>typeobjects.md</td><td>Specified: the Type Object and the computed types of <emu-xref href="#sec-computed-types"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>typeprogramming.md</td><td>Specified: builders, canonicalization, the evaluation budget, structural matching (<emu-xref href="#sec-structural-matching"></emu-xref>), provenance (<emu-xref href="#sec-provenance"></emu-xref>), and the expansion artifact (<emu-xref href="#sec-expansion-artifact"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>higherkindedtypes.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-higher-kinded-parameters"></emu-xref>, which states the [[Arity]] on a ~parameter~ Type Record, what may be bound to a higher-kinded parameter, and how the four clauses of <emu-xref href="#sec-generics"></emu-xref> read when a parameter is one. The refusals of the quantified constraint and of inference are normative, and both rest on <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>generics.md</td><td>Partially specified: the parameters that are types and values, specialization, the `where` constraints, and variance are <emu-xref href="#sec-generics"></emu-xref>. Specialized overloads and their resolution, constraint families as specialization selectors, variadic packs, argument-bound value generics and inference from an expected type, the constraint-family forms, and generic parameters on a decorator are deferred, and <emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref> records them as this clause's hook.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>dependentrecordtypes.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-dependent-record-types"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>primitivemetadata.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-metadata"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>memorylayout.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>, with placement `new` in <emu-xref href="#sec-type-arguments-and-placement-new-in-expression-position"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>operatoroverloading.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-operators"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-operator-declarations"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>operators.md</td><td>Specified: the result of an operator by family (<emu-xref href="#sec-operator-results"></emu-xref>), of which that document is the informative presentation, with the instructions a target lowers each family to.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>decorators.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-decorators"></emu-xref> - the reflection contexts and the shape of each one's reflection, the metadata channel, replacement values, `addInitializer`, the retrieval forms, and the resolution and order of a decoration. Decorator syntax itself rides the decorators proposal.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>complex.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-complex-types"></emu-xref>, <emu-xref href="#sec-complex-numbers"></emu-xref>, and the imaginary literal.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>rational.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-rational-types"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>decimal.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-decimal-floating-point-types"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>composites.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-composites"></emu-xref>, layered on the upstream Composites proposal with the deviations of <emu-xref href="#sec-composite-deviations"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>patternmatching.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-pattern-matching"></emu-xref>, with the discriminated `where` chains it consumes in <emu-xref href="#sec-discriminated-where-chains"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The iteration types</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-iteration-types"></emu-xref>, owed to the protocols the core defines rather than to any extension, and the clause the generator types are now stated in terms of.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>pipelineoperator.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-pipeline-operator"></emu-xref>, with the token recorded as the proposal's current spelling and every rule stated in terms of the topic rather than the token.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>doexpressions.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-do-expressions"></emu-xref>, with the generator types and the divergence analysis it consumes in <emu-xref href="#sec-generator-types"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-divergence"></emu-xref>, both owed to the core rather than to it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>simd.md</td><td>Specified: the vector type's formation is <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-types"></emu-xref>; its lanes, the broadcast cast, the horizontal sum, and the conversion between a bit vector and an integer are <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-lanes"></emu-xref>; permutation across one and two sources is <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-permutation"></emu-xref>; the component accessors with their desugaring are <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-component-accessors"></emu-xref>; the lane-wise comparisons with their result forms are <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-comparisons"></emu-xref>; the operations that consume a mask are <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-masks"></emu-xref>; the target's preferred width is <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-widths"></emu-xref>; and what an alias of a vector type has that a class wrapping one does not is <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-wrapping"></emu-xref>. Gather, scatter, and masked load and store are deferred by the design itself, which <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-widths"></emu-xref> records.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>standardlibrary.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-numeric-library"></emu-xref> and the overloaded library rules.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>errorhandling.md</td><td>Specified: <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-catch"></emu-xref>; the errors the proposal raises are stated at their checks.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>references.md</td><td>Specified: the `ref` type is in the grammar and the borrowing rules are <emu-xref href="#sec-references-and-borrowing"></emu-xref>; a returned reference is consumed as a location by the forms of <emu-xref href="#sec-location-consuming-contexts"></emu-xref>, and a borrow of an `SoA` element is <emu-xref href="#sec-soa-references"></emu-xref>; a destructuring `ref` member is <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-destructuring"></emu-xref>; the deferred form (a user iterator or callback yielding references) is noted there.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>threading.md</td><td>Specified by <emu-xref href="#sec-threading"></emu-xref>: threads and their lifetime, the scheduling of promise reactions across them, `callThread` and cancellation, the `shared` modifier's placement and stability rules, shared typed memory as events of the memory model, `Atomics` over typed values, Lock, Condition, and ThreadLocal, and parallel iteration. The host latitude that remains is listed at its hook (<emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ranges.md</td><td>Partially specified: the range literals, the Range value, and its iteration are <emu-xref href="#sec-range-literals"></emu-xref> atop the hook of <emu-xref href="#sec-ranges"></emu-xref>; the interval in the type and its specialization, the `uint8.&lt;1..=6&gt;` bounds desugaring, slicing to a view, and the random and Temporal integrations are deferred.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>serialization.md</td><td>Partially specified: typed JSON parsing (the `JSON.parse.&lt;_T_&gt;` overload and its converting, validating walk) is <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-json-parsing"></emu-xref>, atop the boundary and metadata machinery the core already gives; the deferred surface (class targets, the exact wide numerics, typed `JSON.stringify`, streaming, typed JSON modules, `structuredClone`, and the binary layout) is noted there. <emu-xref href="#sec-types-across-agents"></emu-xref> is the adjacent normative text.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>soa.md</td><td>The type and its layout: <emu-xref href="#sec-structure-of-arrays"></emu-xref>; array views, which its own view form follows, are <emu-xref href="#sec-array-views"></emu-xref>. A reference into an `SoA` is <emu-xref href="#sec-soa-references"></emu-xref>, with its liveness rules in <emu-xref href="#sec-reference-liveness"></emu-xref>; the element and view semantics are still to come.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>regexp.md</td><td>Partially specified: the capture-type inference of a literal is <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-regular-expressions"></emu-xref>; the match-result surface it enables (the typed exec result and group-name checking, the typed String methods, the flag-dependent shapes, composition, and string narrowing) is deferred and noted there.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>random.md</td><td>Deferred; a library extension.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>temporal.md</td><td>Deferred; a library extension.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>specnotes.md</td><td>The plan this document follows; not itself a source of requirements.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-references-and-borrowing">
    <h1>References and Borrowing</h1>
    <p>A reference is a borrow: a handle to a storage location, a variable, an object property, or an array element, that reads and writes through to the original rather than to a copy. It is what lets a function mutate its caller's value and a loop write into an array in place. This clause specifies the borrowing forms of the design's references extension (<emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>): the `ref` argument and `ref` return, the `ref` parameter, the `ref` lexical binding and its rebinding, and the reference `for`-`of` loop. The `ref` <emu-xref href="#sec-type-references">type</emu-xref> is already in the grammar; this clause adds the syntax of the borrowing forms and the runtime that the type describes.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reference-syntax">
      <h1>Syntax</h1>
      <p>`ref` is a contextual keyword, not a reserved word, and every borrowing form claims it by one rule: `ref` is claimed only when the token that follows it, with no line terminator between, can begin the operand as written &mdash; an |IdentifierReference| or `this` for a |RefExpression|, whose operand is an expression, and a |BindingIdentifier| (an identifier, or `yield` or `await` where those are identifiers) for the binding forms, whose operand is a name. Everywhere else `ref` is the identifier it is today: `f(ref)` and `f(ref, x)` pass it, `ref(x)` calls it, `ref[i]` and `ref.x` read from it, `ref = 1` assigns to it, and `ref:` labels a statement. The rule is what makes every addition below conservative: each new alternative derives only source text that is a Syntax Error in the base language.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        <ins>RefExpression[Yield, Await] :
          `ref` [no LineTerminator here] LeftHandSideExpression[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>The operand is a |LeftHandSideExpression| because a borrow names a place, and the claim rule restricts which tokens may begin it: `ref a`, `ref a[i]`, `ref o.x`, and `ref this.buffer` are borrows, while `ref (x)` and `ref [0]` keep their base readings as a call and an element access of an identifier named `ref`. In argument position one further token is admitted, the pipeline topic `%`, not to make `ref %` mean anything but so that its refusal (<emu-xref href="#sec-pipeline-static-semantics"></emu-xref>) carries its own diagnostic rather than surfacing as an unexplained token. A |RefExpression| appears in exactly two positions, an argument and a return; the rest of this clause gives each, and the binding forms, in turn.</p>

      <p>An argument may be a borrow. |Argument| is restated from <emu-xref href="#sec-named-arguments"></emu-xref> with the one added alternative; per <emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref> this block is the production's complete form.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        Argument[Yield, Await] :
          AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]
          `...` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]
          NamedArgument[?Yield, ?Await]
          <ins>RefExpression[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>A return may be a borrow. This is the first restatement of |ReturnStatement|.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        ReturnStatement[Yield, Await] :
          `return` `;`
          `return` [no LineTerminator here] Expression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `;`
          <ins>`return` [no LineTerminator here] RefExpression[?Yield, ?Await] `;`</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>`return ref` followed by a line terminator keeps its base meaning: the restriction inside |RefExpression| makes the new alternative unavailable, the |Expression| alternative derives the identifier, and automatic semicolon insertion completes the statement, exactly as it does today. `return ref x` derives only through the new alternative, since two adjacent identifiers have no expression reading.</p>

      <p>A lexical binding may be a borrow. |LexicalBinding| is restated from <emu-xref href="#sec-type-annotations"></emu-xref>; the `ref` alternative requires its |Initializer| structurally, since an alias without a location to alias is nothing, and it joins neither the |BindingPattern| alternative, because the borrow binds one name to one location, nor the |TypedInitializer| alternative, because `:=` infers a binding's type from a value and the initializer of a `ref` binding must denote a location.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        LexicalBinding[In, Yield, Await] :
          BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? Initializer[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]?
          BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypedInitializer[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]
          BindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await] Initializer[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]
          <ins>`ref` [no LineTerminator here] BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? Initializer[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>It is a Syntax Error if the `ref` alternative of |LexicalBinding| is contained in the |LexicalDeclaration| of a |ForStatement| head.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The per-iteration environment of a `for` statement copies each head binding by value into every iteration, which would silently decay the alias into a copy after the first. The form is refused rather than given that meaning; lifting the restriction awaits per-iteration semantics for a location binding, which no design document yet states.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A mutable `ref` binding may be re-aimed by a statement of its own. This is the first restatement of |Statement|.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        Statement[Yield, Await, Return] :
          BlockStatement[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]
          VariableStatement[?Yield, ?Await]
          EmptyStatement
          ExpressionStatement[?Yield, ?Await]
          IfStatement[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]
          BreakableStatement[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]
          ContinueStatement[?Yield, ?Await]
          BreakStatement[?Yield, ?Await]
          [+Return] ReturnStatement[?Yield, ?Await]
          WithStatement[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]
          LabelledStatement[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]
          ThrowStatement[?Yield, ?Await]
          TryStatement[?Yield, ?Await, ?Return]
          DebuggerStatement
          <ins>RefRebindingStatement[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>

        <ins>RefRebindingStatement[Yield, Await] :
          `ref` [no LineTerminator here] BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] `=` LeftHandSideExpression[?Yield, ?Await] `;`</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>The claim is the exact shape: after the same-line name, the next token must be `=`, and otherwise the identifier reading stands &mdash; `ref = 1` assigns to a binding named `ref`, `ref(x)` calls one, and `ref:` labels a statement, all as today. The `=` itself may begin a new line, since `ref b` followed by anything but `=` has no reading in the base language either; an implementation that reads the name before deciding restores it on any other token. The production derives only strings an |ExpressionStatement| cannot, so no statement changes meaning.</p>

      <p>A reference `for`-`of` head marks its binding. |ForDeclaration| gains the marked alternative; the claim declines when the token after `ref` is `of`, so `for (const ref of a)` continues to bind an identifier named `ref`.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        ForDeclaration[Yield, Await] :
          LetOrConst ForBinding[?Yield, ?Await]
          <ins>LetOrConst `ref` [no LineTerminator here] ForBinding[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>It is a Syntax Error if the `ref` alternative's |ForBinding| is a |BindingPattern|. It is a Syntax Error if the `ref` alternative of |ForDeclaration| occurs in a `for`-`in` or `for` `await` alternative of |ForInOfStatement|: the borrow aliases an element slot, which the index-based `for`-`of` of <emu-xref href="#sec-reference-iteration"></emu-xref> supplies, and which neither the property keys a `for`-`in` walks nor the values an async iterator yields can.</p>

      <p>A parameter's `ref` is already in the grammar (|FormalParameter| in <emu-xref href="#sec-type-annotations"></emu-xref>, and the parameter lists of function types in <emu-xref href="#sec-function-types"></emu-xref>); this clause adds its two Early Errors. It is a Syntax Error if a |FormalParameter| has `ref` and its |BindingElement| is a |BindingPattern|: the borrow binds one name to one location, and destructuring a borrow is the pattern's own `(ref a)` member form (<emu-xref href="#sec-typed-destructuring"></emu-xref>). It is a Syntax Error if a |FormalParameter| has `ref` and its |BindingElement| contains an |Initializer|: a default runs exactly when the caller supplied no argument, and a value the callee builds has no caller-side location to borrow, so the combination could never bind and is refused at the declaration rather than at the first defaulted call. The optional marker `?` remains admitted, as the function type grammar already admits it; a call that omits an optional `ref` parameter fails the borrow at that call, a *TypeError* exception.</p>

      <p>An arrow function takes `ref` parameters through its covers. The parenthesized cover admits a `ref` parameter directly, refined exactly as <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-arrow-functions"></emu-xref> refines an annotated parameter, so `(ref a: int32) =&gt; {}` and the design's `zip(a, b, (ref x, ref y) =&gt; { x = x + y; })` are covered; and through the async call cover a |RefExpression| argument refines to the same parameter, so `async (ref a) =&gt; {}` is the one form reached by the other road. A cover containing a `ref` parameter has no expression reading, so claiming it is arrow-only and conservative &mdash; `(ref a)` without a following `=&gt;` is a Syntax Error, as it is today. The Initializer Early Error above applies to the refined parameter as to a declared one.</p>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-typed-destructuring">
      <h1>Typed Destructuring Members</h1>
      <p>A member of an object binding pattern may state the type of the binding it introduces, and may borrow the property rather than copy it. The annotation is written inside parentheses, because the position after a member's `:` is already taken: `{ a: uint8 }` binds property `a` to a new name `uint8`, so an annotation cannot follow the colon. The parenthesized member leaves that colon free, so a member may be annotated and renamed at once.</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        BindingProperty[Yield, Await] :
          SingleNameBinding[?Yield, ?Await]
          PropertyName[?Yield, ?Await] `:` BindingElement[?Yield, ?Await]
          <ins>`(` `ref`? BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] `?`? TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? `)` Initializer[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]?</ins>
          <ins>`(` `ref`? BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] `?`? TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? `)` `:` BindingElement[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>Without a rename the parenthesized name is both the property read and the binding introduced, so `{ (a: uint8) }` binds `a` to the value of property `a`, enforced at that binding as an annotated declaration is (<emu-xref href="#sec-runtime-enforcement"></emu-xref>). With a rename the parenthesized name is the property and the binding follows the colon, so `{ (a: uint8): b }` reads property `a` and binds `b`. An |Initializer| supplies the value when the property is absent, as it does for any member.</p>

      <p>A member marked `?` may be absent from the object being destructured: where the property is absent the binding is *undefined* and the annotation is not enforced against it, as an omitted argument to an optional parameter is not. A value that is present is enforced whether the member is marked or not.</p>

      <p>An object rest may state the type of what it collects, `{ (a: uint8), ...rest: object }`, which is an object type rather than a member type, as an array rest's annotation is the type of the array it collects rather than of an element. It is written without parentheses: the parentheses of a member exist because the position after a member's `:` is already taken, and a rest has no such collision.</p>

      <p>A member marked `ref` borrows the property's location on the object being destructured, by the rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-requireborrowablereference"></emu-xref>, and binds its name to that location; reads and writes through the binding reach the property. It is a Syntax Error if such a member has an |Initializer|, for the reason a `ref` parameter may not have one (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-syntax"></emu-xref>): a default is a value the callee builds and has no location to borrow. It is a Syntax Error for such a member to be marked `?`, and likewise for a `ref` parameter: the marker says the value may be absent, and a `ref` binds the location rather than the value, which is there whether or not the property is. A form that cannot honour what it declares is refused where it is written rather than at each use of it. An annotation on a `ref` member is checked against the referent and never converts it.</p>

      <p>A member of an object <em>assignment</em> pattern may be marked `ref` in the same parentheses, and then re-borrows: the named binding, which must already be a `ref` binding, is pointed at the location of the property on the object being destructured. `(` `ref` _r_ `)` `:` _x_ borrows the property _x_, and `(` `ref` _x_ `)` without a rename borrows the property of the binding's own name. A target that is not a `ref` binding, and a value that is not an Object, are each a *TypeError*. This is the only destructuring form whose targets exist before it runs, which is what it is for: one borrow retargeted across the turns of a loop.</p>

      <p>An assignment member may not carry a type annotation. An annotation states the type of a binding the pattern introduces, and an assignment pattern introduces none; its targets already carry whatever type they were declared at, so an annotation here would be a second type for one target. A program that means to check a value writes `is` or `:=`.</p>

      <p></p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The borrowed location belongs to the object, not to the caller's variable, so a function whose parameter is such a pattern takes an ordinary argument: `g(o)` suffices and `g(ref o)` is not required. `ref o` would state something different &mdash; that the callee may rewrite the caller's binding &mdash; and it could not reach the pattern in any case, since a parameter that is a |BindingPattern| consumes its argument as a value and a Reference Value decays there (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-values"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reference-values">
      <h1>Reference Values and the Absence of Identity</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="reference-value">Reference Value</dfn> is a value produced by a `ref` expression that holds a storage location: a Reference Record (in the sense of the base specification) whose base is an Environment Record for a variable or an Object for a property or an array element. A Reference Value is a primitive value in the sense that it is not an Object; nothing is allocated when one is created.</p>
      <p>A Reference Value has no observable identity. Every operation that reads a binding holding one dereferences to the value at the location, and a Reference Value that reaches any position consuming a value <dfn id="reference-decay">decays</dfn> to the value at its location first. In consequence `typeof`, `Reflect.typeOf`, `===`, and `instanceof` see the referenced value and never the reference, and a Reference Value cannot be stored in a binding that outlives its location, a property, an array, or a collection: the only bindings that hold one are the `ref` parameter and `ref` lexical binding below, which alias the location rather than store the reference. There is therefore no way to distinguish a reference from the value it refers to, which is what keeps it a location and an index rather than an object.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Decay is where the absence of identity is realized. A reference reaches a value position at an ordinary call boundary, an operand of an operator, an argument to a non-`ref` parameter, or a conversion, and at each the value at the location is taken. The reference is observable only through the aliasing behaviour of the binding that holds it, not as a value in its own right, so no reflected type and no equality can name it.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>Decay is total, and the channels through which a Reference Value could otherwise be observed are these. A Reference Value decays when it becomes the base of a property access or of an optional chain, when it is the callee of a call, when it is gathered by a rest parameter, when it is the argument of a parameter that is a |BindingPattern|, when the arguments object of a call is created, and on entry to any built-in function. It decays at an ordinary call's return, so a call whose callee returned a borrow yields the referent's value at every position but the location-consuming ones, which <emu-xref href="#sec-reference-parameters-and-arguments"></emu-xref> defers. The rule for each is the same rule: the position takes a value, so the value at the location is what it gets.</p>

      <p>A `ref` parameter makes a parameter list not simple, as a default value or a destructuring pattern does. The arguments object of a function with a `ref` parameter is therefore unmapped, and its elements hold the decayed values of the arguments; neither a read of `arguments` nor a store into it reaches the caller's location, and strict and sloppy code agree on this. The `ref` parameter itself is unaffected: it takes its location from the argument list, which the arguments object is a separate view of.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Naming the channels is not a list of special cases but the closure of one rule over the places a value can go. Two of them are worth stating for what they exclude. A rest parameter gathers into an array, and an array cannot hold a reference, so a borrow forwarded through a variadic wrapper arrives as a value &mdash; the alternative, refusing the call, would make `(...args) =&gt; f(...args)` hostile to a borrow for no safety gained, since the forwarded reference could not have been written into the array anyway. And the built-in boundary is what makes the reflective call paths decay channels without naming them one by one: `Function.prototype.call`, `apply`, `bind`, and `Reflect.apply` are built-ins, so a Reference Value handed to one decays on entry, and the list such a function forwards, or stores as bound arguments, holds values.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reference-parameters-and-arguments">
      <h1>Reference Parameters and Arguments</h1>
      <p>A `ref` argument `f(ref e)` evaluates _e_ to a storage location and passes a Reference Value holding it. The location is obtained by RequireBorrowableReference (<emu-xref href="#sec-requireborrowablereference"></emu-xref>), so it is a *TypeError* exception if _e_ does not denote a location: a value, a private member, a super property, and a property of a primitive have no location a borrow could alias. A `ref` parameter binds its name to the location the argument carries, so a read in the callee reads through to the referent and a write in the callee writes through to it. It is a *TypeError* exception if the argument to a `ref` parameter is not a Reference Value, since a plain value has no location to borrow, and a Reference Value passed to a parameter that is not `ref` decays to its referent.</p>
      <p>A type annotation on a `ref` parameter is checked against the referent and never converts it. The referent is of the annotated type or a *TypeError* exception is thrown; the value at the caller's location is not rewritten, because a borrow that converted its referent would silently change storage it does not own. A `ref` parameter's binding is not re-enforced by the parameter type pass of <emu-xref href="#sec-runtime-enforcement"></emu-xref> for the same reason.</p>
      <p>A function may return a reference with `return ref e`, evaluated as a `ref` argument is. The operand may denote storage of any lifetime, including a binding of the returning function itself: an environment is an object like any other and stays alive while anything refers to it, so a reference to a local outlives the frame that made it, exactly as a closure over that local does. At an ordinary call the returned reference decays to its referent, so a caller that consumes the call as a value observes the referent. A caller may instead consume it as a location, which is what makes `first(a)++` write through to the element; <emu-xref href="#sec-location-consuming-contexts"></emu-xref> gives those positions.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-requireborrowablereference" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          RequireBorrowableReference (
            _V_: an ECMAScript language value or a Reference Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing a Reference Record or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns _V_ if _V_ denotes a storage location a borrow may alias, and throws a *TypeError* exception otherwise. Every borrowing form &mdash; the `ref` argument, the `ref` return, and the `ref` binding &mdash; obtains its location through it, so all of them refuse the same operands.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _V_ is not a Reference Record, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If IsUnresolvableReference(_V_) is *true*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If IsPrivateReference(_V_) is *true*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If IsSuperReference(_V_) is *true*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If IsPropertyReference(_V_) is *true* and _V_.[[Base]] is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If _V_ denotes a bit-field member of a laid-out aggregate (<emu-xref href="#sec-layout-control"></emu-xref>), throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return _V_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>What counts as a storage location is decided at the Reference Record and not at the property behind it. A reference to an object property is borrowable whether the property is a data property, an accessor property, absent, or answered by a Proxy: reads through the borrow perform an ordinary Get and writes an ordinary Set, so a borrow of an accessor calls the getter and the setter, a borrow of an absent property reads *undefined* and creates the property when written, and a borrow of a Proxy-trapped property triggers the traps in the order those operations already specify. This is the only line that can be held, because the shape of a property is not stable for the life of a borrow &mdash; a data property can be redefined as an accessor, or deleted, between the borrow and the write &mdash; so a rule that admitted only data properties would have to re-check at every use and would still be wrong at the moment of redefinition.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The exclusions are the cases where no location exists rather than the cases where one is inconvenient. A private member and a super property are not property references over an ordinary base; a property of a primitive is a property of a fresh wrapper that the write would discard; a bit-field is a run of bits within a byte, which is addressable only by reading and writing the containing scalar and so cannot be aliased as a location of its own. An unresolvable reference is a name that is not bound, and borrowing one would create the binding a plain read refuses.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reference-bindings">
      <h1>Reference Bindings and Rebinding</h1>
      <p>A `ref` lexical binding aliases a storage location for its scope. `let ref b = e` and `const ref b = e` evaluate _e_ to a location by RequireBorrowableReference (<emu-xref href="#sec-requireborrowablereference"></emu-xref>), the same rule a `ref` argument applies, and bind _b_ to it; it is a *TypeError* exception if _e_ does not denote a location, and it is a Syntax Error for a `ref` binding to omit its initializer. A read of _b_ reads through to the location. A `let ref` binding may be written through, so `b = v` writes _v_ to the location, and may be rebound; a `const ref` binding may not be written through, so `b = v` is the ordinary assignment-to-constant *TypeError* exception, while a write to a member of the referent is permitted as for any `const`. An annotation on a `ref` binding is checked against the referent without conversion, as for a `ref` parameter.</p>
      <p>Reassigning a `let ref` binding with `ref b = e` rebinds it: _b_ is redirected to the location _e_ denotes, and the location it previously aliased is not written. It is a *TypeError* exception to apply the rebinding form to a name that is not a rebindable `ref` binding.</p>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reference-iteration">
      <h1>Reference Iteration</h1>
      <p>A `for` `(` LetOrConst `ref` _p_ `of` _e_ `)` loop binds _p_ to a reference to each element of the array _e_ in turn, so the body writes into the array in place. The iteration is index-based: it reads the length of _e_ once, evaluates the body once for each index below that length with _p_ bound to a reference to the element at that index, and does not go through `%Symbol.iterator%`, so patching the iterator protocol does not affect it and no result object is allocated. A `let ref` element binding may be written through and rebound and a `const ref` element binding may not, as for a `ref` lexical binding. It is a *TypeError* exception if _e_ is not an array.</p>
      <p>The length read at the start of the loop is pinned for its duration. An operation that changes the length of the array while a reference into it is live, `push`, `pop`, `shift`, `unshift`, `splice`, or a store to `length`, is a *TypeError* exception, which a `ref` loop makes easy to reach since it holds a reference across the whole iteration. The rule is the loop half of <emu-xref href="#sec-reference-liveness"></emu-xref>, which gives the rule for a reference held outside a loop. A user-defined iterator that yields references is not supported and belongs to the design's extension (<emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>); a reference into an `SoA` column set is <emu-xref href="#sec-soa-references"></emu-xref>. The callback idiom needs no support of its own: a container that passes `ref a[i]` to a callback with a `ref` parameter composes from the argument and parameter forms above, which is how the design's `zip` writes through both arrays.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The liveness rules are the whole of what the borrow needs, in place of a borrow checker with lifetimes. A reference cannot dangle in the Rust sense because the storage it points into is reachable from it and a garbage-collected object stays alive as long as anything refers to it; what remains to prevent is a reference into storage that moves, which <emu-xref href="#sec-reference-liveness"></emu-xref> handles, and a reference escaping its location, which the absence of identity (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-values"></emu-xref>) already forecloses. Both are local checks and neither needs a lifetime system. This specification checks the length rule at run time; the design also permits a static rejection wherever the array's type makes the offending operation visible, which an implementation may add as a diagnostic without changing the meaning of any program this clause accepts.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-location-consuming-contexts">
      <h1>Location-Consuming Contexts</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="location-consuming-context">location-consuming context</dfn> is a position that consumes the storage location an expression denotes rather than the value at it. A call that returns a reference does not decay in such a position, so the reference reaches the operation and the operation reads and writes through it. There are three, and each is a position in which the base language already refuses a call outright, so no existing program changes meaning.</p>

      <p>The first is the operand of `++` or `--`. The Early Error of the base language refuses a call there, because a call has no assignment target; that error is relaxed for a |CallExpression| whose Static Type is a reference type, and for such an operand the update reads the referent, computes the new value as it otherwise would, and stores it back through the same location. The design's element accessor is written this way:</p>
      <pre><code class="javascript">
        function first(a) { return ref a[0]; }
        first(a)++; // Writes through to a[0]
      </code></pre>

      <p>The second is the operand of a `ref` argument, which re-borrows the location a call returned, as in `g(ref first(a))`. The location the callee returned is passed on to the `ref` parameter unchanged; nothing is copied and no second borrow of a different location is created.</p>

      <p>It is a type error for the operand of `++` or `--`, of a `ref` argument, or of an assignment target, to be a call whose Static Type is not a reference type. Where the callee's return type is not known statically the check is deferred to run time, per <emu-xref href="#sec-type-errors"></emu-xref>: it is a *TypeError* exception if the call's result in such a position is not a Reference Value.</p>

      <p>The third is an assignment target. `first(a) = v` stores through the location the call returned, and so does every form that assigns to a target: a compound or logical assignment, which reads the referent and stores the result back through the same location; an element or property of a destructuring assignment, at any depth; and the target of a `for`-`in` or `for`-`of` head. These all follow from one static semantic rather than from a rule stated per position.</p>

      <p>The static semantic AssignmentTargetType of a |CallExpression| is *~invalid~* in the base language, which is what refuses a call as a target. It is instead *~simple~* when the |CallExpression|'s Static Type is a reference type, and remains *~invalid~* where that Static Type is known and is not one. Where the Static Type is not known &mdash; the callee is reached through an unannotated binding, or its signature is otherwise unavailable &mdash; AssignmentTargetType is *~simple~* and the check is the deferred run-time one of the paragraph above, which is imprecise rather than wrong: a call that does not yield a Reference Value is a *TypeError* at the assignment rather than an Early Error at the source text. Every Early Error that governs an assignment target is phrased in terms of AssignmentTargetType, so each of the positions above inherits the rule without being named in it, and a call whose callee is KNOWN not to return a reference stays refused exactly where it is today.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Which of the two the program gets therefore depends on what the checker can see of the callee, and that boundary moves as more of a program is typed: a callee whose return type is unavailable defers to run time, and the same call is refused before the program runs once that type is known. This is the ordinary gradual-typing direction &mdash; more type information turns a run-time failure into an earlier one, never a working program into a broken one &mdash; and it is why the two rules must be stated as one rule with a known and an unknown case rather than as a rule and an exception.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A returned reference still decays everywhere this clause does not name, so `first(a).x = v` writes through the referent's property rather than the element's location, and `let v = first(a)` copies. What remains unspecified is a reference yielded by a user-defined iterator or callback (<emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-soa-references">
      <h1>References into a Structure of Arrays</h1>
      <p>A borrow of an element of an `SoA` (<emu-xref href="#sec-structure-of-arrays"></emu-xref>) denotes a <dfn id="soa-element-location">column set and an index</dfn> rather than a property slot. The element's fields are stored one column each, so the element has no single address a reference could hold, and the location is the storage together with the position within it. Reads and writes through such a reference go to the columns at that index.</p>

      <p>This is a location like any other, and every borrowing form produces it: `ref s[i]` as an argument or a return, and `let ref` or `const ref` as a binding, all yield the same reference, and all of them obtain it through RequireBorrowableReference (<emu-xref href="#sec-requireborrowablereference"></emu-xref>). A `ref` parameter therefore accepts a borrow of an `SoA` element exactly as it accepts a borrow of an array element, which is what lets one function serve either storage: a parameter declared with a reference type receives a borrow without its caller stating which layout produced it.</p>

      <p>Reading through the reference yields the element, whose field accesses reach the columns; writing a whole value through it stores each of that value's fields into its column at the index. Decay is different from reading, and the difference is observable here: a Reference Value that reaches a position consuming a value decays (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-values"></emu-xref>) to the <em>gathered</em> element, the same detached copy the expression `s[i]` produces, so no aliasing survives a boundary that takes a value.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>For an ordinary reference these two coincide, since the referent of a variable or a property is just the value stored there, and nothing distinguishes reading a borrow from decaying one. An `SoA` element separates them: the element that field accesses must reach is a live position within the columns, while the value a boundary consumes must be a copy detached from them. The rule is stated in terms of what the position does &mdash; a position that goes on to read or write <em>through</em> the result dereferences, and a position that consumes a value decays &mdash; and it is only the second that copies.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>The liveness rules of <emu-xref href="#sec-reference-liveness"></emu-xref> apply to such a reference however it was taken. The `SoA` is the container in this specification that relocates, so a borrow into one is invalidated when growth moves the columns, and a use through an invalidated borrow is a *TypeError* exception; a borrow of an element that has since been removed is invalidated in the same way; and within a reference `for`-`of` over an `SoA`, an operation that changes its length is a *TypeError* exception at that operation.</p>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reference-liveness">
      <h1>Reference Liveness</h1>
      <p>A reference is valid while the storage it aliases is where it was borrowed from. Two rules make that so, and they apply at different moments: the loop rule refuses the offending operation, and the relocation rule refuses the later use.</p>

      <p>The loop rule is the one above (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-iteration"></emu-xref>). Within a reference `for`-`of`, an operation that changes the length of the iterated container throws a *TypeError* exception at that operation. It applies to every container a reference loop can iterate, and it is structural: the loop holds a reference for its whole duration, so the length is pinned for its whole duration, and no accounting of individual references is needed to know that one is live.</p>

      <p>The relocation rule governs a reference held outside such a loop &mdash; a `ref` binding, or a `ref` parameter whose caller borrowed an element. A reference into storage that can be relocated is <dfn id="reference-invalidation">invalidated</dfn> when that storage is relocated, and a read or a write through an invalidated reference throws a *TypeError* exception. A reference to an element of a container is likewise invalidated if that element is removed, since a reference names an element and not merely an address. Relocation is what an implementation does when a growable container outgrows its allocation, and the operations that can cause it include the ones that add elements and the capacity operations `reserve` and `withCapacity`.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>An implementation is not required to move an allocation that it need not move, but a program may not depend on its staying put: the rule is stated so that an implementation backing a `[` `]` `.` `&lt;` _T_ `&gt;` with a packed allocation is conforming without having to track which references are live. That is the same reason the loop rule is structural rather than counted &mdash; the alternative would make growth depend on discovering every reference into the container.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The rules divide this way because they can detect different things. A length comparison can see `push` but never `reserve`, which changes an allocation's capacity while leaving its length alone; a relocation test sees exactly the event that invalidates a reference, whatever operation caused it. Together they leave no gap: within a loop the length change is refused before anything moves, and outside one a reference that survives a move is caught at its next use. A `reserve` performed inside a reference loop is not itself a length change, so the loop rule does not fire, and the relocation rule then refuses the loop's own reference at its next use.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>Storage that cannot relocate needs no rule beyond the loop rule. A reference to a property of an ordinary object, including an element of an ordinary Array, aliases that property; properties do not move, so such a reference remains valid, and it continues to denote the same property even if the values around it are rearranged. `a.shift()` while a reference to `a[0]` is live is not an error and leaves that reference denoting `a[0]`, whose value the shift has changed &mdash; the same thing the expression `a[0]` has always meant.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The alternative design, counting live borrows and refusing a resize while the count is above zero, was not chosen. A reference reachable only from a suspended generator frame that is never resumed would leave a count that no execution decrements, so a container could become permanently unresizable through no fault of any running code. Both rules here are computed from state the container already keeps &mdash; its length, and whether its allocation has moved &mdash; and neither requires that a reference be discoverable from the container.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>The `SoA` of <emu-xref href="#sec-structure-of-arrays"></emu-xref> is the storage for which these rules are presently normative in full, since it is the container in this specification that relocates: a reference into an `SoA` is a column set and an index (<emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>), growth beyond the current capacity moves every column, and a reference taken before such a growth is invalidated by it. A growable `[` `]` `.` `&lt;` _T_ `&gt;` is governed by the same rule. It has a backing allocation whose capacity is distinct from its length, reported by `capacity` and grown by `reserve`; growth beyond the current capacity, whether by `reserve` or by adding elements, relocates the allocation and invalidates every reference into the array, so a use through one is a *TypeError* exception. Reserving the needed capacity before taking a reference is how a program keeps its references valid across growth.</p>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-capacity-operations">
      <h1>Capacity Operations</h1>
      <p>A growable `[` `]` `.` `&lt;` _T_ `&gt;` has a backing allocation whose capacity is distinct from its length (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-liveness"></emu-xref>). Four operations concern it, and each is defined here on an array with an element type; on an array without one they are absent.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-get-array.prototype.capacity">
        <h1>get Array.prototype.capacity</h1>
        <p>`Array.prototype.capacity` is an accessor property whose set accessor function is *undefined*. Its get accessor function performs the following steps:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _O_ be ? ToObject(*this* value).
          1. If _O_ does not have a [[TypedElement]] internal slot, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If _O_ has a [[TypedExtent]] internal slot, return 𝔽(_O_.[[TypedExtent]]).
          1. Let _len_ be ? LengthOfArrayLike(_O_).
          1. If _O_ has a [[TypedCapacity]] internal slot, return 𝔽(max(_O_.[[TypedCapacity]], _len_)).
          1. Return 𝔽(_len_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>An accessor and not a method: as a method, `a.capacity` answered the function object rather than the count, so `if (a.capacity > 1000)` was silently true and never reported the mistake. A fixed extent returns the extent itself and not the length, since such an array's storage holds exactly that many elements whether or not they have been written.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-toindextype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          ToIndexType (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
          ): either a normal completion containing a mathematical value, or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It checks that _value_ is a count and answers its mathematical value.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _value_ is not a value of the <emu-xref href="#index-type">index type</emu-xref>, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return the mathematical value of _value_.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>A COUNT passed to one of these operations is checked as a count rather than coerced like an ordinary argument. `length` and `capacity` READ at the index type, so a count that could be written as a String and silently converted would make the operations that accept a count disagree with the ones that report one &mdash; `a.reserve("4")` would be accepted while `a.length` could never be a String.</p>
          <p>This is stricter than `ToLength`, which these clauses previously specified: it refuses rather than converts.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-array.prototype.reserve">
        <h1>Array.prototype.reserve ( _n_ )</h1>
        <p>This method grows the backing allocation to hold at least _n_ elements without changing the length. It performs the following steps:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _O_ be ? ToObject(*this* value).
          1. If _O_ does not have a [[TypedElement]] internal slot, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _wanted_ be ? ToIndexType(_n_).
          1. If _O_ has a [[TypedExtent]] internal slot, then
            1. If _wanted_ &gt; _O_.[[TypedExtent]], throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Return *undefined*.
          1. Let _len_ be ? LengthOfArrayLike(_O_).
          1. Let _capacity_ be max(_O_.[[TypedCapacity]], _len_), where a [[TypedCapacity]] that is absent is 0.
          1. If _wanted_ &le; _capacity_, return *undefined*.
          1. If _wanted_ &gt; 2**64 - 1, throw a *RangeError* exception.
          1. Set _O_.[[TypedCapacity]] to _wanted_.
          1. Set _O_.[[TypedGeneration]] to _O_.[[TypedGeneration]] + 1.
          1. Return *undefined*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>A request for room the array already has is a no-op and not an error, so a caller need not know the array's capacity to ask about it &mdash; which is why the comparison starts at the length rather than at zero. Growth increments [[TypedGeneration]], and that is what invalidates a reference into the array; a reserve that does not grow must not increment it, or a borrow into storage that has not moved would be refused.</p>
          <p>The *RangeError* is the range of the <emu-xref href="#index-type">index type</emu-xref>, not a statement about the element type. It is NOT the maximum length of an ordinary Array: that range is allocatable and usable, an array type not being bounded by `ArrayCreate`'s limit. The check is on the growth path only, so a `reserve` within an existing capacity is unaffected.</p>
          <p>An implementation unable to reach a count the index type holds reports it as unimplemented rather than raising here. A *RangeError* says the LANGUAGE forbids the value; an implementation limit is a different statement, and collapsing the two would make a host's inability read as a rule of the language.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-array.prototype.shrinktofit">
        <h1>Array.prototype.shrinkToFit ( )</h1>
        <p>This method releases the backing allocation down to the length. It performs the following steps:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _O_ be ? ToObject(*this* value).
          1. If _O_ does not have a [[TypedElement]] internal slot, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _len_ be ? LengthOfArrayLike(_O_).
          1. Let _capacity_ be max(_O_.[[TypedCapacity]], _len_), where a [[TypedCapacity]] that is absent is 0.
          1. If _capacity_ &le; _len_, return *undefined*.
          1. Set _O_.[[TypedCapacity]] to _len_.
          1. Set _O_.[[TypedGeneration]] to _O_.[[TypedGeneration]] + 1.
          1. Return *undefined*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>It is the mirror of `reserve` and takes its shape from it: asking to release room the array does not have is a no-op rather than an error, so a caller need not know the capacity in order to ask. A fixed `[`_N_`]` `.` `&lt;` _T_ `&gt;` needs no case of its own &mdash; its capacity is its extent and its length is its extent, so it is always already at fit and the step above returns.</p>
          <p>Where it does release, the allocation RELOCATES, so it invalidates every live reference into the array including references to elements that remain (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-liveness"></emu-xref>). That is what distinguishes it from shortening the length: removing an element invalidates the reference to the element removed and leaves the rest readable, because that storage has not moved, while releasing capacity moves all of it. The distinction is worth stating because the two read as the same operation from outside &mdash; both make an array smaller &mdash; and they differ in exactly the way the liveness rules are about.</p>
          <p>This is a BINDING release and not a request an implementation may decline, unlike the similarly named operation in some languages. An optional relocation would make an optional invalidation, and a program correct on one implementation would raise on another; the liveness rules can only describe an operation whose effect on references is fixed.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-array-type-withcapacity">
        <h1>withCapacity ( _n_ )</h1>
        <p>The array type constructor of a growable `[` `]` `.` `&lt;` _T_ `&gt;` has a `withCapacity` method that constructs an empty array of that type with room for _n_ elements. It performs the following steps:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _T_ be the element type of the array type whose constructor is *this* value.
          1. Let _wanted_ be ? ToIndexType(_n_).
          1. If _wanted_ &gt; 2**64 - 1, throw a *RangeError* exception.
          1. Let _A_ be ! ArrayCreate(0).
          1. Set _A_.[[TypedElement]] to _T_.
          1. Set _A_.[[TypedCapacity]] to _wanted_.
          1. Return _A_.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>It reserves rather than fills: the result has length 0, so a zero-filled array of a known length is a fixed `[`_N_`]` `.` `&lt;` _T_ `&gt;` and not this. The array it returns is growable, the capacity being a property of the allocation and not of the type.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-typed-regular-expressions">
    <h1>Typed Regular Expressions</h1>
    <p>A regular expression literal is a compile-time constant whose pattern an engine already parses before the program runs. This clause puts that parse into the type system: the Static Type (<emu-xref href="#sec-static-type-of-an-expression"></emu-xref>) of a regular expression literal records the number and kind of its capture groups and the names of its named groups. This is the core of the design's regular expression types (<emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>); the fuller surface it enables, the typed match result and the group-name checking on it, the typed String methods, the flag-dependent result shapes, pattern composition, and string narrowing by pattern, is that extension's to complete and is specified in terms of this core.</p>

    <p>The Static Type of a regular expression literal is a ~nominal~ Type Record whose [[LibraryName]] is *"RegExp"* and whose [[Arguments]] are two Type Records, the <dfn id="regexp-captures">Captures</dfn> and the <dfn id="regexp-groups">Groups</dfn>, derived from the pattern:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Captures is a ~tuple~ Type Record with one element per capturing group, in the order the groups' opening parentheses appear, each element the type of that capture. Where the pattern has no capturing group, Captures is the empty array type rather than an empty tuple, so that it is the type a bare `[]` denotes (<emu-xref href="#sec-arrays"></emu-xref>).</li>
      <li>Groups is an ~object~ Type Record with one property per named group, its key the group's name and its type the type of that capture. A name that occurs on more than one capturing group, which the pattern grammar admits only across alternatives, contributes one property.</li>
      <li>The type of a capture is the *"string"* ~primitive~ where every path through the pattern that produces a match enters the group exactly once, and the union of *"string"* with the ~void~ type otherwise. A group is in the second case when it is quantified with a minimum of zero, when it lies within a disjunction of more than one alternative, or when it lies within a lookahead or lookbehind, and when any group that encloses it is.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A non-capturing group, a lookaround, and a backreference add no capture and contribute nothing to Captures or Groups. Because the type is a parameterization of the *"RegExp"* library type, it is invariant in Captures and Groups (<emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>): a `RegExp.&lt;Captures, Groups&gt;` annotation whose arguments differ from a literal's inferred shape is a type error. A bare `RegExp`, the raw library type, is the supertype of every such parameterization, so it holds a literal of any shape while a written parameterization does not hold a value of another.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The analysis is syntactic and reads only the pattern, which is why it costs nothing an engine does not already spend: the same parse that reports an invalid pattern yields the capture count, the group names, and the nesting that decides optionality. The rule for optionality is the design's, that a group is required exactly when every matching path enters it once; a group behind `?`, `*`, or `{0,`_n_`}`, a group in one arm of an alternation whose other arm a match may take instead, and a group inside a lookaround are the cases a match may complete without entering, and each widens the capture with the ~void~ type.</p>
      <p>The empty-capture case is written as the array type rather than an empty tuple only to agree with the type a bare `[]` denotes in this grammar, so that the common `RegExp.&lt;[], {}&gt;` annotation holds a no-capture literal. A pattern with captures is a tuple, so its arity is exact and a capture-count mismatch is caught. The flag-dependent result shapes, in particular the `indices` a `d` flag adds and the `match` overload a `g` flag selects, are part of the deferred match-result surface, so the inferred type carries the two arguments the annotation forms in common use write and not a third for the flags.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-generics">
    <h1>Generics</h1>
    <p>A generic declaration introduces parameters that a later application binds to types or values, and each distinct application is a distinct type with its own Type Object and its own specialized body. The core relies on this clause in three places already stated: the deferred applications of <emu-xref href="#sec-deferred-applications"></emu-xref> carry the ~parameter~ Type Records this clause substitutes, the computed constraints of <emu-xref href="#sec-computed-constraints"></emu-xref> bind parameters through the ordering this clause defines, and the invariance step of <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref> is relaxed by the variance this clause declares. The full surface of the design's generics extension, specialized overloads, constraint families, variadic packs, and their inference, builds on the model here and is that extension's to complete (<emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>).</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-generic-parameters-as-values">
      <h1>Parameters as Types and as Values</h1>
      <p>A generic parameter is a ~parameter~ Type Record whose [[Declaration]] is its |TypeParameter|. A type parameter, declared with `extends` or unbounded, denotes that Record in a type position and, in an expression position, the Type Object bound to it, exactly as a type name does. A value parameter, declared with `:` and a value type, is the <dfn id="value-generic-parameter">value generic parameter</dfn> of the design: its argument is a value of the named type rather than a type, and this specification binds it to the literal type of that value (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref>) with the value recoverable as that type's one value. The literal-type binding is primitive and the value is its view, the decision <emu-xref href="#sec-computed-constraints"></emu-xref> records: a body that reads `V` where a value is required, as `2 ** V`, reads the [[Value]] of the literal type, and a body that reads `V` where a type is required, as `Reflect.typeOf(V)`, reads the type itself.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Two applications name one specialization when their arguments are pairwise the same: a type argument under SameType, and a value argument under SameValue, which is the identity the literal type already has, so `A.&lt;0&gt;` and `A.&lt;-0&gt;` are distinct and `A.&lt;NaN&gt;` is usable, following from identity rather than as special cases. A reference value cannot be an argument, since specialization identity would then turn on object identity; the permitted value types are the primitive value types the design lists.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-generic-specialization" type="abstract operation">
      <h1>
        SubstituteType (
          _t_: a Type Record,
          _bindings_: a List of Records each with a [[Declaration]] and a [[Value]],
        ): a Type Record
      </h1>
      <dl class="header">
        <dt>description</dt>
        <dd>It replaces the generic parameters bound by _bindings_ throughout _t_ and evaluates any deferred application thereby closed, the operation full specialization performs at each distinct application.</dd>
      </dl>
      <emu-alg>
        1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~parameter~, then
          1. For each Record _b_ of _bindings_, do
            1. If _b_.[[Declaration]] is _t_.[[Declaration]], return _b_.[[Value]].
          1. Return _t_.
        1. If _t_.[[Kind]] is ~application~, then
          1. Let _args_ be a new empty List.
          1. For each element _a_ of _t_.[[Arguments]], do
            1. If _a_ is a Type Record, append SubstituteType(_a_, _bindings_) to _args_.
            1. Else, append _a_ to _args_.
          1. If some element of _args_ is a Type Record with a ~parameter~ within it, return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~application~, [[Builder]]: _t_.[[Builder]], [[Arguments]]: _args_ }.
          1. Return the [[TypeRecord]] of the Type Object that results from evaluating the deferred application of _t_.[[Builder]] to _args_ by EvaluateBuilderCall, which is a normal completion because _args_ is now closed.
        1. Let _u_ be a Type Record with the same fields as _t_, except that every Type Record reachable from _t_ through its fields and the elements of any List or structured Record those fields hold, without descending into a nested ~application~ or ~parameter~, is replaced by the Type Record that substituting _bindings_ into it yields.
        1. Return CanonicalizeType(_u_).
      </emu-alg>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Substitution and evaluation are one pass: a deferred application becomes evaluable exactly when substitution has closed every parameter it read, at which point <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluatebuildercall"></emu-xref> runs under the budget and memo already specified, so a builder used at two instantiations evaluates twice under two distinct memo keys and interns each result. A value generic contributes its literal type where a type is substituted and that type's value where the body computes, which is the one mechanism seen from two sides. Where substitution leaves a ~parameter~ in place, in the body of a still-generic outer declaration, the result is itself generic and specializes when the outer one does.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-generic-where">
      <h1>The where Clause</h1>
      <p>A generic declaration may carry a `where` clause, written with the |WhereClauses| of <emu-xref href="#sec-where-clauses"></emu-xref> where <emu-xref href="#sec-checked-contracts"></emu-xref> admits them: a compile-time-evaluable Boolean expression over its parameters, checked at each specialization once its parameters are bound. Where the expression is *false* for an application's bindings, that application is a type error, reported against the clause's source. A `where` over value generics, as `where I.every(i =&gt; i &lt; N)` on a variadic pack, is the design's compile-time bound on the values a parameter admits; a `where` that inspects a runtime value rather than a generic parameter is the dependent record type extension's, checked at the boundaries <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref> enumerates rather than at specialization; and a `where` naming `return` on a compile-time-evaluable function is a contract (<emu-xref href="#sec-checked-contracts"></emu-xref>). One syntax carries all three because one rule does: a predicate is evaluated where its subject exists, which is at specialization for a parameter, at a boundary for a value, and at evaluation for a result.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The clause is evaluated by <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref> like any other compile-time expression, so it may call builders and read a value generic's value, and its determinism and budget are those already fixed. A `where` is a check, not an inference: it narrows which applications are legal, and never changes what a parameter is bound to.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-generic-variance">
      <h1>Declaration-Site Variance</h1>
      <p>This clause states what a variance declaration means; a parameter carrying none is invariant, which is the rule <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref> gives and the conservative default. A type parameter is declared covariant by the |VarianceModifier| `out` and contravariant by `in` (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-parameters"></emu-xref>). Where a parameter is covariant, one instantiation is a subtype of another along that position when the argument in the first is a subtype of the argument in the second; where contravariant, when the reverse holds; where neither is declared, the position is invariant, the default of <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>. A covariant parameter is well-formed only where it appears in output positions of the declaration, a method return or a `readonly` field, and a contravariant parameter only in input positions, a method or constructor parameter; a parameter appearing in a position of the wrong polarity is an early error, since the variance would be unsound there for the reason the invariance note gives of a mutable container.</p>
      <emu-table id="table-variance-positions" caption="Positions by Polarity">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Polarity</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A method or function return type</td><td>Output.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A `readonly` field or property type</td><td>Output.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A method, function, or constructor parameter type</td><td>Input.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A non-`readonly` field or property type</td><td>Both, so only an invariant parameter may appear.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An argument to a covariant parameter of another type</td><td>The enclosing polarity.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An argument to a contravariant parameter of another type</td><td>The enclosing polarity inverted.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>
      <emu-note>
        <p>This is the variance of C# and Kotlin, sound because the well-formedness rule confines a covariant parameter to positions a value is read from and a contravariant one to positions a value is written to. A `Producer.&lt;Dog&gt;` is usable as a `Producer.&lt;Animal&gt;` where `Producer` is declared covariant and only produces, which the container invariance of <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref> forbids for a `Map` precisely because a `Map` also consumes. The relation composes with the others: an instantiation is a subtype along a covariant position by the ordinary subtyping of the arguments, so a covariant position holding another generic type carries the polarity inward, which the last two table rows state.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-clause id="sec-variance-static-semantics-early-errors" type="static semantics">
        <h1>Static Semantics: Early Errors</h1>
        <emu-grammar>TypeParameter : VarianceModifier BindingIdentifier TypeParameterConstraint? TypeParameterDefault?</emu-grammar>
        <ul>
          <li>
            It is a Syntax Error if any occurrence of the type named by |BindingIdentifier| within the declaration that introduces this |TypeParameter| is at a position whose polarity, as given by <emu-xref href="#table-variance-positions"></emu-xref>, is not ~both~ and does not match |VarianceModifier|: ~output~ for `out`, ~input~ for `in`.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <emu-note>
          <p>A declaration is a claim rather than a derivation, so it can be wrong, and this is the rule that catches it: `out` on a parameter appearing in a non-`readonly` field is refused, since <emu-xref href="#table-variance-positions"></emu-xref> gives that position ~both~ and only an invariant parameter may stand there. A parameter carrying no |VarianceModifier| is unconstrained by this rule, which is why invariance needs no check.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>
    <emu-clause id="sec-higher-kinded-parameters">
      <h1>Higher-Kinded Parameters</h1>
      <p>A generic parameter with an [[Arity]] of zero stands for a type. A <dfn>higher-kinded parameter</dfn> has an [[Arity]] of _n_ &gt; 0 and stands for a generic declaration of _n_ parameters: something that becomes a type once applied, rather than a type. This clause states how one is declared, what may be bound to it, what applying it means, and how the four clauses above read when a parameter is one (design, higherkindedtypes.md).</p>

      <p>A higher-kinded parameter's arity is written and never inferred. Each hole in its own parameter list contributes one:</p>
      <p>A parameter is higher-kinded when its |BindingIdentifier| is followed by a bracketed list of `_` tokens, as `W&lt;_&gt;` for an [[Arity]] of 1 and `W&lt;_, _&gt;` for 2. The count of tokens is the [[Arity]].</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>`_` is the pattern wildcard of the pattern matching extension, and the reuse is positional in the way `%` already is: `%` is the remainder operator between two operands and the pipeline topic where an operand is expected, and here a type parameter list is a position where a pattern cannot appear. The token means *a hole* at both.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>Within the declaration that introduces it, a higher-kinded parameter may appear only applied. It is not a type, so a type position naming one unapplied is a type error, and the message states the arity the parameter expects. Applied, it is an ordinary application: the parameter is resolved to whatever the enclosing application bound it to, and that is then applied to the given arguments, so `W.&lt;`_X_`&gt;` means the same as writing the bound declaration applied to _X_ directly.</p>

      <p>Before any application has bound it — while the declaration's own interior is checked — a higher-kinded parameter resolves to itself, exactly as a parameter of arity zero does. This is what lets a declaration be checked once rather than once per application.</p>

      <p>A higher-kinded parameter may carry a default, on the same terms as any other parameter and with the same well-formedness rule: a parameter carrying a default may not precede one that does not. This clause adds no exception, so a declaration wanting a defaulted wrapper places it after its required parameters, and `Iterator.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` reads as it did before the parameter existed.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The rule is what decides the parameter's position rather than the other way round. A wrapper defaulting to the identity alias is the least interesting parameter at most use sites, so placing it last costs nothing and keeps every existing annotation naming the type unchanged; a design that needed the exception would be one whose parameter is in the wrong place. Where both forms want names, the second is an alias rather than a second declaration — `type AsyncIterator&lt;`_T_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt; = Iterator&lt;`_T_`, `_R_`, `_N_`, Promise&gt;` — so what is deduplicated is the members, not the names.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>An argument bound to a higher-kinded parameter must be a generic class, interface, or type alias whose parameter count equals the parameter's [[Arity]]. Two failures are distinguished, because they are different mistakes and a single diagnostic naming neither would leave a reader to work out which occurred:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An argument that is not a generic declaration at all is a type error naming the argument and stating that the parameter expects a declaration taking arguments.</li>
        <li>An argument that is a generic declaration of the wrong parameter count is a type error naming both counts.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Specialization (<emu-xref href="#sec-generic-specialization"></emu-xref>) substitutes a bound declaration for the parameter and then applies it, so a specialized type contains no higher-kinded parameter and the resulting application is an ordinary one. Two applications binding different declarations are distinct types on the same terms as any other pair of applications, and interning follows from that with no rule of its own.</p>

      <p>A `where` constraint (<emu-xref href="#sec-generic-where"></emu-xref>) on a higher-kinded parameter constrains its application at a stated argument, as in `where W.&lt;`_T_`&gt; is `Iterable`. A constraint quantified over every argument the parameter could receive is a type error.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The quantified form is refused for a reason of correctness rather than of difficulty, and the reason is this specification's own. Type-position evaluation is metered (<emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref>): a budget of evaluation steps and constructed Type Records bounds it, and exhausting the budget abandons the evaluation and reports. That bounds a *computation*, which either finishes or does not. A constraint quantified over every argument is not a computation but a search over the types that could be supplied, and truncating a search under a budget yields a WRONG answer rather than no answer — whether the constraint held would depend on how much budget remained when the search stopped. A rule whose truth varies with a host-defined limit is not a rule, so the form is refused rather than metered.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>Declared variance (<emu-xref href="#sec-generic-variance"></emu-xref>) applies to a higher-kinded parameter on the same terms as to any other, meaning at the applied form what it means for a parameter of arity zero, and a parameter carrying no annotation is invariant. An application binding one declaration is therefore not a subtype of an application binding another, which is what keeps a synchronous iterator from being accepted where an asynchronous one is expected.</p>

      <p>A higher-kinded parameter read as a value (<emu-xref href="#sec-generic-parameters-as-values"></emu-xref>) is the unapplied declaration bound to it. Applying that value yields the same Type Object the corresponding type-position application yields, since both reach the same interned record.</p>

      <p>A higher-kinded parameter is bound only by explicit application. It is never inferred from an argument's type.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Inference is declined for the reason the quantified constraint is. Recovering _W_ and _T_ from a single argument of type `Promise.&lt;uint8&gt;` admits at least two answers — _W_ is `Promise` and _T_ is `uint8`, or _W_ is `Identity` and _T_ is the whole promise — and both are consistent with the argument. Choosing between them is a search, and this specification meters computation rather than search. Requiring the argument costs a reader one type argument at each application and buys an evaluation that terminates with the same answer under every budget.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-dependent-record-types">
    <h1>Dependent Record Types</h1>
    <p>A type alias declaration may carry `where` clauses, each a predicate over the value the type describes. What they state is what a property's own type cannot: that a credit card and a billing address are present together, or that a postal code's pattern follows from a country. The narrowing form `v is T` (<emu-xref href="#table-narrowing-forms"></emu-xref>) and the boundary checks of <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref> are what this clause makes real.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-where-clauses">
      <h1>The where Clause</h1>
      <p>The |WhereClauses| that <emu-xref href="#sec-type-alias-declarations"></emu-xref> admits after a type alias's |Type| are given by:</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        <ins>WhereClauses[Yield, Await] :
          WhereClause[?Yield, ?Await]
          WhereClauses[?Yield, ?Await] WhereClause[?Yield, ?Await]

        WhereClause[Yield, Await] :
          `where` RefinementPredicate[?Yield, ?Await]

        RefinementPredicate[Yield, Await] :
          AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]
          `if` `(` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `)` `{` RefinementPredicate[?Yield, ?Await] `}`
          `if` `(` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `)` `{` RefinementPredicate[?Yield, ?Await] `}` `else` `{` RefinementPredicate[?Yield, ?Await] `}`</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>A |RefinementPredicate| is evaluated with `this` bound to the value under test, and it holds when ToBoolean of its result is *true*. A `match` expression is an |AssignmentExpression| and so is a predicate like any other, `where match (this.status) { ... }`, and in this position it carries an implicit `default: *false*;` clause: a predicate that matches no clause has answered that the value is not of the type, which is the answer a predicate exists to give. It is therefore exhaustive by construction (<emu-xref href="#sec-match-exhaustiveness"></emu-xref>), and an explicit `default` overrides the implicit one. The conditional form evaluates its |AssignmentExpression| and then the predicate of the branch taken, and holds vacuously where that expression is falsy and no `else` is present. It is an early error for a |RefinementPredicate| not to be compile-time evaluable (<emu-xref href="#sec-iscompiletimeevaluable"></emu-xref>) as a function of `this`, which is what admits the same predicate at compile time, where it decides a constant, and at run time, where it checks a value.</p>

      <p>`type` _N_ `=` _T_ `where` _P_ `;` binds _N_ to the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~parameterized~, [[Base]]: the Type Record _T_ denotes, [[Metadata]]: OrdinaryObjectCreate with a `where` property whose value is _P_ as a function of `this` }. Each further clause refines the type the clauses before it produced, so `_T_ where _A_ where _B_` is the refinement by _B_ of the refinement by _A_ of _T_, and conjunction, subtyping, and membership follow from that nesting rather than from a rule of their own.</p>

      <p>The `where` meta type (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-metadata"></emu-xref>) claims the `where` key. Its `validate` of a value against a constraint is the evaluation of the constraint's `where` with `this` bound to that value; its `subtype` holds where the required constraint is unconstrained, so a predicate is shed, or where both carry the same predicate; its `default` is the unconstrained constraint, and it defines no other hook. A dependent record type therefore admits a value of its base only where every predicate holds of it, is a subtype of its base and sheds its predicates upward freely, and is reached from its base only by a crossing that runs them.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The refinement is <emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>'s, not a kind of its own, which is why nothing here restates layout, defaults, or the relations: a predicate changes no representation, and the branding rule of that clause is already the rule this one wants, that a `Payment` is usable wherever its bare object type is and that going the other way is a check. The design writes a postal code's pattern as `string.&lt;{ pattern: &hellip; }&gt;` in the same document that writes a payment's cross-field rule as `where`, and the two are one mechanism over two bases.</p>
        <p>Because a predicate is a function, the structural equivalence of <emu-xref href="#sec-deferred-applications"></emu-xref> compares it by SameValue, so a dependent record type's identity is its declaration's identity: two modules that write the same `where` text declare two types, where two that write `stringPattern(/^[0-9]{5}$/)` declare one. That asymmetry is deliberate and is not an accident of the encoding. A predicate is code and may close over its scope, so `where this.total &lt; LIMIT` in two modules means two things whenever their `LIMIT` differs; comparing predicate source would make those one type and would be unsound. A regular expression is data and carries no scope, so comparing it structurally is safe. A program that wants one dependent record type across modules declares it once and imports it, as it would a class.</p>
        <p>Conjunction by nesting also gives the diagnostics: a value failing `_T_ where _A_ where _B_` fails at the innermost refinement whose predicate is false, so the clause that rejected it is the clause named.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-is-operator">
      <h1>The is Operator</h1>
      <p>`v is T` is *true* when the value of `v` is of the type _T_ denotes and *false* otherwise. It performs IsOfType (<emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref>) and never throws for a value the type rejects, which is the difference between it and the check a boundary performs: a boundary requires, and `is` asks. Its grammar is given with the conversion operator (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-in-expression-position"></emu-xref>), whose precedence it shares; the pattern matching extension widens its right operand to a full pattern, of which a type is one form (<emu-xref href="#sec-is-pattern"></emu-xref>), changing nothing written before it.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>`is` is the branch a program takes instead of the throw: `if (payment is Payment)` tests what `example(payment)` would have required. Against a dependent record type it runs the predicates, so it is also the only way to ask whether a value the program has been mutating is valid again. Against any other type it is the same structural test every boundary makes, so `v is uint8` and `v is { x: float32 }` are ordinary questions with ordinary answers, and the accessor and higher-order reading of <emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref> is the reading here too.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-discriminated-where-chains">
      <h1>Discriminated where Chains</h1>
      <p>A |RefinementPredicate| is a <dfn>discriminating chain</dfn> in either of two forms. In the conditional form, when: every condition is an equality comparison, `==` or `===`, between one member access on `this` and a compile-time constant, every condition tests the same member; that member's declared type is a union of the ~literal~ types of exactly such constants, or an enum over them; and the chain is total, ending in an `else` or its conditions exhausting that type. In the `match` form, when the subject is one member access on `this` whose declared type is such a union or enum, every clause is unguarded with a pattern that is a constant of that type or an `or` of them, no two clauses name one constant, and the clauses name every constant of the type; each clause is then a branch, its condition being its pattern and its predicate its arm body. Such a chain <dfn>denotes</dfn> a union: one member per branch, and where a branch tests several constants one member per constant, each the type's base with the discriminant narrowed to the constant tested and with each shape assertion of the branch, `this is T` or `this.p is T`, applied by NarrowTo at the asserted path; a branch predicate that is not a shape assertion refines its member as a `where` of its own and contributes no members.</p>
      <p>The denoted union serves the exhaustiveness check of <emu-xref href="#sec-match-exhaustiveness"></emu-xref> and nothing else: the dependent record type remains one ~parameterized~ Type Record, `Reflect.typeOf` reports it, and assignability compares against it. A chain that does not qualify, a nullish or ordering condition, conditions over two members, a chain with no `else` over a type it does not exhaust, denotes nothing but itself and its `match` writes a `default`.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The design's `Address` qualifies, one member tested against two literals with a final `else`, and it denotes exactly the discriminated union its document prints "for reference", so this clause writes down an equivalence the design asserts rather than inventing one. The qualification is syntactic so that no predicate reasoning enters the checker; the alternative was a prover with a budget, and no other rule here asks for one (design, patternmatching.md).</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-refinement-narrowing">
      <h1>Narrowing Through a Refinement</h1>
      <p>Where the Static Type of `v` is a dependent record type whose predicate is the conditional form, and the narrowing facts in force at a position decide that predicate's |AssignmentExpression|, the branch so taken contributes its narrowing facts at that position, reading `this` as `v`: a branch predicate that is a narrowing form of <emu-xref href="#table-narrowing-forms"></emu-xref> over a path rooted at `this` contributes what that form contributes over the corresponding path on `v`, so `this is T` contributes what `v is T` does, `this.p is T` what `v.p is T` does, and `this.data !== undefined` what `v.data !== undefined` does; a conjunction contributes both sides' facts; any other predicate contributes none. The conditional and `match` forms both take their branch this way, and the `match` form's chosen clause is the one whose pattern the facts decide.</p>
      <p>An expression is decided by the narrowing facts when evaluating it is compile-time evaluable given them, reading `this` as `v` and reading a path whose narrowed type is a literal type as that type's [[Value]]. No other means of deciding it is specified: a predicate the facts leave open contributes nothing, and the boundary check stands.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>This is what lets `if (addr.country == 'US')` narrow `addr.postalCode` to `USPostalCode` without the program writing a discriminated union, which is the case the design contrasts its `where` against. The chain is short and each link is already specified: the literal-equality row of <emu-xref href="#table-narrowing-forms"></emu-xref> gives `addr.country` the Static Type `'US'`, that fact makes `this.country == 'US'` a compile-time-evaluable expression over a known value, deciding it selects the `then` branch, and that branch's `this is { postalCode: USPostalCode }` narrows exactly as the `is` would if the program had written it.</p>
        <p>The rule is deliberately not a prover. Where a predicate is not decided, nothing is narrowed and the boundary check runs, which is the design's own account of the case where a `'strict'` name is assigned a `'loose'` mode: the checker cannot know that one pattern's language contains the other's, so the check at the next boundary is what catches it. Sharpening that particular case is the pattern subtype judgment's business (<emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>), not narrowing's.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-primitive-metadata">
    <h1>The Metadata Protocol</h1>
    <p>A metadata parameterization carries a value that <emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref> gives one default meaning: a brand, shed upward freely and crossed downward only at a construction boundary. This clause is what lets that value mean more. A <dfn id="meta-type">meta type</dfn> teaches the checker the semantics of the fields it claims, so that `float32.&lt;{ m: 1, s: -2 }&gt;` is an acceleration whose dimensions combine under multiplication, `float32.&lt;{ bounds: 0.. }&gt;` is a bound that a comparison can narrow, and `decimal128.&lt;{ scale: 2 }&gt;` is a price that rounds where it is stored and not before.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-meta-declarations">
      <h1>Meta Declarations</h1>
      <p>The production below restates the |Declaration| of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-alias-declarations"></emu-xref> and carries the declaration forms the extensions add (<emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        Declaration[Yield, Await] :
          HoistableDeclaration[?Yield, ?Await, ~Default]
          ClassDeclaration[?Yield, ?Await, ~Default]
          LexicalDeclaration[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]
          <ins>TypeAliasDeclaration[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>MetaDeclaration[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>PrimitiveOperatorDeclaration[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>InterfaceDeclaration[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
          <ins>EnumDeclaration[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>

        <ins>MetaDeclaration[Yield, Await] :
          `meta` [no LineTerminator here] TypeName[?Yield, ?Await] TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]? `{` MetaHookList[?Yield, ?Await]? `}`

        MetaHookList[Yield, Await] :
          MetaHook[?Yield, ?Await]
          MetaHookList[?Yield, ?Await] MetaHook[?Yield, ?Await]

        MetaHook[Yield, Await] :
          `default` `=` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `;`
          MethodDefinition[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>

      <p>A |MetaDeclaration| names an object type, the meta type's <dfn id="constraint-shape">constraint shape</dfn>, and supplies hooks for it. The |MethodDefinition|s it may contain are exactly those named in <emu-xref href="#table-meta-hooks"></emu-xref>; a method of any other name is an early error, as is a second declaration for one type, a missing `default` or `subtype`, a `default` whose value is not of the constraint shape, or a hook whose signature does not match the table.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-meta-hooks" caption="The Meta Protocol">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Hook</th><th>Required</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`default`</td><td>Yes</td><td>The unconstrained constraint: what a value carries where it has no field of this meta type.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`subtype(sub, sup)`</td><td>Yes</td><td>Whether a value constrained by `sub` may be received where `sup` is required. This is the meta type's half of the metadata subtype judgment.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`validate(value, constraint)`</td><td>No</td><td>Whether a value of the base satisfies `constraint`. This is the meta type's half of the validation judgment.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`narrow(current, op, value)`</td><td>No</td><td>The constraint that a comparison `op` against `value` establishes, given `current` (<emu-xref href="#sec-metadata-narrowing"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`conversionFactor(from, to)`</td><td>No</td><td>The Number by which a value is multiplied when its constraint is converted from `from` to `to` (<emu-xref href="#sec-metadata-conversion"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`rescale(constraint, factor)`</td><td>No</td><td>The constraint `constraint` denotes once its value has been multiplied by `factor`, so that a bound in metres survives a conversion into kilometres.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`quantize(value, constraint)`</td><td>No</td><td>The value that `value` becomes under the representation `constraint` requires, such as a decimal rounded onto a scale.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`describe(constraint)`</td><td>No</td><td>A String naming the constraint, for a diagnostic.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>Every hook is required to be a pure function of its arguments, compile-time evaluable (<emu-xref href="#sec-iscompiletimeevaluable"></emu-xref>), and deterministic, the obligation <emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref> states of the judgments they compose. It is an early error for a hook not to be compile-time evaluable.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The obligation is what makes one hook serve two phases. `validate` decides a literal at compile time and a value at run time; `quantize` folds a constant and rounds a computed result; `subtype` contributes only to the metadata subtype judgment: the checking pass (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-errors"></emu-xref>) invokes it to decide a pair of portions, the decision is a pure function of its interned operands and is remembered, and the run-time operations that reach the judgment &mdash; a conversion between two parameterizations, an ~any~-typed value arriving at a parameterized position &mdash; consult the remembered relation, invoking the hook only for a pair no check has yet decided. A hook that read the clock or a global would make a program's types depend on when it ran, which <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref> already forbids of everything else in type position.</p>
        <p>`meta` is a contextual keyword under the restriction `is` takes (<emu-xref href="#sec-is-operator"></emu-xref>) and for the same reason: `meta T` on one line is a Syntax Error today, and `meta` alone on a line is an expression statement that must stay one.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-metadata-decomposition">
      <h1>Decomposition and the Judgments</h1>
      <p>A metadata value is drawn from a closed language, given by <emu-xref href="#table-metadata-values"></emu-xref>. The top level is a flat record whose keys are claimed keys; the values under them may nest freely. Two metadata values are equivalent when they are equivalent by the comparison that table gives, and it is that comparison, and not object identity, that interning uses.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-metadata-values" caption="Metadata Values">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Equivalence</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A Number, a String, a Boolean, a BigInt, or *null*</td><td>SameValue, so a NaN is equivalent to a NaN and a negative zero is not equivalent to a positive zero, as literal types already compare.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A pattern, carried as its source and its flags</td><td>Identical source and identical flags.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A Symbol</td><td>SameValue. A registered Symbol is therefore equivalent to itself in every agent; an unregistered one is equivalent only to itself.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A record of metadata values</td><td>The same keys, and equivalent values at each, without regard to order.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A list of metadata values</td><td>The same length, and equivalent values at each index, in order.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A range, of one of the four shapes of <emu-xref href="#sec-ranges"></emu-xref></td><td>The same shape, the same bound at each endpoint the shape has, and SameValue at each endpoint's value.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>Nothing else is a metadata value. A function, an object other than the forms above, and *undefined* are not: absence is the absence of a key rather than a key holding nothing.</p>

      <p>A range is admitted as a VALUE and not as an implementation of `RangeBounds.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`: the four shapes are the whole of what a metadata range may be. A class of a program's own that implements the interface has no structural comparison, could not be written into an expansion artifact, and would give interning no answer, which are the three reasons the language is closed at all. A meta type's constraint shape may still DECLARE such a field as a `RangeBounds.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`, since that is what a hook receives and what a hook returns; what this table closes is the set of values that may appear under it.</p>

      <p>Each endpoint a shape has is a compile-time constant, as every metadata value is, and it is taken at the type the metadata parameterizes rather than at the type its literal would otherwise carry. That normalization is what makes one spelling one type. SameValue under this specification requires its operands to be values of one type (<emu-xref href="#sec-value-types"></emu-xref>), so a `uint8` endpoint and a Number endpoint of one mathematical value are never equivalent, and without the rule `uint8.&lt;{ bounds: 1..=6 }&gt;` written in two modules would intern apart on nothing but the inference context of each.</p>

      <p>An empty range is a metadata value like any other, and it constrains its parameterization to no value at all: a validation admits nothing and the constraint lies below every bound. Equivalence over it remains structural, so two empty ranges with different endpoints are two types, both uninhabited. A range that constrains nothing is the full range, and a meta type whose key means an unconstrained bound says so in its `default`, which is what makes a parameterization that omits the key and one that writes the full range under it one portion by the completion rule below, rather than two spellings of one thing.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The language is closed because three things depend on it and each would break for a different reason. Interning compares metadata rather than identity, which is what makes `brand(uint32, 'UserId')` written in two modules one type with no registry, and a form with no structural comparison would have no answer there. An expansion artifact (<emu-xref href="#sec-expansion-artifact"></emu-xref>) is a serialization of the interning table, so a form that cannot be written down and read back could not appear in one. And a hook receives the metadata, so every form must be presentable to a program.</p>
        <p>The representation is this specification's business and the cost is an implementation's. Metadata is settled where a type is written, so an implementation is expected to compare these forms directly and to materialize an object only where a hook is actually called. Nothing here is intended to cost a running program anything.</p>
        <p>An unregistered Symbol is admitted deliberately, and it is the one form whose equivalence is not structural. That is what it is for: a module that mints a Symbol it neither exports nor registers has a metadata key no other module can write, so its parameterizations are its own, and two of them are one type exactly when they carry that Symbol. A registered Symbol behaves the other way, being the same Symbol in every agent that asks for the key, and is the form to reach for where two libraries must agree. A type whose metadata carries an unregistered Symbol cannot appear in an expansion artifact and loses nothing by it: an artifact carries the concrete types a module graph's public surface produces, and a Symbol deliberately not exported is not part of one. An artifact is an optimization and never a semantics, so a consumer evaluates such a type as it evaluates any other.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A |MetaDeclaration| may instead name a PRIMITIVE type rather than an object type, declaring a <dfn id="base-form-meta-type">base-form meta type</dfn> for it. Such a declaration has no constraint shape and so claims no keys: its hooks receive the WHOLE metadata of a parameterization of that base rather than a portion of it, and the judgments and conversions of this clause consult it in addition to the meta types that claim keys. Because it speaks for every key of such a parameterization, a metadata object whose base carries a base-form meta type is exempt from the unclaimed-key error below. This is how a brand is written: a meta type that constrains a base without naming any field of it.</p>

      <p>A |MetaDeclaration| may declare ONE type parameter, which is bound to the base at each parameterization the meta type governs. It is the name of what the base is, and it exists because a constraint shape whose fields live in the base's own value space cannot otherwise mention it: the shape is a type of its own that other code may name, so it is parameterized like any other. The parameter is never written at a use — `uint8.&lt;{ bounds: 1..=6 }&gt;` binds it to `uint8` because `uint8` is what is being parameterized — and it is the constraint shape that fixes how tightly it must be bounded, a shape holding a `RangeBounds.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` requiring an _T_ that is ordered. A meta type that needs the base only within a hook declares no parameter, since a hook already receives values of the base; a bound expressed in the base's values is what the parameter is for. It is an early error for a |MetaDeclaration| to declare more than one type parameter, or for its constraint shape to take a number of type parameters other than the number the declaration takes.</p>

      <p>A meta type <dfn id="claims">claims</dfn> the property keys of its constraint shape. Claiming is global and flat, and is a property of the declaration rather than of an instantiation, so a parameterized meta type claims its keys once however many bases it governs: it is an early error, reported at the second |MetaDeclaration| rather than at any use, for two meta types to claim one key. A metadata object whose own key no meta type claims is a type error at the parameterization that writes it.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-metadataportion" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          MetadataPortion (
            _m_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _M_: a meta type,
          ): an Object
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the part of the metadata _m_ that _M_ governs.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _p_ be a copy of _M_'s `default`.
          1. If _m_ is an Object, then
            1. For each own property key _k_ of _m_ that _M_ claims, set _p_'s _k_ to _m_'s _k_.
          1. Return _p_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>Throughout this clause, a portion is _M_'s `default` when the two are equivalent as <emu-xref href="#table-metadata-values"></emu-xref> defines, not when they are the same object: MetadataPortion returns a fresh copy, so identity would make every meta type take part in every judgment and a brand refuse its own default.</p>

      <p>The <emu-xref href="#metadata-subtype-judgment">metadata subtype judgment</emu-xref> holds of two metadata values _s_ and _t_ when, for every meta type _M_ declared in the surrounding realm for which MetadataPortion(_s_, _M_) or MetadataPortion(_t_, _M_) is not _M_'s `default`, `subtype` of _M_ holds of MetadataPortion(_s_, _M_) and MetadataPortion(_t_, _M_). The meta types declared in the surrounding realm are those declared as of the judgment's application: when the checking pass applies it, that is after the pass has processed the source text's own `meta` declarations (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-errors"></emu-xref>) and after the evaluation of every source text evaluated before it.</p>

      <p>The <emu-xref href="#metadata-validation-judgment">validation judgment</emu-xref> holds of a value _v_ and a metadata value _m_ when, for every meta type _M_ whose MetadataPortion(_m_, _M_) is not _M_'s `default`, _M_ defines `validate` and it holds of _v_ and that portion. A meta type that constrains and defines no `validate` therefore admits no bare value of the base at all, which is what makes a brand a brand and what makes `const v: Velocity = 10;` a type error until <emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-operator-blocks"></emu-xref> gives `number` a way in.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Decomposition is why one flat object serves every meta type at once: `float32.&lt;{ m: 1, s: -2, bounds: 0.. }&gt;` is read by the dimensions meta type as an acceleration and by the bounds meta type as a non-negative number, each seeing its own keys and the defaults of the rest, and neither seeing the other's. It also fixes what an omitted key means, since a portion is completed from `default` before any hook sees it: `float32.&lt;{ m: 1 }&gt;` is a metre because the remaining exponents default to zero, and a plain `float32` is every meta type's default at once, which is what makes it dimensionless and unbounded rather than untyped. A meta type both of whose portions are its `default` takes no part in a judgment or a conversion, so declaring a meta type cannot change the relation between types that carry none of its metadata, and no hook is required to be reflexive at its own default.</p>
        <p>Claiming is global so that a key means one thing wherever it is written, and the diagnostic is at the declaration because that is where the collision is, not at the unlucky program that parameterized on `m` last. The cost is that a meta type cannot be extended by a third party: an extension ships as its own meta type with its own keys, and the Symbol keys of <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref> are what keep two libraries from colliding at all.</p>
        <p>The `where` of a dependent record type (<emu-xref href="#sec-dependent-record-types"></emu-xref>), the brand, and the string pattern are meta types of this protocol declared by this specification rather than by a program, which is why nothing about them is special-cased above: a brand's `subtype` is the identity of its tag and it defines no `validate`, a pattern's `validate` is a full match and its `subtype` is the judgment <emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref> sanctions, and a `where`'s `validate` runs its predicate while its `subtype` holds only where the required side is unconstrained or carries the same predicate.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-metadata-conversion">
      <h1>Conversion at a Boundary</h1>
      <p>Two parameterizations of one base that differ in their metadata convert by this clause, which is the fourth case of <emu-xref href="#table-implicit-conversions"></emu-xref> and applies at every boundary of <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>, an operator's argument among them.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-convertparameterization" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          ConvertParameterization (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _from_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _to_: an ECMAScript language value,
          ): either a normal completion containing an ECMAScript language value or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It converts _value_, whose metadata is _from_, to the metadata _to_.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _v_ be _value_.
          1. For each meta type _M_ declared in the surrounding realm whose MetadataPortion(_from_, _M_) or MetadataPortion(_to_, _M_) is not _M_'s `default`, do
            1. Let _fp_ be MetadataPortion(_from_, _M_).
            1. Let _tp_ be MetadataPortion(_to_, _M_).
            1. If `subtype` of _M_ holds of _fp_ and _tp_, then
              1. NOTE: _M_ admits the crossing, and requires nothing of the value.
            1. Else if _fp_ is _M_'s `default` and an implicit cast operator into _M_ is declared on the base (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-operator-blocks"></emu-xref>), then
              1. Set _v_ to the result of applying that operator to _v_.
              1. If _M_ defines `validate` and it does not hold of _v_ and _tp_, throw a *TypeError* exception whose message names _M_ and, where _M_ defines `describe`, its description of _tp_.
            1. Else,
              1. Throw a *TypeError* exception whose message names _M_ and, where _M_ defines `describe`, its descriptions of _fp_ and _tp_.
          1. Let _f_ be 1.
          1. For each meta type _M_ declared in the surrounding realm that defines `conversionFactor` and whose MetadataPortion(_from_, _M_) or MetadataPortion(_to_, _M_) is not _M_'s `default`, set _f_ to _f_ &times; the result of `conversionFactor` of _M_ applied to MetadataPortion(_from_, _M_) and MetadataPortion(_to_, _M_).
          1. If _f_ is not 1, set _v_ to _v_ &times; _f_.
          1. For each meta type _M_ declared in the surrounding realm that defines `quantize` and whose MetadataPortion(_to_, _M_) is not _M_'s `default`, set _v_ to the result of `quantize` of _M_ applied to _v_ and MetadataPortion(_to_, _M_).
          1. Return _v_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>Where the required metadata leaves a meta type unpinned, as an operator parameter typed `float32.&lt;D&gt;` leaves every meta type but the dimensions of its receiver, that meta type's portion of _from_ flows to the position rather than the target's: scaled, it is the result of `rescale` of _M_ applied to MetadataPortion(_from_, _M_) and _f_ where _M_ defines `rescale`, and _M_'s `default` where it does not.</p>

      <p>Each meta type gates the crossing independently, and there are exactly two ways through: `subtype` admits it, or the value carries nothing of that meta type and a cast supplies what it lacks. `Meter` reaches `Kilometer` by the first, since the exponents agree and only the ratio differs; a bare `number` reaches `Velocity` only by the second, which is why the design says that without a cast on `number` the assignment is a type error, and why a brand, for which no cast is declared, is reachable only by construction. `validate` is what the second way costs, and it is where a bound is enforced: `subtype` cannot prove an unconstrained value non-negative, so the cast into the bounds meta type checks it.</p>

      <p>The rest runs in the order above: the factor once, as the product over every meta type that contributes one; `rescale` on the portions that flow, so a bound stated in metres arrives stated in kilometres; and `quantize` after the scaling, so a value rounds onto the representation it will be stored in rather than one it passes through. Where every operand is a compile-time constant the whole of it folds and no check survives to run time (<emu-xref href="#sec-check-elision"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`Kilometer(5) + Meter(300)` is the worked case. The parameter is the receiver's dimensions, so the argument's metre constraint must pass `subtype`, which it does since the exponents agree; `conversionFactor` yields 1/1000, so the value becomes 0.3; the argument's bounds are not pinned by the parameter and so flow through `rescale` with that factor, arriving in kilometre space; and the operator body adds 5 and 0.3 with both operands in one unit system, which is the point of doing all of this before the body runs. Had the bound not been rescaled, a sum of bounds would have added a metre-space number to a kilometre-space one and produced a bound that means nothing.</p>
        <p>Taking the factor as a product over the meta types that contribute one, rather than applying each in turn, is a decision this specification makes where the design leaves an order: sequential application would make the result depend on the order meta types were declared in whenever a `rescale` is not linear, and an order-dependent type is the one thing the determinism invariant cannot have. Only the dimensions meta type contributes a factor in the design's own examples, so the two readings agree there and differ only where a second one appears.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-metadata-narrowing">
      <h1>Narrowing a Constraint</h1>
      <p>A comparison against a compile-time constant narrows the metadata of a parameterized value, as <emu-xref href="#table-narrowing-forms"></emu-xref> records. The metadata it narrows to is NarrowMetadata(_m_, _op_, _c_), where _m_ is the value's metadata, _op_ the comparison, and _c_ the constant: the metadata whose portion for each meta type _M_ defining `narrow` is the result of `narrow` of _M_ applied to MetadataPortion(_m_, _M_), the String naming _op_, and _c_, and whose portion for each other meta type is unchanged. The false branch narrows by the negation of _op_, pairing `&gt;=` with `&lt;`, `&gt;` with `&lt;=`, and `==` with `!=`, in both directions.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>This is what lets `if (v &gt;= 0)` give `v` the type `float32.&lt;{ &hellip;, bounds: 0.. }&gt;` inside the branch, and a further `if (v &lt;= 343)` intersect that bound to `0..=343`, so that a `return v` into a type requiring both bounds passes `subtype` and needs no check at all. It is a narrowing and not a proof: a meta type that defines no `narrow` learns nothing from a comparison and keeps the constraint it had, which costs a check at the next boundary and nothing else.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-primitive-operator-blocks">
      <h1>Operators on a Parameterized Primitive</h1>
      <p>A declaration of the form `primitive` _T_ _P_ `{` &hellip; `}`, where _T_ names a primitive type and _P_ is an optional |TypeParameters| constrained by a meta type, or by an application of one where the meta type takes the base as a parameter, declares operators on _T_ for each parameterization its parameters admit, and is the form that <emu-xref href="#sec-user-defined-operators"></emu-xref> allows an extension to supply. Its grammar, and that of the operator definitions within it, is <emu-xref href="#sec-operator-declarations"></emu-xref>'s, which declares the same definitions on a class. What this clause fixes is what those definitions mean for metadata, which is this specification's to say because the metadata is.</p>

      <p>An operator body evaluates on raw values: no operator declared by any block is re-entered within one, so `return this + rhs;` inside `operator+` is the primitive addition rather than itself. The metadata of a result comes from the return type annotations alone. For one operator invocation: at most one definition with a body may match, and two that do is an early error; any number of definitions without a body may match, each contributing its own meta type's portion of the result through its return type; where no definition with a body matches, the primitive operation runs; and the portions the matching return types evaluate to are merged into one flat metadata object, each meta type contributing its `default` where no matching definition mentions it.</p>

      <p>Such a declaration may also define an <dfn id="implicit-cast-operator">implicit cast operator</dfn>, written as an operator whose name is a parameterization of the primitive, which supplies the conversion of <emu-xref href="#table-implicit-conversions"></emu-xref> from the bare primitive into a parameterization. At a boundary, one is invoked for each meta type the required metadata constrains and the supplied value's does not, and its absence is why such a boundary is otherwise a type error: `const v: Velocity = 10;` compiles exactly where `number` declares a cast into the dimensions meta type, and a bare number reaches a bounded type only through a cast whose boundary runs `validate`.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A cast's declared parameterization names what its result BECOMES, and is not a boundary its body must already satisfy. The raw-body rule above applies to a cast as it does to any other operator, so the body evaluates on raw values and the usual whole of one is `return this;`: its result is taken AT the target rather than checked against it. Reading the annotation as a boundary instead makes the plainest cast anyone will write fail inside its own body, because the return would re-enter the very crossing the cast defines - which is the case the raw-body rule exists to prevent, arriving from the other direction.</p>
        <p>What a cast does not do is replace the check. Its result reaches the boundary as any other value does, so a `validate` hook still judges it: a cast is how a value gets IN, not a way past what the metadata requires. A bounds meta type therefore still refuses an out-of-range value a cast produced, which is why `const v: Velocity = 10;` and a bounded type behave differently though both cross through one.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The raw-body rule is what makes the whole protocol terminate: an operator body is the definition of the operation, so an expression inside it cannot mean the thing being defined. It is also why the boundary conversion of <emu-xref href="#sec-metadata-conversion"></emu-xref> happens before the body and not within it: a body sees operands already converted, quantized, and in one unit system, and needs to know nothing about metadata to be correct.</p>
        <p>Splitting a value definition from metadata-only definitions is what lets two meta types share an operator without either knowing of the other. The dimensions block computes the sum and says the exponents are unchanged; the bounds block says the sum's bounds are the sum of the bounds and computes nothing; the result carries both. A meta type that has nothing to say about an operator says nothing, and its portion of that result is its `default`, which is the correct answer for a constraint that the operation does not preserve.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-decorators">
    <h1>Declaration Reflection and Decorators</h1>
    <p>The reflection of <emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-getreflection"></emu-xref> reads a type. This clause reads a <em>declaration</em>: the fields a class declares, the parameters a function takes, the enumerators an enum lists, and what a decorator has recorded about any of them. The two are one facility under one name, selected by a first type argument, and they answer different questions: a type is shared by everything of that shape, and a declaration is the one place a program wrote it down.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-token-records">
      <h1>Token Records</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="token-record">Token Record</dfn> is one token of source, as the lexical grammar produced it. It has the fields of <emu-xref href="#table-token-record"></emu-xref>.</p>
      <p>Tokens are what a decorator reads of the syntax it decorates, and what a <emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-decorators">replacement decorator</emu-xref> returns. They are deliberately below a syntax tree: the lexical grammar of this specification is already normative and changes far more slowly than the syntactic grammar, so a token vocabulary is a surface that can be exposed without inventing one.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-token-record" caption="Token Record Fields">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>[[Kind]]</td><td>~identifier~, ~punctuator~, ~numeric~, ~string~, ~template~, ~regexp~, or ~group~</td><td>Which lexical form the token is.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[Value]]</td><td>a String</td><td>The token's source text. For a ~group~, the opening delimiter.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[Span]]</td><td>a Span Record</td><td>Where the token came from.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[Tokens]]</td><td>a List of Token Records or ~empty~</td><td>For a ~group~, the tokens it delimits. ~empty~ otherwise.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[LineTerminatorBefore]]</td><td>a Boolean</td><td>Whether a |LineTerminator| preceded the token in its source.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>[[LineTerminatorBefore]] is carried because <b>a LineTerminator is semantically significant</b>: automatic semicolon insertion depends on it, so text produced from tokens that emitted a space where the source had a newline would join a statement to the one above it. A separator is enough to keep two tokens from merging; it is not enough to preserve what they mean.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>~regexp~ and ~punctuator~ are distinct kinds because the lexical grammar of this specification is not context-free: |InputElementDiv| and |InputElementRegExp| resolve `/` differently, so a token stream is parse-informed rather than purely lexical. A token a replacement decorator PRODUCES must therefore say which it means, and the kind carries it — the ambiguity is resolved by construction rather than by re-scanning the output.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Delimited runs are grouped rather than flat, so a macro cannot produce unbalanced output: the delimiters of a ~group~ are the record's, not two tokens a producer must remember to pair. The engine already matches delimiters to find a decoration's boundary, so the grouping costs nothing it was not doing.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A <dfn id="span-record">Span Record</dfn> says where a token came from. It has the fields of <emu-xref href="#table-span-record"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-span-record" caption="Span Record Fields">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>[[Source]]</td><td>a Source Reference Record</td><td>Which buffer the token's text is in.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[Start]]</td><td>a non-negative integer</td><td>The index of the token's first code unit in that buffer.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[End]]</td><td>a non-negative integer</td><td>The index after the token's last code unit.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>A <dfn id="source-reference-record">Source Reference Record</dfn> names a buffer. It has the fields of <emu-xref href="#table-source-reference-record"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-source-reference-record" caption="Source Reference Record Fields">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>[[URL]]</td><td>a String or ~empty~</td><td>The module's URL, for a token from written source.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[Macro]]</td><td>a String or ~empty~</td><td>The replacement decorator that produced the token, or ~empty~ for one that was written.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>[[Generation]]</td><td>a non-negative integer</td><td>0 for written source; _n_ for the _n_th expansion that produced the token.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A Span Record is not a source map. It says where ONE token came from, and it travels with that token wherever a replacement decorator moves it, so a chain of expansions needs no composition step: a token copied by one decorator and relocated by another still names the position a program was written at. The alternative — an offset map per expansion, composed across a chain — is where source-map correctness usually fails, and it fails silently.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-tokensof" type="abstract operation">
      <h1>
        TokensOf (
          _node_: a Parse Node,
        ): a List of Token Records
      </h1>
      <dl class="header">
        <dt>description</dt>
        <dd>It returns the tokens of _node_'s source text, with each token's [[Span]] naming the position it was written at. Delimited runs are returned as ~group~ tokens rather than as separate delimiters.</dd>
      </dl>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _text_ be the source text matched by _node_.
        1. Let _source_ be the Source Reference Record for the buffer containing _text_.
        1. Let _tokens_ be a new empty List.
        1. For each token _t_ matched by the lexical grammar over _text_, in order, do
          1. Let _span_ be a Span Record with [[Source]] _source_, [[Start]] the index of _t_'s first code unit in that buffer, and [[End]] the index after _t_'s last code unit.
          1. If _t_ opens a run delimited by `{`, `[`, or `(`, then
            1. Let _inner_ be the result of performing this operation over the tokens the run delimits.
            1. Append a Token Record with [[Kind]] ~group~, [[Value]] _t_'s source text, [[Span]] _span_, and [[Tokens]] _inner_ to _tokens_.
            1. Advance past the run.
          1. Else,
            1. Append a Token Record with [[Kind]] the lexical form of _t_, [[Value]] _t_'s source text, [[Span]] _span_, and [[Tokens]] ~empty~ to _tokens_.
        1. Return _tokens_.
      </emu-alg>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The lexical goal symbol at each position is the one the enclosing parse used, so `/` is already resolved to ~punctuator~ or ~regexp~ and no ambiguity reaches the caller.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-tokenstream-objects">
      <h1>TokenStream Objects</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="tokenstream-object">TokenStream</dfn> is an Array exotic object whose elements are Token Records exposed as objects, and whose prototype is %TokenStream.prototype%. It is what a reflection's syntax-valued fields hold and what a <emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-decorators">replacement decorator</emu-xref> exchanges.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-tokenstream.prototype.tostring">
        <h1>TokenStream.prototype.toString ( )</h1>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _stream_ be the *this* value.
          1. Return the source text the tokens of _stream_ were taken from, in order, including the trivia between adjacent tokens of one buffer.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Comments and whitespace are recoverable rather than discarded: consecutive tokens of one buffer have the text between their spans, so the gap IS the trivia. This is why no separate source-text field exists on a reflection — a second field would be a second way to say one thing, and the two would have to agree forever.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>%TokenStream% is the intrinsic object holding the operation below; %TokenStream.prototype% is the prototype of every TokenStream.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-tokenstream.prototype.parse">
        <h1>TokenStream.prototype.parse ( _start_, _end_, _goal_ )</h1>
        <p>Parses a range of this stream's source text and answers its tokens.</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _stream_ be the *this* value.
          1. If _stream_ is not a TokenStream, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _source_ be the [[Source]] of the [[Span]] of the first element of _stream_'s [[TokenRecords]]; if there is none, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _text_ be _source_.[[Text]].
          1. Let _from_ be ? ToIntegerOrInfinity(_start_).
          1. Let _to_ be ? ToIntegerOrInfinity(_end_).
          1. If _from_ &lt; 0, _to_ &gt; the length of _text_, or _from_ &gt; _to_, throw a *RangeError* exception.
          1. If _goal_ is neither *"expression"* nor *"statements"*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _parsed_ be ParseText(the substring of _text_ from _from_ to _to_, |AssignmentExpression| if _goal_ is *"expression"* and |StatementList| otherwise), with the [Yield], [Await], and [Return] parameters and the strictness of the region this stream came from.
          1. If _parsed_ is a List of errors, throw a *SyntaxError* exception whose position is _from_ within _source_.
          1. Return a TokenStream over TokensOf(_parsed_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>This is what lets a replacement decorator define a syntax the implementation does not know. A macro captures its region, reads whatever it likes, and delegates the ranges that are ECMAScript - and what it gets back is threaded from THAT parse, so a |RegularExpressionLiteral| is one token and a |TemplateLiteral| is one token.</p>
          <p>It is the one thing a macro cannot do for itself. Whether `/` begins a regular expression or a division is not decidable lexically: after `}` it depends on whether the brace closed a block or an ObjectLiteral, which needs a parse. A macro re-lexing a range gets four tokens where there is one regular expression, and no way to tell it from a division.</p>
          <p>The context parameters come from where the REGION sits rather than from the caller. `await`, `yield` and `return` are legal only under a context the enclosing parse established, and a macro has nothing sensible to pass - passing anything would let it parse `await` into a synchronous function.</p>
          <p>*"module"* is not a goal: a range of a region is never one, and admitting it would put an ImportDeclaration inside a macro's region.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-tokenstream.gensym" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          TokenStream.gensym (
            _hint_: a String,
          ): a Token Record
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns an ~identifier~ Token Record that cannot collide with any identifier in any source text or in any other stream.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Return a Token Record with [[Kind]] ~identifier~, a [[Value]] derived from _hint_ that is distinct from every identifier produced by any other call and from every |IdentifierName| in any source text, and a [[Span]] whose [[Source]] names the caller.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>This is the hygiene mechanism. A replacement decorator uses it for every identifier it INTRODUCES, so a macro's temporaries cannot capture a binding at the position it is spliced into. It does not address a macro's REFERENCE to an identifier it did not create — a macro naming `Date` still meets a shadowing `Date` — which no mechanism short of syntax contexts on every binding addresses, and which the spans above make detectable even where they do not prevent it.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-preprocessor-modules">
      <h1>Preprocessor Modules</h1>
      <p>*"preprocessor"* is a supported import attribute key: <emu-xref href="#sec-hostgetsupportedimportattributes"></emu-xref> returns a List containing it. A module imported with an <emu-xref href="#importattribute-record">ImportAttribute Record</emu-xref> whose [[Key]] is *"preprocessor"* and whose [[Value]] is *"true"* is a <dfn id="preprocessor-module">preprocessor module</dfn>. Its exports may be used as <emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-decorators">replacement decorators</emu-xref> in the importing module.</p>

      <p>A decoration whose |IdentifierReference| is a name a preprocessor import bound, and which is followed by `{`, takes that brace and its match as a <dfn>region</dfn> rather than decorating an ObjectLiteral. No attribute declares this: being a preprocessor decoration is what makes the braces a region, and `preprocessor` is as lexically visible as any other key - so a tool that does not resolve imports recognises a region exactly as it would have from a dedicated one.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The cost is stated rather than left to be found: a preprocessor name cannot decorate an object LITERAL, so `@m { a: 1 }` is a region for every preprocessor `m`. Parentheses do not restore it, `@name (` beginning an argument list, and the only way out is a second import of the same name without the attribute.</p>
        <p>A decoration on a declaration is unaffected. `@m class C {}` decorates the class, and `@m function f() {}` the function; only a `{` following the decoration directly is displaced.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>Whether the region's text is ECMAScript is the macro's to declare. Where the value the import binds has a `capture` own property whose value is *true*, the region is <dfn>captured</dfn>: its extent is found by matching delimiters, and its text reaches the decorator as tokens of the ordinary lexical grammar. Otherwise the region is a |Block|, parsed, and its tokens are those of that parse.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The engine provides no grammars, so a decorator is not choosing among them. A macro whose region is not ECMAScript - a query language, a markup dialect - captures it, reads its text, and hands back the ranges that ARE ECMAScript through <emu-xref href="#sec-tokenstream.prototype.parse"></emu-xref>. That is what lets a variant of such a language be written without the implementation changing, which naming a grammar could never do.</p>
      </emu-note>

            <p>A preprocessor module is fetched and evaluated before the importing module is parsed. The loader blocks on it as it blocks for a module with top-level await; the preprocessor module itself cannot await, because <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref> gives it nothing asynchronous to name.</p>
      <p>How the macro reaches the parse: the importing module's source is scanned for preprocessor imports before it is parsed, which is possible because an import declaration is at the top of a module and lexes as ordinary ECMAScript. Each module so named is loaded, linked and evaluated, and the value a decoration is spelled with is the export its import BINDS. `import { jsx as h }` declares `@h` and asks the module for `jsx`; a default import, a namespace import and a bare |ModuleSpecifier| introduce no name a decoration can use, so a preprocessor module imported any of those ways provides no replacement decorators.</p>

      <p>The load is <dfn>synchronous</dfn>: an implementation completes it before the parse that is waiting on it, and a host that cannot answer in time is reported at the import rather than waited for. The specification says the loader blocks; what a host unable to block is owed is this.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Blocking here does not require asynchrony. The loading operations call the host and the host calls back, re-entering the graph - a callback arrangement rather than an asynchronous one - so a host that answers before returning resolves the whole graph in time.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A preprocessor module may itself import preprocessor modules, and they are loaded and evaluated before it is parsed, recursively. A <dfn>preprocessor cycle</dfn> - a preprocessor module that imports, directly or transitively, the module importing it - is a Syntax Error, reported at the import that closes the cycle.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is a stronger rule than ECMAScript modules have, which permit cycles, and the difference is worth stating rather than leaving a reader to assume the module graph's behaviour carries over. An ordinary cycle works because evaluation can observe a partially-initialised binding. Here the PARSE depends on the evaluation, and a partial parse is not a thing: to parse _A_ you evaluate its preprocessor _B_, to parse _B_ you evaluate its preprocessor _A_, and to evaluate _A_ you parse _A_. Template Haskell's stage restriction refuses the same shape for the same reason.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A preprocessor module is evaluated in the realm of the module importing it.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Expansion is per-realm, a `constant` expression's cache is keyed per-realm (<emu-xref href="#sec-constant-expressions"></emu-xref>), and Type Objects are interned per-realm. Evaluating a preprocessor elsewhere would put half of that somewhere else, and two realms importing one preprocessor would share a macro object across them.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A preprocessor module and every export of it used as a replacement decorator must be <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability">compile-time evaluable</emu-xref>. It is a Syntax Error otherwise, reported at the declaration that is not evaluable.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Evaluability is the whole of the sandbox. An evaluable function reads only its parameters, constants, and other evaluable functions, so it cannot NAME the clock, randomness, I/O, or ambient state — `Date.now`, `Math.random`, `WeakRef`, and locale-sensitive operations are all unreachable by construction rather than withheld by a realm. Expansion is therefore a pure function of its input, which is what lets an implementation cache an expansion beside the code it compiled to, and what lets a build tool perform an expansion ahead of time and get the answer an implementation would.</p>
        <p>The constraint binds evaluation as well as expansion: a module that could read the network while evaluating would close over what it read, and its decorators would not be pure however pure their bodies were.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Local mutation is evaluable — a Set of seen keys, an accumulator — and shared module-level mutable state is not, which is the same line <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref> already draws for type builders and `where` clauses. A replacement decorator and a type builder are both user code an implementation runs while compiling, and they are one discipline.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-captured-regions">
      <h1>Captured Regions</h1>
      <p>A <dfn>captured region</dfn> is the region a decoration takes where the decorator declared `capture` (<emu-xref href="#sec-preprocessor-modules"></emu-xref>). The region begins at the `{` that follows the decoration, optionally preceded by `do`, and ends at the matching `}` as <emu-xref href="#sec-scanbalancedrun"></emu-xref> finds it. Its contents are not parsed; its text reaches the replacement decorator as tokens of the ordinary lexical grammar, and the decorator reads it however its own syntax requires.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p><emu-xref href="#sec-scanbalancedrun"></emu-xref> counts brackets it does not interpret, so a captured region's text must BALANCE its brackets outside comments and literals. `@m { &lt;p&gt;a } b&lt;/p&gt; }` ends at the brace in the text and not at the one the author meant, which is a property of capturing rather than of any grammar a decorator implements.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>The `do` is optional in every position and means nothing to the decorator: the token stream begins at the region, so `@m do { ... }` and `@m { ... }` deliver the same tokens. Both spellings are available wherever the other is.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`do { }` exists because a `{` beginning an expression is an ObjectLiteral and a block that produces a value needs distinguishing from one. A captured region is parsed as neither - its extent is found by matching delimiters and its contents are never read as ECMAScript - so the distinction does not arise, and requiring `do` would buy nothing.</p>
        <p>It has a cost, which is stated rather than left to be discovered: a preprocessor name's `{` always begins a region, so the decorated object literal form - `@name { a: 1 }` - is unavailable for that name. Importing a name as a preprocessor therefore changes what `@name { ... }` means throughout the module. Parentheses do not restore it - `@name (` begins an argument list, so `@name ({ a: 1 })` is a decoration taking an object as an ARGUMENT - and the only way to decorate an object literal with a preprocessor name is to import it a second time without the attribute.</p>
        <p>A decoration's arguments are read as ECMAScript even where its region is read otherwise: a capture governs the region, not the decoration.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A captured region is never evaluated. It is replaced by whatever its decorator returns, and what a decorator returns is ECMAScript however its region was scanned: <strong>a capture governs ingestion only</strong>. The grammar outside a captured region is unaffected, so `a &lt; b` and a type argument list are read as they always were.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Without this a region must be lexable as ECMAScript to reach a decorator at all, which forecloses most of what a syntax replacement is for. `&lt;` cannot begin an expression, so `&lt;div/&gt;` fails at the `&lt;` before the closing tag is reached - the same failure `&lt; 2` gives, and not the ambiguity the `/` in a closing tag raises. That second ambiguity is real and downstream of it: `a &lt;/div&gt;/` parses as `a` less-than the regular expression `/div&gt;/`.</p>
        <p><emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-decorators"></emu-xref> settles the outgoing direction of that ambiguity, by having a decorator return a token whose kind resolves it. The incoming direction is what a capture settles, and the two are not the same problem: outgoing, the engine is told; incoming, it must decide before it has anything to be told by.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>Because a captured region is found rather than tokenized, an implementation records its source range and the tokens the region produced. Expansion replaces a decoration's target by source range (<emu-xref href="#sec-expansion"></emu-xref>), so a region that is scanned but not recorded cannot be replaced.</p>

      <p>A captured region's text reaches the replacement decorator <strong>as written</strong>. No entity is decoded and no whitespace is normalized, because both are conventions of the embedded language rather than facts about it: a whitespace run that one dialect drops another preserves, and two implementations of the same dialect ship different entity tables. A decorator applies whichever its language defines.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-scanbalancedrun" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          ScanBalancedRun (
            _source_: a String,
            _start_: a non-negative integer,
          ): an integer or ~unbalanced~
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It finds the end of a delimited run whose contents it does not interpret. It is used to find the extent of a captured region, whose contents are not ECMAScript and so cannot be found by scanning tokens.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Assert: The code unit at _start_ within _source_ is one of `(`, `[`, or `{`.
          1. Let _stack_ be a new empty List.
          1. Let _index_ be _start_.
          1. Repeat, while _index_ &lt; the length of _source_,
            1. Let _c_ be the code unit at _index_ within _source_.
            1. If _c_ begins a single-line comment, a multi-line comment, a string literal, or a template literal, then
              1. Set _index_ to the index just past that comment or literal. Within a template literal, each substitution is itself a delimited run and balances.
              1. If no such end exists, return ~unbalanced~.
            1. Else if _c_ is one of `(`, `[`, or `{`, then
              1. Append the matching close delimiter to _stack_.
              1. Set _index_ to _index_ + 1.
            1. Else if _c_ is one of `)`, `]`, or `}`, then
              1. If _stack_ is empty or the last element of _stack_ is not _c_, return ~unbalanced~.
              1. Remove the last element of _stack_.
              1. Set _index_ to _index_ + 1.
              1. If _stack_ is empty, return _index_.
            1. Else,
              1. Set _index_ to _index_ + 1.
          1. Return ~unbalanced~.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Recognising comments and string forms is not optional: a `}` within one is not a delimiter, and counting braces without them ends a region in the wrong place.</p>
          <p>A regular expression is deliberately not recognised, and this is not an omission. Within a captured region the contents are not ECMAScript, so `/` is whatever the decorator says it is - in a JSX-shaped syntax it opens a closing tag - and a `{` within it is a real delimiter. Outside a region the opposite holds, which is why `a &lt;/{/` scans a regular expression and swallows the brace. No single scanner serves both readings, which is the reason a region declares its mode rather than being guessed at.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-replacement-decorators">
      <h1>Replacement Decorators</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="replacement-decorator">replacement decorator</dfn> is a decorator whose name was introduced by a <emu-xref href="#sec-preprocessor-modules">preprocessor module</emu-xref>'s import clause. It receives the tokens of what it decorates and returns tokens that replace them, before the importing module is parsed.</p>

      <h2>The name is decided syntactically</h2>
      <p>A decoration names a replacement decorator if and only if its |IdentifierReference| is an element of ReplacementDecoratorNames of the |Module|.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-static-semantics-replacementdecoratornames" type="sdo">
        <h1>Static Semantics: ReplacementDecoratorNames ( ): a List of Strings</h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the names a module's preprocessor imports introduce. It is computed from the |ImportDeclaration|s alone, so it is available before the module is expanded and cannot be changed by expanding it.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _names_ be a new empty List.
          1. For each |ImportDeclaration| _d_ of the ModuleItemList of this |Module|, do
            1. If _d_ has a |WithClause| with an ImportAttribute Record whose [[Key]] is *"preprocessor"* and whose [[Value]] is *"true"*, then
              1. For each |ImportSpecifier| _i_ of _d_'s |NamedImports|, append the StringValue of _i_'s |ImportedBinding| to _names_.
          1. Return _names_.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Only |NamedImports| contribute. A default import, a namespace import, and a bare |ModuleSpecifier| introduce no name a decoration can be spelled with, so a preprocessor module imported that way provides no replacement decorators.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <h2>Static Semantics: Early Errors</h2>
      <emu-grammar>Module : ModuleBody?</emu-grammar>
      <ul>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if the BoundNames of |ModuleBody| contains any element of ReplacementDecoratorNames of |Module|.</li>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if a decoration whose |IdentifierReference| is an element of ReplacementDecoratorNames of |Module| appears closer to the decorated declaration than a decoration whose |IdentifierReference| is not.</li>
      </ul>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The first makes a name introduced by a preprocessor import unshadowable, which is what lets the second be decided without resolving a binding. Both are computed from the module's own text, so neither depends on anything expansion produces.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The rule is syntactic rather than scoped because a scope query would be circular: a replacement decorator may introduce declarations, so the scope to resolve against is not complete until expansion has finished, which is the thing the query would decide. Fixing the names by inspection breaks the cycle, and the cost is that a replacement decorator cannot be aliased or locally rebound.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A consequence for the decorator's own output: a replacement decorator cannot emit a call to the name it is spelled with. A preprocessor import is an ordinary import declaration carrying an attribute, so the name it introduces is a live binding whose value is the macro, and the first rule above forbids the module binding it to anything else. Emitted code naming it therefore calls the MACRO at run time, and no runtime helper can take the name back - a global of that name is shadowed by the import as it would be by any other. A macro pairing itself with a runtime dispatch gives the two different names, which is what React's automatic runtime does from the other direction when it imports its dispatch as `_jsx`.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <h2>Placement</h2>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Replacement decorators are written outermost because they run first, so source order agrees with execution order between the two kinds. It also gives them the capability the arrangement exists for: a replacement encloses the runtime decorations, so it can rewrite them, or remove them together with what it replaces.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <h2>Arguments</h2>
      <p>The arguments of a replacement decorator are Token Records and are not evaluated. `@f(0)` passes a ~numeric~ token and `@f(Serialize)` passes an ~identifier~ token.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>This is an exception to the rule that decorator expressions are evaluated in document order (<emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-application"></emu-xref>): a replacement decorator runs before the module is parsed, so there is no scope to evaluate an argument against.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <h2>One function per name</h2>
      <p>A replacement decorator name denotes one function. It is not resolved by overload resolution.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Overload resolution selects on argument types, and the arguments of a replacement decorator are all Token Records. There is nothing for a type-directed rule to select on, and a macro that wishes to distinguish its arguments inspects them.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <h2>Frequency</h2>
      <p>A replacement decorator runs once for each decoration, at expansion. This includes a decoration on a block, which a runtime block decorator would run on every entry to that block.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>A replacement block decorator rewrites the block once and may emit whatever per-entry work is wanted inline, so the work happens per entry where the decorator does not. The per-iteration cost a runtime block decorator carries does not arise.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-expansion">
      <h1>Expansion</h1>
      <p><dfn id="expansion">Expansion</dfn> is the phase in which replacement decorators run. It occurs after a module's source text has been scanned far enough to find its decoration boundaries, and before the module is parsed and checked.</p>

      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _names_ be the replacement decorator names of the module (<emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-decorators"></emu-xref>).
        1. Let _depth_ be 0.
        1. Repeat, while some decoration in the module names an element of _names_,
          1. If _depth_ &gt; the implementation's expansion limit, throw a *SyntaxError* exception naming the decoration and _depth_.
          1. Let _d_ be the outermost such decoration in source order.
          1. Let _result_ be ? ApplyReplacementDecorator(_d_).
          1. Replace the tokens _d_ decorated, and _d_ itself, with _result_.
          1. Set _depth_ to _depth_ + 1.
        1. Return the module's tokens.
      </emu-alg>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Expansion is a fixpoint: the tokens a replacement decorator returns may themselves contain decorations, and those are expanded in turn. The order is OUTERMOST first, so a replacement decorator receives the decorations it encloses UNEXPANDED and may rewrite or remove them. The alternative — innermost first — would make an outer decorator unable to remove an inner one, because the inner one would already have run, which is the capability conditional compilation depends on.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Nothing is re-scanned. What a replacement decorator returns is already tokens, so the implementation walks the returned stream for further decorations rather than lexing it. This is why the phase costs one pass over what was produced rather than a re-lex of the region per expansion.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>_names_ is fixed before the loop and nothing in the loop changes it, so a decoration that an expansion introduces cannot name a replacement decorator no import brought in. Such a decoration is an ordinary decorator and is resolved at run time, or is an error if nothing of that name exists. Re-resolving imports mid-expansion would be the alternative, and it is unavailable: <emu-xref href="#sec-preprocessor-modules"></emu-xref> leaves an expansion nothing to fetch with.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The expansion limit is an implementation limit, but a specified one: a program that expands in one implementation and not another because the limits differ is the failure a limit exists to make loud.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-clause id="sec-when-expansion-happens">
        <h1>When Expansion Happens</h1>
        <p>ParseModule is modified: after a module's source text has been scanned and before it is parsed as a |Module|, if ReplacementDecoratorNames of the module is not empty, Expansion is performed and the module is parsed from the resulting token stream rather than from its source text.</p>
        <p>A module whose ReplacementDecoratorNames is empty is parsed unchanged, so a program that uses no replacement decorator observes no phase.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>ReplacementDecoratorNames is a static semantic of the |ImportDeclaration|s, so deciding whether to expand at all needs the module's imports and nothing else — which is what lets an implementation skip the phase entirely for the modules that do not use it, and what lets a loader know from the import clauses alone which modules it must fetch first.</p>
        </emu-note>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Type checking follows parsing, so it sees only expanded code and never an unexpanded decoration. This ordering is normative: a program's validity depends on it, and an implementation that checked before expanding would reject syntax a replacement decorator was about to produce.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-applyreplacementdecorator" type="abstract operation">
      <h1>
        ApplyReplacementDecorator (
          _d_: a decoration,
        ): either a normal completion containing a List of Token Records, or a throw completion
      </h1>
      <dl class="header">
        <dt>description</dt>
        <dd>It calls the replacement decorator _d_ names with the tokens _d_ decorates and its own arguments, and returns the tokens the call produced.</dd>
      </dl>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _f_ be the function _d_'s name denotes.
        1. Let _target_ be the Token Records of the source text from the end of _d_ to the end of the declaration _d_ decorates.
        1. Let _args_ be a List of the Token Records of _d_'s arguments, unevaluated.
        1. Let _result_ be Completion(Call(_f_, *undefined*, « _target_ » followed by _args_)).
        1. If _result_ is an abrupt completion, throw a *SyntaxError* exception whose message is _result_'s value and whose position is _d_.
        1. If _result_ is not a List of Token Records, throw a *SyntaxError* exception.
        1. If _result_ does not match the grammar the position of _d_ requires (<emu-xref href="#sec-syntax-replacement"></emu-xref>), throw a *SyntaxError* exception whose position is given by the [[Span]] of the first token of _result_ that does not.
        1. For each Token Record _t_ of _result_ whose [[Span]].[[Source]].[[Generation]] is 0 and which did not come from _target_, set _t_.[[Span]].[[Source]] to a Source Reference Record with [[URL]] ~empty~, [[Macro]] _d_'s name, and [[Generation]] the current expansion depth.
        1. Return _result_.
      </emu-alg>
      <emu-note>
        <p>_target_ is everything the decoration ENCLOSES, not the declaration alone. <emu-xref href="#sec-expansion"></emu-xref> replaces _d_ together with what it decorates, so any decoration written between them is inside the replaced range - and handing the macro only the declaration would drop those decorations silently, which contradicts a replacement decorator being able to rewrite or remove the runtime decorations it encloses.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A token the decorator COPIED from what it was given keeps the Span it arrived with, so it still names the position a program was written at however many expansions move it. Only a token the decorator CREATED is attributed to the decorator, which is what makes a diagnostic inside generated code point at the macro that generated it rather than at the splice — and why no offset map is needed anywhere: attribution travels on the token.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <emu-note>
        <p>A replacement decorator rejects its input by throwing, which is what a function does to reject its arguments and which cannot be ignored. The exception becomes a Syntax Error at the decoration site carrying the macro's own message, so a decorator applied to something it does not handle says so where it was written.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <emu-note>
        <p>A replacement decorator receives tokens and no reflection context. Every field of a context — a type, metadata, an initializer to add — needs either a resolved type or a running program, and expansion has neither.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-syntax-replacement">
      <h1>Syntax Replacement</h1>
      <p>A replacement decorator is called with the tokens it decorates, a CONTEXT, and its own arguments where it has any - `(tokens, context, args)`. The context is an ordinary object with one own property, `kind`, whose value names the position - the same name its reflection context has, `"Class"`, `"ClassMethod"`, `"IfBlock"` and the rest, with `"Region"` for a captured region. It is frozen, and it carries one other property only where the decorated statement is LABELLED, `label`, which is absent otherwise.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`label` is carried because it is the one syntactic fact the tokens cannot hold. A label PRECEDES the decoration - `lbl:` then `@m` then the statement - so a span reaching back to include it would contain the decoration being expanded.</p>
        <p>Two positions are unreachable for a replacement decorator, though a runtime decorator reaches them: a preprocessor name's `{` always begins a region, so a decoration on a block reports `"Region"` rather than `"Block"`, and one on a `do` block reports `"Region"` rather than `"DoBlock"`. That is the same rule that makes `@m { a: 1 }` a region rather than an ObjectLiteral.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Nothing else, because a replacement decorator receives the TOKENS OF WHAT IT DECORATES. A name, `static`, a `for`'s binding, a match arm's pattern are all in those tokens already, and a field beside a token stream is two ways to say one thing. A runtime decorator needs them in its context because it is handed no tokens.</p>
        <p>Typing the context parameter declares WHERE a decorator applies, and a decoration in another position is then refused where it is written rather than inside the macro. It is optional: a decorator declaring no context is usable wherever its return parses, which is what lets one serve several positions without being told which it is in.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A decorator answers a <dfn>token sequence</dfn>: an array-like of Token Records. Anything else is a Syntax Error, *undefined* included - a decorator ALWAYS replaces, and one that has nothing to change returns the stream it was given, which is a token sequence by this rule.</p>

      <p>An empty sequence is a replacement like any other, and is legal exactly where an empty token sequence parses: it removes a statement or a class member, and does not remove a formal parameter from the middle of a list or a region whose value is wanted.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>What a decorator RECEIVES is a TokenStream and what it RETURNS is a token sequence, and the asymmetry is deliberate. A TokenStream carries spans this specification assigns, and <emu-xref href="#sec-tokenstream-objects"></emu-xref> refuses construction for that reason: a stream a caller built would carry spans locating nothing. So a decorator that rewrites assembles ordinary records, and one that does not returns what it was handed.</p>
        <p>There is no way to decline. A decorator that wants to inspect without rewriting returns its input, and that is the whole of the no-op - which is why *undefined* is refused rather than being read as one: a decorator that fell off the end of a branch would silently erase what it decorated.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>The tokens a replacement decorator receives are those of the region it decorates, produced as ECMAScript unless that region is captured (<emu-xref href="#sec-captured-regions"></emu-xref>). The tokens it returns must parse as the grammatical form the position it decorated requires. It is a Syntax Error otherwise, reported at the returned token whose [[Span]] locates the failure.</p>

      <p>Where the decorated position was a Statement, the replacement text is TERMINATED: a semicolon is appended unless what the decorator returned already ends in one, or in a block. A decorator may therefore return an expression in either position, which is what allows one decorator to serve both without being told which it is in.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is a rule about TEXT rather than about how the returned tokens are read, and it has to be. Expansion replaces a source range and the result is parsed again, so a decorator returning `f()` where a statement stood leaves `f()` against whatever followed it; with no LineTerminator between them, automatic semicolon insertion does not apply and the program does not parse. Reading the return as an ExpressionStatement would not help, because the adjacency is in the text.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p></p>
      <p>Every decorable position may be syntax-replaced, including the positions that do not admit value replacement: a block produces no value but has syntax, and so do a statement, a parameter, a return annotation, an enum, a tuple, a record, and a lexical declaration.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>This is a grammatical constraint where value replacement's is a typed one, and the two are different axes rather than one rule with exceptions. It is a Syntax Error for a decoration on a statement that declares nothing to name anything other than a replacement decorator: a statement has syntax to replace, but a runtime decorator runs when the declaration it decorates is evaluated and there is no declaration there to run at. A position may admit both: a `do` block's value may be replaced by a runtime decorator and its syntax by a replacement decorator, and which a return means is decided by which kind of decorator returned it.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <emu-note>
        <p>An empty result needs no separate permission. It parses where an empty token stream parses — as a statement, or as a class member — and does not where one does not, so removing a decorated statement is legal and removing a parameter from the middle of a list is the same error as returning a class declaration for one.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-decorator-contexts">
      <h1>Reflection Contexts</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="reflection-context">reflection context</dfn> is a type in the `Reflect` namespace naming a kind of declaration or a part of one. `Reflect.getReflection.&lt;`_C_`, `_T_`&gt;(&hellip;)` reflects the part of _T_ that the context _C_ names, and is resolved by the specialized overloads of <emu-xref href="#sec-generics"></emu-xref>: the first argument selects among the signatures and the second is the declaration reflected, which is why one name serves every context with no `any` in it.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-reflection-contexts" caption="Reflection Contexts by Family">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Contexts</th><th>Reflects</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Type</td><td>`Type`</td><td>A type's structure (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-getreflection"></emu-xref>). This is the one context this specification defines; the rest are the decorators extension's.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Layout</td><td>`ClassFieldLayout`</td><td>A field's placement in its class - offset, widths, alignment, and bit position (<emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>). Like `Type`, a reflection context that is not a decorator context: nothing decorates a placement. Shape: <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-class-field-layout"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Class</td><td>`Class`, `ClassField`, `ClassAccessor`, `ClassGetter`, `ClassGetterReturn`, `ClassSetter`, `ClassSetterParameter`, `ClassMethod`, `ClassMethodParameter`, `ClassMethodReturn`, `ClassOperator`, `ClassOperatorParameter`, `ClassOperatorReturn`</td><td>A class and each part of one. A constructor is a `ClassMethod` whose name is *"constructor"*, and its parameters are that method's, so a construct signature needs no context of its own. A constructor is the one method that may not be overloaded, so its `signatures` has exactly one entry: a class body declaring the name twice is a Syntax Error of the base language, which this proposal does not relax, and the arms would in any case have to agree on the instance they initialise and on the `super` call that initialises it. Shape: <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-class"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Function</td><td>`Function`, `FunctionParameter`, `FunctionReturn`</td><td>A function declaration, its parameters, and its return. Shape: <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-function"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Binding</td><td>`Let`, `Const`</td><td>A declared binding. Shape: <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-binding"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Object</td><td>`Object`, `ObjectField`, `ObjectGetter`, `ObjectGetterReturn`, `ObjectSetter`, `ObjectSetterParameter`, `ObjectMethod`, `ObjectMethodParameter`, `ObjectMethodReturn`</td><td>An object literal and its members, keyed on the instance rather than on a declaration, since that is where an object's metadata lives. Shape: <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-object"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Block</td><td>`Block`, `IfBlock`, `ElseIfBlock`, `ElseBlock`, `WhileBlock`, `DoWhileBlock`, `ForBlock`, `ForInBlock`, `ForOfBlock`, `MatchArmBlock`, `DoBlock`, `DoGeneratorBlock`</td><td>A statement's block, and the block of a `match` clause's arm (<emu-xref href="#sec-match-expression"></emu-xref>). These are the one per-evaluation position of this extension: a block decorator runs on every entry to its block rather than once where the declaration is evaluated, which is what makes a block in a loop, or an arm of a `match`, countable. Shape: <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-block"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Enum</td><td>`Enum`, `EnumEnumerator`</td><td>An enum and its enumerators, parameterized by the underlying type. Shape: <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-enum"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Structural</td><td>`Tuple`, `Record`</td><td>A tuple or record declaration. Shape: <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-structural"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>A context that names a set of members has two signatures: one taking no name, returning an object keyed by member name, and one taking a name, returning that member alone. The forms and the options they take are given by <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-retrieval"></emu-xref>.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The contexts are the reason the facility needs no separate function per kind of declaration: `getReflection.&lt;Reflect.ClassField, Point&gt;('x')` and `getReflection.&lt;Reflect.Type&gt;(Point)` are the same call resolved by its selector, and the selector is an ordinary type. It is also the design's own demonstration of specialized overloads, one signature per context, which is why <emu-xref href="#sec-generics"></emu-xref> records that form as the thing this extension needs from it.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-shapes">
      <h1>Reflection Shapes</h1>
      <p><emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-contexts"></emu-xref> says which contexts there are. This clause says what each one answers, field by field. The shape of the one context the core defines, `Reflect.Type`, is given by <emu-xref href="#table-reflection-nodes"></emu-xref> instead and is not repeated here.</p>

      <p>The rules of this clause hold of every reflection; the family clauses that follow give the fields.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-shape-rules">
        <h1>Rules Common to Every Reflection</h1>

        <p>Every reflection has a `kind` property whose value is a String naming the context that produced it, *"ClassField"* for `Reflect.ClassField` and so on for each context of <emu-xref href="#table-reflection-contexts"></emu-xref>. A reader handed a reflection dispatches on it rather than inferring the context from which fields are present, which is what a decorator whose parameter is a union of contexts would otherwise have to do.</p>

        <p>The fields a family clause lists are all of a reflection's properties, and every one is present. An implementation may not omit a field, and it may not add a property beside them: anything an implementation exposes of its own goes under `host`, a single reserved property that is absent unless the implementation puts something there. So every bare property of a reflection is one this specification gives, and an implementation's own vocabulary is under one key a reader can see and ignore.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>A field an implementation may leave out is a field every reader tests for before using, and the tests are not written: a program is written against the implementation in front of it, and its portability is discovered later. Fixing the shape completely is what `java.lang.reflect`, `System.Reflection`, and `inspect` each do, and what makes a reflection worth reading at all.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <p>`Reflect.getReflection` returns a new ordinary object each time it is called. Two calls naming the same declaration return two objects, which are not the same value; a reflection is extensible and is not frozen. Type Objects are interned and a reflection is not (<emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref>), the difference being that a Type Object <em>is</em> the type while a reflection is a report about a declaration.</p>

        <p>A metadata object is the exception and is stable: reflecting one declaration twice reaches the same metadata object, and every decorator applied to one declaration is given that object. That identity is what makes a decorator's write visible to a later reader, and what lets a derived class's metadata inherit from its base's prototypically (<emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-metadata"></emu-xref>).</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Identity belongs to the thing that carries state and not to the report about it. The Block contexts show that it could not have been otherwise: a block decorator runs on every entry to its block, so its reflection is produced once per evaluation and there is nothing for an interning rule to key on. A rule that interned some contexts and not others would be worse than either.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <p>Where a family's reflection has a `metadata` property, the property is present and holds a metadata object even where nothing has written to it; it is never absent and never *null*. The contexts that carry no metadata at all, which are `Reflect.Type` and the Binding, Block, and Structural families of <emu-xref href="#table-reflection-contexts"></emu-xref> as <emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-metadata"></emu-xref> gives them, have no `metadata` property, which is a different thing from an empty one.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Present-and-empty is what keeps a read of a key that nothing set a missing key rather than a *TypeError*. The undecorated declaration is the ordinary input and the decorated one is the tested input, so a shape that is absent on the ordinary input fails where it is least likely to have been tried. It costs one object per declaration rather than one per reflection, since an undecorated derived class inherits its base's metadata object rather than being given a new one.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <p>An `initial` property holds a declared default only where that default is a constant, and *undefined* otherwise. Evaluating a non-constant initializer would run the program's code where the declaration is evaluated rather than where the value is wanted, so the declaration is reported through `initializer` as a TokenStream (<emu-xref href="#sec-tokenstream-objects"></emu-xref>) instead. The two are a value and the expression that produced it rather than two spellings of one thing, and a reflection may carry one, the other, or both.</p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-shape-class">
        <h1>The Class Family</h1>
        <p>Every reflection of this family has the fields <emu-xref href="#table-reflection-shape-class"></emu-xref> gives it, together with the `kind` of <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-rules"></emu-xref>. A `type` field holds a Type Object, an `initial` field is governed by the rule of that clause, and an `initializer` field holds a TokenStream (<emu-xref href="#sec-tokenstream-objects"></emu-xref>) or *undefined*.</p>

        <emu-table id="table-reflection-shape-class" caption="Class Family Reflections">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>`Class`</td><td>`name`, a String or *undefined* for an anonymous class; `type`, the class itself; `abstract`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassField`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String or Symbol; `static`; `private`; `protected`; `readonly`; `initial`; `initializer`; `offset`, a signed integer count of bytes from the start of the instance or *undefined* where the field has no laid-out placement, a negative offset being one that overlaps a base (<emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>); `byteLength`, a non-negative integer or *undefined*; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassAccessor`</td><td>`type`; `name`; `static`; `private`; `protected`; `readonly`; `initial`; `initializer`; `access`, an object with `get` and `set` methods; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassGetter`</td><td>`type`, the getter's function type; `name`; `static`; `private`; `protected`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassGetterReturn`</td><td>`type`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassSetter`</td><td>`type`, the setter's function type; `name`; `static`; `private`; `protected`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassSetterParameter`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String; `initial`; `initializer`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassMethod`</td><td>`type`; `name`; `static`; `private`; `protected`; `abstract`, *true* for a signature with no implementation; `signatures`, a List of signature reflections in declaration order, of length 1 where the method is not overloaded, and absent where the method declares no type at all - a member that annotates nothing reports nothing rather than a signature of all-`any` parameters, an unannotated declaration remaining unannotated for the same reason an unannotated binding remains `any`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassMethodParameter`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String; `index`, a non-negative integer; `initial`; `initializer`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassMethodReturn`</td><td>`type`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassOperator`</td><td>`type`; `operator`, the operator the declaration overloads; `static`; `signatures`, as a method's, a return-type overload appearing as a further entry; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassOperatorParameter`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String; `index`; `initial`; `initializer`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ClassOperatorReturn`</td><td>`type`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </emu-table>

        <p>A <dfn>signature reflection</dfn>, the element of a `signatures` List, has `parameters`, a List of parameter reflections of the family the declaration belongs to, and `return`, the return reflection of that family. A parameter reflection is the same shape wherever it appears: `ClassMethodParameter`, `ClassOperatorParameter`, `ClassSetterParameter`, `FunctionParameter`, and `ObjectMethodParameter` differ in which declaration they reflect and not in what they report, and `ClassSetterParameter` carries no `index` because a setter has exactly one parameter.</p>

        <p>A constructor is reflected as a `ClassMethod` whose `name` is *"constructor"*, and its parameters as that method's, so a construct signature needs no context of its own (<emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-contexts"></emu-xref>).</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>A reflection and the decorator context built from it are not the same object. A context is a reflection with the fields a decorator needs to act rather than to read: `addInitializer` where <emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-contexts"></emu-xref> gives it, and a reference to the enclosing declaration's context, so a field's decorator can reach its class and a parameter's can reach its method. Everything a context reports about the declaration itself is this clause's, which is why the two are specified in one place and not two.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <emu-note>
          <p>`offset` and `byteLength` are *undefined* rather than absent where a field has no laid-out placement, since a field of a class that is not a value type has no offset to report and the shape does not vary with the class it came from. A reader wanting the full placement of a laid-out field, including its bit width and alignment, has <emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>; these two are here because a decorator that reorders or packs fields needs them at the declaration and should not have to reach for a second facility to get them.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-shape-class-field-layout">
        <h1>The Layout Family</h1>
        <p>A `ClassFieldLayout` reflection reports where a field sits in its class, and is reached by `Reflect.getReflection.&lt;Reflect.ClassFieldLayout, `_T_`&gt;(`_name_`)`. It is a *TypeError* exception where _T_ has no layout: asking a class with none for a field's placement is the mistake, as reading `byteLength` from a `string` is (<emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>). A program that does not know should ask `hasLayout` first.</p>

        <emu-table id="table-reflection-shape-class-field-layout" caption="Layout Family Reflections">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>`ClassFieldLayout`</td><td>`name`, the name the slot was declared under, a String or Symbol; `offset`, a signed integer count of bytes from the start of the instance; `byteLength`; `bitLength`, the packed bit size; `alignment`; `offsetBit`, bits from the start of the allocation; `isBitField`.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </emu-table>

        <p>It carries no `metadata`, no `type`, and none of `static`, `private`, or `protected`: those are facts about the declaration and are reported by the `ClassField` reflection (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-class"></emu-xref>), which this one deliberately does not duplicate.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The two are separate contexts because they answer different questions and are wanted by different readers: a decorator asks what a field was declared as, and a serializer or a vertex-attribute descriptor asks where its bytes are. Every system that offers both keeps the same seam - .NET has `FieldInfo` beside `Marshal.OffsetOf`, C's `offsetof` connects to nothing else, and Rust had no stable field-offset reflection at all until `offset_of!`.</p>
          <p>An `accessor` reports the name it was declared under rather than the private field it desugars to: the backing is unnameable, and a slot no program can name would leave a hole in a layout a program is trying to read.</p>
          <p>The two also answer differently for a class with no layout, and both are right. `ClassField` reports `offset` and `byteLength` as *undefined*, because a reflection's shape does not vary with what it reflects (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-rules"></emu-xref>). This one throws, because it exists only to answer a question that has no answer there.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-shape-function">
        <h1>The Function Family</h1>
        <emu-table id="table-reflection-shape-function" caption="Function Family Reflections">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>`Function`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String, a Symbol, or *undefined* where the function is anonymous; `signatures`, a List of signature reflections (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-class"></emu-xref>) in declaration order, of length 1 where the function is not overloaded; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`FunctionParameter`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String; `index`, a non-negative integer; `initial`; `initializer`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`FunctionReturn`</td><td>`type`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </emu-table>

        <emu-note>
          <p>`FunctionParameter` and `FunctionReturn` report one signature's parameter and return, which is what a decorator on a parameter of an overloaded function needs: the decoration is written on one signature and reflects that one. `signatures` on the `Function` reflection is the whole set, so a reader wanting every overload takes it from there and one wanting the decorated position takes the parameter's own.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-shape-binding">
        <h1>The Binding Family</h1>
        <emu-table id="table-reflection-shape-binding" caption="Binding Family Reflections">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>`Let`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String; `initial`; `initializer`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`Const`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String; `initial`; `initializer`.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </emu-table>

        <p>Neither has a `metadata` property, this family being one of those <emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-metadata"></emu-xref> gives none.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>A `const` initialized with a constant always reports an `initial`, where a `let`'s may be *undefined*; the rule is the same one (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-rules"></emu-xref>) and it is the declarations that differ, a `const` with a non-constant initializer being as possible as a `let` with one. The two reflections have one shape rather than two because a reader of a binding wants the same four answers either way.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-shape-object">
        <h1>The Object Family</h1>
        <p>These reflections are reached from an <em>instance</em> rather than from a declaration: `Reflect.getReflection.&lt;Reflect.ObjectField&gt;(`_instance_`, `_name_`)` takes the object itself, where the Class family takes the class. An object literal has no declaration a second evaluation of it would share, so its metadata lives on the instance and so does the reflection that reports it (<emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-contexts"></emu-xref>).</p>

        <emu-table id="table-reflection-shape-object" caption="Object Family Reflections">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>`Object`</td><td>`type`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ObjectField`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String or Symbol; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ObjectGetter`</td><td>`type`, the getter's function type; `name`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ObjectGetterReturn`</td><td>`type`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ObjectSetter`</td><td>`type`, the setter's function type; `name`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ObjectSetterParameter`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String; `initial`; `initializer`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ObjectMethod`</td><td>`type`; `name`; `signatures`, as a class method's (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-class"></emu-xref>); `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ObjectMethodParameter`</td><td>`type`; `name`, a String; `index`; `initial`; `initializer`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ObjectMethodReturn`</td><td>`type`; `metadata`.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </emu-table>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The family mirrors the Class family member for member and reports less of each: an object's members have no `static`, `private`, or `protected` to report, and its fields have no `offset` or `byteLength`, an object literal not being laid out. What is left is the same questions asked of a different kind of container, which is why a decorator written against one family reads almost unchanged against the other.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Two objects of one literal are two instances and reflect separately, so what a decorator wrote about one is not visible through the other. That follows from the metadata living on the instance rather than on a declaration and is the one place this family's answer differs in kind from the Class family's rather than in detail.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-shape-block">
        <h1>The Block Family</h1>
        <p>A block reflection reports the syntax around the block rather than a type: every field but `index` and `async` holds a TokenStream (<emu-xref href="#sec-tokenstream-objects"></emu-xref>) or *undefined*. A block has no type to report, and what a decorator on one wants is what the block is and what governs its running.</p>

        <p>Every reflection of this family has `label`, a String naming the block's label or *undefined* where it has none, and `block`, the tokens the block delimits. A field a particular block form does not have is present and *undefined* rather than absent, as <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-rules"></emu-xref> requires of every reflection: a `for` without an update clause reports `update` as *undefined*, so a reader walks one shape rather than testing which form it was handed.</p>

        <emu-table id="table-reflection-shape-block" caption="Block Family Reflections">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Fields beyond `label` and `block`</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>`Block`</td><td>None.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`IfBlock`</td><td>`condition`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ElseIfBlock`</td><td>`condition`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ElseBlock`</td><td>None.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`WhileBlock`</td><td>`condition`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`DoWhileBlock`</td><td>`condition`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ForBlock`</td><td>`initializer`, `condition`, `update`, each *undefined* where the header omits that clause.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ForInBlock`</td><td>`binding`, the tokens of the binding the head introduces.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`ForOfBlock`</td><td>`binding`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`DoBlock`</td><td>None.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`DoGeneratorBlock`</td><td>`async`, *true* for `async do *` and *false* for `do *`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`MatchArmBlock`</td><td>`subject`, the tokens of the `match`'s argument; `pattern`, *undefined* for a `default` clause; `guard`, *undefined* where the clause is unguarded; `index`, a non-negative integer giving the clause's position among its siblings.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </emu-table>

        <p>No reflection of this family has a `metadata` property, the Block family being one of those <emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-metadata"></emu-xref> gives none.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>A block reflection is produced on every entry to its block (<emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-application"></emu-xref>), so a block in a loop produces one per iteration. That is what a reflection being fresh per call amounts to here, and it is why the family carries no metadata: metadata is written once where a declaration is, and a block has no once.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-shape-enum">
        <h1>The Enum Family</h1>
        <emu-table id="table-reflection-shape-enum" caption="Enum Family Reflections">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>`Enum`</td><td>`type`, the enum type; `name`, a String; `valueType`, the Type Object of the type the enumerators take their values in; `size`, a non-negative integer count of enumerators; `metadata`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`EnumEnumerator`</td><td>`name`, a String; `value`, the enumerator's value, of the enum's `valueType`; `index`, a non-negative integer giving its position in declaration order; `metadata`.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </emu-table>

        <emu-note>
          <p>An enum reflection reports both a `name` and a `type`, as a class's does and for the same reason: the name is what a reader wants directly, and reaching it by reflecting the type and reading its display would be an indirection with nothing to recommend it. An object literal has no `name` to report, which <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-object"></emu-xref> says by omitting one.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <emu-note>
          <p>An enumerator reflection reports its `value` and no `type`: the type is the enum's and is reached through the enum's reflection, where reporting it per enumerator would be the same Type Object repeated once per member. `index` is the declaration order rather than the value, which are different whenever a program assigns values explicitly.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-shape-structural">
        <h1>The Structural Family</h1>
        <emu-table id="table-reflection-shape-structural" caption="Structural Family Reflections">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>`Tuple`</td><td>`type`.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>`Record`</td><td>`type`.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </emu-table>

        <p>Neither has a `metadata` property, this family being one of those <emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-metadata"></emu-xref> gives none.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>These two reflect a composite VALUE where `Reflect.Type` reflects a type, and which of them a decoration selects is decided by the value rather than by the syntax: a composite built over a List selects `Tuple` and one built over a set of keyed members selects `Record`, the same division a composite's own kind already makes. A reader wanting the structure walks `type` with `Reflect.Type` (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-getreflection"></emu-xref>), which is why one field is the whole shape.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-replacement-values">
      <h1>Replacement Values</h1>
      <p>A decorator may return a value that replaces what it decorated. Returning *undefined* replaces nothing, so a decorator that only reads or only writes metadata returns nothing and is done. Where a value is returned, it replaces the decorated target and must be of the type <emu-xref href="#table-replacement-values"></emu-xref> gives that context; a value that is not is a *TypeError*.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-replacement-values" caption="What a Context Admits as a Replacement">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Replaces</th><th>Type admitted</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`Class`</td><td>The class</td><td>The class or a subclass of it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`ClassField`</td><td>The field's initial value</td><td>The field's type</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`ClassAccessor`</td><td>The accessor's get and set pair</td><td>An object with `get` and `set` methods over the accessor's type</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`ClassGetter`</td><td>The getter function</td><td>A function of no parameters returning the getter's type</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`ClassSetter`</td><td>The setter function</td><td>A function of one parameter of the setter's type returning *undefined*</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`ClassMethod`</td><td>The method</td><td>The method's type, signature for signature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`ClassOperator`</td><td>The operator function</td><td>The operator's type, signature for signature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`Function`</td><td>The function</td><td>The function's type, signature for signature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`ObjectGetter`</td><td>The getter function</td><td>As `ClassGetter`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`ObjectSetter`</td><td>The setter function</td><td>As `ClassSetter`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`ObjectMethod`</td><td>The method</td><td>As `ClassMethod`</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`DoBlock`</td><td>The `do` expression's value</td><td>The type of that expression</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`DoGeneratorBlock`</td><td>The `do *` expression's generator</td><td>A Generator of the expression's yield, return, and next types</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>Every other context admits no replacement, and a decorator on one that returns a value other than *undefined* is a *TypeError*. Those are the sub-target contexts, which name a part of a declaration rather than a thing that exists on its own - a parameter, a return - and the structural and binding contexts, and the blocks other than a `do`'s.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The `Class` row admits a subclass because a class type is nominal - a ~nominal~ Type Record (<emu-xref href="#table-type-record-kinds"></emu-xref>) is a subtype along its declared inheritance and not by its shape: a structurally identical class of another declaration is not a value of the decorated class's type, so "the same shape" would not have been a rule an implementation could check, where "the class or something extending it" is.</p>
        <p>The two `do` rows are the reason the block exclusion is stated over what a block produces rather than over blocks: a block produces nothing, so there is nothing to replace, and the exclusion was never about blocks being structural. A `do` expression produces a value and a `do *` produces a generator, so those two have something a replacement could be.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This table and <emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-decorators"></emu-xref> are different axes, and a position may appear in both. A decorator of this clause runs when the declaration is evaluated and returns a VALUE; a replacement decorator runs at expansion and returns SYNTAX. The reasoning that excludes a block here does not carry there: a block produces no value, but it has syntax, and a replacement decorator on one rewrites it.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-addinitializer">
      <h1>addInitializer</h1>
      <p>`addInitializer(`_callback_`)` registers _callback_ to run once the declaration it was registered from is fully decorated. It is a *TypeError* if _callback_ is not callable. The registered callbacks run after every decorator of that declaration has been applied, in the order they were added (<emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-application"></emu-xref>), and their return values are discarded.</p>

      <p>Only the contexts of <emu-xref href="#table-addinitializer"></emu-xref> have an `addInitializer` property. On every other context it is absent, so a decorator written for a position that cannot register one finds nothing to call rather than a call that does nothing.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-addinitializer" caption="Contexts Carrying addInitializer">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Carries it</th><th>Does not</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`Class`, `ClassField`, `ClassAccessor`, `ClassGetter`, `ClassSetter`, `ClassMethod`, `ClassOperator`, `ObjectGetter`, `ObjectSetter`, `ObjectMethod`</td><td>Every other context of <emu-xref href="#table-reflection-contexts"></emu-xref>: the sub-target contexts naming a parameter or a return, `Function`, `ObjectField`, the Binding, Block, Enum, and Structural families, and `Type`.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The list is closed rather than derivable, and `Function` is the case that makes it so: a function declaration admits a replacement (<emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-values"></emu-xref>) and carries no `addInitializer`, so no rule of the form "whatever can be replaced can be initialized" holds. What the ten have in common is a declaration site inside a class or an object literal that something else constructs - an initializer runs at construction, and a free function has no construction to run at.</p>
        <p>`ObjectField` is the other case worth naming: it is a member of an object literal, and the literal is finished when the field's decorator runs, so there is no later moment for a callback to be later than.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Callbacks running after every decorator of the declaration is what lets a decorator register work that depends on what a decorator applied closer to the declaration did, since application is innermost first (<emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-application"></emu-xref>) and registration is not application. Running them in the order added rather than in reverse is the ordinary reading of a queue, and the two orders together mean a stack of decorators applies outward and initializes forward.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-retrieval">
      <h1>Retrieving a Reflection</h1>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflect-getreflectionbyindex">
        <h1>Reflect.getReflectionByIndex</h1>
        <p>`Reflect.getReflectionByIndex.&lt;`_C_`, `_T_`&gt;(&hellip;)` answers the same reflections `Reflect.getReflection` of the same context and arguments would, as a List in declaration order rather than as an object keyed by member name. It exists for the positions a name does not order: a parameter list is read by position, and an object keyed by parameter name loses the order the positions have.</p>
        <p>It is defined for the contexts that name a set of members, and for a context naming exactly one thing it is not defined, there being no order to answer.</p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflection-own-option">
        <h1>The own Option</h1>
        <p>Reflecting the members of a class answers the members it inherits as well as those it declares. Passing `{ own: *true* }` in place of a member name answers only the members the class declares itself. Passing `{ static: *true* }` answers the class's static members; an enumeration with neither option answers its instance members.</p>

        <p>An enumeration answers members of ONE staticness, because its result is keyed by name and a name does not identify a member: `m` and `static m` may both be declared in one class body, and both reach the same owner, a static member's home object being the constructor and an instance member's the prototype whose `constructor` names it. A program wanting both asks twice and merges the results BY VALUE, each reflection carrying its own `name` and `static`; merging them by key would lose one member per shared name, which is the collision the option exists to resolve.</p>

        <p>The enumerated methods of a class include its `constructor` (<emu-xref href="#table-reflection-contexts"></emu-xref>), a class always having one whether or not its body writes it. A program listing the methods it means to expose skips that name.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Inherited by default because a decorator asking what fields a class has usually means all of them: an inherited field is a field of the instance, and a serializer or a validator that skipped it would be wrong in a way its author would not see until a subclass appeared. The option is for the reader that wants the declaration rather than the shape - a documentation generator listing what this class adds, say.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-retrieval-overloaded-targets">
        <h1>Overloaded Targets</h1>
        <p>A parameter of an overloaded function or method cannot be reached by name or by position, since neither says which signature it belongs to. `Reflect.getReflection` and `Reflect.getReflectionByIndex` of the `ClassMethodParameter`, `ClassOperatorParameter`, `FunctionParameter`, and `ObjectMethodParameter` contexts therefore throw a *TypeError* where the target is overloaded; the parameters are read through the `signatures` of the declaration's own reflection, each entry of which carries its own parameter and return reflections.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>This is the rule an ambiguous overloaded reference already follows at a call site, applied to reflection: answering one signature's parameter would be answering a question the caller did not ask, and answering all of them would not be a parameter reflection. Where the target is not overloaded the flat forms are defined and are the shorter way to write the common case.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-reflect-getmetadata">
        <h1>Reflect.getMetadata</h1>
        <p>`Reflect.getMetadata.&lt;`_C_`, `_T_`&gt;(&hellip;)` performs the following steps when called:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _reflection_ be ? `Reflect.getReflection.&lt;`_C_`, `_T_`&gt;` of the same arguments.
          1. If _reflection_ has no `metadata` property, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return _reflection_.`metadata`.
        </emu-alg>
        <p>It is defined only for the contexts whose reflection carries a `metadata` property (<emu-xref href="#sec-decorator-metadata"></emu-xref>), which is the reading half of what a decorator writes. Where the reflection answers a set of members rather than one, the result is an object keyed as that reflection is, holding each member's metadata object.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Sugar over `Reflect.getReflection`, and specified as such rather than independently, so the two cannot answer differently about the same declaration. The metadata object it returns is the stable one of <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-rules"></emu-xref>, so a write through a decorator's context is visible through this and the reverse.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-decorator-metadata">
      <h1>The Metadata Channel</h1>
      <p>A decorator may be written before a TYPE only in a position that has a reflection context of its own, which is a RETURN: a method's, a getter's, an operator's, an abstract method's, and a function's. `Reflect.Type` is a reflection target and not a decorator context - a bare type expression carries no decorator - so a decorator before the type of a binding, a field, a parameter, or an interface member is a Syntax Error rather than a decoration that names nothing.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The rule is stated over positions rather than over spellings because the same text means different things in the two places: in `m(): @f T` the decoration is of the RETURN, whose context is `ClassMethodReturn`, and in `let x: @f T` there is nothing for it to be a decoration OF. Admitting the second and dropping it would read as support for a feature that does not exist.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A reflection context of the Class, Function, Object, and Enum families has a `metadata` property holding the <dfn id="metadata-object">metadata object</dfn> of what it names; the `Type`, Binding, Block, and Structural contexts have none, their reflections carrying no metadata. A metadata object is an instance of the intrinsic interface corresponding to its context, `%ClassMetadata%` for `Reflect.Class`, `%ClassFieldMetadata%` for `Reflect.ClassField`, and so on for each context of those four families; this specification declares the interfaces, and their names are ordinary global bindings of their interned Type Objects, as the built-in type names are, which is what a `partial` declaration over one resolves. A program adds to one by declaring a `partial interface` over it whose members are typed and Symbol-keyed, and the members it adds are the only ones there are: the intrinsic interfaces declare none. An interface rather than a class, because a metadata object must be an ORDINARY OBJECT: the decorators extension has a subclass's metadata inherit its superclass's PROTOTYPICALLY, falling through for a key it does not set and shadowing without mutating for one it does, and an instance of a class with a typed field is not extensible (<emu-xref href="#sec-sealed-classes"></emu-xref>) and so cannot be prototypically linked at all. An interface declares the shape without adding instance state, which keeps the whole set of keys and their types known at compile time - what an implementation needs to specialize access - while leaving a metadata object with only the members something set.</p>

      <p>`Reflect.getMetadata.&lt;`_C_`, `_T_`&gt;(&hellip;)` returns the metadata object that `Reflect.getReflection` of the same context and arguments would report, and is the reading half of what a decorator writes.</p>

      <p>A metadata object is inherited through the prototype chain. Where a derived class does not redeclare an inherited member, the member's metadata object is the base's, so a Symbol lookup finds what the base's decorators wrote. Where it redeclares the member and decorates it, the member's metadata object is a new one whose prototype is the base's, so what the derived class's decorators write shadows the base's without mutating it.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Typed Symbol-keyed members on a partial interface are what make this a channel rather than a property bag: a key cannot collide, because a Symbol cannot; a value cannot be of the wrong type, because the member is declared; and nothing is reachable that was not declared, so a reader of `%ClassFieldMetadata%` sees exactly the vocabulary the program's decorators established. This is the same discipline the metadata protocol takes for a meta type's keys (<emu-xref href="#sec-metadata-decomposition"></emu-xref>), reached from the other direction.</p>
        <p>Prototypal inheritance of the metadata object is what makes `@tag('base')` on a field of `A` visible on `B`'s inherited copy and invisible once `B` redeclares and retags it, without either class knowing of the other. It also means metadata is read the way a property is read, so a key absent from a derived member's own object is not absent but inherited, which is usually what a decorator's reader wants and is worth knowing when it is not.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-decorator-application">
      <h1>Applying a Decorator</h1>
      <p>A decorator is an ordinary function whose last parameter is annotated with a reflection context. Which function a decoration applies is decided by the overload resolution of <emu-xref href="#sec-resolveoverload"></emu-xref> over the arguments written and the context of the position decorated, so `@f`, `@f(0)`, and `@f('a')` may name three declarations of `f` and select among them the way any call does. `@f` and `@f()` are one form: both resolve with no explicit argument, and a signature taking the context alone is preferred over one whose remaining parameters are satisfied by defaults. Where no such signature exists and more than one is satisfied by defaults alone, the decoration is ambiguous and a *TypeError* is thrown where the decorated declaration is evaluated.</p>

      <p>A decorator runs when the declaration it decorates is evaluated, and what it may do there is write to the `metadata` of its context, register an initializer, and return a replacement for what it decorated. The replacement a context admits is given by <emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-values"></emu-xref>, and the initializer registration by <emu-xref href="#sec-addinitializer"></emu-xref>. So is the ORDER, which that extension now fixes in two phases: decorator expressions are evaluated in document order, and decorators are applied INNERMOST FIRST AND IN REVERSE SOURCE ORDER - several on one declaration apply closest-first, a declaration's parameters and return apply before the declaration itself, and members apply before their container, so a class decorator sees a class whose members are already decorated. Registered initializers run after every decorator of that declaration has been applied, in the order they were added (<emu-xref href="#table-extension-hooks"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <p>A block is the exception, and it is the one position in this extension whose decorator runs more than once. A block decorator runs on every <em>entry</em> to its block rather than once where the block was written, so a block inside a loop runs its decorator once per iteration and a block inside a function once per call. This follows from the rule above rather than qualifying it - a block is evaluated each time control reaches it, and a decorator runs when what it decorates is evaluated - but it is worth stating, because the cost is real and is not visible at the decoration.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>What it buys is the reason the family exists: instrumentation, tracing, and scoped resources are per-entry questions, and a decoration that ran once at the declaration could not answer them. What it costs is a context and a call per iteration, which a decorator in a hot loop pays and the reader of the loop cannot see. A <emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-decorators">replacement decorator</emu-xref> on a block runs once, at expansion, and can emit the same instrumentation inline - so the per-iteration work happens where the per-iteration decorator does not.</p>
        <p>This is also why a block reflection carries no metadata and cannot be interned (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-rules"></emu-xref>): both would need a once for the block to have, and it has none.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Types are what remove the `(value, context)` return from a decorator that takes arguments: an erased system cannot tell `@f` from `@f(0)` without one, since it cannot see which `f` is meant, and this proposal resolves it by the annotation on the last parameter. So a decorator looks the same whether or not it takes arguments, and giving one an argument is editing its parameter list rather than rewriting it into a factory.</p>
      </emu-note>
          <p>Both rules above are rules for decorators that run when the declaration they decorate is evaluated. A <emu-xref href="#sec-replacement-decorators">replacement decorator</emu-xref> runs at expansion, and neither applies to it: its arguments are Token Records and are not evaluated, and its name denotes one function rather than a set of overloads.</p>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-declarative-checker-facts">
    <h1>Declarative Checker Facts</h1>
    <p>A signature carries two facts that no rule of the language computes and that a program states instead: the `this` a function expects, and what a call of it establishes about its arguments. They are data the checker reads, never code it runs, which is what distinguishes them from the meta protocol's hooks (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-metadata"></emu-xref>) and keeps them outside the boundary <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref> draws: nothing evaluates, and each is consumed by machinery that already exists.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-this-adoption">
      <h1>The Expected this Type</h1>
      <p>A signature's [[ThisType]] is the type its `this` is required to have. It is contravariant, as a parameter is, so a signature with a [[ThisType]] is usable where one requiring a NARROWER `this` is required and never the reverse. A signature with none supplies no `this` rather than accepting any, so it is usable nowhere a `this` is required at all, and one that has a [[ThisType]] is usable nowhere a `this` is absent (<emu-xref href="#sec-issignaturesubtype"></emu-xref>). A method extracted from its class and called free of it is the case this decides: its `this` is not of the type its body assumes, and the extraction is a type error at the boundary that took it rather than a *TypeError* inside it.</p>

      <p>Where a non-arrow function literal's contextual type (<emu-xref href="#table-contextual-types"></emu-xref>) is a ~function~ type whose applicable signature has a [[ThisType]], the literal adopts it: `this` within the body has that type, and the literal's own signature has that [[ThisType]]. An arrow adopts nothing, since it has no `this` of its own to give a type to, and the `this` it closes over is already typed where it was written.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is the whole of what an erased system's `ThisType&lt;T&gt;` marker does, said as data rather than as a marker the checker recognizes by name. It is also why the design's `options(Data, Methods)` builder is three lines: it constructs an intersection of the data and the methods, and calls `withThisType` to put it on every method's signature, at which point a method written in the object literal has `this` typed as the finished object. Nothing about that reaches into inference; the type is built first and the literal is checked against it, which is the order <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref> requires of everything.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-declared-narrowing">
      <h1>Declared Narrowing</h1>
      <p>A <dfn id="narrowing-record">Narrowing Record</dfn> has a [[Target]], a String naming a parameter of the signature or *"this"*, and a [[Type]], a Type Record. A signature's [[Narrows]] is a List of them, and it says what a call establishes: that the argument passed at [[Target]] is of [[Type]]. Two forms follow from the signature's [[Return]], and no third is defined.</p>

      <p>Where the [[Return]] is `boolean`, the call is a test and its narrowings apply where its result is *true*, exactly as `v is T` applies (<emu-xref href="#table-narrowing-forms"></emu-xref>), so `if (isFish(pet))` narrows `pet` in the branch it guards and narrows it away in the other. Where the [[Return]] is ~void~, the call establishes its narrowings by returning at all, since there is no result to condition on, so they apply in every position the call dominates. Where the [[Return]] is any other type, a non-empty [[Narrows]] is a type error: nothing would consume it.</p>

      <p>A narrowing declared by a signature is checked against nothing. It is a claim the declaration makes and the body is not required to honour, in the way that `v is T` is a question the language answers. This is the same reading a declared type of an unchecked boundary gets, and the same one an erased system's type predicate gets; what makes it sound here is that a value crossing a boundary is checked against its declared type regardless (<emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>), so a narrowing that lied costs a check that fails rather than a value that was never what it claimed.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The two forms are the type predicate and the assertion function of an erased system, and they are one field here rather than two spellings, because the difference between them is not in the fact declared but in what the declaration returns. A function that answers a question returns the answer, and its narrowing applies where the answer was yes; a function that establishes a fact returns nothing, and its narrowing applies once it has returned. Deriving the two from [[Return]] is why no `asserts` operator appears here.</p>
        <p>Neither [[ThisType]] nor [[Narrows]] has a source spelling. A signature acquires them by construction (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-maketype"></emu-xref>), which is how the design's `withThisType` writes one, and a program uses the result by annotating with it: a binding declared of a constructed guard type narrows at every call through it. They join the index signature and the symbol literal type as types this specification admits and no declaration writes (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-maketype"></emu-xref>), and giving them one is a question for the syntax the design has not yet posed, not a gap in what they mean.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-memory-layout">
    <h1>Memory Layout</h1>
    <p>A value type has a defined layout in memory, and this clause defines it. It is what makes a `[`_N_`].&lt;T&gt;` a contiguous buffer rather than a list of references, what gives <emu-xref href="#sec-default-values"></emu-xref>'s zero-fill something to fill, and what a serializer, a placement construction over existing bytes, or a vertex descriptor reads. The default is the rule C, C++, and Rust follow, so a value type class is laid out as the same declaration is in those languages, and the controls below are their `repr` attributes together with the field offsets those languages keep to themselves.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-layout-properties">
      <h1>Layout Properties</h1>
      <p>A Type Object whose type has a layout has three own properties. `bitLength` is the size in bits, which is what an arbitrary-width integer needs in order to describe itself; `byteLength` is the laid-out size in bytes, the bits rounded up to a byte and then to the type's alignment, so that every element of an array of the type is aligned; and `alignment` is the byte alignment. Each has the attributes { [[Writable]]: *false*, [[Enumerable]]: *false*, [[Configurable]]: *false* }, and each is compile-time evaluable (<emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>), so `[Vertex.byteLength * 1024].&lt;uint8&gt;` is an array extent and `T.byteLength` inside a generic is a constant once `T` is specialized.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-layout-by-type" caption="Which Types Have a Layout">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Layout</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A numeric type, `boolean`, or a SIMD vector</td><td>Yes. An `int.&lt;N&gt;` or `uint.&lt;N&gt;` has `bitLength` _N_; a named type has the `bitLength` its width states.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An enum</td><td>Yes, its underlying type's.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A value type class (<emu-xref href="#sec-typed-classes"></emu-xref>)</td><td>Yes, by <emu-xref href="#sec-natural-alignment"></emu-xref>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A fixed-length array `[`_N_`].&lt;T&gt;`</td><td>Yes: _N_ times the `byteLength` of `T`, aligned as `T` is.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A fixed-length `SoA.&lt;T, `_N_`&gt;`</td><td>Yes, but not `T`'s: each field of `T` is a column of _N_ elements, padded and aligned on its own, and the size is the sum of the columns. An element's fields are not adjacent, so there is no interior padding between them and the size may be smaller than the `[`_N_`].&lt;T&gt;` of the same `T`.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A parameterized type</td><td>Yes, its [[Base]]'s exactly (<emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`bigint`, `string`, `any`</td><td>No. The size is a property of the value and not of the type.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A reference type, including a nullable union of a value type class</td><td>No. A reference's width is the implementation's business.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A dynamic-length array `[].&lt;T&gt;`</td><td>No as a type. An instance has a `byteLength`, its length times its element's.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A class with an untyped field, or one declared `dynamic`</td><td>No.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A union of value types</td><td>No. It has no one layout.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>A Type Object whose type has no layout has none of the three properties, so reading one is *undefined* and calling it a size is a *TypeError* rather than a number that hides the mistake.</p>

      <p>A type's `alignment` is its `byteLength` rounded up to a power of two, and there is no upper bound on it: `uint.&lt;4&gt;` aligns to 1, `uint.&lt;24&gt;` to 4, `uint.&lt;128&gt;` to 16, and a `float32x4` to 16. A value type class takes the alignment <emu-xref href="#sec-natural-alignment"></emu-xref> gives it rather than this rule, since its size is a consequence of its fields rather than a width of its own.</p>

      <p>A Type Object has a `family`, a String naming the kind of value the type describes. <emu-xref href="#table-family-operations"></emu-xref> is organised by this concept already, deciding what an operator does by the family of its operands; `family` is how a program asks the same question.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-type-families" caption="Type Families">
        <table>
          <tr><th>`family`</th><th>Types</th></tr>
          <tr><td>*"integer"*</td><td>`uint8` through `uint128`, `int8` through `int128`, the general `uint.&lt;`_N_`&gt;` and `int.&lt;`_N_`&gt;` forms, and `boolean1`, which is a one-bit unsigned integer</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"float"*</td><td>`float16`, `float32`, `float64`, `float128`</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"decimal"*</td><td>`decimal32`, `decimal64`, `decimal128`</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"rational"*</td><td>`rational.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"complex"*</td><td>`complex.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"vector"*</td><td>every SIMD type, `vector.&lt;`_T_`, `_N_`&gt;`, and the bit-vector `boolean`_N_ forms for _N_ above one, `boolean8` being `vector.&lt;boolean1, 8&gt;`</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"bigint"*</td><td>`bigint`</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"string"*</td><td>`string`</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"boolean"*</td><td>`boolean`, the ordinary two-valued type</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"type"*</td><td>the `type` type</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"class"*</td><td>a class or an interface</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"enum"*</td><td>an enum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>*"any"*, *"void"*, *"never"*</td><td>each of those types</td></tr>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>A type carrying metadata reports the family of the type it parameterizes, so `float32.&lt;{ m: 1 }&gt;` is *"float"* and `uint8.&lt;{ bounds: 1..=6 }&gt;` is *"integer"*. A parameterization refines the type it parameterizes rather than replacing it (<emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>), and reporting otherwise would put `family` at odds with `instanceof`, with the operator table, and with `bitLength` beside it, each of which already answers about the parameterized type.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`family` names the family and nothing further. For a class it is *"class"*, which is the name of the family and not of the declaration: a nominal type never exposes its declaration (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-object-opacity"></emu-xref>), and this exposes none of its fields, its name, or its shape.</p>
        <p>A program wanting the metadata a type carries rather than the family it belongs to reflects, where the *"parameterized"* node gives the `base` and the `metadata` (<emu-xref href="#table-reflection-nodes"></emu-xref>). The two are different questions - what kind of value this is, and what is known about it besides - and are answered in the two places that already describe them.</p>
        <p>`family` is not a substitute for `is`, for `instanceof`, or for `Reflect.isAssignable`. Whether a value may be stored at a type is those operations' question, and this proposal performs no implicit numeric widening, so two types of one family are no more assignable to each other than two of different families are.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A Type Object has a `min` and a `max`, the least and the greatest value the type admits. They are the answer to the question a range check asks and a saturating operation obeys, and they are determined in this order.</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. If the type carries `bounds` metadata (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-metadata"></emu-xref>), let _r_ be that range. `min` is the least value of _r_ and `max` is the greatest, each being a value the range ADMITS: an exclusive endpoint reports the last value on its side rather than the endpoint itself, so a `bounds` of `0` `..` `10` over an integer type has a `max` of 9 where `0` `..=` `10` has 10.
        1. Else if the type is an integer type, let _w_ be the type's width in bits. `min` and `max` are the least and greatest values that width admits: 0 and 2<sup>_w_</sup> - 1 unsigned, and -2<sup>_w_-1</sup> and 2<sup>_w_-1</sup> - 1 signed. They are Numbers where _w_ is 53 or fewer and BigInts above it, a wider integer having values no Number holds exactly.
        1. Else if the type is a binary floating-point type, `min` and `max` are the finite extremes of its format: `min` is the MOST NEGATIVE finite value and not the smallest positive one, and `max` is its negation.
        1. Else, reading either throws a *TypeError* exception, as reading `byteLength` from a `string` does.
      </emu-alg>

      <p>A binary floating-point type additionally has a `minPositive`, the least positive value its format represents, and an `epsilon`, the difference between 1 and the next value above it. An integer type has neither, rather than having them as *undefined*.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The order of the first two steps is the substance: a refinement narrows what a type admits, so a narrowed type reports the narrowed answer, and a program asking what values a type holds gets one answer from one member whether the constraint came from the width or from the declaration. Without it, every reader that wants the question answered keeps a table of widths beside the code that reads the metadata, and the two say the same thing in two places.</p>
        <p>These are `min` and `max` and not `MIN_VALUE` and `MAX_VALUE`, though the Number properties of those names are the nearest existing thing, because `Number.MIN_VALUE` is the least POSITIVE Number rather than the least: the most negative is `-Number.MAX_VALUE`. A `float32.MIN_VALUE` would have two defensible readings and no way for a reader to tell which was meant, and the integer and floating-point families would disagree about what the one name said. The lowercase names also match the members already on a Type Object, which are facts about the type rather than constants of a namespace, and `minPositive` gives the other reading a name of its own.</p>
        <p>`min` and `max` report values and carry no exclusivity of their own. A program that needs the endpoints as they were WRITTEN, which a schema emitter does in order to distinguish an exclusive bound from an inclusive one over the value below it, reads the range from the `bounds` metadata, where it remains whole. The two are not alternatives: the members are a projection for the reader who wants the extremes, and the metadata is there for the reader who wants the range.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
              <p>A Type Object has a `hasLayout` property, *true* where the type has a layout and *false* where it has none. Reading `byteLength`, `bitLength`, or `alignment` from a type with no layout throws a *TypeError* exception, which is right for a program that believes it has one; `hasLayout` is for the program that is asking rather than asserting, and answers without throwing.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A generic serializer walking a heterogeneous structure asks this of every type it meets, and without a predicate its only route is to read one of the three and catch - control flow by exception for a question that is not a mistake, and a `catch` that swallows a misspelled type along with the answer. Testing for the property does not substitute: `'byteLength' in `_T_ is *true* for every type, the property being present and throwing when read.</p>
        <p>`hasLayout` sits beside the three it guards rather than in a function of its own, so it is compile-time evaluable for the same reason they are - which is what a serializer wants, the branch being one it would rather have folded away than taken. Whether a FIELD has a placement needs nothing new: its `offset` is *undefined* where it has none (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-class"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-note>

<p>This is the rule of C and of Rust, which the layout of this clause follows: each scalar is aligned to its own size, and Rust's `u128` is 16-byte aligned in order to match C. Capping the alignment at 8 was considered and is not what this specification does, because it would make two types of one width disagree for no reason a program could observe - a 16-byte integer aligned at 8 beside a 16-byte SIMD vector aligned at 16 - and would need the vector to be stated as an exception. Uncapped, the vector is not an exception: a width that is already a power of two rounds up to itself. Raising an alignment past the natural one is what `align` is for (<emu-xref href="#sec-layout-control"></emu-xref>), as `#[repr(align(N))]` is in Rust, and lowering it to 1 is what `packed` is for.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>These are properties of an interned Type Object rather than a `sizeof` operator, which is why they need no grammar and work wherever a type does, including on the result of `Reflect.typeOf`. The layout they report is the declared one, so the controls of <emu-xref href="#sec-layout-control"></emu-xref> are accounted for in it: `byteLength` is what the program asked for, not what an implementation would have chosen.</p>
        <p>The alignment of a named numeric type is its `byteLength`. For an `int.&lt;N&gt;` or `uint.&lt;N&gt;` of a width no named type has, the alignment is the smallest power of two that is at least the bits rounded up to a byte, and at most 8; the design fixes only `uint.&lt;4&gt;`, whose `byteLength` is 1, and this rule agrees with it and with every named width. A width above 64 therefore aligns to 8 and pads to a multiple of it, which is the only reading on which an array of them stays aligned.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-natural-alignment">
      <h1>Natural Alignment and Padding</h1>
      <p>The layout of a value type class places its fields in declaration order. Let a bit cursor begin at 0, at the end of the layout of the class it extends where it extends one and at 0 otherwise. For each field in order:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Where the field's type has a `bitLength` under 8, it is a <dfn id="bit-field">bit-field</dfn>: it is placed at the cursor, or at the bit its `offsetBit` names, and the cursor advances by its `bitLength`.</li>
        <li>Otherwise the cursor is first rounded up to a byte. The field is placed at the byte its `offset` names, where it has one; at the cursor, where the class is packed; and at the next multiple of its alignment otherwise, that alignment being the one its `align` names where it has one and its type's otherwise. The cursor advances to the end of the field.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The class's alignment is the one its `alignAll` names, or 1 where it is packed, or the largest alignment among its fields otherwise. Its `bitLength` is the cursor's furthest extent, and its `byteLength` is the size its `size` names, or that extent rounded up to a byte and then to the class's alignment. It is a type error for a field to be placed outside the size a `size` fixes, or for an inherited field to be redeclared, since the redeclaration would have no defined offset.</p>

      <p>A field's offset is read through the reflection of its declaration rather than from the type, because it belongs to the field: `Reflect.getReflection.&lt;Reflect.ClassFieldLayout, T&gt;(`_name_`)` reports its placement, compile-time evaluable for the reason the layout is (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflection-shape-class-field-layout"></emu-xref>). The `ClassField` context beside it reports what the field was DECLARED as and carries `offset` and `byteLength` because those two are what a decorator commonly wants; the bit-level placement is meaningful only for a class that has a layout at all, which is why the two are separate contexts rather than one. This is the `offsetof` a serializer, a placement construction, or a vertex attribute descriptor needs.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Rounding a class's `byteLength` up to its alignment is what makes an array of it an array: without the trailing pad, the second element would be misaligned. `class A { a: uint8; b: uint16; }` is therefore 4 bytes and not 3, and `[10].&lt;A&gt;` is 40.</p>
        <p>Because layout follows declaration order and this specification never reorders, field order is a performance decision the program makes: `{ a: uint8, b: float64, c: uint8 }` occupies 24 bytes where `{ b: float64, a: uint8, c: uint8 }` occupies 16. Reordering would be sound for a program that only reads its own fields and wrong for every one that does not, and views, serialization, and interop are exactly the cases that do not. Since `byteLength` is a constant, a program that cares can assert the size it meant to have.</p>
        <p>Inheritance appends, so a base's fields keep their offsets and a subclass's follow under the same rule, and `class B extends A` is laid out as the flattening of both. That is what lets a `B` be passed where an `A` is expected without copying: the prefix is an `A` already.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-layout-control">
      <h1>Explicit Control</h1>
      <p>Seven reserved names control the layout above. Each is a property-descriptor key and each has a decorator of the same name that sets it, so a program may write either and a tool that generates classes need not write decorators.</p>

      <emu-table id="table-layout-controls" caption="Layout Controls">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Applies to</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>`packed`</td><td>A class</td><td>Each field is placed immediately after the one before it and the class's alignment is 1. A field may then be unaligned, which costs a little on every access and buys exact offsets, which is the trade a wire format wants.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`alignAll`</td><td>A class</td><td>The alignment of the instance as a whole. It composes with `packed`, which decides the fields' placement while this decides the instance's.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`size`</td><td>A class</td><td>The allocated size, zero-padded to it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`align`</td><td>A field</td><td>Requires the field's address to be a multiple of its value, in place of its type's alignment and in either direction: a field may be given a stricter alignment than its type needs, for a cache line or a wide move, or a weaker one, which is what a packed wire format does to a wide field.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`offset`</td><td>A field</td><td>Places the field that many bytes from the start of the allocation. The origin is 0 for each class, so in a subclass a positive offset lands after the base and a negative one reaches back into it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`offsetBit`</td><td>A bit-field</td><td>Places it that many bits from the start of the allocation, which is what fixes bit order exactly.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>`endian`</td><td>A multi-byte field</td><td>*"little"* or *"big"*, fixing the field's byte order. The default is the platform's, which is what a TypedArray already uses, so a class may mix native fields with network-order ones.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </emu-table>

      <p>These apply only where every field of the class and of its whole prototype chain is typed, since a class with an untyped field has no fixed layout to place a field within. A `dynamic` class may gain a typed field later through `Object.defineProperty` with a `type` key, and that field is part of no layout; on any other class such a call is a *TypeError*, because the layout closed at the declaration.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>An `align` replaces its field's alignment rather than strengthening it, which is the design's own reading: a `float32x4` at `@align(4)` follows a `float32` at byte 2 at byte 8, not at byte 16. That is what makes the control useful in both directions, and it is the program's to get right, since an implementation may hold a field's natural alignment to be a precondition of the instruction it would otherwise use.</p>
        <p>Two fields given one `offset` occupy one memory, which is the C union, and a negative offset overlaps a base's. That is deliberate reinterpretation, for reading a `float32`'s bits as a `uint32`, and it is not how a program should write a tagged union: a `sealed abstract class` hierarchy is that, and the language checks it for exhaustiveness (<emu-xref href="#sec-enums"></emu-xref>).</p>

        <p>Overlapping fields are the one place a typed read can produce a value the type system did not construct, and this specification permits it knowingly. The store check of <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref> applies PER FIELD, against the type that field declares: a store through `bits` is checked against `bits`'s type and a store through `value` against `value`'s, and neither knows the other shares its bytes. So a program that writes a `float32` through one field and reads a `uint32` through another gets the bit pattern reinterpreted, which is exactly what it asked for and is the reason the C union is expressible at all. The type system's guarantee is over what a field ACCEPTS, not over what the bytes beneath it came to hold; a program that overlaps two fields of different types has taken responsibility for the correspondence, in the same way that a program writing through a `DataView` over a typed array has. Nothing else in this specification has this property, and nothing else needs it: it follows from `offset` placing a field outright, which is the point of `offset`.</p>
        <p>Reading or writing a bit-field is a shift and a mask, and taking a reference to one is a type error, since it has no byte address to refer to. A bit-field is also not a SIMD lane mask: `boolean8` and its family are one bit per lane and belong to the vector types, where a bit-field is a sub-byte integer member of a class.</p>
        <p>Automatic packing reaches only a field under 8 bits, so a `uint.&lt;12&gt;` occupies 2 bytes unless an `offsetBit` places it, which is worth knowing for a wire format whose fields are 12 bits wide. The design states the rule for the sub-byte case and leaves the wider one to the explicit control, and whether automatic packing should follow `bitLength` rather than a byte is a question it should answer.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-layout-finiteness">
      <h1>Finiteness</h1>
      <p>The layout of a value type class is defined exactly where the rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-classes"></emu-xref> holds, that a value type class may not contain itself, directly or through a cycle of value type fields. The two are one condition. Computing a layout recurses from a class into the type of each of its fields; the recursion terminates exactly where that field relation is acyclic; and every field contributes a size of at least zero and at least one field of a cycle contributes more, so a cycle admits no finite fixpoint and every acyclic graph reaches one in as many steps as it has edges. The rule is therefore not a restriction placed on top of the layout but a statement of when the layout exists, and the diagnostic a program gets names the cycle rather than a size that could not be computed.</p>
      <p>A field written `T | null` closes a cycle because it is a reference, which <emu-xref href="#table-layout-by-type"></emu-xref> gives no layout and whose width is the implementation's; the recursion stops there rather than descending, which is why a linked list is expressible and a class containing itself by value is not.</p>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

            <emu-clause id="sec-span-type">
        <h1>The Span Type</h1>
        <p>`Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` is a FIXED-LENGTH WINDOW over a run of elements of _T_ that it does not own. A second argument is optional and states the length: `Span.&lt;`_T_`, `_N_`&gt;` is a window of exactly _N_ elements, and `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` one whose length is not stated. Its elements may be read and written; its length cannot change. An array view (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-views"></emu-xref>) is a `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` over the bytes of a buffer, and a projection of an `SoA` column (<emu-xref href="#sec-structure-of-arrays"></emu-xref>) is a `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` over that column; both were this type before it had a name.</p>

        <p>The array types say what is known about an EXTENT: `[`_N_`].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` states one and `[].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` states that it varies. Ownership is a separate question, and `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` answers it: the value does not own its storage, which is why it has no `capacity`, no `reserve`, and no `shrinkToFit` (<emu-xref href="#sec-capacity-operations"></emu-xref>) &mdash; those describe an allocation, and a window has none. It is also why `new Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;()` is a *TypeError* exception and a class may not derive from it: there is nothing for such a construction to allocate. `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;(`_buffer_`)` reinterprets bytes that already exist, which is why it is a call and not a construction.</p>

        <p>A `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` is INVARIANT in _T_, for the reason the array types are (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-and-tuple-types"></emu-xref>): a `Span.&lt;`uint8`&gt;` used as a `Span.&lt;`number`&gt;` would admit a Number into storage typed `uint8`. `Span.&lt;`any`&gt;` is admissible on the same ground the array types admit `[].&lt;`any`&gt;`, that a store through it is checked against the storage's own element type at run time (<emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>).</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The window is writable, and calling it a read-only view would give the wrong answer here: a read-only type could be covariant in _T_ and this one cannot, because a write through it reaches storage whose element type it does not get to choose.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <emu-note>
          <p>An access outside the window's length is a *RangeError* exception, for a READ and for a WRITE alike. The write is the part worth stating: a window's length is fixed, so a write past the end has nowhere to go. The three containers differ here, and the difference is the design rather than an accident &mdash; a write past a `[`_N_`]` `.` `&lt;` _T_ `&gt;` is attempted GROWTH and a *TypeError* exception (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-defaults-and-stores"></emu-xref>), a write past a `[` `]` `.` `&lt;` _T_ `&gt;` GROWS the array, and only an array with no element type answers *undefined* to a read.</p>
        </emu-note>

        <emu-clause id="sec-span-coercion">
          <h1>Coercion to a Span</h1>
          <p>`[].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` and `[`_N_`].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` are both assignable to `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`, and a tuple is assignable to it when every position's type is _T_. The reverse never holds: a window is not assignable to either array type, since neither the storage nor the right to grow it is the window's to give. `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` is a member of the array and tuple family, so a type parameter bounded by `[]` admits one.</p>

          <p>A source reaches `Span.&lt;`_T_`, `_N_`&gt;` only where its length is known to be _N_: a `[`_N_`].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` does, and a `[].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` does not, its length being a run-time fact. `Span.&lt;`_T_`, `_N_`&gt;` is assignable to `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` and not the reverse, since forgetting a length is always safe and inventing one is not.</p>

          <emu-note>
            <p>The length is optional rather than always present because the two cases are genuinely different. A view over a resizable buffer cannot carry its length in its type &mdash; that is why <emu-xref href="#sec-array-views"></emu-xref> takes a count as an ARGUMENT &mdash; while a window taken over a known run of elements can, and something has to carry it for a bounds check to be elided.</p>
            <p>`std::span&lt;T, Extent&gt;` and `&amp;[T; N]` are the same shape reached from the same pressure.</p>
          </emu-note>

          <p>A coercion MATERIALIZES: it produces a window value distinct from the array coerced, and two coercions of one array need not be the same value. An implementation may elide the construction where the window does not escape. Views and `SoA` projections already answer this way, and a coercion that instead passed the array through would make one static type stand for two different kinds of value, which is the confusion this type exists to end.</p>

          <p>A window does not confer ownership, so obtaining an owned array from one is explicit: a spread or a `slice` copies the elements into a new array, and no implicit conversion performs that allocation.</p>
        </emu-clause>

        <emu-clause id="sec-span-liveness">
          <h1>Liveness of a Span</h1>
          <p>A window names a run of elements in storage it does not own, so it is a reference in the sense of <emu-xref href="#sec-reference-liveness"></emu-xref> and takes that clause's rules. A `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` over a growable array is INVALIDATED when that array's allocation relocates &mdash; by a `push` that outgrows it, by a `reserve` that reallocates, or by a `shrinkToFit` that releases &mdash; and a read or a write through an invalidated window throws a *TypeError* exception. An operation that does not relocate does not invalidate: a `reserve` for room the array already has, and a `shrinkToFit` on an array already at fit, leave every window over it valid.</p>

          <p>A window over a `[`_N_`].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` is never invalidated, a fixed extent having nothing that relocates. A window over a buffer follows that buffer's rules instead: it tracks a resizable buffer's length, or detaches where a fixed view's bytes cease to exist (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-views"></emu-xref>).</p>

          <emu-note>
            <p>This is the same rule an `SoA` projection already states and the same one a `ref` into an array already obeys, which is what makes it one rule rather than three: a window is a reference to many elements where a `ref` is a reference to one, and relocation is what invalidates either.</p>
          </emu-note>
        </emu-clause>
      </emu-clause>

            <emu-clause id="sec-array-views">
        <h1>Array Views</h1>
        <p>`Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;(`_buffer_`, `_byteOffset_`, `_count_`, `_byteElementLength_`)` is a VIEW over bytes that already exist, as distinct from the allocations of <emu-xref href="#sec-default-values"></emu-xref>. A view IS a `Span.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` (<emu-xref href="#sec-span-type"></emu-xref>) and this clause states the buffer-specific half of that type: how its length is derived, and what becomes of it when the bytes beneath it change. It was spelled `[].&lt;`_T_`&gt;(`_buffer_`)`, which named the growable array type to produce a value that owns nothing and cannot grow; the spelling now says which type it makes. Each is a call on the type rather than a `new`, because nothing is constructed: a view aliases storage a program already has.</p>

        <p>_buffer_ may be an ArrayBuffer, a SharedArrayBuffer, or any typed array including another view, so that two views over one buffer alias the same memory - which is the point of reinterpreting a buffer, and why an implementation may not use the element types to rule aliasing out. _byteOffset_ defaults to 0. _count_ decides whether the view is FIXED or LENGTH-TRACKING and is described below. _byteElementLength_ defaults to _T_'s `byteLength` and may be smaller or larger than it, so a view may read one field out of a larger element by giving the element's stride; an element is addressed at _byteOffset_ plus its index times that stride. It comes last because no other view constructor in the language has it: `%TypedArray%` and `DataView` cannot address interleaved data at all, so a signature that puts the stride where they put a count would read as familiar and mean something else.</p>

        <p>A view with no _count_ is LENGTH-TRACKING: its `length` is the bytes available past _byteOffset_ divided by the stride, so it grows and shrinks as a resizable buffer is resized. A view given a _count_ is FIXED at that many elements and records its byte extent at construction. This is the distinction `%TypedArray%` and `DataView` already make by the same means &mdash; `new Uint8Array(`_buffer_`, 0)` tracks and `new Uint8Array(`_buffer_`, 0, 8)` does not &mdash; and it is spelled the same way here so that a reader who knows one knows the other. A fixed view whose bytes cease to exist reports a `length` of *+0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, as a `%TypedArray%` in the same position does, and refuses every access; it is a *RangeError* exception if the buffer does not hold that extent past _byteOffset_, and if the buffer later shrinks below it the view is detached and any access throws a *TypeError* exception. Growth never invalidates a view, and a view over a SharedArrayBuffer never detaches, since a shared buffer never shrinks.</p>

        <p>A store through a view is checked against _T_ as any store to a typed element is (<emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>), and the converted value is what reaches the bytes.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>A length-tracking view answers a shorter `length` where a fixed one detaches, and the difference is not a preference: a fixed view's extent is part of what it promised at construction, so bytes disappearing from under it invalidates the promise, while a tracking view promised only to describe whatever is there.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-structure-of-arrays">
        <h1>Structure of Arrays</h1>
        <p>`SoA.&lt;`_T_`, `_Length_`&gt;` stores its elements as one array per field of _T_ rather than as a run of _T_. It is a built-in exotic in the same way `[`_N_`].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` is: no user-defined class can express it, because declaring one array per field of a type is a mapped type this specification does not have, and because a reference into one denotes a column set and an index rather than a byte offset.</p>

        <p>_Length_ follows the array types: 0, which is the default, is growable, and a non-zero value is a fixed extent. _T_ must be a value type class or a primitive. A primitive _T_ degenerates to a single column, so a generic that may be handed one needs no special case; a class with a field of no layout has nothing to split and is a type error.</p>

        <p>The layout is the row <emu-xref href="#table-layout-by-type"></emu-xref> gives it: each field of _T_ is a column of _Length_ elements, padded and aligned on its own, in declaration order, and the type's `byteLength` is the sum of the columns rounded up to its alignment. The split is ONE LEVEL: a field that is itself a value type stays a single column, interleaved within itself, so a consumer wanting that field as a contiguous run of its own type has one and a consumer wanting each of its leaves contiguous flattens the class. Which is wanted is a property of the data's use, and this specification does not decide it.</p>

        <p>An element's fields are not adjacent, so there is no interior padding between them, and a _T_ whose interleaved layout pads to a larger stride has a smaller `byteLength` here than `[`_Length_`].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` does. A Type Object of this type additionally has an `elementByteLength`, the per-element sum of the column strides, which for the same reason is not the interleaved stride.</p>

        <p>A growable `SoA.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` has no layout as a type, exactly as `[].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` has none; its instances have a `byteLength`.</p>

        <p>Each column is aligned to its element type's alignment, and to nothing wider. A vector width is a property of the host rather than of the type, so folding one into the layout would make the same `SoA` a different size on different implementations and would cost `byteLength` its place among the compile-time constants of <emu-xref href="#sec-layout-properties"></emu-xref>. Where an `SoA`'s allocation is PLACED is a separate question and the implementation's own: the base's address is not part of what the type describes, so an implementation may put every allocation on a vector or cache-line boundary and is encouraged to.</p>

        <p>`Length` is defaulted, so `SoA.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` and `SoA.&lt;`_T_`, 0&gt;` name ONE type, as a defaulted parameter does everywhere.</p>

        <p>An `SoA` and a `[`_N_`].&lt;`_T_`&gt;` are distinct types with distinct layouts and neither is assignable to the other, whatever their elements. Conversion is explicit and copies.</p>

        <p>`fields` projects each of _T_'s immediate fields as a `Span.&lt;`_F_`&gt;` (<emu-xref href="#sec-span-type"></emu-xref>) over that field's column, and the views alias: a write through one is visible through the element operations and the reverse. A projection of a growable `SoA` is INVALIDATED by a growth that reallocates, and an access through it afterwards throws a *TypeError* exception, exactly as a reference into the same `SoA` does — a projection that kept reading the abandoned allocation would disagree with its container and say nothing. A projection of a fixed `SoA.&lt;`_T_`, `_N_`&gt;` can never be invalidated, since a fixed extent does not grow.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Invalidating rather than preventing is what a garbage-collected language can express. A borrow checker refuses to compile a program that grows a container while a projection of it is alive, and pays nothing at run time for the guarantee; here nothing marks the end of a projection's life, so a container that could not grow while one had ever been taken could never grow again. The check is loop-invariant — the generation cannot change without a call — so it is hoisted rather than paid per element, and the fixed form has no check at all.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-checked-contracts">
    <h1>Checked Contracts</h1>
    <p>A function declaration may carry `where` clauses, the |WhereClauses| of <emu-xref href="#sec-where-clauses"></emu-xref>, between its return annotation and its body. On an ordinary function they are the compile-time bound over its generic parameters that <emu-xref href="#sec-generic-where"></emu-xref> describes. On a compile-time-evaluable function they are more, because such a function returns a type and a predicate over that type is a <dfn id="checked-contract">checked contract</dfn>: a fact about the type the builder returns, stated at the declaration and checked at every evaluation.</p>

    <p>The production below restates the one <emu-xref href="#sec-type-annotations"></emu-xref> gives, with |WhereClauses| added (<emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref>).</p>

    <emu-grammar type="definition">
      FunctionDeclaration[Yield, Await, Default] :
        `function` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] <ins>TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `(` FormalParameters[~Yield, ~Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> <ins>WhereClauses[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` FunctionBody[~Yield, ~Await] `}`
        [+Default] `function` <ins>TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `(` FormalParameters[~Yield, ~Await] `)` <ins>TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> <ins>WhereClauses[?Yield, ?Await]?</ins> `{` FunctionBody[~Yield, ~Await] `}`
    </emu-grammar>

    <p>Within a |WhereClause| of a function declaration, `return` is a |PrimaryExpression| denoting the value the function returns. It is a Syntax Error for `return` to occur as an expression anywhere else, which costs nothing, since the token is ungrammatical in expression position today and no program can be using it.</p>

    <p>A contract is read two ways, and both are load-bearing. It is <dfn id="contract-verified">verified</dfn>: at every concrete evaluation of the builder, once <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluatebuildercall"></emu-xref> has a result, each clause is evaluated with `return` bound to it, and a clause that is falsy is a type error naming the builder, the arguments it was given, and the clause. A contract is never trusted. It is <dfn id="contract-assumed">assumed</dfn>: before specialization, where the application is deferred and no result exists, the checker takes each clause as a known fact about the ~application~ Type Record. The second is sound because of the first: any specialization that would falsify an assumption is stopped at the builder, before the code that relied on it runs.</p>

    <p>A generic declaration may carry an `exemplars` decorator naming types, which forces specialization of the declaration at those arguments during compile-time evaluation, so that a builder's contracts are evaluated where the declaration is rather than only where a caller reaches it.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>This is the trade C++ concepts make, recovered inside a specialization model: the body of a generic is checked against declared bounds at its declaration, and per-instantiation verification is the backstop that makes the bounds safe to believe. `function sanitize&lt;T&gt;(value: T): omit(T, ['password'])` checks at its declaration because `omit`'s contract says `Reflect.isAssignable(T, return)`, and `{ ...value }` is a `T`.</p>
      <p>Direction is the thing to get right and is easy to invert. An upper bound, that the result is assignable to something, serves a consumer holding the result. Checking a body that produces the result needs a lower bound, and for `omit` the true one is that `T` is assignable to the result, since removing properties yields a supertype. A contract stating the reverse would be both true of nothing and useless to the body.</p>
      <p>Two limits are worth stating rather than discovering. A contract is a proposition about one evaluation, so universally quantified knowledge, the variance of `omit(T, K)` in `T` or any parametricity fact, is not expressible as one and stays unavailable before specialization; after specialization the types are simply evaluated and compared, so nothing is lost where a program actually needs it. And a contract does not check the builder's body ahead of any evaluation, which is what the `exemplars` decorator is for: it turns testing a library's generics from advice into an annotation the checker reads. Beneath contracts sits the cast, which is legal with a deferred application as its target and resolves at specialization into the ordinary checked conversion of <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>; that is the honest division, with contracts stating what an author can promise and a checked cast bounding what an author merely asserts.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-inference-through-results">
    <h1>Inference Through Computed Positions</h1>
    <p>Inference reads plain parameter positions (<emu-xref href="#sec-computed-constraints"></emu-xref>): a parameter under a deferred application is opaque, because binding it from the application's result would require running the builder backwards, and a builder is an opaque function. Where a computed type sits in a return position nothing is needed, since the argument infers plainly and the result is a function of it. This clause is the remainder, for a parameter that occurs only in computed positions, and it adds two rules that propose a binding and verify it forward. Neither runs a builder backwards and neither admits anything forward checking would not.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-literal-freshness">
      <h1>Literal Freshness</h1>
      <p>An object literal checked against an expected object type is checked <dfn id="fresh">freshly</dfn>: an own property the expected type neither declares nor admits through an index signature is a type error, reported against the property. Freshness is a property of the literal and not of its type, so it is lost the moment the value is bound to a name and read back: `f({ a: 1, b: 2 })` is checked freshly against `f`'s parameter, and `f(o)` is not.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Without this an all-optional shape is a supertype of nearly everything, and width subtyping admits any literal against it, which is correct for a value that reached the position through a binding and useless for one written at the position. A value type class already imposes the rule, since a literal converted to one fills a layout that has no room for a property it did not declare (<emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>); freshness is that rule stated for the structural case, and it is what makes the trials below discriminate at all.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-trial-specialization">
      <h1>Trial Specialization</h1>
      <p>A generic parameter that ordinary inference leaves unbound and whose constraint denotes a <dfn id="closed-candidate-set">closed candidate set</dfn> is inferred by trial. A constraint denotes one when it is a union of types, an enum of types, or a `sealed` class, whose candidates are the members in declaration order, the enumerators in declaration order, and the class with its direct subclasses transitively, the set that is fixed once the declaring module finishes evaluating (<emu-xref href="#sec-enums"></emu-xref>). Any other constraint denotes none, and a parameter so constrained does not trial.</p>

      <p>A parameter is trialed against exactly the arguments whose declared types mention it and no other unbound parameter; an argument whose type mentions two or more is not separable and takes part in no trial. For each candidate in order, that candidate is substituted for the parameter throughout those arguments' declared types (<emu-xref href="#sec-generic-specialization"></emu-xref>), which evaluates each deferred application under the memo already specified, and each argument is checked against the result, freshly where it is a literal. A candidate every argument satisfies survives. Exactly one survivor binds the parameter; zero or more than one is a type error asking for explicit arguments and naming the constraint.</p>

      <p>Trials are counted against the ceiling of <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref>, and the count is a sum over the parameters trialed rather than a product across them, since each is trialed alone. Exceeding it is a type error asking for explicit arguments, and because the ceiling is fixed rather than host-tunable, whether a program's inference succeeds is a fact about the program.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>This is overload resolution's logic pointed at specialization: enumerate, evaluate forward, keep what checks. It terminates in as many memoized evaluations as the constraint has members and is sound because nothing is accepted that forward checking did not accept. `apply({ kind: 'circle', radius: 3 })` against `apply&lt;T: Shape&gt;(patch: partial(T))` trials `Circle`, whose `partial` the literal satisfies, and `Rect`, whose `kind` it contradicts, and infers `Circle` because exactly one survived.</p>
        <p>Separability is a determinism rule before it is a cost rule. `merge&lt;A: Shape, B: Shape&gt;(a: partial(A), b: partial(B))` runs the members of `Shape` for `A` and again for `B`, never their product, and a site whose argument mentions both, as `pair(A, B)` does, asks for explicit arguments rather than searching a space whose size is the product of two constraints.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-declared-inverses">
      <h1>Declared Inverses</h1>
      <p>A compile-time-evaluable function may carry an `inverse` decorator naming another, which the checker consults for a parameter that ordinary inference and trial specialization both leave unbound. The declared inverse is applied once per site, memoized and counted against the budget, to the type of the argument whose computed type mentions the parameter, and what it returns is a <dfn id="proposal">proposal</dfn>: for a builder with one inferable slot, a type; for one with several, a record keyed by parameter name, whose proposals are verified together.</p>

      <p>A proposal binds nothing until it is verified. It is substituted for the parameter, the deferred applications are evaluated forward, and the arguments are checked against the results, which is the check an explicitly specialized call would face and is performed by the same operations. Where it holds, the proposal binds. Where it does not, the parameter stays unbound and the site is a type error asking for explicit arguments. A declared inverse that does not invert can therefore only make inference fail, never make it succeed wrongly.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The association lives on the declaration because that is the only place with one possible author and no timeline: an imperative registration would make inference depend on module evaluation order and let unrelated modules contend for one builder's meaning, and a decorator has neither failure mode and is read where the checker is already reading. The proposal is what user code contributes, and it contributes it as an oracle whose answer is checked, which is why letting user code propose here does not reopen the boundary <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref> draws.</p>
        <p>This rung is last on purpose. The first two need nothing from an author: a builder in a return position infers plainly, and a closed constraint trials. An inverse is for the remainder, and it should be adopted when porting demonstrates the need.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-operator-declarations">
    <h1>Operator Declarations</h1>
    <p>An operator is declared in one of three places, and they differ only in what the receiver is: on a class, where the receiver is the left operand; on a class reopened elsewhere, by the extension form, which appends operators to a type declared in another module; and in a `primitive` block (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-operator-blocks"></emu-xref>), where the receiver is the primitive. This clause gives the form; <emu-xref href="#sec-user-defined-operators"></emu-xref> gives what an operator declaration means for the operators of the language, and <emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-operator-blocks"></emu-xref> what it means for metadata.</p>

    <emu-grammar type="definition">
      <ins>PrimitiveOperatorDeclaration[Yield, Await] :
        `primitive` [no LineTerminator here] TypeName[?Yield, ?Await] TypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]? `{` OperatorDefinitionList[?Yield, ?Await]? `}`

      OperatorDefinitionList[Yield, Await] :
        OperatorDefinition[?Yield, ?Await]
        OperatorDefinitionList[?Yield, ?Await] OperatorDefinition[?Yield, ?Await]

      OperatorDefinition[Yield, Await] :
        `static`? `operator` OperatorName OperatorTypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]? `(` FormalParameters[~Yield, ~Await] `)` TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? `{` FunctionBody[~Yield, ~Await] `}`
        `static`? `operator` OperatorName OperatorTypeParameters[?Yield, ?Await]? `(` FormalParameters[~Yield, ~Await] `)` TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? `;`
        `operator` Type[?Yield, ?Await] `(` `)` TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? `{` FunctionBody[~Yield, ~Await] `}`
        `*` `operator` `...` `(` `)` TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? `{` GeneratorBody `}`
        `*` `operator` `...` `(` `)` TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? `;`

      OperatorTypeParameters[Yield, Await] :
        `.&lt;` TypeParameterList[?Yield, ?Await] `&gt;`

      OperatorName :: one of
        `+` `-` `*` `/` `%` `**` `==` `&lt;` `&gt;` `&lt;=` `&gt;=` `&amp;` `|` `^` `~` `&lt;&lt;` `&gt;&gt;` `&gt;&gt;&gt;`</ins>
    </emu-grammar>

    <p>A |PrimitiveOperatorDeclaration| is a |Declaration|; <emu-xref href="#sec-meta-declarations"></emu-xref> restates that production with it added. An |OperatorDefinition| is a |ClassElement|, restated below, and an interface member, so a class declares operators among its methods and an interface declares the operators a type must have, which is what makes an operator expressible as a constraint. The form without a body is the declaration-only one an interface and a metadata-only definition take. The cast production is the implicit cast operator of <emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-operator-blocks"></emu-xref>.</p>

    <p>The `...` productions are the typed iteration protocol: a generator operator whose declared return type types the elements it yields, and whose declaration defines the class's `Symbol.iterator` method. Like any operator it may be overloaded on its return type, and untyped iteration uses the first declared overload. It yields values rather than references.</p>

    <emu-grammar type="definition">
      ClassElement[Yield, Await] :
        MethodDefinition[?Yield, ?Await]
        `static` MethodDefinition[?Yield, ?Await]
        FieldDefinition[?Yield, ?Await] `;`
        `static` FieldDefinition[?Yield, ?Await] `;`
        ClassStaticBlock
        `;`
        <ins>OperatorDefinition[?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
        <ins>`abstract` ClassElementName[?Yield, ?Await] `(` UniqueFormalParameters[?Yield, ?Await] `)` TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]? `;`</ins>
    </emu-grammar>

    <p>The `abstract` alternative is the abstract method of <emu-xref href="#sec-abstract-classes"></emu-xref>, restated here because a later restatement carries a production's complete form (<emu-xref href="#sec-conventions"></emu-xref>).</p>

    <p>An operator's own type parameters are written `.&lt;`&hellip;`&gt;` rather than `&lt;`&hellip;`&gt;` because the operator name may itself end in `&lt;` or `&gt;`: `operator&lt;.&lt;D2: Dimensions&gt;` lexes where `operator&lt; &lt;D2: Dimensions&gt;` would collide with the `&lt;&lt;` token. This is the one place a declaration site uses the application spelling, and the reason is lexical rather than principled.</p>

    <p>Resolution reads the left operand's type alone. For `a * b`, the operators of `a`'s type are searched for a signature whose parameter accepts `b`, and the operands are never reversed to find a match. Return type overloading applies as it does to any function (<emu-xref href="#sec-overloading-on-return-type"></emu-xref>), and two applicable definitions with the same parameter types and no expected return type is an ambiguity and a type error. A `primitive` block adds to a global operator table; two definitions for one pair of types is a type error at the second declaration. A `static operator` defines an operator on the class object rather than on its instances, so that `A += 5` may mean something, and takes no part in any of the above.</p>
    <p>`|&gt;` may not be declared (<emu-xref href="#sec-pipeline-operator"></emu-xref>). Resolution reads the left operand's type and searches it for a signature describing the operation, and a pipeline has no operation to describe: it binds one operand and evaluates the other rather than computing a value from both. The same reasoning excludes any future operator that binds rather than computes, and is why the exclusion is stated here, where resolution is defined, rather than only where the operator is.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>Never reversing the operands is the rule that makes `Matrix4 * Vector4` and `Vector4 * Matrix4` different operations, which in a column-major library they are: one transforms a column vector and the other a row vector. A language that reverses operands to find any match makes that bug unfindable. It is also why `2 * v` needs a `primitive` block rather than falling back to `v`'s operators: the left operand is a number, and the declaration belongs where the library author can write it.</p>
      <p>The block is made generic by an interface constraint rather than written once per pair, so `primitive float32 { operator*.&lt;T extends ScalarMultiply.&lt;float32, T&gt;&gt;(rhs: T): T }` covers every type that defines scalar multiplication, including ones declared later. `2 * v` reaches it because viability filtering precedes ranking (<emu-xref href="#sec-resolveoverload"></emu-xref>): a `Vector4` satisfies `ScalarMultiply.&lt;float32, Vector4&gt;` and not the `number` instantiation, so the `number` candidate is eliminated before the number-first order could prefer it. Division does not commute, so no such block exists for it and `2 / v` stays a type error, which is correct, since it is not defined.</p>
      <p>A global table is why an operator-bearing type is a nominal class and not an alias. Were `Vector4` an alias for `float32x4`, its operators would land on `float32x4` itself and any other library aliasing that lane type would collide on every shared pair. Two libraries with two nominal `Vector4` classes coexist, because the parameter types differ. The division the design draws is to alias for storage and accessors and to declare a class where the type carries operators.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-vector-comparisons">
    <h1>Vector Comparisons</h1>
    <p>A comparison between two vectors of one shape yields one lane per input lane, and what it yields for each is decided by the expected type, since the comparison is overloaded on its return type (<emu-xref href="#sec-overloading-on-return-type"></emu-xref>). Three results are defined: the wide mask, a vector of the boolean type of the same width as the compared element, so a `float32x4` comparison yields a `boolean32x4` of lanes all-set or all-clear; the compact mask, a bit vector of one bit per lane; and the compared vector type itself, its matching lanes all-ones. Left with no expected type the expression is ambiguous among them and is a type error, so the result's type is written.</p>

    <p>Which comparison each operator performs on a lane is what it performs on a scalar. A hardware comparison of floating-point values chooses among predicates that differ on two axes: an <dfn>ordered</dfn> comparison is *false* when either operand is *NaN* and an <dfn>unordered</dfn> one is *true*, and a signaling comparison raises the invalid-operation flag where a non-signaling one does not. `&lt;`, `&lt;=`, `&gt;`, `&gt;=`, and `==` are ordered and `!=` is unordered, which is the reading that makes a lane agree with the scalar operator of the same spelling: `NaN == NaN` is *false* and `NaN != NaN` is *true*. All are non-signaling, this specification having no floating-point exception surface to signal into. A comparison of integer lanes has no such choice, integers having no *NaN*. The predicates with no operator are not exposed; a program requiring the ordered test alone writes `a == a &amp;&amp; b == b`.</p>

    <p>The lane counts the shorthand names cover are the register widths, which is why `float32x4` has a name and a three-lane float vector does not. A vector of any other lane count is written `vector.&lt;`_T_`, `_N_`&gt;`, which is what a narrowing accessor produces, and a library wanting a name for one declares it.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The three forms are three instructions, which is the point of the overloading rather than a consequence of it: the wide mask is what a comparison natively produces on SSE and NEON, the compact mask is an AVX-512 mask register, and the all-ones vector is what a program blends with. Choosing among them by the annotation lets one spelling reach each without an intrinsic per form, and makes the ambiguity rule load-bearing rather than pedantic: an unannotated `a &lt; b` has no defensible default among three instructions, and a coin flip here is a performance cliff a program cannot see.</p>
      <p>The bit vectors are the one place a lane and a bit coincide, and the numbering is stated as a rule in <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-lanes"></emu-xref> rather than here: a bit vector converts to and from an integer whether or not a comparison produced it. `movmskps` and `vpmovmskb` number their results the same way, which is why the rule costs nothing to honour. They are not the bit-fields of <emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>, which are sub-byte integer members of a class.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-complex-numbers">
    <h1>Complex Numbers</h1>
    <p>`complex.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` is a value type holding a real and an imaginary component of the real numeric type _T_. The width-named shorthands count total bits, following the convention of the numeric libraries the design cites, so a `complex64` holds two `float32` components; the bare name `complex` is `complex.&lt;number&gt;`.</p>

    <p>A numeric literal in a complex position takes the complex type, with the literal as its real component and zero as its imaginary one, so `let` _r_`: complex = 5` is `complex(5, 0)` and an imaginary literal beside a real one reads as the mathematics does. A real VALUE does not convert on its own: `_z_ + _x_` for a complex _z_ and a `number` _x_ is a type error, written `_z_ + complex(_x_)`, which is the rule that holds between any two numeric types here. The literal's representability is the component type's, so a literal no `float32` can hold is no `complex64` either.</p>

    <p>ToBoolean of a complex is *false* where both components are zero and *true* otherwise, so `complex(0, 0)` is falsy as every other numeric zero is. A complex is not one of the existing language types, and ToBoolean of an object is otherwise always *true*, so this is stated rather than inherited: without it the origin would be the one numeric zero that is truthy.</p>

    <p>An imaginary literal (<emu-xref href="#sec-imaginary-literals"></emu-xref>) has the type `complex`, with the literal's value as its imaginary component and zero as its real one, so `4i` is `complex(0, 4)`. Literal propagation applies as it does to any numeric literal (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref>), so an imaginary literal in a `complex64` position is a `complex64`. `complex64` and `complex128` convert to and from `complex` explicitly and not implicitly, exactly as `float32` and `float64` convert to and from `number`, and the treatment of a value outside the component type's range is <emu-xref href="#table-out-of-range-treatment"></emu-xref>'s as it is for that component.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>Bare `complex` has `number` components for the reason the language keeps `number` and `float64` distinct: `number` is what an untyped literal and every `Math` function produce, so a `complex` built from `Math.PI` composes without a cast, and the fixed widths exist for an interleaved buffer and for interop. Being a value type, a `complex` copies on assignment and lies inline, so a `[].&lt;complex128&gt;` is the interleaved buffer an FFT expects and <emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref> gives it the layout to be one.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-ranges">
    <h1>Ranges</h1>
    <p>A range is a value type class over an ordered element type and a bound at each endpoint it has. There are four shapes: `Range.&lt;`_T_`, `_S_`, `_E_`&gt;` carrying both endpoints, `RangeFrom.&lt;`_T_`, `_S_`&gt;` and `RangeTo.&lt;`_T_`, `_E_`&gt;` carrying one, and `RangeFull.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` carrying neither. Each implements `RangeBounds.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`, which is the interface a consumer of an arbitrary range is written against and the type a bound-valued position takes. _S_ and _E_ are values of `Bound`, either `Bound.Closed` or `Bound.Open`, one for each endpoint the shape has, so the four intervals of a two-endpoint range, the closed, the two half-open, and the open, are the four pairs and nothing further is expressible. The four-way name of a pair is an `Interval`, which a range exposes and a diagnostic prefers over the parameterization, and which is never itself a parameter: a shape with one endpoint would otherwise carry a bound for an endpoint it does not have. A range is a value, so it allocates nothing and copies; and it is an expression, so it appears wherever one does, including a `case` label and an argument list, without further rule.</p>

    <p>`RangeBounds.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` carries the operations that are total over the four shapes, because it is what a holder of an arbitrary range has. `contains` takes either a value or a range: against a value it is membership, and against a range it is containment of one point set in another, so an empty range is contained in every range and the full range contains every range. `intersect` is the point-set intersection of two ranges. It is commutative and associative with the full range as its identity; where two ranges share an endpoint the exclusive bound is the one the result carries; and where they are disjoint the result is the greater start with the lesser end, which is descending and therefore empty, so an empty intersection has no separate representation. The shape of an intersection follows from its operands rather than from either one, which is why the result is a `RangeBounds.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`: the intersection of a `RangeFrom` and a `RangeTo` is a `Range`, and the intersection of any range with the full range is that range.</p>

    <p>`scale` multiplies both endpoints by a scalar and is present on the instantiations whose element type defines scalar multiplication, which an ordering does not imply: a range over `Temporal.Instant` has an order and no arithmetic, and has no `scale`. A negative factor exchanges the endpoints and the bounds that go with them, since the image of a half-open range under negation is half-open at the other end, and exchanges `RangeFrom` with `RangeTo` for the same reason. A zero factor yields the closed range at zero rather than the result of multiplying each endpoint, which for a half-open range would be empty, and leaves an already empty range empty.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The bound is per endpoint rather than one four-valued interval because a shape with one endpoint has one bound, and a `RangeFrom` parameterized by an interval would admit a type no value inhabits while still being nameable by an overload. The split also gives each marker in a literal one parameter to set, makes an operation that replaces one endpoint a substitution rather than a computation over an enumeration, and lets a signature fix one bound and quantify the other, which a single parameter could express only as a union of interval values.</p>
      <p>`scale` is conditional on the element type rather than declared for every range because an interface that advertises a member no instantiation of it can run is not a contract. The mechanism is the ordinary one for a member that exists on some instantiations, narrowing a parameter of a partial declaration, which <emu-xref href="#sec-vector-masks"></emu-xref> already uses to declare the mask operations on the vectors whose lanes are bits and on no others. A narrowing to an interface differs from that one only in admitting every element type that satisfies the interface rather than a single lane type.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <emu-clause id="sec-range-literals">
      <h1>Range Literals</h1>
      <p>A range is written with a member of the `..` family. The four two-endpoint forms `a..&lt;b`, `a..=b`, `a&lt;..&lt;b`, and `a&lt;..=b` produce the half-open, the inclusive, the open, and the open-start inclusive-end interval, and the open-ended forms `a..`, `a&lt;..`, `..&lt;b`, `..=b`, and `..` omit an endpoint. A range that has an end always marks whether it includes it, and a range marks its start only where the start is exclusive, so there is no `a..b` and no `..b`: a bare `..` appears only where the endpoint it faces is absent and has no inclusivity to state.</p>

      <p>The family is six tokens, `..`, `..&lt;`, `..=`, `&lt;..`, `&lt;..&lt;`, and `&lt;..=`, each taken whole by longest match and each decided by a fixed lookahead. At a `.` the second character separates the type argument list `.&lt;` from the rest of the family and the third separates the spread `...`, `..&lt;`, and `..=` from `..`; at a `&lt;` the second character separates `&lt;&lt;` and `&lt;=` from the family and the fourth separates `&lt;..&lt;` and `&lt;..=` from `&lt;..`. The `.&lt;` that opens a type argument list and the `..&lt;` of a range are therefore told apart by their second character, before a third is read, and no input reaches a state where both are live. Claiming `&lt;..` costs the grammar one reading, a relational comparison whose right operand begins a range, and costs it nothing else, because generic application is spelled `.&lt;` and a bare `&lt;` is always relational or a shift.</p>

      <p>The family collides with the numeric literal grammar, since `1.` is already a complete numeric literal, and the collision is resolved by one lexical rule: a `.` is not the decimal point of a numeric literal when the character after it is also a `.`. So `1..&lt;6` is the three tokens `1`, `..&lt;`, `6` rather than `1.` then `.6`, while `1.`, `1.5`, and the spread `...` are unchanged, and a form that marks its start needs the rule not at all, since the `&lt;` of `1&lt;..&lt;6` ends the literal before a decimal point is in question. The one construction the rule changes is an integer literal followed by `..` and a member name, the `1..toString()` idiom, which is a Syntax Error rather than a member access on a number, because `1..` is a complete from-range and an identifier cannot continue it. One further lookahead is extended by a character: `?.` is not the optional chaining punctuator where a decimal digit follows it, and is likewise not the punctuator where a `.` follows it, so that `cond?..&lt;b:c` is a conditional whose consequent is a range. The extension changes no program, since `?.` followed by `.` is a Syntax Error under every production that consumes the punctuator.</p>

      <p>A range binds tighter than assignment and looser than `||` and `??`, so its operands are ShortCircuitExpressions, and it is non-associative. The pipeline operator (<emu-xref href="#sec-pipeline-operator"></emu-xref>) binds looser still, so `0..&lt;10 |&gt; f(%)` pipes the range rather than ranging over a pipeline. Continuing: `a..&lt;b..&lt;c` is a Syntax Error rather than a nesting. Member access binds tighter than the range, so `0..&lt;arr.length` is `0..&lt;(arr.length)` and a range reaches its own members through parentheses, as in `(0..&lt;10).length`. Because a range is an expression it appears in a `case` label, an object literal, and an argument list with no further rule, and a statement that begins with `..`, `..&lt;`, `..=`, or `&lt;..` continues the preceding expression, joining the other leading-operator forms on the list of automatic-semicolon hazards.</p>

      <p>Because each token is taken whole, the spacing of the family is significant at its edges. `a..&lt;b` is the half-open range where `a.. &lt; b` is a relational comparison against the from-range `a..`; `a&lt;..` is the open-start from-range where `a &lt; ..` is a comparison against the full range; and `a &lt;.. &lt; b` is a comparison against `a&lt;..` rather than the open range, since `&lt;..&lt;` is one token and spacing it apart does not assemble it. None of the spaced readings is a program at all: a range binds looser than the relational operators, so a range is never a relational operand and each of these is a Syntax Error. Parentheses put a range back under them, and there a range is REJECTED as a relational operand: a range does not implement `Ordered`, so `(0..&lt;3) &lt; 5` is a *TypeError* rather than the *false* an ordinary object's comparison would yield. Answering a meaningless comparison with *false* is the failure this rejection exists to prevent, and it is the only reading the family costs, so the cost is paid where it can be seen.</p>

      <p>The value carries its start and its end, and its bounds are in its type rather than in a field. Its `start` and `end` are the endpoints, absent for an omitted one, and `interval` is the four-way name derived from the two bounds of a two-endpoint range; `contains` tests a value against the endpoints and their bounds; An endpoint may be INFINITE, and an infinite endpoint is not the same as an absent one: `0..&lt;Infinity` has an end and `0..` has none, so they report different `endBound` and `interval` and only the latter is `isFull` when both sides are absent. They contain the same values and iterate the same, an infinite end stopping the iteration nowhere exactly as an absent end does. `length` is the count of the members of a bounded integer range, which is one more than the difference of its endpoints less one for each endpoint whose bound excludes its own value, and never less than zero; and `isEmpty` is whether it has none, a descending range being empty rather than reversed and a range whose endpoints are equal being empty unless both of its bounds are closed; and `reverse` iterates the same members in the opposite order, which is how a descending traversal is written given that a descending range is empty. The nth value of an iteration is the start plus n times the step, counted from 0 where the start's bound is closed and from 1 where it is open, since a start that excludes its own value cannot yield it. A range iterates through its members by the rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-ranges"></emu-xref>: over a bounded integer interval the step is 1 and implicit, and `step` supplies a step where the interval is not one an implicit step fits or widens the stride where it is. A range with no start is not iterable, and a range whose endpoints are not a whole integer interval is a *TypeError* to iterate without an explicit step, rather than a silent choice of 1.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The lexical rule is the whole of what the family costs the numeric grammar, and it is the trade Rust made in requiring `1.0` rather than `1.` so that `..` could be a range. The alternatives were weighed and set aside: `...` reads as spread to every reader and Ruby's `..`/`...` pairing is a known hazard, and a contextual keyword reads poorly in an index position. An earlier draft set `..&lt;` aside as well, on the ground that `.&lt;` already opens a type argument list; that was an error, since the two are told apart by their second character and never compete, and the syntax specified here rests on the correction.</p>
        <p>That every end is written is what makes `for (const i of 0..&lt;array.length)` the loop written without thinking rather than one short. The reason is not that the reader recalls which reading of a two-dot form this language chose, but that there is no two-dot reading to recall: the languages that have `a..b` do not agree on what it means, half-open in Rust and inclusive in Kotlin, Ruby, Perl, Pascal, Ada, and Haskell, so a default would be a convention a reader has to carry rather than a fact the source states. The cost is charged where the disagreement is. An end is two characters longer in every range that has one, and a start, which every language spells inclusive, is marked only in the ranges that exclude it.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <p>A `case` whose label is a range matches where the range contains the discriminant, which is what a `switch` over a float discriminant needs, since a float has no enumerable cases to list. Matching is by containment rather than by `===`, and the cases of such a `switch` are tested in source order, so overlapping ranges resolve to the first that contains the value rather than being an error.</p>

    <p>A range iterates where it has a step, which over an integer element type is 1 and implicit. The counter of a `for` over a range has the range's element type refined by the range's interval (<emu-xref href="#sec-primitive-metadata"></emu-xref>), so `for (const i of 0..&lt;`_N_`)` binds `i` at a type bounded below by 0 and above by _N_ minus 1. Where _N_ is a compile-time constant that refinement is one too, and an index of a `[`_N_`].&lt;T&gt;` by that counter needs no check, which is the case <emu-xref href="#sec-check-elision"></emu-xref> names.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The bound is the range's own interval, so nothing about the elision is special to a `for`: the counter's type carries the fact, and the ordinary subtyping of a bounded parameterization discharges the check the way it discharges any other. That the range states its end is what makes `for (const i of 0..&lt;array.length)` right rather than off by one, and it is why the interval is part of the type rather than a convention a reader has to remember.</p>
      <p>The nth value of a stepped range is the start plus n times the step rather than the previous value plus the step, and iteration stops against the value rather than a computed count. Both halves matter over a float element type, where a computed count can round up and yield one value too many and repeated addition accumulates error; specifying the arithmetic rather than the loop is what makes a float range give the same values on every implementation.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-structural-matching">
    <h1>Structural Matching</h1>
    <p>`Reflect.inferSlot(`_name_`)` returns a fresh <dfn id="slot">slot</dfn>, a Type Object standing for a position a match is to bind. `Reflect.matchType(`_pattern_`, `_subject_`)` performs one-sided structural unification of _pattern_ against _subject_: where every slot in _pattern_ can be bound consistently so that the pattern becomes _subject_, it returns an object mapping each slot's name to the Type Object bound there, and otherwise it returns *null*. A slot occurring twice binds once and must bind alike. A constraint on a binding is an ordinary check of the result afterwards, so `Reflect.isAssignable` supplies what an `infer S extends string` says.</p>

    <p>A pattern is structural: it is a node, and its slots stand where a Type Object would. A slot inside an ~application~ Type Record is a type error at the match rather than *null*, because evaluating a builder against an unbound slot is exactly the inversion no operation of this specification performs (<emu-xref href="#sec-inference-through-results"></emu-xref>). A pattern for the result of a builder is therefore written against the shape the builder produces, an optional property or a wrapped element, and never against the application.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>This closes the question of whether anything an erased system's `infer` does is out of reach, and the answer is that it is not. A library author who wants an explicit inverse has one that is ordinary code (<emu-xref href="#sec-declared-inverses"></emu-xref> is where the checker will consult it).</p>
      <p>The operation was proposed as optional on the measurement that the design's catalog needed it exactly zero times. That measurement has since changed: the decorators extension dispatches on the structure of a type it is handed, which is what a decorator that emits a schema, a validator, or documentation does, and there is no way to write that without this operation. The `extends` pattern of <emu-xref href="#sec-matchtypepattern"></emu-xref> is its syntactic form, standing to it as `is` stands to IsOfType, and consumes the unification defined here rather than defining a second one. Reflective use remains available and remains the escape hatch for what the syntax does not spell.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-provenance">
    <h1>Provenance</h1>
    <p>A Property Type Record and a Type Record may carry an <dfn id="origin">[[Origin]]</dfn>, a List of references to the declaration sites the record came from. It is not part of any type's identity: SameType does not read it, IsSubtype does not read it, and CanonicalizeType neither reads it nor writes it into the comparison that decides whether two Records are one. What canonicalization does with it is union it: where two Records that differ only in [[Origin]] are merged by interning, the merged Record's [[Origin]] is the union of theirs, ordered by <emu-xref href="#sec-canonical-total-order"></emu-xref>. It is a HOST-FACING channel: an implementation makes it available to a tool, and no program reads it. That is a deliberate restriction rather than an omission. Because canonicalization unions origins, a reflected `origin` would change what a program observes about its own type when an unrelated module declared a structurally identical shape, which is a surprise with no upside for the program and none for the tool, since a tool already has the whole program in view. A builder that spreads a property record therefore spreads no origin, and one that mints a record has none to spread.</p>

    <p>A record a builder mints carries no [[Origin]], which is the correct answer rather than a missing one: nothing declared it. An implementation is likewise free to hold an origin as an opaque handle, a source and a position, rather than as a reference to the declaration itself, and is encouraged to: retaining the declaration would keep a parse tree alive for as long as the type is interned, which in an agent that does not exit is a leak and not a cost. Documentation is not carried here and is not carried in the node model at all. It stays with the source, and a tool resolves it from an origin at the moment it displays something, falling back for a minted record on the application that produced it, which is the expression a diagnostic already names.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The reason to want this is that `partial(User)` should not cost an editor its memory of where `name` came from. The reason not to put a documentation string in the node instead is worth stating, because it is the same argument this specification makes twice already: anything inside a node rides the round trip through <emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-maketype"></emu-xref>, so a doc string either joins the canonical form, and two structurally identical types with different comments become different types, or interning crowns one winner and the rest is lost silently and by load order. Provenance escapes both because it is a reference rather than a payload and because it unions rather than wins.</p>
      <p>That union is also why [[Origin]] does not reopen what <emu-xref href="#sec-signature-records"></emu-xref> forecloses. The rule there is not that no second non-canonical field may exist; it is that no field may depend on the order modules loaded. A [[Name]] is first-won and so does depend on it, which is tolerated because a parameter has one name and there is nothing else to do. An [[Origin]] accumulates every site and is ordered canonically, so the same program produces the same origins in the same order however its modules were reached, and the accident the rule exists to prevent does not arise.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-expansion-artifact">
    <h1>The Expansion Artifact</h1>
    <p>Because evaluation is deterministic and its results are interned canonical forms, they may be computed once and shipped. An <dfn id="expansion-artifact">expansion artifact</dfn> is a serialization of the interning table for the concrete types a module graph's public surface produces, every closed application among them pre-evaluated, keyed by a hash of the module graph that produced it. A consumer verifies the hash and reads the types out of it rather than evaluating them; on a mismatch it evaluates, and determinism is what makes the two agree.</p>

    <p>An artifact contains no open application. `partial(T)` inside an exported generic evaluates against the consumer's `T`, at the consumer, so the evaluator is not removed by the artifact and cannot be: what the artifact removes is the common path, a dependency's concrete surface, which is the part that would otherwise be recomputed by every tool on every build. An artifact is an optimization and never a semantics: a consumer that ignores every artifact computes the same types, and one that reads a stale artifact detects it by the hash rather than by disagreeing.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>This is what makes the standalone conformance of <emu-xref href="#annex-evaluable-fragment"></emu-xref> pay. A tool that reads an artifact is trusting that whoever produced it computed what this specification says, and the conformance suite is what makes that claim checkable rather than hopeful; the hash is what makes the trust revocable. The artifact also carries the extracted documentation for the origins of <emu-xref href="#sec-provenance"></emu-xref>, since a dependency's sources are exactly what a consumer does not have on disk, which is the one thing provenance alone cannot supply.</p>
      <p>The format is not fixed here. What is fixed is the contract: the key is a hash of the module graph, the contents are canonical forms, a mismatch re-derives, and nothing in it can change what a program means.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-composites">
    <h1>Composites</h1>
    <p>A composite is a frozen object with a *null* prototype that is <em>interned</em>: two composites created from the same contents are the same object. Equality of contents is therefore identity, and `===`, `Map`, `Set`, and `Array.prototype.includes` compare composites structurally with no change to any collection or operator, because the comparison each already performs finds one object where the contents are one. This clause specifies the design's composites extension, which integrates the <a href="https://github.com/tc39/proposal-composites">Composites proposal</a> under this type system; where the two differ, <emu-xref href="#sec-composite-deviations"></emu-xref> records the difference.</p>

    <p>Interning is defined over SameValueZero, and SameValueZero under this specification requires its operands to be values of one type before it compares them (<emu-xref href="#sec-value-types"></emu-xref>). Interning inherits that: a `uint8` field and a Number field of the same mathematical value are different keys, so the value read back from a composite is the value that was stored, at its type, however many creation sites produced the object. The nondeterminism the upstream proposal resolves for `-0` by normalization cannot arise between types here, because values of two types are never SameValueZero-equal; within a type it is resolved by <emu-xref href="#sec-canonicalizecompositevalue"></emu-xref>, of which the `-0` normalization is one instance.</p>

    <pre><code class="javascript">
      const a = Composite({ x: 1, y: 4 });
      const b = Composite({ y: 4, x: 1 });
      a === b; // true, one object
      new Map([[a, 'ship']]).get(Composite({ x: 1, y: 4 })); // 'ship'

      Composite({ (x: uint8): 1 }) === Composite({ x: 1 }); // false: a uint8 field and a Number field
    </code></pre>

    <emu-clause id="sec-composite-objects">
      <h1>Composite Objects</h1>
      <p>A <dfn variants="composite objects">composite object</dfn> is an object contained in the Composite Registry of its heap (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-registry"></emu-xref>). Composite objects are created only by FindOrCreateComposite and FindOrCreateTupleComposite, which are the only operations that add an object to a Composite Registry. Membership in the registry is what an object's status as a composite consists of, so the status cannot be forged: a Proxy exotic object whose target is a composite object is not itself a composite object.</p>

      <p>There are two kinds of composite object. A <dfn>record composite</dfn> is an ordinary object whose [[Prototype]] is *null*, whose [[Extensible]] is *false*, and whose properties are own data properties with String keys, each with [[Writable]] *false*, [[Enumerable]] *true*, and [[Configurable]] *false*. A <dfn>tuple composite</dfn> is an Array exotic object whose [[Prototype]] is *null* and whose [[Extensible]] is *false*, whose integer-indexed properties are own data properties with [[Writable]] *false*, [[Enumerable]] *true*, and [[Configurable]] *false*, and whose *"length"* property is non-writable, non-enumerable, and non-configurable. `Array.isArray` answers *false* for a record composite and *true* for a tuple composite, and `typeof` answers *"object"* for both.</p>

      <p>Every composite object has a [[RuntimeType]] internal slot holding the composite type of its contents (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-types"></emu-xref>), which is what RuntimeTypeOf (<emu-xref href="#sec-runtimetypeof"></emu-xref>) reports for it. A composite object is frozen from its creation, so the slot never becomes stale.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A record composite holds what upstream's composites hold. The tuple kind is this design's addition: an array-shaped composite whose *"length"* is own and non-enumerable, so that `Object.keys` lists the elements, and whose iteration <emu-xref href="#sec-composite-modifications"></emu-xref> supplies without a prototype to carry `%Symbol.iterator%`. The two kinds never intern together (<emu-xref href="#sec-compositematch"></emu-xref>): `Composite([1])` and `Composite({ 0: 1 })` are different objects, because a record with the entry *"0"* and a tuple of one element disagree about shape even where they agree about every entry.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-composite-types">
      <h1>Composite Types</h1>
      <p>A Type Record is a <dfn variants="composite types">composite type</dfn> when its [[Kind]] is ~primitive~ and its [[Name]] is *"Composite"*. The composite type over a shape _S_, where _S_ is an ~object~ or ~tuple~ Type Record, has [[Arguments]] &laquo; _S_ &raquo;, and _S_ is called its <dfn>shape</dfn>. The <dfn>top composite type</dfn>, written `Composite` with no arguments, has [[Arguments]] &laquo; &raquo;, states no shape, and is the type of every composite object. `Composite` joins the built-in type names of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-names"></emu-xref>, resolving in type position by the rule stated there, and `Composite.&lt;T&gt;` is a |TypeReference| with |TypeArguments| like any other parameterized family. These Type Records canonicalize by canonicalizing the shape in [[Arguments]] (<emu-xref href="#sec-canonicalizetype"></emu-xref>), so `Composite.&lt;{ x: int32; y: int32 }&gt;` written anywhere is one Type Object. The Static Type of a call of the Composite function is the top composite type where the call supplies no |TypeArguments| and no contextual type reaches it, and the composite type over _T_'s structural form for the typed creation `Composite.&lt;T&gt;(source)`; `Reflect.typeOf` on a composite returns the Type Object of its [[RuntimeType]], the same object either spelling denotes.</p>

      <p>The shape recorded for a composite object is derived from its stored contents by CompositeShape (<emu-xref href="#sec-compositeshape"></emu-xref>), so that objects with the same contents have the same shape and interning is coherent. The type of each field is the runtime type of its value, except that a field whose value is an Object records the `any` type, a field whose value is a composite object records that composite's own type, and a field whose value is a Type Object records the `type` type. An ordinary object's structural runtime type describes the properties it happens to have and can change as the object does, which is why it is not recorded: a frozen object's recorded type must not go stale, and the identity a reference field contributes is the reference, not a snapshot of its shape.</p>

      <p>The subtype rules for composite types are stated in IsSubtype (<emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>), where they precede the step that separates the kinds: every composite type is a subtype of the top composite type; a composite type over a shape is a subtype of a composite type over a wider shape; and a composite type over a shape is a subtype of that shape's ~object~ or ~tuple~ type and of an interface whose structural form it satisfies.</p>

      <p>A composite type is therefore a subtype of the ~object~ type of its shape, which is what makes a composite satisfy an interface: `const q: IPoint = p` holds for a `p` of type `Composite.&lt;IPoint&gt;` with no cast and no write, since a frozen object trivially cannot be modified out of an implementation. Composite types are covariant in their shapes, which the frozenness of every composite makes sound: no store can later violate a wider reading. A composite type over a ~tuple~ shape is a subtype of the ~tuple~ types its shape is a subtype of; it is a subtype of no ~array~ type, because an ~array~ type describes a mutable container and every mutating operation on a composite throws.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>No change to IsOfType (<emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref>) is needed. A composite value reaching an ~object~ target is checked structurally by the existing steps, reading its properties as it would any object's, and a composite target is decided by the operation's final step, IsSubtype of the value's runtime type, which the rules above extend. A record composite created without type arguments has Number fields where its source had numeric literals, so checking it against an interface of `uint32` members fails on those fields by the no-implicit-conversion rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-conversions"></emu-xref>; the remedy is to type the creation, which is the same advice every boundary of this specification gives.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-composite-registry">
      <h1>The Composite Registry</h1>
      <p>Each heap has an associated <dfn>Composite Registry</dfn>: a List of Objects, initially empty, whose elements are exactly the composite objects of that heap. Objects are added to it only by FindOrCreateComposite and FindOrCreateTupleComposite. The registry is a property of a heap in the same way the type-object intern table is (<emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref>): agents that share a heap share one registry, so composites are `===` across every realm and every agent of one heap, like symbols obtained from `Symbol.for`, and an agent that does not share the heap is not reached by it (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-across-agents"></emu-xref>). A composite object has a [[Prototype]] of *null* and so bears no relationship to the realm whose `Composite` function created it; only its contents matter.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Under the agent model of the pinned edition each agent has its own heap and the registry is per-agent, which is the upstream proposal's rule. The threading extension is what makes the scope worth stating as the heap's: it gives several agents one heap, objects cross between their threads by reference with no serialization step, and a registry scoped more finely than objects are reachable would put two composites of equal contents, both reachable from both threads, in one heap, falsifying the invariant the feature is. Creation against a shared registry must accordingly be safe under concurrent callers; the observable requirement is only the one FindOrCreateComposite states, that equal contents yield one object, and implementations are expected to reach it without serializing unrelated creations through one lock.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Membership in the registry does not keep a composite object alive; an implementation may remove one that is no longer <emu-xref href="#sec-liveness">live</emu-xref>. The removal is unobservable, because composite objects cannot be held weakly (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-modifications"></emu-xref>) and so cannot be placed in a WeakMap, WeakSet, WeakRef, or FinalizationRegistry, which are the only instruments that observe liveness.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-composite-abstract-operations">
      <h1>Abstract Operations for Composite Objects</h1>

      <emu-clause id="sec-iscomposite" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          IsComposite (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns *true* if _value_ is a composite object.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _value_ is not an Object, return *false*.
          1. If the Composite Registry of the surrounding agent's heap contains _value_, return *true*.
          1. Return *false*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The operation is defined by searching the registry; an implementation is free to answer it any faster way, such as a bit on the object, that yields the same result.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-canonicalizecompositevalue" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          CanonicalizeCompositeValue (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
          ): an ECMAScript language value
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the canonical representative of the SameValueZero equivalence class of _value_ within its type: the one value stored for every value the class contains, chosen so that the contents of a composite do not depend on which member reached the creation. Values of distinct types are never SameValueZero-equal (<emu-xref href="#sec-value-types"></emu-xref>), so the class is always within one type.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _value_ is *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, return *+0*<sub>𝔽</sub>.
          1. If _value_ is a negative zero of a binary floating-point type (<emu-xref href="#sec-binary-floating-point-types"></emu-xref>), return the positive zero of that type.
          1. If _value_ is a finite value of a decimal floating-point type (<emu-xref href="#sec-decimal-floating-point-types"></emu-xref>) whose type does not restrict the exponents of its values, then
            1. Let _c_ be the coefficient of _value_.
            1. Let _q_ be the exponent of _value_.
            1. Let _qMax_ be the largest exponent of _value_'s type.
            1. If _c_ = 0, return the value of _value_'s type with positive sign, coefficient 0, and exponent 0.
            1. Repeat, while _c_ modulo 10 = 0 and _q_ &lt; _qMax_,
              1. Set _c_ to _c_ / 10.
              1. Set _q_ to _q_ + 1.
            1. Return the value of _value_'s type with the sign of _value_, coefficient _c_, and exponent _q_.
          1. If _value_ is a negatively signed zero of a decimal floating-point type whose type restricts the exponents of its values, return the positively signed zero of that type with the exponent of _value_.
          1. Return _value_.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The rule generalizes the upstream proposal's normalization of `-0`, which is its Number instance. A SameValueZero class with one member canonicalizes to itself, which covers most of the type universe with no step naming it: every integer, String, BigInt, Boolean, Symbol, and Object value is alone in its class; a rational is because its type keeps every value in lowest terms (<emu-xref href="#sec-rational-types"></emu-xref>), so each rational number has exactly one representation. The classes with more than one member are the signed zeros, handled above for the Number type and each binary float width, and the decimal cohorts.</p>
          <p>A decimal type whose metadata fixes the exponents of its values, such as one parameterized by a `DecimalContext` scale (<emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>), has collapsed each cohort before this operation sees a value: conversion into the type quantizes at the boundary, so the only remaining choice is the sign of a zero, which the fourth step settles. A bare decimal type stores the reduced cohort member, trailing zeros stripped, which is the one member computable from the numerical value alone and the value any hash of the field must be computed from in any case. The division of labour is deliberate: canonicalization is the type's wherever the type has an opinion, and this operation's only where it does not. A value whose significance matters to a program belongs in a type that declares its scale, at which point a composite stores exactly that scale; an unconstrained decimal in a composite does not carry significance, as it would not survive any other typed boundary either.</p>
          <p>Each floating-point type of this specification has a single NaN value, so a NaN canonicalizes to itself and no step names it. Where an implementation nonetheless distinguishes NaN encodings, as the pinned edition permits and the byte views of the memory layout extension can observe (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-views"></emu-xref>), the NaN stored in a composite must have one fixed encoding per type, the canonical quiet NaN with positive sign and zero payload, so that reading a stored NaN's bytes reveals nothing about which creation interned the composite or what payload it supplied. This is the requirement the upstream proposal states for the Number type, applied to every float width the byte views reach.</p>
          <p>A program that needs a signed zero, a specific cohort member, or a NaN payload to participate in a key carries it as data, a sign field or an exponent field, which keys correctly with no special case. No option restores the distinction: an argument that changed interning identity would fragment the registry into flag-keyed families, so the `preserveNegativeZero` option the upstream FAQ floats is not adopted.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-composite-entry-records">
        <h1>Composite Entry Records</h1>
        <p>A <dfn variants="Composite Entry Records">Composite Entry Record</dfn> is a Record value describing one key/value pair of a record composite. It has a [[Key]] field (a String) and a [[Value]] field (an ECMAScript language value).</p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-compositecanonicalorder" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          CompositeCanonicalOrder (
            _entries_: a List of Composite Entry Records,
          ): a List of Composite Entry Records
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns _entries_ in the canonical key order of record composites, which does not depend on the order in which the entries were provided.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _integerEntries_ be a new empty List.
          1. Let _stringEntries_ be a new empty List.
          1. For each Composite Entry Record _entry_ of _entries_, do
            1. If _entry_.[[Key]] is an array index, append _entry_ to _integerEntries_; otherwise, append _entry_ to _stringEntries_.
          1. Sort _integerEntries_ into ascending order by the numeric values of the array indices their [[Key]] fields denote.
          1. Sort _stringEntries_ into ascending lexicographic order of their [[Key]] fields, compared code unit by code unit.
          1. Return the list-concatenation of _integerEntries_ and _stringEntries_.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>This is the ordinary own-key order except that the non-index String keys sort lexicographically rather than by creation order, which is what makes a record composite a canonical form of its contents.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-compositefieldtype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          CompositeFieldType (
            _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
          ): a Type Record
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the Type Record recorded for a composite field holding _value_. The result is a function of _value_ alone and is stable over the composite's lifetime, which interning requires.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If IsComposite(_value_) is *true*, return _value_.[[RuntimeType]].
          1. If _value_ is a Type Object, return the Type Record for `type` (<emu-xref href="#sec-the-type-type"></emu-xref>).
          1. If _value_ is an Object, return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~any~ }.
          1. Return RuntimeTypeOf(_value_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>A value of a value type contributes its runtime type, so a `uint8` field is a `uint8` field and an untyped creation's numeric literals are Number fields, which is where the typed and untyped boundary of this clause's introduction becomes visible to reflection. An ordinary object contributes `any` rather than its structural runtime type, because that type is a mutable object's current shape and a frozen composite must not record something that can go stale; the identity a reference field contributes to interning is the reference itself. A nested composite and a Type Object are frozen and interned, so their types are stable and are recorded.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-compositeshape" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          CompositeShape (
            _kind_: ~record~ or ~tuple~,
            _contents_: either a List of Composite Entry Records or a List of ECMAScript language values,
          ): a Type Record
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the shape of a composite with the given contents: an ~object~ Type Record for a record composite and a ~tuple~ Type Record for a tuple composite. _contents_ is a List of Composite Entry Records where _kind_ is ~record~ and a List of ECMAScript language values where _kind_ is ~tuple~.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _kind_ is ~record~, then
            1. Let _props_ be a new empty List.
            1. For each Composite Entry Record _entry_ of _contents_, do
              1. Append the Property Type Record { [[Key]]: _entry_.[[Key]], [[Type]]: CompositeFieldType(_entry_.[[Value]]), [[Optional]]: *false*, [[Readonly]]: *true*, [[Initial]]: ~none~ } to _props_.
            1. Return CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~object~, [[Properties]]: _props_, [[IndexSignatures]]: &laquo; &raquo; }).
          1. Let _elements_ be a new empty List.
          1. For each element _v_ of _contents_, do
            1. Append the Tuple Element Record { [[Type]]: CompositeFieldType(_v_), [[Rest]]: *false*, [[Initial]]: ~none~ } to _elements_.
          1. Return CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~tuple~, [[Elements]]: _elements_ }).
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-findorcreatecomposite" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          FindOrCreateComposite (
            _entries_: a List of Composite Entry Records,
          ): a record composite
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the interned record composite for the key/value pairs of _entries_, creating it if none exists. No two elements of _entries_ have the same [[Key]], and every [[Value]] is already the result of CanonicalizeCompositeValue.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _sortedEntries_ be CompositeCanonicalOrder(_entries_).
          1. Let _registry_ be the Composite Registry of the surrounding agent's heap.
          1. For each composite object _c_ of _registry_, do
            1. If CompositeMatch(_c_, ~record~, _sortedEntries_) is *true*, return _c_.
          1. Let _c_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(*null*).
          1. For each Composite Entry Record _entry_ of _sortedEntries_, do
            1. Perform ! DefinePropertyOrThrow(_c_, _entry_.[[Key]], PropertyDescriptor { [[Value]]: _entry_.[[Value]], [[Writable]]: *false*, [[Enumerable]]: *true*, [[Configurable]]: *false* }).
          1. Set _c_.[[Extensible]] to *false*.
          1. Set _c_.[[RuntimeType]] to the composite type over CompositeShape(~record~, _sortedEntries_).
          1. Add _c_ to _registry_.
          1. Return _c_.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The search over the registry describes the required observable result and is not an implementation model. Interning must be implemented by hash tables or other means whose cost per creation is, on average, sublinear in the registry's population; a typed creation site whose shape is statically known additionally admits a fixed hashing sequence with no key sorting and no descriptor walk, since the order and the field types are known before the program runs.</p>
        </emu-note>
        <emu-note>
          <p>No step of canonicalization, matching, or interning calls user code. The creation algorithms read the source's values once, getters included, and everything from CanonicalizeCompositeValue onward operates on the values read, over objects that cannot be Proxy exotic objects, so a composite's identity is a function of its contents and of nothing a program can interpose on. What a collection treats as one key is a separate question from what a composite is, and only the second is settled here.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-findorcreatetuplecomposite" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          FindOrCreateTupleComposite (
            _elements_: a List of ECMAScript language values,
          ): either a normal completion containing a tuple composite or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the interned tuple composite whose elements are _elements_, creating it if none exists. Every element is already the result of CanonicalizeCompositeValue.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _registry_ be the Composite Registry of the surrounding agent's heap.
          1. For each composite object _c_ of _registry_, do
            1. If CompositeMatch(_c_, ~tuple~, _elements_) is *true*, return _c_.
          1. Let _len_ be the number of elements of _elements_.
          1. If _len_ &ge; 2<sup>32</sup>, throw a *RangeError* exception.
          1. Let _c_ be ! ArrayCreate(_len_).
          1. Set _c_.[[Prototype]] to *null*.
          1. Let _i_ be 0.
          1. For each element _v_ of _elements_, do
            1. Perform ! DefinePropertyOrThrow(_c_, ! ToString(𝔽(_i_)), PropertyDescriptor { [[Value]]: _v_, [[Writable]]: *false*, [[Enumerable]]: *true*, [[Configurable]]: *false* }).
            1. Set _i_ to _i_ + 1.
          1. Perform ! _c_.[[DefineOwnProperty]](*"length"*, PropertyDescriptor { [[Writable]]: *false* }).
          1. Set _c_.[[Extensible]] to *false*.
          1. Set _c_.[[RuntimeType]] to the composite type over CompositeShape(~tuple~, _elements_).
          1. Add _c_ to _registry_.
          1. Return _c_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-compositematch" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          CompositeMatch (
            _c_: a composite object,
            _kind_: ~record~ or ~tuple~,
            _contents_: either a List of Composite Entry Records or a List of ECMAScript language values,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns *true* if _c_ is a composite of the given kind with exactly the given contents. _contents_ is a List of Composite Entry Records where _kind_ is ~record~, in the canonical order of CompositeCanonicalOrder, and a List of ECMAScript language values where _kind_ is ~tuple~.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _kind_ is ~tuple~ and _c_ is not a tuple composite, return *false*.
          1. If _kind_ is ~record~ and _c_ is a tuple composite, return *false*.
          1. If _kind_ is ~tuple~, then
            1. Let _len_ be ! LengthOfArrayLike(_c_).
            1. If _len_ &ne; the number of elements of _contents_, return *false*.
            1. Let _i_ be 0.
            1. For each element _v_ of _contents_, do
              1. If SameValue(! Get(_c_, ! ToString(𝔽(_i_))), _v_) is *false*, return *false*.
              1. Set _i_ to _i_ + 1.
            1. Return *true*.
          1. Let _keys_ be ! _c_.[[OwnPropertyKeys]]().
          1. If the number of elements of _keys_ &ne; the number of elements of _contents_, return *false*.
          1. Let _i_ be 0.
          1. For each Composite Entry Record _entry_ of _contents_, do
            1. If _entry_.[[Key]] is not _keys_[_i_], return *false*.
            1. If SameValue(! Get(_c_, _entry_.[[Key]]), _entry_.[[Value]]) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Set _i_ to _i_ + 1.
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Both sides are canonicalized before this operation runs, so within one type SameValue and SameValueZero coincide here, and across types both are *false* because the values are distinct. Comparing with SameValue is therefore comparing with SameValueZero, stated with the finer relation so that the assertion is checkable.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-composite-function">
      <h1>The Composite Function</h1>
      <p>The Composite function:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>is <dfn>%Composite%</dfn>.</li>
        <li>is the initial value of the *"Composite"* property of the global object.</li>
        <li>has a [[Prototype]] internal slot whose value is %Function.prototype%.</li>
        <li>returns a composite object when called as a function, creating one only when no interned composite of the same contents exists.</li>
        <li>does not have a [[Construct]] internal method; `new Composite(...)` throws a *TypeError* exception.</li>
        <li>does not have a *"prototype"* property.</li>
        <li>is the head of the composite type family: applying |TypeArguments| to it, in type position or in expression position (<emu-xref href="#sec-type-arguments-and-placement-new-in-expression-position"></emu-xref>), yields the Type Object of the composite type over the argument. It is a type error if the argument is not an ~object~, ~tuple~, or interface type, or if it has a Symbol-keyed member, since a composite's keys are Strings. Calling that Type Object is the typed creation of <emu-xref href="#sec-composite-typeobject-call"></emu-xref>, the construction boundary a parameterized type already has (<emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>).</li>
      </ul>

      <emu-clause id="sec-composite-arg">
        <h1>Composite ( _source_ )</h1>
        <p>This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _source_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _isArray_ be ? IsArray(_source_).
          1. If _isArray_ is *true*, then
            1. Let _len_ be ? LengthOfArrayLike(_source_).
            1. Let _elements_ be a new empty List.
            1. Let _i_ be 0.
            1. Repeat, while _i_ &lt; _len_,
              1. Let _v_ be ? Get(_source_, ! ToString(𝔽(_i_))).
              1. Append CanonicalizeCompositeValue(_v_) to _elements_.
              1. Set _i_ to _i_ + 1.
            1. Return ? FindOrCreateTupleComposite(_elements_).
          1. Let _entries_ be a new empty List.
          1. Let _keys_ be ? _source_.[[OwnPropertyKeys]]().
          1. For each element _key_ of _keys_, do
            1. Let _desc_ be ? _source_.[[GetOwnProperty]](_key_).
            1. If _desc_ is not *undefined* and _desc_.[[Enumerable]] is *true*, then
              1. If _key_ is a Symbol, throw a *TypeError* exception.
              1. Let _v_ be ? Get(_source_, _key_).
              1. Append the Composite Entry Record { [[Key]]: _key_, [[Value]]: CanonicalizeCompositeValue(_v_) } to _entries_.
          1. Return FindOrCreateComposite(_entries_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The kind is decided by `Array.isArray` on the argument: an array source creates a tuple composite from its elements 0 through `length` - 1, and any other object creates a record composite from its own enumerable String-keyed properties. Inherited and non-enumerable properties are ignored, getters are invoked eagerly and exactly once each in the order the keys are reported, and an own enumerable Symbol-keyed property is a *TypeError*; a Symbol remains an ordinary <em>value</em>. The source is never itself converted; it only supplies values, and `Composite(template) !== template` for any non-composite template. `Composite` is idempotent as a consequence of interning: `Composite(c)` on a composite reads `c`'s own contents, an array source for a tuple and enumerable entries for a record, and interns to `c` itself.</p>
        </emu-note>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The upstream proposal reads every array through the record path, where `length`, being own but non-enumerable, is dropped, so its `Composite([1, 2])` yields the record `{ 0: 1, 1: 2 }`. The tuple kind is therefore a behavioural change to an existing call rather than an addition, recorded in <emu-xref href="#sec-composite-deviations"></emu-xref>. It resolves the reservations upstream states against a list form, an enumerable `length` and a missing `%Symbol.iterator%`, by making `length` own and non-enumerable and by supplying iteration without a prototype (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-modifications"></emu-xref>); the reservation about creation cost growing with element count stands, and a long list remains a poor key.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-composite-typeobject-call">
        <h1>Calling a Composite Type Object</h1>
        <p>Calling the Type Object of a composite type over a shape _S_, written `Composite.&lt;T&gt;(source)` for the _S_ that is _T_'s structural form, returns the result of CompositeFromShape(_S_, _source_), where _source_ is the call's first argument, or *undefined* where none is supplied. This is the construction boundary of the composite types, as calling any parameterized Type Object is its type's (<emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>).</p>

        <emu-clause id="sec-compositefromshape" type="abstract operation">
          <h1>
            CompositeFromShape (
              _S_: an ~object~ or ~tuple~ Type Record,
              _source_: an ECMAScript language value,
            ): either a normal completion containing a composite object or a throw completion
          </h1>
          <dl class="header">
            <dt>description</dt>
            <dd>It performs the typed creation of a composite: each supplied value is converted to its member's type, a required absence throws, an undeclared property throws, and an optional member's declared default is filled, before canonicalization and interning.</dd>
          </dl>
          <emu-alg>
          1. If _source_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If _S_.[[Kind]] is ~tuple~, then
            1. Let _len_ be ? LengthOfArrayLike(_source_).
            1. If _len_ is not a length _S_ admits (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-membership"></emu-xref>), throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Let _elements_ be a new empty List.
            1. Let _i_ be 0.
            1. Repeat, while _i_ &lt; _len_,
              1. Let _v_ be ? Get(_source_, ! ToString(𝔽(_i_))).
              1. Let _converted_ be ? the conversion of _v_ to the [[Type]] of the element of _S_ that receives position _i_, as at a typed argument (<emu-xref href="#sec-conversions"></emu-xref>).
              1. Append CanonicalizeCompositeValue(_converted_) to _elements_.
              1. Set _i_ to _i_ + 1.
            1. For each element _e_ of _S_.[[Elements]] whose position is not less than _len_, do
              1. If _e_.[[Rest]] is *false*, then
                1. Assert: _e_.[[Initial]] is not ~none~, since a tuple's defaults are trailing (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-and-tuple-types"></emu-xref>) and _len_ is a length _S_ admits.
                1. Append CanonicalizeCompositeValue(_e_.[[Initial]]) to _elements_.
            1. Return ? FindOrCreateTupleComposite(_elements_).
          1. Assert: _S_.[[Kind]] is ~object~.
          1. If any Property Type Record of _S_.[[Properties]] has a [[Key]] that is a Symbol, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _entries_ be a new empty List.
          1. Let _keys_ be ? _source_.[[OwnPropertyKeys]]().
          1. For each element _key_ of _keys_, do
            1. Let _desc_ be ? _source_.[[GetOwnProperty]](_key_).
            1. If _desc_ is not *undefined* and _desc_.[[Enumerable]] is *true*, then
              1. If _key_ is a Symbol, throw a *TypeError* exception.
              1. Let _memberType_ be ~empty~.
              1. Let _p_ be the Property Type Record of _S_.[[Properties]] whose [[Key]] is _key_, or ~empty~ if there is none.
              1. If _p_ is not ~empty~, then
                1. Set _memberType_ to _p_.[[Type]].
              1. Else,
                1. Let _sig_ be the first Index Signature Record of _S_.[[IndexSignatures]] that accepts _key_, or ~empty~ if there is none.
                1. If _sig_ is not ~empty~, set _memberType_ to _sig_.[[ValueType]].
              1. If _memberType_ is ~empty~, throw a *TypeError* exception.
              1. Let _v_ be ? Get(_source_, _key_).
              1. Let _converted_ be ? the conversion of _v_ to _memberType_, as at a typed argument (<emu-xref href="#sec-conversions"></emu-xref>).
              1. Append the Composite Entry Record { [[Key]]: _key_, [[Value]]: CanonicalizeCompositeValue(_converted_) } to _entries_.
          1. For each Property Type Record _p_ of _S_.[[Properties]], do
            1. If no element of _entries_ has [[Key]] _p_.[[Key]], then
              1. If _p_.[[Optional]] is *false*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
              1. If _p_.[[Initial]] is not ~none~, append the Composite Entry Record { [[Key]]: _p_.[[Key]], [[Value]]: CanonicalizeCompositeValue(_p_.[[Initial]]) } to _entries_.
          1. Return FindOrCreateComposite(_entries_).
          </emu-alg>
        </emu-clause>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Typed creation is the untyped algorithm with each supplied value converted to its member's type at the boundary, which is what makes the resulting fields carry _T_'s types and the result's Static Type `Composite.&lt;T&gt;`. It is that algorithm literally: the read set is the same one, the source's own enumerable String-keyed properties in the order its [[OwnPropertyKeys]] internal method reports them, each read exactly once, so the two spellings hold the same data and run the same getters in the same order. What the shape adds is the conversion of each value, the two rejections, and the defaults; what it does not add is a second way of finding a value. Three of its rules deserve their reasons. A required member that is absent throws rather than filling the type's default: a composite is a key, and a key half of whose contents were invented silently would collide with the key that stated them. A property the type does not name throws for the mirror reason: dropping it silently would intern two different sources to one object. And an <em>optional</em> member's declared default, the [[Initial]] of `page?: uint8 = 0`, is written here, before freezing and before interning, so `Composite.&lt;CacheKey&gt;({ id: 7 })` and `Composite.&lt;CacheKey&gt;({ id: 7, page: 0 })` are the same object; an optional member with no declared default simply stays absent. A default is a construction feature (<emu-xref href="#sec-object-types"></emu-xref>), typed composite creation is a construction site, and a check of a composite that already exists writes nothing to it, which for a frozen shared object is not a preference but the only possibility.</p>
          <p>The contextual type of _source_ is _T_'s structural form (<emu-xref href="#sec-contextual-types"></emu-xref>), so numeric literals in the source take the member types by <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref> and the conversions above are checks rather than coercions at a literal creation site. A binding annotated `Composite.&lt;T&gt;` gives an unadorned `Composite(...)` initializer the same treatment through the return-type direction of overload resolution (<emu-xref href="#sec-overloading-on-return-type"></emu-xref>). An unannotated, uncontextualized `Composite({ id: 7 })` in typed code produces Number fields; code that means anything else says so at the creation site.</p>
          <p>That the read set is the enumerable own properties, rather than everything a lookup would answer, is a decision and the alternative is worth naming. A boundary check (<emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref>) reads through accessors and the prototype chain, because it asks whether a value can be used as the type; a creation asks what the new value's contents are, and every operation in the language that copies contents, `Object.assign`, object spread, `JSON.stringify`, and structured cloning among them, reads the enumerable own properties. A composite is a copy, so it reads what a copy reads. The cost is that a member supplied only by a prototype accessor or a non-enumerable own property does not satisfy the shape; the benefit is that the case fails loudly, with a *TypeError* naming the absent member, where the untyped creation silently produces a composite without it. Reading through the chain instead cannot be made symmetric in any case: the rejection of an undeclared property needs a finite set of keys to walk, and a lookup has none, since every object inherits *"toString"*.</p>
          <p>A rejection therefore throws part way through, after the getters preceding it in key order have run, exactly as the untyped creation's Symbol-key rejection does. A trailing tuple position the source does not supply is filled from its declared default, as an absent optional member is, and for the same reason: `Composite.&lt;[uint8, uint32 = 10]&gt;([1])` and `Composite.&lt;[uint8, uint32 = 10]&gt;([1, 10])` are one object. A position the source does not supply always carries a default, since a tuple's defaults are trailing and the admitted lengths stop only where one begins (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-and-tuple-types"></emu-xref>). One consequence of filling is worth stating: `Composite.&lt;T&gt;` is not idempotent where _T_ declares an optional member with a default, since `Composite.&lt;CacheKey&gt;(_c_)` fills `page` onto a composite _c_ that lacks it and so returns a different object. That is the rule working rather than failing, because every creation through `Composite.&lt;CacheKey&gt;` fills it and they therefore agree with each other; a composite lacking the member satisfies the type by width subtyping and is a different key, which is what it is.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-composite.iscomposite">
        <h1>Composite.isComposite ( _arg_ )</h1>
        <p>This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Return IsComposite(_arg_).
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-composite-hasinstance">
        <h1>Composite [ %Symbol.hasInstance% ] ( _value_ )</h1>
        <p>This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Return IsComposite(_value_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The Composite function has no *"prototype"* property, so without this method `value instanceof Composite` would throw through OrdinaryHasInstance. With it, `instanceof` against the bare function answers membership in the top composite type, and `instanceof` against `Composite.&lt;T&gt;`, a Type Object, answers membership in that type by <emu-xref href="#sec-instanceof-for-type-objects"></emu-xref>, narrowing the static type on success as any type-object `instanceof` does.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-composite-custommatcher">
        <h1>Composite [ %Symbol.customMatcher% ] ( _subject_, _hint_ )</h1>
        <p>The function performs the following steps when called:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Return IsComposite(_subject_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>`Composite` needs this method because it is a function, not a Type Object: a name pattern that evaluates to a Type Object tests membership, but a plain function is a constant compared by SameValue (<emu-xref href="#sec-patternmatches"></emu-xref>), so without the matcher `when Composite:` would compare the subject against the function itself, a test nothing sensible ever passes. With it, the bare name means what `Composite.&lt;T&gt;` already means narrowed: is this value a composite. In an ecosystem whose default matcher instead invokes a callable as a predicate, the method is load-bearing rather than merely useful, since a bare `when Composite:` would there call `Composite(subject)`, match every object, run the subject's getters, and intern a composite as the side effect of a test. A composite constant used as a pattern remains one pointer comparison, since a constant pattern tests SameValue and an interned composite's SameValue is its contents.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-composite-json">
      <h1>Composites and JSON</h1>
      <p>`JSON.stringify` requires no change: a record composite is an object whose own enumerable properties are its contents, and a tuple composite is an array, so each serializes as its data. Interning is deliberately not on the wire; it is an identity within one heap, not a serialization format.</p>

      <p>The typed parse re-interns: CoerceJSONValue (<emu-xref href="#sec-coercejsonvalue"></emu-xref>) carries a case for composite types, placed before its ~primitive~ case since a composite type is of that kind, which coerces the parsed value against the shape, canonicalizes each resulting value, and obtains the result through FindOrCreateComposite or FindOrCreateTupleComposite. The top composite type is rejected there with a *TypeError* exception, because it states no shape to validate against.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>`JSON.parse.&lt;Composite.&lt;T&gt;&gt;(text)` therefore validates against _T_ by the walk of <emu-xref href="#sec-typed-json-parsing"></emu-xref> and interns the result, so whether a configuration has changed between two polls is `===` between two parses. This answers the mapping the design's serialization document left open for composites. Absent members follow the construction rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-object-types"></emu-xref> through the coercion of the shape, since the shape's own coercion is what fills them: an optional member's declared default is filled and an optional member without one stays absent, and a document whose array is shorter than a tuple shape takes that shape's trailing defaults. A parsed composite therefore interns with the typed creation of the same document.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>A host that serializes objects for transfer between heaps, as the HTML structured clone algorithm does, must serialize a composite as its kind and its contents and, on deserialization, obtain the result through FindOrCreateComposite or FindOrCreateTupleComposite in the receiving heap, each value having been deserialized and canonicalized first. Equality between composites is thereby preserved within every heap, two messages carrying equal contents arriving `===`, and never across heaps, whose registries are disjoint; a composite is never the same object on two sides of a boundary that copies.</p>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-composite-modifications">
      <h1>Modifications to Existing Abstract Operations</h1>

      <emu-clause id="sec-composite-canbeheldweakly" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          CanBeHeldWeakly (
            _v_: an ECMAScript language value,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns *true* if and only if _v_ is suitable for use as a key of a WeakMap, an element of a WeakSet, the target or unregister token of a FinalizationRegistry, or the target of a WeakRef.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _v_ is an Object<ins>, IsComposite(_v_) is *false*, and _v_ is not an instance of a typed class that seals its instances (<emu-xref href="#sec-weak-references-and-typed-objects"></emu-xref>)</ins>, return *true*.
          1. If _v_ is a Symbol and KeyForSymbol(_v_) is *undefined*, return *true*.
          1. Return *false*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The one operation gates every weak position, so this change makes each of WeakMap, WeakSet, WeakRef, and FinalizationRegistry reject a composite with a *TypeError* exception. A composite is excluded for the reason a value type is (<emu-xref href="#sec-weak-references-and-typed-objects"></emu-xref>), reached from the other side: its identity coincides with its contents, so the registry may always resurrect an equal object and there is no liveness for a weak reference to observe, only the registry's own entry, which holding weakly would keep alive. A cache that must not retain composite keys holds their constituent trackable objects weakly instead.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-composite-getiterator" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          GetIterator (
            _obj_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _kind_: ~sync~ or ~async~,
          ): either a normal completion containing an Iterator Record or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It obtains an iterator for _obj_. The algorithm restates the pinned edition's, with the inserted steps marked.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. <ins>If _obj_ is a tuple composite, then</ins>
            1. <ins>Let _iterator_ be CreateArrayIterator(_obj_, ~value~).</ins>
            1. <ins>Let _nextMethod_ be ! Get(_iterator_, *"next"*).</ins>
            1. <ins>Let _iteratorRecord_ be the Iterator Record { [[Iterator]]: _iterator_, [[NextMethod]]: _nextMethod_, [[Done]]: *false* }.</ins>
            1. <ins>If _kind_ is ~async~, return CreateAsyncFromSyncIterator(_iteratorRecord_).</ins>
            1. <ins>Return _iteratorRecord_.</ins>
          1. If _kind_ is ~async~, then
            1. Let _method_ be ? GetMethod(_obj_, %Symbol.asyncIterator%).
            1. If _method_ is *undefined*, then
              1. Let _syncMethod_ be ? GetMethod(_obj_, %Symbol.iterator%).
              1. If _syncMethod_ is *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
              1. Let _syncIteratorRecord_ be ? GetIteratorFromMethod(_obj_, _syncMethod_).
              1. Return CreateAsyncFromSyncIterator(_syncIteratorRecord_).
          1. Else,
            1. Let _method_ be ? GetMethod(_obj_, %Symbol.iterator%).
          1. If _method_ is *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return ? GetIteratorFromMethod(_obj_, _method_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>A tuple composite has a *null* prototype, so no `%Symbol.iterator%` can reach it by lookup, and being frozen it cannot receive one. The inserted step recognizes the kind directly, so `for`-`of`, spread, and array destructuring iterate a tuple composite's elements with no method on the object; the manual protocol spelling is `Array.prototype.values.call(m)`, which builds the same iterator. In typed code the iteration is additionally type-directed, an index loop over known element types, exactly as an array of statically known element type iterates; the step above is what extends that behaviour to untyped code, where nothing else could. Record composites are unaffected and are not iterable, matching ordinary objects.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>The non-mutating array generics apply to a tuple composite explicitly, as `Array.prototype.map.call(m, f)`, or through the typed signatures the standard library gives them; the mutating ones throw on a frozen receiver as they do for any frozen array, and `delete` and every store throw as <emu-xref href="#sec-array-defaults-and-stores"></emu-xref> already provides for typed elements.</p>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-composite-deviations">
      <h1>Deviations from the Upstream Proposal</h1>
      <p>For feedback in both directions, the deltas between this clause and the upstream Composites proposal, in one place:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Interning is type-sensitive, because SameValueZero is: `Composite({ (x: uint8): 1 })` and `Composite({ x: 1 })` are distinct objects. Upstream has one number type and no such distinction to make.</li>
        <li>Canonicalization is stated as a rule over SameValueZero equivalence classes, of which upstream's `-0` normalization is the Number instance; the decimal cohorts and the per-width float zeros are further instances, the NaN encoding requirement extends upstream's to every width a byte view reaches, and the `preserveNegativeZero` option is not adopted.</li>
        <li>The tuple kind is added, and it changes the result of `Composite` on an array source, which upstream reads through the record path with `length` dropped.</li>
        <li>The registry is scoped to the heap rather than to the agent. The two coincide under the pinned edition's agent model; they come apart exactly where the threading extension shares one heap among agents.</li>
        <li>Typed creation through the composite Type Objects, the `Composite.&lt;T&gt;` types with their subtyping and interface satisfaction, and the [[RuntimeType]] a composite carries for reflection are added; they change nothing observable about an untyped creation beyond what canonicalization states.</li>
        <li>`Composite[%Symbol.hasInstance%]` is added so that `instanceof` answers membership rather than throwing, and `Composite[%Symbol.customMatcher%]` is added so that the pattern `Composite` tests membership rather than invoking the function as a predicate; the latter applies to the upstream proposal as it stands and is independent of types.</li>
        <li>The typed JSON parse and the host serialization requirement of <emu-xref href="#sec-composite-json"></emu-xref> are defined.</li>
        <li>Everything else, the creation semantics and eager getters, the Symbol-key refusal, the canonical key order, frozenness and the *null* prototype, `Composite.isComposite`, the weak-position refusal, the cross-realm identity within one registry, and the impossibility of cycles, since a composite is frozen at birth and can reference only values that already existed, is adopted as upstream specifies.</li>
      </ul>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-pattern-matching">
    <h1>Pattern Matching</h1>
    <p>This clause specifies the design's pattern matching extension: the `match` expression, the pattern grammar, the widening of `is` to take a pattern, and the exhaustiveness and narrowing rules that make a `match` over a closed type checked complete. A pattern is a test, a destructuring, and a proof at once; the runtime semantics here perform the first two, and the static semantics hand the third to the narrowing machinery of <emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref>.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-match-expression">
      <h1>The match Expression</h1>
      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        <ins>PrimaryExpression[Yield, Await] :
          MatchExpression[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchExpression[Yield, Await] :
          `match` [no LineTerminator here] `(` Expression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `)` `{` MatchClauses[?Yield, ?Await] `}`
          `match` [no LineTerminator here] `all` [no LineTerminator here] `(` Expression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `)` `{` MatchAllClauses[?Yield, ?Await] `}`

        MatchAllClauses[Yield, Await] :
          MatchAllClause[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchAllClauses[?Yield, ?Await] MatchAllClause[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchAllClause[Yield, Await] :
          `when` MatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await] MatchGuard[?Yield, ?Await]? `:` MatchArmBody[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchClauses[Yield, Await] :
          MatchClause[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchClauses[?Yield, ?Await] MatchClause[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchClause[Yield, Await] :
          `when` MatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await] MatchGuard[?Yield, ?Await]? `:` MatchArmBody[?Yield, ?Await]
          `default` `:` MatchArmBody[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchGuard[Yield, Await] :
          `if` `(` Expression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `)`

        MatchArmBody[Yield, Await] :
          AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `;`
          `throw` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `;`
          Block[?Yield, ?Await, ~Return]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>
      <p>The |Block| of a |MatchArmBody| carries the [Return], [Yield], and [Await] permissions of the function enclosing the `match`, so `return`, `break`, `continue`, `await`, and `yield` inside a block arm mean what they mean in that function; the ~Return~ above is notational for the production and the permission is threaded in the obvious way by the enclosing contexts. The `;` of an expression arm is subject to automatic semicolon insertion as the `;` of an expression statement is.</p>

      <p>The second form answers with <strong>every</strong> clause that matched rather than the first, as an Array in clause order. It differs from the first in three ways and in no others - patterns, guards, bindings, per-clause narrowing, arm bodies, and the single evaluation of the subject are all the same:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>the value is an Array of the matched clauses' values, empty when none matched;</li>
        <li>exhaustiveness is not required (<emu-xref href="#sec-match-exhaustiveness"></emu-xref>);</li>
        <li>subtraction narrowing between clauses does not apply, since a later clause is reached whether or not an earlier one matched.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>A |MatchAllClause| admits no `default`. `default` and the wildcard pattern are interchangeable in a |MatchClause|, and a form in which one meant "always" and the other "only where nothing else did" would give the same spelling two meanings one form apart. A clause that always contributes is written `when _`.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>`all` is a contextual keyword: it is the modifier only where a `(` follows it on the same line, and it remains an ordinary identifier everywhere, including as a binding, a property, and a function name. The [no LineTerminator here] restriction before it is what keeps `match` on one line followed by `all(x)` on the next meaning the two statements it means today.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <p>It is a Syntax Error if a `default` |MatchClause| is not the last of its |MatchClauses|.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>`match`, `when`, `and`, `or`, and `not` are contextual keywords, treated as keywords only in the positions above and in the pattern grammar, which are Syntax Errors today. `match(x) {}` remains a call followed by a block, because |MatchClauses| requires at least one clause; in statement position the braced sequel of `match (`&hellip;`)` is parsed under a cover, as the `type` operator's parenthesized operand is (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-in-expression-position"></emu-xref>), and refined to |MatchClauses| where they parse as such, otherwise remaining the block it is today. Every program the refinement accepts was a Syntax Error: a clause's pattern is never empty, so the label `when:` never reads as a clause, `default` is reserved and cannot label a statement, and `when` followed by a pattern is not a statement sequence. In expression position there is no overlap at all, since a call followed by `{` is a Syntax Error there.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-clause id="sec-match-evaluation">
        <h1>Runtime Semantics: Evaluation</h1>
        <p>A <dfn variants="Match Cache Records">Match Cache Record</dfn> has a [[Reads]] field, a List of Records { [[Object]], [[Key]], [[Present]], [[Value]] }, and an [[Iterations]] field, a List of Records { [[Object]], [[Elements]] (a List), [[Done]] (a Boolean), [[Iterator]] (an Iterator Record) }. It memoizes what a `match` reads of its subject, so that a property is read at most once and an iterator is obtained at most once however many patterns look, and every pattern of one `match` sees the same values.</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _subject_ be ? GetValue(? Evaluation of |Expression|).
          1. Let _cache_ be the Match Cache Record { [[Reads]]: a new empty List, [[Iterations]]: a new empty List }.
          1. For each |MatchClause| _clause_ of |MatchClauses|, in source order, do
            1. Let _outerEnv_ be the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment.
            1. Let _env_ be NewDeclarativeEnvironment(_outerEnv_).
            1. For each element _name_ of the BoundNames of _clause_, do
              1. If the |MatchBindingPattern| binding _name_ has `const`, perform ! _env_.CreateImmutableBinding(_name_, *true*).
              1. Else, perform ! _env_.CreateMutableBinding(_name_, *false*).
            1. Set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _env_.
            1. If _clause_ is a `default` clause, let _matched_ be *true*.
            1. Else, let _matched_ be Completion(PatternMatches(the clause's |MatchPattern|, _subject_, _cache_, _env_)).
            1. If _matched_ is an abrupt completion, set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _outerEnv_ and return ? _matched_.
            1. Set _matched_ to ! _matched_.
            1. If _matched_ is *true* and _clause_ has a |MatchGuard|, then
              1. Let _guard_ be Completion(GetValue(Completion(Evaluation of the |MatchGuard|'s |Expression|))).
              1. If _guard_ is an abrupt completion, set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _outerEnv_ and return ? _guard_.
              1. Set _matched_ to ToBoolean(! _guard_).
            1. If _matched_ is *true*, then
              1. Let _result_ be Completion(MatchArmEvaluation of the clause's |MatchArmBody|).
              1. Set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _outerEnv_.
              1. Return ? _result_.
            1. Set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _outerEnv_.
          1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
        </emu-alg>

        <p>The second form collects rather than returning:</p>

        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _subject_ be ? GetValue(? Evaluation of |Expression|).
          1. Let _cache_ be the Match Cache Record { [[Reads]]: a new empty List, [[Iterations]]: a new empty List }.
          1. Let _results_ be a new empty List.
          1. For each |MatchAllClause| _clause_ of |MatchAllClauses|, in source order, do
            1. Let _outerEnv_ be the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment.
            1. Let _env_ be NewDeclarativeEnvironment(_outerEnv_).
            1. For each element _name_ of the BoundNames of _clause_, do
              1. If the |MatchBindingPattern| binding _name_ has `const`, perform ! _env_.CreateImmutableBinding(_name_, *true*).
              1. Else, perform ! _env_.CreateMutableBinding(_name_, *false*).
            1. Set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _env_.
            1. Let _matched_ be Completion(MatchPatternMatches of the clause's |MatchPattern| with arguments _subject_ and _cache_).
            1. If _matched_ is an abrupt completion, set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _outerEnv_ and return ? _matched_.
            1. Set _matched_ to ! _matched_.
            1. If _matched_ is *true* and _clause_ has a |MatchGuard|, then
              1. Let _guard_ be Completion(GetValue(Completion(Evaluation of the |MatchGuard|'s |Expression|))).
              1. If _guard_ is an abrupt completion, set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _outerEnv_ and return ? _guard_.
              1. Set _matched_ to ToBoolean(! _guard_).
            1. If _matched_ is *true*, then
              1. Let _result_ be Completion(MatchArmEvaluation of the clause's |MatchArmBody|).
              1. Set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _outerEnv_.
              1. Append ? _result_ to _results_.
            1. Else,
              1. Set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _outerEnv_.
          1. Return CreateArrayFromList(_results_).
        </emu-alg>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The subject is evaluated once and the cache is shared across clauses, exactly as in the first form, so a property is read at most once however many patterns look at it.</p>
          <p>An abrupt completion from any clause propagates, which is what makes an arm that `throw`s abort the collection: the clauses before it have already evaluated their arm bodies, and those values are lost with the expression. An arm that RETURNS a failure rather than throwing one is what collecting failures looks like, and needs nothing further - the value is the failure and the result is already the collection.</p>
          <p>Reaching the end without a match returns an empty Array rather than throwing, which is the whole of why exhaustiveness is not required of this form.</p>
        </emu-note>
        <p>MatchArmEvaluation of `AssignmentExpression ;` returns ? GetValue(? Evaluation of |AssignmentExpression|). Of `throw AssignmentExpression ;`, it evaluates as the `throw` statement does. Of a |Block|, it is the evaluation of a `do` expression's |Block| (<emu-xref href="#sec-do-expressions"></emu-xref>): the block is evaluated and the result is its completion value.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>An arm's block was given a narrower rule than this while do expressions were outstanding - its value was its final statement's only where that statement was an |ExpressionStatement|, and ~void~ otherwise - and that rule was a strict subset of this one, so no program changes meaning by its replacement. What it could not do was let an arm end in an `if` with an `else` or a `try`, and what it did badly was make an arm ending in a declaration a silent ~void~ rather than the Syntax Error <emu-xref href="#sec-do-expression-early-errors"></emu-xref> now gives. The bindings of a clause are created immutable and are initialized by the pattern as it matches; a clause that does not match discards its environment, so a binding never leaks between arms, and the guard and body of the arm that runs see exactly what its own pattern bound.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-match-patterns">
      <h1>Patterns</h1>
      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        <ins>MatchPattern[Yield, Await] :
          MatchOrPattern[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchOrPattern[Yield, Await] :
          MatchAndPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchOrPattern[?Yield, ?Await] `or` MatchAndPattern[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchAndPattern[Yield, Await] :
          MatchUnaryPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchAndPattern[?Yield, ?Await] `and` MatchUnaryPattern[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchUnaryPattern[Yield, Await] :
          `not` MatchUnaryPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchPrimaryPattern[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchPrimaryPattern[Yield, Await] :
          `(` MatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await] `)`
          `_`
          `extends` TypePattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchBindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          `${` Expression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `}`
          ObjectMatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          ArrayMatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchNamePattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchNamePattern[?Yield, ?Await] `(` MatchPatternList[?Yield, ?Await]? `)`
          MatchNamePattern[?Yield, ?Await] ObjectMatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchNamePattern[?Yield, ?Await] ArrayMatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchLiteralPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          Type[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchBindingPattern[Yield, Await] :
          `let` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?
          `const` BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] TypeAnnotation[?Yield, ?Await]?

        TypePattern[Yield, Await] :
          Type[?Yield, ?Await]

        TypeArgument[Yield, Await] :
          BindingIdentifier[?Yield, ?Await] `:` Type[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchNamePattern[Yield, Await] :
          IdentifierReference[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchNamePattern[?Yield, ?Await] `.` IdentifierName
          MatchNamePattern[?Yield, ?Await] TypeArguments[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchPatternList[Yield, Await] :
          MatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchPatternList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` MatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await]

        ObjectMatchPattern[Yield, Await] :
          `{` `}`
          `{` MatchPropertyList[?Yield, ?Await] `,`? `}`
          `{` MatchPropertyList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` `...` MatchBindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await] `}`
          `{` `...` MatchBindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await] `}`

        MatchPropertyList[Yield, Await] :
          MatchProperty[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchPropertyList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` MatchProperty[?Yield, ?Await]

        MatchProperty[Yield, Await] :
          PropertyName[?Yield, ?Await] `:` MatchPattern[?Yield, ?Await]
          MatchBindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await]

        ArrayMatchPattern[Yield, Await] :
          `[` `]`
          `[` MatchPatternList[?Yield, ?Await] `,`? `]`
          `[` MatchPatternList[?Yield, ?Await] `,` `...` MatchBindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await] `]`
          `[` `...` MatchBindingPattern[?Yield, ?Await] `]`</ins>
      </emu-grammar>
      <p>The identifier `_` is the wildcard wherever a pattern is expected, taking precedence over its |MatchNamePattern| reading, so a program that binds the name `_` and wants its value writes `${_}`. A |MatchProperty| that is a |MatchBindingPattern| is the shorthand: `let x` is `x: let x`. Within a |MatchPattern|, `${` is recognized as a punctuator, a lexical goal the pattern context sets as the template contexts do; outside one it lexes as it does today. A `MatchLiteralPattern` is a |ShortCircuitExpression| restricted by the early errors below to a literal-like form; the restriction is applied after the cover parse, as the parenthesized operand of `type` is refined (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-in-expression-position"></emu-xref>).</p>
      <emu-grammar type="definition">
        <ins>MatchLiteralPattern[Yield, Await] :
          ShortCircuitExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
      </emu-grammar>
      <ul>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if a |MatchLiteralPattern|'s |ShortCircuitExpression| is not one of: a |NumericLiteral|, optionally preceded by `+` or `-`; a |StringLiteral|; a |NoSubstitutionTemplate|; `true`, `false`, or `null`; a |RegularExpressionLiteral|; or a range literal of <emu-xref href="#sec-range-literals"></emu-xref>, each of whose present endpoints is one of the preceding forms or a |MatchNamePattern| form.</li>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if the two alternatives of a `MatchOrPattern` do not have the same BoundNames.</li>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if the BoundNames of a |MatchPattern| contain any duplicate entries, except that the same name bound by both alternatives of an `or` is one binding.</li>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if the `BindingIdentifier : Type` form of |TypeArgument| appears outside a |TypePattern|. That alternative is the <dfn id="pattern-slot" variants="pattern slots">pattern slot</dfn>, the syntactic form of the slot that <emu-xref href="#sec-structural-matching"></emu-xref> creates reflectively, and it is meaningful only where a type is being matched rather than written.</li>
        <li>It is a type error if the |MatchNamePattern| of a juxtaposition or of an extractor form resolves to a binding: the juxtaposed head must denote a type, and the extractor head must denote a value with a %Symbol.customMatcher% method, decided at the site where the head's Static Type is known and at run time otherwise.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A |MatchNamePattern| and a |Type| overlap where a name is both; the name form is preferred, and the two readings agree wherever both exist, since a name denoting a type matches by membership under either. The |Type| alternative exists for the forms only a type can spell, a union of literal types being the or-pattern for constants with no combinator: `'GET' | 'HEAD'` is one type pattern whose test is two comparisons. A |MatchLiteralPattern| and a ~literal~ |Type| likewise coincide; the literal form is preferred and the semantics are one.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-patternmatches" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          PatternMatches (
            _P_: a Parse Node,
            _subject_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _cache_: a Match Cache Record,
            _env_: an Environment Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing a Boolean or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It decides whether the pattern _P_ matches _subject_, initializing the bindings _P_ declares in _env_ as it does. Sub-patterns are tried in source order and the operation returns at the first failure, so user code a pattern can reach, a getter, a matcher, an interpolation, runs at most once and only up to the deciding test.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _P_ is `MatchOrPattern : MatchOrPattern or MatchAndPattern`, then
            1. Let _left_ be ? PatternMatches(the first alternative, _subject_, _cache_, _env_).
            1. If _left_ is *true*, return *true*.
            1. Return ? PatternMatches(the second alternative, _subject_, _cache_, _env_).
          1. If _P_ is `MatchAndPattern : MatchAndPattern and MatchUnaryPattern`, then
            1. Let _left_ be ? PatternMatches(the first operand, _subject_, _cache_, _env_).
            1. If _left_ is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Return ? PatternMatches(the second operand, _subject_, _cache_, _env_).
          1. If _P_ is `MatchUnaryPattern : not MatchUnaryPattern`, then
            1. Let _inner_ be ? PatternMatches(the operand, _subject_, _cache_, _env_).
            1. If _inner_ is *true*, return *false*; else return *true*.
          1. If _P_ is a parenthesized pattern, return ? PatternMatches(its |MatchPattern|, _subject_, _cache_, _env_).
          1. If _P_ is `MatchPrimaryPattern : _`, return *true*.
          1. If _P_ is `MatchPrimaryPattern : extends TypePattern`, return MatchTypePattern(the |TypePattern|, _subject_, _env_).
          1. If _P_ is a |MatchBindingPattern|, then
            1. If _P_ has a |TypeAnnotation|, then
              1. Let _ok_ be ? IsOfType(_subject_, the Type Record it denotes).
              1. If _ok_ is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Perform ! _env_.InitializeBinding(the StringValue of _P_'s |BindingIdentifier|, _subject_).
            1. Return *true*.
          1. If _P_ is `${ Expression }`, then
            1. Let _v_ be ? GetValue(? Evaluation of |Expression|).
            1. Return MatchConstant(_subject_, _v_).
          1. If _P_ is a |MatchLiteralPattern|, then
            1. Let _v_ be ? GetValue(? Evaluation of its |ShortCircuitExpression|), except that a numeric literal takes the contextual type of the pattern's position (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref>).
            1. If _v_ is a Range, return ? MatchRange(_subject_, _v_).
            1. If _v_ is an Object with a [[RegExpMatcher]] internal slot, return ? MatchRegExp(_subject_, _v_, ~none~, _cache_, _env_).
            1. If _P_'s |ShortCircuitExpression| is a |NumericLiteral| with no preceding `+` or `-` whose value is a zero, return the result of the sameValueZero comparison of _subject_ and _v_ within their type, *false* where their types differ.
            1. Return MatchConstant(_subject_, _v_).
          1. If _P_ is a |Type|, then
            1. Let _t_ be the Type Record _P_ denotes.
            1. Let _structural_ be ? MatchStructuralType(_t_, _subject_, _cache_).
            1. If _structural_ is not ~not-applicable~, return _structural_.
            1. Return ? IsOfType(_subject_, _t_).
          1. If _P_ is a |MatchNamePattern| alone, then
            1. If _P_ resolves to a type, then
              1. Let _t_ be the Type Record it denotes.
              1. Let _structural_ be ? MatchStructuralType(_t_, _subject_, _cache_).
              1. If _structural_ is not ~not-applicable~, return _structural_.
              1. Return ? IsOfType(_subject_, _t_).
            1. Let _v_ be ? GetValue(? Evaluation of _P_ read as the member expression it spells).
            1. If _v_ is a Type Object, return ? IsOfType(_subject_, the Type Record _v_ reifies).
            1. If _v_ is a Range, return ? MatchRange(_subject_, _v_).
            1. If _v_ is an Object with a [[RegExpMatcher]] internal slot, return ? MatchRegExp(_subject_, _v_, ~none~, _cache_, _env_).
            1. If _v_ is an Object, then
              1. Let _matcher_ be ? GetMethod(_v_, %Symbol.customMatcher%).
              1. If _matcher_ is not *undefined*, then
                1. Let _r_ be ? Call(_matcher_, _v_, &laquo; _subject_ &raquo;).
                1. If _r_ is an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
                1. Return ToBoolean(_r_).
            1. Return MatchConstant(_subject_, _v_).
          1. If _P_ is `MatchNamePattern ( MatchPatternList? )`, then
            1. Let _v_ be ? GetValue(? Evaluation of the |MatchNamePattern| read as the member expression it spells).
            1. If _v_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Let _matcher_ be ? GetMethod(_v_, %Symbol.customMatcher%).
            1. If _matcher_ is *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Let _r_ be ? Call(_matcher_, _v_, &laquo; _subject_ &raquo;).
            1. If _r_ is *null*, return *false*.
            1. If _r_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Let _len_ be ? LengthOfArrayLike(_r_).
            1. If _len_ &ne; the number of patterns in the |MatchPatternList|, or the list is absent and _len_ &ne; 0, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Let _i_ be 0.
            1. For each pattern _p_ of the |MatchPatternList|, in order, do
              1. Let _e_ be ? Get(_r_, ! ToString(𝔽(_i_))).
              1. If ? PatternMatches(_p_, _e_, _cache_, _env_) is *false*, return *false*.
              1. Set _i_ to _i_ + 1.
            1. Return *true*.
          1. If _P_ is a juxtaposition of a |MatchNamePattern| and an |ObjectMatchPattern| or |ArrayMatchPattern|, then
            1. Let _t_ be the Type Record the |MatchNamePattern| denotes; it is a *TypeError* exception at run time if it denotes none.
            1. Let _ofType_ be ? IsOfType(_subject_, _t_).
            1. If _ofType_ is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Return ? PatternMatches(the structural pattern, _subject_, _cache_, _env_).
          1. If _P_ is an |ObjectMatchPattern|, return ? MatchObjectPattern(_P_, _subject_, _cache_, _env_).
          1. Assert: _P_ is an |ArrayMatchPattern|.
          1. Return ? MatchArrayPattern(_P_, _subject_, _cache_, _env_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>`NaN` needs no rule of its own: it is a name pattern holding a Number, so MatchConstant compares it with the type's sameValue, under which a NaN equals a NaN, and a `float32` NaN does not match a `float64` one because their types differ. MatchConstant(_a_, _b_) is the sameValue comparison within one type: *false* where the types of _a_ and _b_ differ, and the type's T::sameValue otherwise, with SameValue for values that are not of a numeric type. For an interned composite that is one pointer comparison, and for a value type class instance it is the fieldwise comparison its `===` performs. The bare-zero step is the one SameValueZero literal: `0` matches both zeros of the position's type at every width, while an explicit `+0` or `-0` distinguishes them, the design's rule stated per type.</p>
          <p>The name form dispatches on what the name turns out to hold, so a range or a regular expression bound to a constant matches as the literal spelling would, by containment and by whole-subject match; the escape to identity in every case is `${name}`. A boolean-returning matcher reached without parentheses is a membership test, the form `Composite` uses (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-custommatcher"></emu-xref>); a matcher that returns an object there is a *TypeError*, as parentheses on a boolean matcher are, so the two protocols cannot be confused silently. Matchers and interpolations are the patterns that run user code, and they run in source order at most once each per match attempt.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-match-structural" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          MatchObjectPattern (
            _P_: a Parse Node,
            _subject_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _cache_: a Match Cache Record,
            _env_: an Environment Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing a Boolean or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It matches an |ObjectMatchPattern|: each named member must be present, by the `in` test, and its value must match its sub-pattern; the rest binding, where present, collects the remaining own enumerable members.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _subject_ is not an Object, return *false*.
          1. For each |MatchProperty| _prop_ of _P_, in order, do
            1. Let _key_ be the property key _prop_ names.
            1. If no element of _cache_.[[Reads]] has [[Object]] _subject_ and [[Key]] _key_, then
              1. Let _present_ be ? HasProperty(_subject_, _key_).
              1. If _present_ is *true*, let _value_ be ? Get(_subject_, _key_); else let _value_ be *undefined*.
              1. Append the Record { [[Object]]: _subject_, [[Key]]: _key_, [[Present]]: _present_, [[Value]]: _value_ } to _cache_.[[Reads]].
            1. Let _read_ be the element of _cache_.[[Reads]] whose [[Object]] is _subject_ and whose [[Key]] is _key_.
            1. If _read_.[[Present]] is *false*, return *false*.
            1. If ? PatternMatches(the sub-pattern of _prop_, _read_.[[Value]], _cache_, _env_) is *false*, return *false*.
          1. If _P_ has a rest |MatchBindingPattern|, then
            1. Let _rest_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(%Object.prototype%).
            1. Perform ? CopyDataProperties(_rest_, _subject_, the List of the property keys the |MatchProperty| list names).
            1. Perform ! _env_.InitializeBinding(the StringValue of its |BindingIdentifier|, _rest_).
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The cache is why arms agree: a getter named by three patterns runs once, and the presence test and the read are one cached touch, a HasProperty and at most one Get per key per match. A composite subject makes the cache unnecessary rather than the guarantee weaker, since its every read is idempotent, and an implementation may skip it there (<emu-xref href="#sec-composites"></emu-xref>). An absent optional member fails the pattern rather than matching *undefined*, which is the design's presence rule; matching the absence is a guard's job. A member the pattern does not name is ignored, since this type system has width subtyping and no exact object type, so a pattern has nothing to say about members it did not name and no token it must spell to stay silent about them; a member whose value does not matter is named against the wildcard, which tests presence and reads nothing.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-match-array" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          MatchArrayPattern (
            _P_: a Parse Node,
            _subject_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _cache_: a Match Cache Record,
            _env_: an Environment Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing a Boolean or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It matches an |ArrayMatchPattern| through iteration rather than through an array test, which is what reaches every array-shaped value of this proposal - a `[`_N_`].&lt;T&gt;` need not be an Array exotic object, a tuple composite is iterable by kind rather than by prototype, and a typed view answers no array predicate - memoized per subject: elements are pulled as patterns need them, a pattern without a rest element requires the iterator exhausted at its length, and a rest binding collects the remainder into a fresh Array.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _subject_ is not an Object, return *false*.
          1. If no element of _cache_.[[Iterations]] has [[Object]] _subject_, then
            1. Let _iteratorRecord_ be ? GetIterator(_subject_, ~sync~).
            1. Append the Record { [[Object]]: _subject_, [[Elements]]: a new empty List, [[Done]]: *false*, [[Iterator]]: _iteratorRecord_ } to _cache_.[[Iterations]].
          1. Let _it_ be the element of _cache_.[[Iterations]] whose [[Object]] is _subject_.
          1. Let _n_ be the number of patterns in _P_'s |MatchPatternList|, or 0 where it is absent.
          1. Perform ? MatchIteratorFill(_it_, _n_ + 1).
          1. If _P_ has no rest |MatchBindingPattern|, then
            1. If the number of elements of _it_.[[Elements]] &ne; _n_ or _it_.[[Done]] is *false*, return *false*.
          1. Else,
            1. If the number of elements of _it_.[[Elements]] &lt; _n_, return *false*.
          1. Let _i_ be 0.
          1. For each pattern _p_ of the |MatchPatternList|, in order, do
            1. If ? PatternMatches(_p_, _it_.[[Elements]][_i_], _cache_, _env_) is *false*, return *false*.
            1. Set _i_ to _i_ + 1.
          1. If _P_ has a rest |MatchBindingPattern|, then
            1. Repeat, while _it_.[[Done]] is *false*,
              1. Perform ? MatchIteratorFill(_it_, the number of elements of _it_.[[Elements]] + 1).
            1. Let _restList_ be the List of the elements of _it_.[[Elements]] from index _n_ onward.
            1. Perform ! _env_.InitializeBinding(the StringValue of its |BindingIdentifier|, CreateArrayFromList(_restList_)).
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-matchtypepattern" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          MatchTypePattern (
            _P_: a |TypePattern| Parse Node,
            _subject_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _env_: an Environment Record,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It matches a type against a pattern of types: _subject_ must be a Type Object, its type must be within the type _P_ writes, and each pattern slot of _P_ binds the component of _subject_ that stands where it does.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _subject_ is not a Type Object, return *false*.
          1. Let _t_ be the Type Record _subject_ reifies.
          1. If _P_ contains no pattern slot, return IsAssignable(_t_, the Type Record _P_ denotes).
          1. Let _pattern_ be the Type Record _P_ denotes with each pattern slot replaced by a fresh slot (<emu-xref href="#sec-structural-matching"></emu-xref>).
          1. Let _bindings_ be the one-sided structural unification of _pattern_ against _t_ (<emu-xref href="#sec-structural-matching"></emu-xref>).
          1. If _bindings_ is *null*, return *false*.
          1. For each pattern slot _s_ of _P_, do
            1. Let _v_ be the component _bindings_ binds for _s_: the Type Object where that component is a Type Record, and the component itself where it is a metadata object or another value.
            1. If IsAssignable(RuntimeTypeOf(_v_), the Type Record _s_'s |Type| denotes) is *false*, return *false*.
          1. For each pattern slot _s_ of _P_, perform ! _env_.InitializeBinding(the StringValue of _s_'s |BindingIdentifier|, the value bound for _s_ above).
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The two halves answer different questions and both are needed. Without slots the test is assignability, so `when extends string:` matches a `string` and every refinement of one, which is what the keyword promises to a reader who has met it in a type position. With slots the test is unification, because a slot has to bind the component that stands where it does, and unification is the operation that finds it; the slot's own |Type| is then an ordinary constraint on what it bound, exactly as <emu-xref href="#sec-structural-matching"></emu-xref> says an `infer S extends string` is a check afterwards.</p>
          <p>The consequence worth stating is that a slotted pattern matches structurally where an unslotted one matches up to subtyping: `extends [].&lt;E: type&gt;` does not match a `[5].&lt;uint8&gt;`, whose extent is 5 where the pattern's is ~dynamic~, although `extends [].&lt;uint8&gt;` does match it. Unification up to subtyping is a different and much larger operation, and this specification does not perform it anywhere; a pattern is written against the shape it expects, which is the same discipline <emu-xref href="#sec-structural-matching"></emu-xref> already imposes in refusing a slot inside an ~application~.</p>
          <p>The positions a slot may stand in are the ones this specification fixes: the [[Metadata]] of a ~parameterized~ Type Record and the [[Arguments]] of a ~nominal~ one. A slot standing where the generics extension rather than this specification defines the position, a variadic pack among them, reaches as far as that extension does (<emu-xref href="#sec-generics"></emu-xref>), and a slot inside an ~application~ is refused outright, which is the boundary <emu-xref href="#sec-structural-matching"></emu-xref> already draws for the same reason: a position no operation here can invert is a position no pattern can bind.</p>
        <p>A slot binds a value rather than always a Type Object because the positions of a parameterized type do not all hold types: `float32.&lt;B: NumberBounds&gt;` binds the metadata object, which is what makes `{ ...B }` in the design's `constraintsFor` an ordinary spread, while `Map.&lt;K: type, V: type&gt;` binds Type Objects. RuntimeTypeOf of the bound value is what the constraint is checked against in either case, and it is also the binding's Static Type (<emu-xref href="#sec-pattern-static-semantics"></emu-xref>).</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-matchiteratorfill" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          MatchIteratorFill (
            _it_: a Record of a Match Cache Record's [[Iterations]],
            _count_: a non-negative integer,
          ): either a normal completion containing ~unused~ or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It advances the memoized iteration until _it_.[[Elements]] has _count_ elements or the iterator is exhausted.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Repeat, while _it_.[[Done]] is *false* and the number of elements of _it_.[[Elements]] &lt; _count_,
            1. Let _next_ be ? IteratorStepValue(_it_.[[Iterator]]).
            1. If _next_ is ~done~, set _it_.[[Done]] to *true*.
            1. Else, append _next_ to _it_.[[Elements]].
          1. Return ~unused~.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-matchstructuraltype" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          MatchStructuralType (
            _t_: a Type Record,
            _subject_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _cache_: a Match Cache Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing either a Boolean or ~not-applicable~, or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It matches a structural object type MEMBER BY MEMBER, reading each member through _cache_, so that the guarantee in <emu-xref href="#sec-match-expression"></emu-xref> — that a property is read at most once however many patterns look — holds for a type pattern as it does for an object pattern. It returns ~not-applicable~ where _t_ is not a type this treatment applies to, and the caller then uses IsOfType.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _t_ is not an ~object~ Type Record and is not a structural nominal type whose [[Structure]] is one, return ~not-applicable~.
          1. Let _shape_ be _t_ or its [[Structure]], as applicable.
          1. If _shape_ has any index signature, return ~not-applicable~.
          1. If any member of _shape_.[[Properties]] is optional or is not named by a String, return ~not-applicable~.
          1. If _shape_.[[Properties]] is empty, return ~not-applicable~.
          1. If _subject_ is not an Object, return *false*.
          1. For each element _p_ of _shape_.[[Properties]], do
            1. If no element of _cache_.[[Reads]] has [[Object]] _subject_ and [[Key]] _p_.[[Key]], then
              1. Let _present_ be ? HasProperty(_subject_, _p_.[[Key]]).
              1. If _present_ is *true*, let _value_ be ? Get(_subject_, _p_.[[Key]]); else let _value_ be *undefined*.
              1. Append the Record { [[Object]]: _subject_, [[Key]]: _p_.[[Key]], [[Present]]: _present_, [[Value]]: _value_ } to _cache_.[[Reads]].
            1. Let _read_ be the element of _cache_.[[Reads]] with [[Object]] _subject_ and [[Key]] _p_.[[Key]].
            1. If _read_.[[Present]] is *false*, return *false*.
            1. If ? IsOfType(_read_.[[Value]], _p_.[[Type]]) is *false*, return *false*.
          1. Return *true*.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Three kinds of object type are excluded, and each is excluded because a member-by-member test cannot express it. An OPTIONAL member is satisfied by its absence, where this test requires presence. An INDEX SIGNATURE names no members to walk. A NOMINAL type rejects a value that merely has the right members, so it can never become a structural test; an interface is structural and is not excluded.</p>
          <p>Without this, `IsOfType` reads the subject outside the cache, and two clauses of one `match` could see different values of one member. That is observable as a WRONG ARM rather than as a slower one: given a getter whose value changes, `when { g: 2 }` followed by `when { g: 1 }` reads twice and matches neither, where a single read matches the second.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-matchconstant" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          MatchConstant (
            _a_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _b_: an ECMAScript language value,
          ): a Boolean
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It is the sameValue comparison within one type: *false* where the types of _a_ and _b_ differ, the type's T::sameValue where they are of one numeric type, and SameValue otherwise.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _a_ and _b_ are not values of one type, return *false*.
          1. If _a_ is a value of a numeric type, return the sameValue comparison of that type applied to _a_ and _b_.
          1. Return SameValue(_a_, _b_).
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-matchrange" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          MatchRange (
            _subject_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _r_: a Range,
          ): either a normal completion containing a Boolean or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It matches a range pattern by containment.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _subject_ is not a value of a type ordered with the element type of _r_, return *false*.
          1. Return ? the result of the containment test the range's `contains` performs on _subject_ (<emu-xref href="#sec-ranges"></emu-xref>), which is at most two comparisons.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-matchregexp" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          MatchRegExp (
            _subject_: an ECMAScript language value,
            _r_: an Object with a [[RegExpMatcher]] internal slot,
            _sub_: a Parse Node or ~none~,
            _cache_: a Match Cache Record,
            _env_: an Environment Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing a Boolean or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It matches a regular expression pattern against the entire subject, and matches the juxtaposed structural pattern _sub_, where present, against the match result.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _subject_ is not a String, return *false*.
          1. Let _result_ be ? RegExpExec(_r_, _subject_) restricted to a match of the entirety of _subject_: the match must begin at index 0 and end at the length of _subject_, as the whole-string discipline of a `pattern` constraint requires (<emu-xref href="#sec-typed-regular-expressions"></emu-xref>), so a search is spelled as one.
          1. If _result_ is *null*, return *false*.
          1. If _sub_ is ~none~, return *true*.
          1. Return ? PatternMatches(_sub_, _result_, _cache_, _env_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The match result's Static Type is the exact result type the regular expression's captures give it, so `groups: { let year }` binds the typed group and a misspelled group name is a type error against that type. The result is constructed once and its reads enter the cache as any subject's do.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-pattern-static-semantics">
      <h1>Static Semantics of Patterns</h1>
      <p>The <dfn>pattern type</dfn> of a pattern _P_, PatternType(_P_), is the Type Record a successful match establishes, defined by the form of _P_: a |Type|, the type it denotes; a name resolving to a type or evaluating to a Type Object of known type, that type; a literal, its ~literal~ type at the position type by <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref>; a name denoting a constant of literal or enumerator type, that type; a binding with an annotation, the annotated type, and without one, ~any~; a range whose endpoints are compile-time constants, the position type refined by the interval's metadata (<emu-xref href="#sec-ranges"></emu-xref>); a regular expression, `string`; an extractor whose head denotes a type, that type, which is the narrowing its matcher's author asserts and the checker takes, as it takes a declared narrowing predicate's, and ~any~ for a head that denotes no type; a juxtaposition, PatternType of its structural part narrowed to its head's type; an |ObjectMatchPattern|, the ~object~ Type Record whose properties are the named members, each required, at the PatternType of its sub-pattern; an |ArrayMatchPattern| without rest, the ~tuple~ of its element PatternTypes, and with rest, ~any~; an `and`, the NarrowTo of its operands' pattern types in order; an `or`, the union of its alternatives'; an `extends` pattern, the `type` type (<emu-xref href="#sec-the-type-type"></emu-xref>), since what a successful match establishes of the subject is that it is a Type Object; a wildcard, a `not`, or an interpolation of non-literal type, ~any~.</p>
      <p>Within a pattern, each sub-pattern's <dfn>position type</dfn> is the subject's Static Type at that position, computed left to right: the subject narrowed by the preceding operand of an `and`, a named member's type in the members of a union position the pattern has not eliminated, an element's type from a ~tuple~ position or the element type of an ~array~ one, an extracted position's type from the matcher's declared return. A binding's Static Type is NarrowTo of its position type and its PatternType. A pattern slot's Static Type is the type its |Type| denotes, which is the constraint the match checked, so `B: NumberBounds` binds a `NumberBounds` and `K: type` binds a `type`; a slot is a binding of the clause for every other purpose, immutable, in scope in the guard and the arm, and subject to the duplicate-name and `or`-consistency errors above. It is a type error if NarrowTo of a sub-pattern's position type and its PatternType is ~empty~, the impossible-test rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref>; and it is a type error if a numeric literal's position type is a union of two or more numeric types, since the literal has two types it could take, resolved by `and` with a type or by an interpolation.</p>
      <p>The rows this clause adds to <emu-xref href="#table-narrowing-forms"></emu-xref>: in the arm of a `when` _P_, NarrowTo(_s_, PatternType(_P_)); in the arms after it, NarrowFrom(_s_, PatternType(_P_)) where the clause is unguarded; and `v is` _P_ narrows as `when` _P_ does, in the positions the test governs. The Static Type of a `match` expression is the canonicalized union of its arm types, a `throw` arm and a diverging block contributing nothing (<emu-xref href="#sec-divergence"></emu-xref>) and a block arm contributing CompletionTypeOf of its |StatementList| (<emu-xref href="#sec-completiontypeof"></emu-xref>); a contextual type on the `match` is the contextual type of each arm body.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-match-exhaustiveness">
        <h1>Exhaustiveness</h1>
        <p>A `match` whose subject is a Type Object has no atoms, the types being an open universe, so <emu-xref href="#sec-match-exhaustiveness"></emu-xref> requires such a `match` to carry a catch-all. The <dfn>atoms</dfn> of a Type Record _t_ are: for an enum, its enumerators; for a sealed class, its direct subclasses and, where instantiable, itself; for `boolean`, *true* and *false*; for `null` and `undefined`, themselves; for a ~union~ of two or more members each of which is one of the preceding, an ~object~ type, a ~tuple~ type, or a composite type, the atoms of each member, an object, tuple, or composite member being its own atom; for a dependent record type whose predicate is a discriminating `where` chain, the atoms of the union that chain denotes (<emu-xref href="#sec-discriminated-where-chains"></emu-xref>); for an ~intersection~ one or more of whose members is a ~union~, the atoms of the union formed by distributing it, one member per choice of one arm from each such union intersected with the remaining members; and ~none~ otherwise. A union with a member of ~literal~ kind has atoms ~none~: a closed set of literals that wants the check is an enum over its base, the standing decision of <emu-xref href="#sec-enums"></emu-xref>, restated rather than reopened.</p>
        <p>An unguarded clause <dfn>covers</dfn> an atom _a_ when: its pattern's PatternType _pt_ satisfies IsSubtype(the type of _a_, _pt_, &laquo; &raquo;), reading an enumerator atom's type as its enumerator, covered by the constant pattern naming it or a type pattern naming its enum, and reading *true*, *false*, `null`, and `undefined` atoms as their literal patterns and types; an object, tuple, or composite atom is additionally covered by a structural pattern each of whose named members is declared required by the atom and whose sub-patterns each cover the member's declared type, the subsumption the impossible-test rule computes run in the other direction. A `default` covers every atom, and a guarded clause covers none.</p>
        <p>A pattern is <dfn>irrefutable</dfn> when it matches every value: the wildcard; a |MatchBindingPattern| with no |TypeAnnotation|, or one whose annotation denotes ~any~; a parenthesized irrefutable pattern; an `and` both of whose operands are irrefutable; and an `or` either of whose alternatives is. A clause is a <dfn>catch-all</dfn> when it is a `default`, or is unguarded with an irrefutable pattern.</p>
        <p>A `match` is <dfn>exhaustive</dfn> when it has a catch-all clause, or when the atoms of its subject's Static Type are not ~none~ and every atom is covered by some clause. It is a type error for a `match` not to be exhaustive. <strong>Neither this rule nor the one that follows applies to the `match all` form</strong> (<emu-xref href="#sec-match-expression"></emu-xref>): no clause of a `match all` need match, since its value is the List of those that did and an empty List is an answer rather than a missing case; and a later clause there is reached whether or not an earlier one matched, so it is narrowed by nothing the earlier clause failed to establish. It is a type error if a clause can match nothing the preceding unguarded clauses have left: where NarrowTo of the subject's remaining type, the Static Type folded by NarrowFrom over the preceding unguarded clauses' pattern types, and the clause's PatternType is ~empty~, or where every atom the clause covers is covered before it; a `default` whose preceding clauses cover every atom of a subject with atoms is that error's instance, so a covered `match` and a defaulted one cannot silently coexist.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The last two rows are derived forms, and they are derived for this check alone. A discriminating `where` chain denotes a union and an intersection over a union distributes into one, but neither changes what the type <em>is</em>: `Reflect.typeOf` reports the dependent record type and the intersection, `CanonicalizeType` flattens an intersection without distributing it (<emu-xref href="#sec-canonicalizetype"></emu-xref>) so identity and interning are untouched, and assignability compares against the type as written. What they change is only that the checker sees the cases the reader sees, which is what the check is for. Distribution is bounded by the type expression, since it multiplies the arm counts of the unions an intersection names, and it needs no new subtyping: IsSubtype already decides an ~intersection~ on the left by finding one member within the target, so an arm naming `A` covers an atom `A &amp; C`.</p>
          <p>Requiring exhaustiveness of every `match` is what makes the *TypeError* of <emu-xref href="#sec-match-evaluation"></emu-xref> unreachable from typed code: a `match` whose subject holds a value of its Static Type has a clause for it, so the throw is left for an untyped subject and for a value that has drifted out of its type, where the boundary check is the backstop as everywhere else (<emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref>). The rule pairs with unreachability into one statement a program can be told: exactly one of "this `match` needs a catch-all" and "this `match` must not have one" holds of it, since a closed subject covered atom by atom rejects the `default` it does not need and everything else requires one. Naming the atoms is also what lets an implementation report which pattern is missing rather than only that one is.</p>
        </emu-note>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Decoration cannot falsify a coverage claim, which is worth stating because a decorator may replace what it decorates and a reader has no reason to assume the interaction was considered. Three rules of the decorators extension carry it, one per way an atom set could move.</p>
          <p>An atom set is read from a declaration, and no context rewrites one. The enum, enumerator, tuple, record, block, `let`, and `const` contexts admit no return replacement at all, so the enumerators a `match` covers are the ones the declaration lists; a decorator that mutates an enum object at run time changes no declared type and so moves no atom.</p>
          <p>A class replacement is a subclass. The `Reflect.Class` context does admit a replacement, and the design's `singleton` returns `class extends T`, but the declared return type is _T_ and a class type is ~nominal~ (<emu-xref href="#table-type-record-kinds"></emu-xref>), identified by the declaration that introduced it, so a structurally identical class of another declaration is not a _T_ and only _T_'s own declaration or a subclass satisfies the return. Coverage is IsSubtype over the atom's type, which is closed downward, so an arm naming a class matches an instance of whatever the decorator returned. Where the class decorated is a `sealed` base rather than one of its cases, a decorator in another module cannot return a subclass at all, since extending it outside its module is what `sealed` forbids (<emu-xref href="#sec-sealed-classes"></emu-xref>), and one in the same module declares a case the checker reads like any other, so the check is stricter rather than wrong.</p>
          <p>A field replacement is a value, not a type. `Reflect.ClassField` replaces the field's initial value at type _T_, and the accessor contexts replace a getter at `() =&gt;` _T_ and a setter at _T_, so a discriminant declared with a ~literal~ type still holds a value of it and a member a pattern reads still has the type it was declared with.</p>
          <p>What is outside the claim is what is outside every claim here: a decorator running arbitrary code may write a value that has drifted out of its type, and the backstop is the one <emu-xref href="#table-check-sites"></emu-xref> already provides, not a property of `match`.</p>
        </emu-note>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The `switch` exhaustiveness of <emu-xref href="#sec-enums"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-sealed-classes"></emu-xref> is the two-atom-kind instance of the coverage half of this rule, and nothing about `switch` changes; a `switch` remains able to fall through to no case, which is the difference between a statement that may do nothing and an expression that must produce a value. What patterns add is the members a pattern can provably blanket, which is what makes a discriminated union of object types and the `T | null` of every `tryParse` checkable with no `default`, and what a lone object or tuple type deliberately does not get, its atoms being ~none~ absent a qualifying `where` chain, because a subset pattern is the point of matching on one. Coverage is syntactic over declared types; the checker evaluates no guard and no predicate.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-is-pattern">
      <h1>The is Operator Takes a Pattern</h1>
      <p>The right operand of `is` (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-in-expression-position"></emu-xref>) is widened from |Type| to |MatchPattern|, of which a |Type| is one form, so every existing `is` keeps its parse and its meaning. `v is` _P_ evaluates PatternMatches(_P_, the value of `v`, a fresh Match Cache Record, _env_) and returns the Boolean, where _env_ is a new declarative Environment Record in which _P_'s BoundNames are created as immutable bindings; the bindings are in scope in exactly the positions the truth of the test governs, the positions <emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref> enumerates, each such position evaluating in _env_. It is a type error to reference one of those bindings anywhere else, and it is a type error for a binding-carrying `is` to occur where no position is governed by its truth, since the bindings would then exist nowhere.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>`subject is P` is the one-arm `match`, exactly: it matches, binds, and narrows as `when P` would, which is what makes `while (read() is Ok(let chunk))` a loop whose body sees `chunk` and whose exit is the miss. The dependent-record `is` of <emu-xref href="#sec-is-operator"></emu-xref> is the |Type| case of this operator, unchanged.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-custommatcher-symbol">
      <h1>%Symbol.customMatcher%</h1>
      <p>This extension adds the well-known symbol <dfn>%Symbol.customMatcher%</dfn> (`Symbol.customMatcher`), the method a name and an extractor pattern consult. The typed protocol is a method from a subject to a tuple or `null`, or to a Boolean for the parenthesis-less membership form; its declared signature is the whole contract, overloads select on the subject's Static Type, and a generic matcher infers its parameters from the subject as any generic call does, which is how an extractor's bindings are typed with nothing annotated (design, patternmatching.md).</p>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-divergence">
    <h1>Divergence</h1>
    <p>This clause, like <emu-xref href="#sec-generator-types"></emu-xref>, is owed elsewhere and stated here because this is the first clause that computes with it. The design defines divergence in its `switch` chapter, and <emu-xref href="#sec-pattern-static-semantics"></emu-xref> already reads a `match` arm's type by it - "a `throw` arm and a diverging block contributing nothing" - without the notion having been defined.</p>

    <p>A statement <dfn>diverges</dfn> when no path of control through it completes normally. The analysis is syntactic and reasons about no values:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A `return`, a `throw`, and a `break` or `continue` targeting an enclosing statement diverge.</li>
      <li>A |Block| diverges when any statement in it diverges.</li>
      <li>An `if` statement diverges when it has an `else` and both branches diverge.</li>
      <li>A `switch` statement diverges when it is exhaustive - every enumerator, every direct subclass, or a `default` - and every clause diverges.</li>
      <li>`while (true)` and `for (;;)` diverge when no `break` targets them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Two consequences follow, both of which the design states. A `switch` clause whose body diverges needs no `break` and is not a fallthrough; and a function whose body diverges satisfies a non-~void~ return type with no trailing `return`.</p>
    <emu-note>
      <p>Syntactic is the whole of it: `while (cond)` does not diverge however plainly `cond` is *true* to a reader, because a rule that reasoned about the condition would have to reason about values, and the design's analyses do not. That is why the list names `while (true)` and `for (;;)` as forms rather than describing a condition.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-iteration-types">
    <h1>The Iteration Types</h1>
    <p>This clause is owed to the iteration protocols the core already defines, not to any extension. `for`-`of` asks whether `[Symbol.iterator]` is callable and never asks what a value declared; the design writes `Iterator.&lt;T&gt;` throughout and this specification has named no such type, so what a helper returns and what a hand-written iterator satisfies have both been undescribable. It precedes <emu-xref href="#sec-generator-types"></emu-xref> because the generator types are stated in terms of it.</p>

    <p>The <dfn>iteration result type</dfn> `IteratorResult.&lt;`_T_`, `_R_`&gt;` is the ~union~ of two ~object~ Type Records, one with members `value` of _T_ and `done` of the literal type *false*, the other with `value` of _R_ and `done` of the literal type *true*. It is discriminated by `done`, so a `match` over an iteration result binds `value` at _T_ in one arm and at _R_ in the other (<emu-xref href="#sec-match-exhaustiveness"></emu-xref>), and reading one requires no narrowing rule of its own.</p>

    <p>The protocols are interface types, satisfied structurally through their structural form (<emu-xref href="#sec-object-types"></emu-xref>):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>`Iterator.&lt;`_T_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;` declares `next`, taking an optional argument of _N_ and returning `IteratorResult.&lt;`_T_`, `_R_`&gt;`, and optionally `return` and `throw`.</li>
      <li>`Iterable.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` declares `[Symbol.iterator]`, returning `Iterator.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`.</li>
      <li>`IterableIterator.&lt;`_T_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;` inherits both.</li>
      <li>`AsyncIterator.&lt;`_T_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;`, `AsyncIterable.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`, and `AsyncIterableIterator.&lt;`_T_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;` are the same three with `[Symbol.asyncIterator]` in place of `[Symbol.iterator]` and `next` returning a promise of the iteration result.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>`Iterator` is also a built-in class, the one on which the iterator helper methods are defined, and it declares that it implements `IterableIterator`. `AsyncIterator` is the same for the asynchronous protocol. Both join the built-in type names of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-names"></emu-xref>. A value of the class satisfies the interface by refinement rather than by inspection (<emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>), so it is decided by the declaration; a value that merely has the members satisfies it through the structural form.</p>

    <p>A bare argument is the first: `Iterator.&lt;uint8&gt;` is `Iterator.&lt;uint8, void, void&gt;`, and likewise for the other five. This is the shorthand <emu-xref href="#sec-generator-types"></emu-xref> states for the generator families, and it is one rule rather than two.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>_R_ and _N_ default to ~void~ because the generator families default them to ~void~, and that agreement is what makes a `Generator.&lt;`_Y_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;` satisfy `IterableIterator.&lt;`_Y_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;`. Had the two been chosen separately a generator would have been *nearly* an iterator, which is a failure mode with a record: TypeScript's builtin iterator type and its generators disagreed on this parameter, and the type could not be given a usable default as a result.</p>
      <p>There are no per-collection iteration types. TypeScript names `ArrayIterator`, `MapIterator`, and `SetIterator` because it needs somewhere to carry a narrower return type; the shorthand carries it here, so the family stays at six interfaces and two classes. The absence is a decision rather than an omission.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The element parameter is invariant, as every generic in this specification is (<emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>), so an `Iterable.&lt;`_Circle_`&gt;` is not an `Iterable.&lt;`_Shape_`&gt;` for a _Circle_ that extends _Shape_. Covariance would be sound for these types and for no container: an iterator produces its element and never accepts one, which is the condition the invariance rule states for a mutable container and which these types do not meet. Admitting a declared variance is the generics extension's to make, and the invariance rule already names C# and Kotlin as the precedent; both spend that feature on exactly these types. It can be admitted later without invalidating a program, since a variance annotation only adds legal conversions, which is how .NET added covariance to its enumerable interfaces after they had shipped invariant.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-generator-types">
    <h1>The Generator Types</h1>
    <p>This clause is owed to the generator forms the core already parses, not to the extension that follows it. <emu-xref href="#sec-annotations-on-the-remaining-function-forms"></emu-xref> admits a return annotation on a generator and says what it means in prose, that "a generator's annotation types the values the iterator yields", and the design writes `Generator.&lt;Y, R, N&gt;` throughout; the type itself has not been stated. It is stated here because <emu-xref href="#sec-do-expressions"></emu-xref> is the first clause that computes one.</p>

    <p>`Generator` names the built-in generic whose instances are the objects a generator function returns, and `AsyncGenerator` the same for an async generator. `Generator.&lt;`_Y_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;` is the ~nominal~ Type Record for the first with those arguments, and `AsyncGenerator.&lt;`_Y_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;` for the second. _Y_ is the type of the values the generator yields, _R_ the type of the value it returns, and _N_ the type of the value a caller sends to `next`. Both are generic classes, so each is invariant in its arguments by the rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>: a `Generator.&lt;uint8, void, void&gt;` is a subtype of no other instantiation.</p>

    <p>A generator's return annotation is read as follows, which is the design's shorthand made precise. Where the annotation denotes a `Generator` instantiation, and an `AsyncGenerator` instantiation for an async generator, it is the declared type as written. Where it denotes any other type _T_, the declared type is `Generator.&lt;`_T_`, void, void&gt;`, or `AsyncGenerator.&lt;`_T_`, void, void&gt;`, so that `function* f(): int32` declares a generator of `int32`. It is a type error for the annotation of an async generator to denote a `Generator` instantiation, or for a synchronous generator's to denote an `AsyncGenerator` instantiation.</p>

    <p>The Static Type of a call of a generator function is its declared type. Within the body, the Static Type of a |YieldExpression| without `*` is _N_, and it is a type error if the operand's type is not assignable to _Y_. The Static Type of a `yield*` is the _R_ of its operand's generator type, since delegation evaluates to what the delegated generator returned, and the operand's _Y_ must be assignable to the enclosing _Y_.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>A `Generator.&lt;`_Y_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;` satisfies `IterableIterator.&lt;`_Y_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;` and `Iterable.&lt;`_Y_`&gt;` (<emu-xref href="#sec-iteration-types"></emu-xref>), and an `AsyncGenerator` the asynchronous three, which is why a generator may be spread, consumed by `for`-`of`, or passed where an iterator is expected. That holds because the two families default _R_ and _N_ the same way; the iteration types were written to match these rather than the reverse. The shorthand below is the one those types state, applied here.</p>
      <p>Reading a bare annotation as the yield type rather than as the whole generator type is the design's choice and is the useful one: a generator's return and next types are `void` in almost every generator anyone writes, and requiring `Generator.&lt;int32, void, void&gt;` at each of them would make the common case the verbose one. The full form remains available and is what a generator with a meaningful `return` or a two-way protocol writes.</p>
      <p>That `yield*` has the delegated generator's _R_ as its type, while contributing its _Y_ to the enclosing generator's, is the one part of the protocol a reader is likely to get backwards. It follows from the run time: `yield*` drives the operand to completion and evaluates to its return value.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-do-expressions">
    <h1>do Expressions</h1>
    <p>A `do` expression is a block that produces a value: its statements are evaluated and its value is the completion value, the value of the last statement that completed normally. This clause specifies the design's do expressions extension, which integrates the <a href="https://github.com/tc39/proposal-do-expressions">do expressions proposal</a> under this type system and adds the generator forms of that proposal's issue 76.</p>

    <emu-grammar type="definition">
      <ins>PrimaryExpression[Yield, Await] :
        DoExpression[?Yield, ?Await]

      DoExpression[Yield, Await] :
        `do` Block[?Yield, ?Await, ~Return]
        `do` `*` `{` GeneratorBody `}`
        `async` [no LineTerminator here] `do` `*` `{` AsyncGeneratorBody `}`</ins>
    </emu-grammar>

    <p>The |Block| of the first form carries the [Return] permission of the function enclosing the `do`, as a |MatchArmBody|'s does (<emu-xref href="#sec-match-expression"></emu-xref>), so `return`, `break`, and `continue` inside it mean what they mean in the surrounding code; the ~Return~ above is notational and the permission is threaded by the enclosing contexts. The `[no LineTerminator here]` is what keeps `async` a contextual keyword: `async` on one line and `do *` on the next must remain an identifier reference followed by something else, which is the restriction the productions above already carry before `function`.</p>

    <p>It is a Syntax Error for a |DoExpression| to appear where a |Statement| is admitted, since `do` there begins a `do`-`while`. In statement position a program writes a |Block|, or parenthesizes.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>`do` is a reserved word, so this form costs the grammar less than `match` did (<emu-xref href="#sec-match-expression"></emu-xref>): there is no cover grammar and no program whose meaning changes, only a position where the word was previously a Syntax Error. The `do *` and `async do *` forms need no statement-position rule of their own, since `*` cannot begin a |Statement| and so cannot be a `do`-`while` body; they are refused there with the first form anyway, because a `do *` evaluated as a statement discards the generator it built, and a reader should not have to know which form the collision applies to.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <emu-clause id="sec-do-expression-early-errors">
      <h1>Early Errors</h1>
      <p>The following apply to the first form only. A `do *` has no completion value, its body being a generator body whose completion is discarded, so nothing here restricts it: a `do *` may end in a loop or a declaration, and the design's own motivating example does end in a loop.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if EndsInIterationOrDeclaration of the |Block|'s |StatementList| is *true*. That operation is the upstream proposal's and is adopted unchanged: it holds when the completion value of the list can be the completion value of an iteration statement or of a declaration, which reaches through nesting, so a |Block| whose last statement is an `if` with an `else` one of whose branches ends in a loop is a Syntax Error too.</li>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if the |Block|'s |StatementList| ends in an `if` statement with no `else`.</li>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error for a |VariableStatement| to appear within a |DoExpression| that occurs in a parameter expression, since the function body its binding would hoist into does not yet exist.</li>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error for an unlabelled `break` or `continue` to appear within a |DoExpression| in the head of an iteration statement.</li>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error for a `return` to appear within a |DoExpression| in a computed property name in a class body, which is inside no function.</li>
      </ul>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Each of the first two is about a completion value a reader would not predict rather than about a shape being hard to evaluate. A declaration's completion is ~empty~, so `do { 'before'; let x = 'after'; }` would be *"before"*; an `if` with no `else` is its consequent's value or *undefined* by a condition the reader has to trace; and a loop's is the last iteration's value, or *undefined* for none. The upstream proposal records that it intends to pursue changing the completion value of declarations in the base language, and this specification's rule follows whatever that settles: the restriction is stated in terms of EndsInIterationOrDeclaration rather than restated, so it moves with it.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-completiontypeof" type="abstract operation">
      <h1>
        CompletionTypeOf (
          _list_: a |StatementList| Parse Node,
        ): a Type Record
      </h1>
      <dl class="header">
        <dt>description</dt>
        <dd>It returns the type of the completion value of _list_: the union of the types of the statements that can be the last one to complete normally.</dd>
      </dl>
      <emu-alg>
        1. If _list_ is empty, return the Type Record for `undefined`.
        1. Let _last_ be the last |StatementListItem| of _list_.
        1. If _last_ diverges (<emu-xref href="#sec-divergence"></emu-xref>), return the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~union~, [[Members]]: &laquo; &raquo; }.
        1. If _last_ is an |ExpressionStatement|, return the Static Type of its |Expression|.
        1. If _last_ is a |Block| or a |LabelledStatement|, return CompletionTypeOf(its own |StatementList|).
        1. If _last_ is an `if` statement with an `else`, return CanonicalizeType(the union of CompletionTypeOf(each branch's statement read as a one-item |StatementList|)).
        1. If _last_ is a `try` statement, then
          1. Let _members_ be a List whose elements are CompletionTypeOf(the |Block|'s |StatementList|) and, where a |Catch| is present, CompletionTypeOf(each |Catch|'s |Block|'s |StatementList|).
          1. Return CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~union~, [[Members]]: _members_ }).
        1. If _last_ is a `switch` statement, then
          1. Let _members_ be a List whose elements are CompletionTypeOf(each |CaseClause| and |DefaultClause|'s |StatementList|).
          1. If the `switch` is not exhaustive (<emu-xref href="#sec-sealed-classes"></emu-xref>), append the Type Record for `undefined` to _members_.
          1. Return CanonicalizeType(the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~union~, [[Members]]: _members_ }).
        1. Return the Type Record for `undefined`.
      </emu-alg>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The Static Type of a |DoExpression| of the first form is CompletionTypeOf(its |Block|'s |StatementList|). Nothing in the operation is new: divergence is the analysis of <emu-xref href="#sec-divergence"></emu-xref>, which is syntactic and reasons about no values, and the Early Errors above have already removed the forms whose completion type would have been hard to state. What is left is a union over the tails.</p>
        <p>A `do` all of whose paths diverge has the empty union, which is `never` (<emu-xref href="#sec-never-type"></emu-xref>) and is a subtype of every type, so `const port: uint16 = do { throw new ConfigError('no port'); };` is accepted. This is the first construct in this specification where ordinary code produces `never` rather than a type computation producing it, and it is the answer a program wants: the binding is unreachable, so its declared type constrains nothing.</p>
        <p>The `switch` step is the one place this operation is not the obvious rule, and it is the design's own. An exhaustive `switch` takes no path where no clause ran, so it contributes no *undefined*, which is the conclusion <emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref> already draws for a `switch` that maps a value to a type: "because enum and sealed-class exhaustiveness is checked, a `switch` used to map a value to a type covers every case its argument can take and therefore yields a Type Object rather than *undefined*". The same expression must not type one way inside a compile-time builder and another inside a `do`. A `switch` that is not exhaustive does contribute *undefined*, which is what makes `const u: uint8 = do { switch (s) { case 'a': 1; } };` a type error.</p>
        <p>Exhaustiveness here is the `switch`'s, which this specification reserves to enums and sealed hierarchies (<emu-xref href="#sec-never-type"></emu-xref> restates the reservation), and it is deliberately narrower than the atoms a `match` reads (<emu-xref href="#sec-match-exhaustiveness"></emu-xref>). A `switch` over a `boolean` covering `true` and `false` is therefore not exhaustive for this operation, and a `do` ending in one has *undefined* in its type, while the corresponding `match` is checked complete. The asymmetry is between the two constructs rather than introduced here: `match` was given a wider analysis when it was added, and widening `switch` to meet it is that clause's decision to make rather than this one's. A `do` that wants the narrower answer writes the `match`.</p>
        <p>An empty `do {}` is *undefined*, the value `void 0`, and not ~void~: ~void~ is the absence of a value and is a return type, while `do {}` produces one.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-do-expression-contextual-type">
      <h1>Contextual Typing</h1>
      <p>A contextual type on a |DoExpression| of the first form (<emu-xref href="#sec-contextual-types"></emu-xref>) is the contextual type of every position CompletionTypeOf reads: the final |ExpressionStatement|'s |Expression|, each branch of a final `if`, each clause of a final `switch`, and the |Block| and each |Catch| |Block| of a final `try`, recursively. A numeric literal in one of those positions therefore takes the expected type by <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref>.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>Without this the form would be typed and not usable: every literal inside a `do` would need the annotation the surrounding declaration already carries, so `const level: uint8 = do { if (verbose) 3; else 0; };` would hold Numbers. It is the same propagation a `match` gives its arms, reaching the same kind of position, the one that carries the value out.</p>
        <p>Narrowing needs no rule of its own. A test inside a `do` narrows inside it by <emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref>, and no fact survives the expression, since which path ran is not visible outside it; what leaves is the type, and the union over the tails is what carries the narrowing outward.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-do-generator-expressions">
      <h1>do * Generators</h1>
      <p>A `do *` evaluates to a generator object, as a call of a generator function does, and its body is a generator body. The Static Type of `do *` `{` |GeneratorBody| `}` is `Generator.&lt;`_Y_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;` (<emu-xref href="#sec-generator-types"></emu-xref>), and of the `async` form `AsyncGenerator.&lt;`_Y_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;`, where the three are found rather than declared, there being no annotation site:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>_Y_ is CanonicalizeType of the union of the type of each |YieldExpression| operand in the body that is not a `yield*`, and of the _Y_ of each `yield*` operand's generator type.</li>
        <li>_R_ is CanonicalizeType of the union of the types of the body's `return` operands, or ~void~ where it has none.</li>
        <li>_N_ is ~void~ unless a contextual type supplies it, in which case it is that type's _N_. A contextual type also supplies _Y_ and _R_, and it is a type error if what the body yields or returns is not assignable to them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A contextual type on a `do *` is the contextual type of each |YieldExpression| operand, so a literal yielded takes the expected type as one in a plain `do`'s tail does.</p>
      <p>A `do *` is a function boundary and a `do` is not, which is where the two forms part company. `this`, `arguments`, and `new.target` inside a `do *` are the enclosing function's, as they are in an arrow: the design's motivating example exists to delete a `function* () { … }.call(this)`, and binding `this` any other way would reintroduce it. But the body is a generator body, so `yield` belongs to the `do *` rather than to the enclosing function, `return` sets the generator's return value rather than returning from the enclosing function, and an unlabelled `break` or `continue` cannot leave the body.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>That `return` means one thing in `do` and another in `do *` is the sharpest edge in this clause and is worth stating rather than deriving. In a `do` it returns from the enclosing function, which is what the upstream proposal's own `getUserId` depends on; in a `do *` it completes the generator. Nothing else in the language turns on a single token that way, and a reader who has just read the first form will assume the second.</p>
        <p>The typed form is what the IIFE it replaces cannot easily give. `Promise.all` takes an iterable of promises, so _Y_ is checked against what it awaits and a `do *` yielding the wrong thing is refused at the `yield`; through `function* () { … }.call(this)` the same relation holds but is read through a call whose `this` is supplied separately.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-do-expression-modifications">
      <h1>Modifications to Existing Clauses</h1>
      <p>A `match` arm's |Block| (<emu-xref href="#sec-match-expression"></emu-xref>) is a |DoExpression|'s |Block| of the first form: its value is CompletionTypeOf(its |StatementList|) and it carries the Early Errors of <emu-xref href="#sec-do-expression-early-errors"></emu-xref>. This replaces the rule that clause stated, under which an arm's value was its final statement's only where that statement was an |ExpressionStatement| and the arm was ~void~ otherwise, and it correspondingly replaces the sentence in <emu-xref href="#sec-pattern-static-semantics"></emu-xref> that gave such an arm ~void~.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>No program changes meaning. The arm rule accepted a strict subset of this one - a block ending in an |ExpressionStatement| has the same value under both - and its own note reserved the difference, recording that it "errors exactly where full completion-value semantics would permit" so that a later general form could only make errors legal. Two things improve. An arm may now end in an `if` with an `else`, a `try`, or a `switch`, which the arm rule made ~void~ and therefore unreadable at the use site. And an arm ending in a declaration or a loop is now a Syntax Error naming what it ended with, where it was a silent ~void~ and a complaint at whatever read the `match`'s value.</p>
        <p>An arm's block is the first form and never `do *`: an arm produces the arm's value, and a `do *` there would produce a generator.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <p>The Block family of <emu-xref href="#table-reflection-contexts"></emu-xref> gains `DoBlock` and `DoGeneratorBlock`, which are two contexts rather than one because what the block is differs, a block in one case and a generator body in the other, as `ForBlock` and `ForOfBlock` are two. They are the first block contexts to admit a return replacement: a `DoBlock` decorator may return a value of the do expression's type, and a `DoGeneratorBlock` decorator a `Generator.&lt;`_Y_`, `_R_`, `_N_`&gt;`, replacing what the expression produced. The exclusion of blocks from replacement was never about their being structural positions but about their having no value to replace, and these two have one.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>With the per-entry rule that family already carries - a block decorator runs on every entry rather than once where the declaration is evaluated - a replacement makes memoization, caching, tracing that substitutes, and stubbing expressible at an expression as small as one wants. The generator context is the more useful of the two, since what a `do *` decorator receives is an iterator and wrapping one is what a decorator over a sequence is for.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-constant-expressions">
    <h1>constant Expressions</h1>
    <p>A `constant` expression is a block whose value belongs to its <dfn>site</dfn>: it is evaluated at most once for a given ConstantExpression in a given realm, and every later evaluation reaching it answers with the value already produced.</p>

    <emu-grammar type="definition">
      <ins>PrimaryExpression[Yield, Await] :
        ConstantExpression[?Yield, ?Await]

      ConstantExpression[Yield, Await] :
        `constant` [no LineTerminator here] Block[?Yield, ?Await, ~Return]</ins>
    </emu-grammar>

    <p>`constant` is a contextual keyword rather than a reserved word, so an existing program that uses it as an identifier keeps its meaning. The [no LineTerminator here] restriction is what keeps the two apart: without it, `constant` alone on a line followed by a block would be an ExpressionStatement and a Block under automatic semicolon insertion.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>This is the rule ECMA-262's GetTemplateObject applies to a tagged template's strings array - it looks the site up in the realm's [[TemplateMap]] and returns the array already built for it - generalized from a frozen List of Strings to the value of a Block. Both answer the same question - what belongs to a position in the source rather than to an evaluation of it - and both answer it by keying on the Parse Node.</p>
      <p>The guarantee is IDENTITY, not speed. Two closures produced by two calls to one factory share the value, and a site inside a loop yields the same value on every iteration, because the key is the site and not any evaluation reaching it. A binding hoisted to enclosing scope cannot do that: it is re-created whenever the declaration containing it is evaluated again.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <p>A Realm Record has an additional field for this purpose.</p>

    <emu-table id="table-realm-constantmap" caption="Additional Field of Realm Records">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Field Name</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>[[ConstantMap]]</td>
            <td>a List of Records with fields [[Site]] (a Parse Node) and [[Value]] (an ECMAScript language value)</td>
            <td>The value each ConstantExpression evaluated in this realm produced. It is the counterpart of [[TemplateMap]], which records a tagged template's strings array against its site.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </emu-table>

    <emu-clause id="sec-constant-expression-early-errors">
      <h1>Static Semantics: Early Errors</h1>
      <emu-grammar>ConstantExpression : `constant` Block</emu-grammar>
      <ul>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if the Block contains an IdentifierReference that does not resolve to a binding declared within the Block.</li>
      </ul>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The Block must be CLOSED: it reads nothing from outside itself. Its value is then a property of the site and of nothing else, which is what makes "evaluated once" unobservable except through identity.</p>
        <p>Without this rule, <emu-eqn>function f(x) { return constant { x + 1 }; }</emu-eqn> would answer every call with the first call's argument, and nothing would signal it. The rule is stated over free references rather than over non-determinism because the two differ in both directions: a parameter is not ambient state, and reading `Math.max` is ambient but cannot observe how many times it ran. Being closed subsumes the non-determinism a replacement decorator is held to (<emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>), since an ambient name is a free reference too.</p>
        <p>It refuses a Block that calls a helper declared outside it. Admitting a reference to an immutable outer binding would relax the rule soundly and is deliberately not specified here.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-runtime-semantics-constant-expression-evaluation" type="sdo">
      <h1>Runtime Semantics: Evaluation</h1>
      <emu-grammar>ConstantExpression : `constant` Block</emu-grammar>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _realm_ be the current Realm Record.
        1. For each Record { [[Site]], [[Value]] } _entry_ of _realm_.[[ConstantMap]], do
          1. If _entry_.[[Site]] is the same Parse Node as this ConstantExpression, return _entry_.[[Value]].
        1. Let _completion_ be Completion(Evaluation of |Block|).
        1. If _completion_ is an abrupt completion, return ? _completion_.
        1. If _completion_.[[Value]] is ~empty~, let _value_ be *undefined*; otherwise let _value_ be _completion_.[[Value]].
        1. Append the Record { [[Site]]: this ConstantExpression, [[Value]]: _value_ } to _realm_.[[ConstantMap]].
        1. Return _value_.
      </emu-alg>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Evaluation is LAZY: the Block runs when an evaluation first reaches it, not when the module containing it is evaluated. A branch never taken therefore costs nothing. Because the Block is closed, the choice between lazy and eager is unobservable except in whether it runs at all.</p>
        <p>An abrupt completion is NOT recorded. Nothing was produced, and a stored failure could not be told from a stored value, so a later evaluation reaching the same site evaluates the Block again.</p>
        <p>The value belongs to a realm as well as to a site, so a module evaluated in two realms builds two, and neither is reachable from the other.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-pipeline-operator">
    <h1>Pipeline Operator</h1>
    <p>`x |> f(%)` evaluates its left operand, binds it to the <dfn>topic</dfn>, and evaluates its right operand with that binding in scope. This clause specifies the design's pipeline operator extension, which integrates the <a href="https://github.com/tc39/proposal-pipeline-operator">pipeline operator proposal</a> under this type system.</p>

    <emu-grammar type="definition">
      <ins>PipelineExpression[In, Yield, Await] :
        ShortCircuitExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]
        PipelineExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await] `|&gt;` ShortCircuitExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]</ins>

      ConditionalExpression[In, Yield, Await] :
        <del>ShortCircuitExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]</del>
        <del>ShortCircuitExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await] `?` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `:` AssignmentExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]</del>
        <ins>PipelineExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]</ins>
        <ins>PipelineExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await] `?` AssignmentExpression[+In, ?Yield, ?Await] `:` AssignmentExpression[?In, ?Yield, ?Await]</ins>

      PrimaryExpression[Yield, Await] :
        IdentifierReference[?Yield, ?Await]
        <ins>TopicReference</ins>

      <ins>TopicReference :
        `%`</ins>
    </emu-grammar>

    <p>`|&gt;` is left-associative, binds looser than `??` and tighter than the conditional operator. The upstream proposal names |ShortCircuitExpression| as its operand, which assumes the base language's precedence table; this design has placed ranges at that level (<emu-xref href="#sec-ranges"></emu-xref>), so a pipeline's operands are ranges and `|&gt;` binds looser than `..`. `0..&lt;10 |&gt; sum(%)` pipes the range, which is what it reads as; the other order would make it `0..&lt;(10 |&gt; sum(%))`, which nothing writing it means. Otherwise the placement is the proposal's: `a |&gt; f(%) |&gt; g(%)` is `(a |&gt; f(%)) |&gt; g(%)`, and `c ? a |&gt; f(%) : b` needs no parentheses. The token `%` is the remainder punctuator, and the two are told apart by position — a |TopicReference| stands where an operand is expected and the remainder operator between two — which no operator declaration can change, since <emu-xref href="#sec-operators"></emu-xref> lets a type supply a *meaning* for the binary form and never a new arity.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The upstream proposal records its grammar as incomplete (<a href="https://github.com/tc39/proposal-pipeline-operator/issues/290">issue 290</a>) and its topic token as unsettled after the longest bikeshed the committee has run (<a href="https://github.com/tc39/proposal-pipeline-operator/issues/91">issue 91</a>). This clause takes `%`, which is the proposal's current spelling; every rule below is stated in terms of the topic rather than the token, so a different token is an edit to the grammar and to nothing else.</p>
      <p>An expression language with operator overloading has fewer free tokens than one without, which is a fact about this design that the committee's discussion has not had before it. `#` is a private name, `?` is optional chaining and the optional marker of <emu-xref href="#sec-type-annotations"></emu-xref>, `@` is a decorator in about twenty positions, `_` is the pattern wildcard (<emu-xref href="#sec-match-patterns"></emu-xref>), and `%` and `^` are operators a class or a `primitive` block may declare. The design's own token would be `_` and it cannot be: one glyph meaning "anything at all" in a pattern and "this exact value" in a pipeline is a worse collision than the remainder one.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <emu-clause id="sec-pipeline-early-errors">
      <h1>Early Errors</h1>
      <ul>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if a |TopicReference| is not contained within the right operand of a |PipelineExpression|. Outside one, `%` is the remainder punctuator and a `%` where an operand is expected is the ordinary Syntax Error it is today.</li>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if the right operand of a |PipelineExpression| contains no |TopicReference| that the operand binds. A step that does not mention the topic discards the value piped into it, which is not a pipeline and is more likely a mistaken tacit call than an intention.</li>
        <li>It is a Syntax Error if a |TopicReference| is the target of an assignment, an update expression, or a destructuring pattern. The topic is an immutable binding: a step that reassigns what is being piped leaves the next step an input it cannot predict.</li>
      </ul>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The second error is what keeps the tacit form from arriving by accident. `x |&gt; f()` is refused rather than read as `f(x)`, so a program that means the call writes `x |&gt; f(%)` and a program that meant something else is told. <emu-xref href="#sec-pipeline-static-semantics"></emu-xref> records what a typed language could do about a tacit form and why this specification does not.</p>
        <p>"That the operand binds" excludes a topic belonging to a *nested* pipeline: in `a |&gt; f(b |&gt; g(%))` the only |TopicReference| is the inner pipeline's, so the outer operand contains none of its own and is a Syntax Error. It is written `a |&gt; f(%, b |&gt; g(%))` or not at all.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-pipeline-runtime-semantics">
      <h1>Runtime Semantics</h1>
      <p>Evaluation of `PipelineExpression : PipelineExpression |&gt; ShortCircuitExpression`:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _ref_ be ? Evaluation of |PipelineExpression|.
        1. Let _value_ be ? GetValue(_ref_).
        1. Let _oldEnv_ be the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment.
        1. Let _topicEnv_ be NewDeclarativeEnvironment(_oldEnv_).
        1. Perform ! _topicEnv_.CreateImmutableBinding(the topic, *true*).
        1. Perform ! _topicEnv_.InitializeBinding(the topic, _value_).
        1. Set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _topicEnv_.
        1. Let _result_ be Completion(Evaluation of |ShortCircuitExpression|).
        1. Set the running execution context's LexicalEnvironment to _oldEnv_.
        1. Return ? _result_.
      </emu-alg>
      <p>Evaluation of `TopicReference : %` returns the value bound in the nearest enclosing topic environment.</p>
      <emu-note>
        <p>The left operand is evaluated once however many times the topic appears, which is the difference between a binding and a substitution and is what `expensive() |&gt; [%, %]` rests on. An immutable binding in its own environment also gives the shadowing rule for free: a nested pipeline creates a second topic environment, so its steps see its own value and the outer one is unreachable from within it.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-pipeline-static-semantics">
      <h1>Static Semantics</h1>
      <p>The Static Type of the topic is the Static Type of the |PipelineExpression| that binds it, and the Static Type of a |PipelineExpression| is the Static Type of its right operand evaluated with that binding. The topic is a binding for every purpose of this specification: it is narrowed by the tests that govern it, it is shadowed by an inner pipeline's, and it is immutable, which the early errors above enforce syntactically rather than through the assignability rules.</p>

      <p>A contextual type (<emu-xref href="#sec-contextual-types"></emu-xref>) on a |PipelineExpression| is the contextual type of its right operand, so a numeric literal in a step takes the expected type by <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-propagation"></emu-xref> rather than defaulting. Without this the form would be typed and unusable, which is the failure <emu-xref href="#sec-do-expressions"></emu-xref> had to avoid in the same way.</p>

      <p>It is a type error for a |TopicReference| to be the operand of `ref` (<emu-xref href="#sec-references-and-borrowing"></emu-xref>). A reference parameter binds the caller's location; the topic holds the value the left operand produced, so `o.a |&gt; f(ref %)` would bind a reference to a temporary and a write through it would reach nothing the caller can observe. It is refused rather than admitted and useless, because the expression reads as though the pipe were transparent to the place it came from.</p>

      <p>Three consequences follow from the topic being an ordinary binding and are stated because a pipeline reads like a substitution. Overload resolution (<emu-xref href="#sec-resolveoverload"></emu-xref>) selects on the topic's type as on any argument. Generic parameters are inferred through it, and may be supplied explicitly as `x |&gt; f.&lt;uint8&gt;(%)`. And the topic is admitted wherever an expression is, including a spread and the argument positions a rest parameter distributes (<emu-xref href="#sec-bindarguments"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <p>A |PipelineExpression| whose right operand diverges has the type of the empty union (<emu-xref href="#sec-divergence"></emu-xref>, <emu-xref href="#sec-never-type"></emu-xref>). A |PipelineExpression| is compile-time evaluable exactly when both operands are: it introduces a binding and substitutes, reading nothing the surrounding code could not, so it is admitted in a type builder on the same terms as any other expression.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The tacit form the proposal calls F# style — `x |&gt; f`, calling `f` with the value and no topic — is refused by the second early error, and the reason is worth recording precisely, because it is not the reason usually given. That reason is that a language cannot see what a function expects, so a two-parameter function piped one argument fails somewhere else. It does not hold here: a function has a Signature Record with Parameter Records (<emu-xref href="#sec-signature-records"></emu-xref>), so a tacit call could be checked, the topic tested against the first parameter and an under-supplied signature rejected at the pipe.</p>
        <p>What survives is narrower and is enough. A tacit form cannot express the steps that are not calls — `% + 1`, `[%, %]`, `await %` — and `await` is not a function, so no signature rescues it. A pipeline whose steps are sometimes tacit and sometimes not would be two forms sharing an operator, and the second early error is what keeps them from being confused.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-pipeline-modifications">
      <h1>Modifications to Existing Clauses</h1>
      <p><emu-xref href="#table-narrowing-forms"></emu-xref> gains a row: a test governing a |TopicReference| narrows it in the positions the test governs, by the same rows the table gives `is`, `typeof`, `instanceof`, `in`, and a discriminant. `shape |&gt; (% is Circle ? %.radius : 0)` is the case, and it is the rule a pipeline cannot do without — a substitution has no identity to narrow, which is a second reason the topic is a binding.</p>
      <p><emu-xref href="#sec-operators"></emu-xref> gains a sentence: `|&gt;` may not be declared as an operator. Resolution reads the left operand's type and searches it for a signature, and a pipeline has no operation for a signature to describe — it binds one operand and evaluates the other rather than computing a value from both. The same reasoning excludes any future operator that binds rather than computes.</p>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-types-across-agents">
    <h1>Types Across Agents</h1>
    <p>The threading extension gives a program more than one executing thread over one heap. There is no copying step between them: a value is shared by being referenced, and no structured clone, no message, and no byte buffer stands in between. The threads of such a program are agents of one agent cluster that share a heap rather than only a block of memory, and the intern table of <emu-xref href="#sec-gettypeobject"></emu-xref> is a property of that heap, as the Composite Registry is (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-registry"></emu-xref>). They therefore share both. A type constructed on one thread is the same Type Object on every other, `===` answers on any thread what it answers on the thread that built it, and nothing about interning, canonicalization, or the total order is per-thread. This is what that clause's note defers to, and the answer is that the extension does not carry a Type Object across a boundary; it removes the boundary.</p>

    <p>An agent that does not share a heap is the other case, and a Type Object does not reach it. A value that crosses to one crosses as data, and the receiving agent types that data by its own declarations and interns the result in its own table. Two structurally identical declarations in two such agents are two Type Objects and are not `===`, which is correct rather than a defect: identity is what interning within one heap establishes, and there is no third table above them in which to establish more. A program that needs the same type on both sides declares it on both sides, and every structural rule of this specification then agrees about it, because structure is what those rules read.</p>

    <p>Two consequences follow for the types whose identity rests on something an agent holds. A symbol literal type (<emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref>) over a registered Symbol has a counterpart in another agent, because the registry is shared and the key is what identifies it; over an unregistered Symbol it has none, and the type does not travel, because the Symbol does not. The canonical order of symbol values is by first interning within an agent (<emu-xref href="#sec-canonical-total-order"></emu-xref>), which is a per-heap fact for the same reason the table is, and two agents may order the same two symbols differently without either being wrong.</p>

    <p>A value type is copied by assignment (<emu-xref href="#sec-typed-classes"></emu-xref>), and that copy is not atomic. A value wider than a single primitive, or an update spanning several fields, may be observed torn or half-applied by a thread that races it. Atomicity is obtained only through `Atomics` operations, whose `compareExchange` compares with SameValueZero so that a loop cycling through *NaN* terminates; there is no rule making some assignments atomic and others not, because leaving that set undefined would make a racing program unspecifiable and would tax every shared write. The `shared` modifier marks the state a program expects to cross threads, which is what lets the boundary be reasoned about statically rather than discovered.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The proposal makes no claim to prove races away. A race here is a stale value and never a corrupted heap, which is the position Java and Go take rather than Rust's; what the types offer in place of a proof is the discipline that `shared` marks what crosses and that mutable shared state is reached through an atomic or a lock. Retrofitting a `Send`/`Sync` proof onto a shared mutable heap without a borrow checker would make this a different language, and the memory safety the core guarantees is unconditional either way.</p>
      <p>That tearing is possible is worth stating next to the value type rules rather than only here, since it is the one place the core's "a value type copies" meets a second thread. It is also why the layout extension's guarantees are the ones a concurrent program leans on: a `shared SoA.&lt;T&gt;` allocates each field its own column, so two threads writing two fields of one element never contend for a cache line, which no interleaved layout can promise.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-threading">
    <h1>Threading</h1>
    <p>The clause before this one settles what a shared heap means for the identity of a type. This one specifies the surface that puts a second thread on that heap: how a thread is created and when it ends, where a promise reaction runs when the thread that settles a promise is not the thread that attached to it, what the `shared` modifier claims about storage and about what a checker may assume of it, the `Atomics` operations over typed values, the Lock, Condition, and ThreadLocal objects, and parallel iteration over a range.</p>

    <p>The whole of it rests on one decision the core has already made elsewhere and this clause only carries through: memory safety is unconditional and race freedom is not. A program that races produces a stale or surprising value, never a corrupted heap and never a value that is not of its declared type. Everything here that constrains an implementation constrains it in service of that line, and everything here that constrains a program is a discipline the program may keep or break without the engine's guarantees depending on which it does.</p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>The clauses that follow are ordered as they depend on each other. <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-agent-cluster"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-scheduling"></emu-xref> establish what a thread is and where a job runs, which <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-callthread"></emu-xref> then uses. <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-shared-modifier"></emu-xref> gives `shared` its meaning in the type system and its placement, which <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-memory-model"></emu-xref> turns into the events the memory model of the pinned edition already reasons over, and only then can <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-atomics"></emu-xref> say what an atomic operation on a typed value is. <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-synchronization"></emu-xref> and <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-parallel-iteration"></emu-xref> are built from those. Cancellation is not a clause of its own: it is an `AbortSignal` observed at points the operations that can observe it each name.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <emu-clause id="sec-threading-agent-cluster">
      <h1>Threads and the Agent Cluster</h1>
      <p>A <dfn>thread</dfn> is an agent. The threads of one program are the agents of one agent cluster, and what distinguishes that cluster from the one an ECMAScript program has today is that its agents share a heap rather than only a block of memory: one realm, one global object, one already-executed module graph, one intern table, one Composite Registry (<emu-xref href="#sec-types-across-agents"></emu-xref>). Nothing is copied between them, so no rule of this specification describes a value crossing from one to another. There is nowhere to cross to.</p>

      <p>Each thread holds what an agent already holds, per-thread: an execution context stack, the microtask queue its jobs are drained from, and its Agent Record fields, of which [[CanBlock]] is the one this extension consults (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-synchronization"></emu-xref>). Each thread drains its own queue, and a job of one thread's queue never runs on another. The main thread is the agent that evaluated the program; it is a thread like the others in everything this clause says, and hosts commonly give it [[CanBlock]] *false*.</p>

      <p>A thread runs to completion and then ends. CreateThread (<emu-xref href="#sec-createthread"></emu-xref>) gives the steps; the shape is that the function is called, a thenable result is adopted, the thread's own microtask queue is drained, the settlement is posted, and the agent is removed from the cluster. The drain comes before the post, so everything the thread did, its trailing microtasks included, happens-before the thread that awaits the result observes it.</p>

      <p>Whether a thread may receive host-defined work &mdash; a timer, an I/O completion, a host job of any kind &mdash; and what becomes of such work still pending when the thread's function has settled, is host-defined. A host that keeps a thread alive to run it does not thereby change what the thread's handle means: the handle settles when the function does, which is the rule the thread's caller was given.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>A thread costs a native stack and the per-thread allocator state an agent needs, and not another evaluation of the program: the module graph is already executed and is shared, so nothing about starting a thread re-runs it. That is what makes a thread per request or per connection a reasonable structure rather than one to be pooled around, and it is the concrete difference from an agent that does not share a heap, which must be given its own copy of everything before it can run a line.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-threading-scheduling">
      <h1>Job Scheduling Across Threads</h1>
      <p>A promise is an ordinary object of the shared heap, so any thread that holds one may settle it and any thread may attach to it. Where the reaction runs is a separate question, and this clause answers it: <strong>a promise reaction runs on the thread that created it</strong>.</p>

      <p>A PromiseReaction Record gains an [[Agent]] field, set to the surrounding agent when the record is created. PerformPromiseThen creates the reactions it appends to a pending promise's lists, and the Await machinery creates the reaction that resumes a suspended execution context, so in both cases [[Agent]] is the agent that attached. NewPromiseReactionJob returns its job paired with [[Agent]] rather than with the settling agent, and HostEnqueuePromiseJob enqueues a job on the microtask queue of the agent it is paired with. Attaching a handler to a promise that has already settled enqueues on the attaching agent, which is the agent running PerformPromiseThen at that moment, so that path needs no rule of its own.</p>

      <p>Settling a promise from another thread therefore posts to the reaction's home queue. The post carries a happens-before edge: every operation the settling thread performed before the settle happens-before every step of the reaction. This is the ordinary release-acquire pairing the memory model already gives a synchronizing operation (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-memory-model"></emu-xref>), and it is what makes a value written before `resolve` visible to the code that awaits it.</p>

      <p>The handle returned by `callThread` is not an exception to this rule. It settles on the thread that created it, which is the thread that called `callThread`, because that is where its reactions were attached &mdash; the same reason every other promise behaves the way it does.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>The alternative rule &mdash; run a reaction on whichever thread settled the promise, saving the post &mdash; does not survive `await`. An `await` resumption IS a promise reaction, so under that rule the continuation of an async function runs wherever the awaited promise happened to settle. `await lock.asyncHold()` would resume the waiter on the thread that released the lock, stacking every waiter's critical section onto the releasing thread; a main-thread function awaiting anything another thread can settle would silently continue off the main thread, which is fatal to a thread-affine host API and invisible at the `await` that caused it; a `ThreadLocal` read would not survive an `await`; and placement would become a race, since a handler attached before a settle would run on one thread and the same handler attached after it on another.</p>
        <p>It would also separate `await` from `.then`, which this language specifies in terms of each other. A threading model in which rewriting one into the other changes which thread runs the body is one a programmer cannot hold in mind. The cost of the rule chosen here is a cross-thread enqueue, and settle-side execution is never *needed*, since the settling thread may always do the work before it settles. Recovering it where a program wants it is a matter for an explicit opt-in, not for the default.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <emu-clause id="sec-finalizationregistry-across-threads">
        <h1>Weak References Across Threads</h1>
        <p>A target is live while it is reachable from any thread of the cluster. Reachability is a property of the heap, and the heap is one, so no thread's stack is privileged in deciding it and a target held only by a thread other than the registering one is not collectable.</p>
        <p>A cleanup callback registered with a FinalizationRegistry is called on the thread that registered it, whichever thread's collection observed the target become unreachable. This is the rule of <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-scheduling"></emu-xref> applied to a callback rather than to a reaction: the work runs where it was attached. What may be held weakly at all is unchanged &mdash; an instance of a typed class may not be (<emu-xref href="#sec-weak-references-and-typed-objects"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-threading-callthread">
      <h1>Function.prototype.callThread</h1>
      <p>`callThread` calls a function on a new thread and returns a promise for its result.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-function.prototype.callthread">
        <h1>Function.prototype.callThread ( ..._args_ )</h1>
        <p>This method performs the following steps when called:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _func_ be the *this* value.
          1. If IsCallable(_func_) is *false*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _options_ and _callArgs_ be the result of ClassifyThreadArguments(_func_, _args_).
          1. Let _signal_ be *undefined*.
          1. If _options_ is not ~empty~, then
            1. Set _signal_ to ? Get(_options_, *"signal"*).
            1. If _signal_ is not *undefined* and _signal_ is not an AbortSignal, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return CreateThread(_func_, _callArgs_, _signal_).
        </emu-alg>
        <p>The declared type of this method is `(options?: { signal?: AbortSignal }, ...args) =&gt; Promise.&lt;`_Return_`, any&gt;`, where _Return_ is the declared return type of the function it is called on. The rejection type is `any` because a function may throw anything.</p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-classifythreadarguments" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          ClassifyThreadArguments (
            _func_: a function object,
            _args_: a List of ECMAScript language values,
          ): a pair of an Object or ~empty~ and a List of ECMAScript language values
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It decides whether the first element of _args_ is the options bag or the first forwarded argument.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _args_ is empty, return the pair (~empty~, _args_).
          1. Let _first_ be _args_[0].
          1. If _first_ is not an ordinary Object, return the pair (~empty~, _args_).
          1. For each signature _sig_ of the declared type of _func_, do
            1. Let _p_ be the type of the first parameter of _sig_, or ~unresolvable~ if _sig_ has no parameters or that type is not resolvable at this point.
            1. If _p_ is not ~unresolvable~ and ! IsOfType(_first_, _p_) is *true*, return the pair (~empty~, _args_).
          1. If _first_ has no own property keys, return the pair (_first_, the List of the elements of _args_ from index 1).
          1. If _first_ has an own property *"signal"* whose value is an AbortSignal, return the pair (_first_, the List of the elements of _args_ from index 1).
          1. Return the pair (~empty~, _args_).
        </emu-alg>
        <p>The bag is recognized by the brand of the value it carries and not by the shape of the object carrying it, because a brand is the stronger evidence: an ordinary object whose `signal` is an actual AbortSignal is an options bag in every program that is not deliberately constructed to look like one. An empty object is admitted so that a bag may be passed explicitly with nothing in it.</p>

        <p>The declared signature overrides the brand, which is the step no untyped resolution can perform. A function that declares a first parameter admitting the object receives it as an argument, whatever it carries, so a program that wants to forward a signal-carrying object as data annotates the parameter and is answered exactly. Argument count plays no part: rest parameters and overloads make a declared length useless for this.</p>

        <p>Admission is membership and not assignability. The operation holds a value rather than a type, so IsOfType is what it can ask, and asking it means an object is admitted by a parameter type it actually satisfies rather than by one it could be converted to. <em>Any</em> signature suffices, not only a sole one: an overloaded callee that can receive the object in at least one of its shapes is a callee the program meant to hand it to, and choosing among the shapes is the ordinary work of overload resolution at the call rather than of this operation.</p>

        <p>A parameter type that is not resolvable at this point &mdash; the unbound type parameter of a generic function, which names a type the call has not yet bound &mdash; does not admit. Both answers are defensible and neither is more correct, but refusing keeps the bag reachable on a generic function, and a program that wants the other reading has the concrete annotation available to say so.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>One case is left over, and it is the intersection of three opt-outs: an untyped variadic function whose one intended argument is an object carrying a real AbortSignal under the key `signal` sees it taken as the bag. Annotating the parameter resolves it, and so does wrapping the value.</p>
          <p>This operation counts <em>own</em> property keys, while `callThread` reads the option with Get, which walks the prototype chain. The asymmetry is deliberate and is what makes `callThread`'s validation step reachable: an object with no own keys is an explicit empty bag, so one created with an inherited `signal` is classified as a bag and the inherited value is then read. An inherited AbortSignal is used, and an inherited value that is not one throws rather than being silently ignored. Were both steps to consult only own properties, no object could reach that step with a bad `signal` and the check would be unreachable.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-createthread" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          CreateThread (
            _func_: a function object,
            _args_: a List of ECMAScript language values,
            _signal_: an AbortSignal or *undefined*,
          ): a Promise
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It creates an agent of the surrounding agent's cluster, calls _func_ on it, and returns a promise for the result.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _capability_ be ! NewPromiseCapability(%Promise%).
          1. If _signal_ is not *undefined* and _signal_ is aborted, then
            1. Perform ! Call(_capability_.[[Reject]], *undefined*, &laquo; the abort reason of _signal_ &raquo;).
            1. Return _capability_.[[Promise]].
          1. Let _thread_ be a new agent of the surrounding agent's agent cluster, sharing its heap and its realm. The [[CanBlock]] field of _thread_ is host-defined.
          1. Let _finish_ be a new Abstract Closure with parameters (_completion_) that captures _capability_, _thread_, and the surrounding agent and performs the following steps when called:
            1. If _completion_ is a normal completion, perform ! Call(_capability_.[[Resolve]], *undefined*, &laquo; _completion_.[[Value]] &raquo;).
            1. Else, perform ! Call(_capability_.[[Reject]], *undefined*, &laquo; _completion_.[[Value]] &raquo;).
            1. Remove _thread_ from the agent cluster.
          1. Enqueue on the microtask queue of _thread_ a job that performs the following steps:
            1. Let _result_ be Completion(Call(_func_, *undefined*, _args_)).
            1. If _result_ is a normal completion and _result_.[[Value]] is an Object with a callable *"then"* property, then
              1. Let _inner_ be ! PromiseResolve(%Promise%, _result_.[[Value]]).
              1. Perform PerformPromiseThen(_inner_, a function that calls _finish_ with a normal completion of its argument, a function that calls _finish_ with a throw completion of its argument).
              1. Return ~unused~.
            1. Perform _finish_(_result_).
          1. Return _capability_.[[Promise]].
        </emu-alg>
        <p>The capability is created on the calling thread, so its reactions are that thread's reactions and the handle settles there (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-scheduling"></emu-xref>).</p>

        <p>Adopting a thenable is a suspension of the thread and not a step it runs through. An async function returns a pending promise the moment it reaches its first `await`, so a thread that settled its handle with the returned value and ended would end while its function was still suspended: the body past the `await` would never run and the handle would resolve with a promise rather than with a result. The reactions on _inner_ are created on _thread_, so they are _thread_'s reactions and run on it, and _thread_ is therefore alive and running its own continuation until the adoption completes. That is the whole of what "the thread ends when the completion settles" means.</p>

        <p>There is no separate step draining the thread's microtask queue. The settlement is delivered by a job on the spawning agent's queue, enqueued when _finish_ runs, and by then every job the thread queued before it has already run &mdash; the drain is a consequence of the queue rather than an operation performed on it. What the clause promises, that everything the thread did happens-before the spawner observes its result, holds for that reason.</p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-thread-cancellation">
        <h1>Cancellation</h1>
        <p>The `signal` option is an AbortSignal, the mechanism the platform already has. A signal already aborted when `callThread` is called rejects the handle with the signal's abort reason and starts no thread.</p>

        <p>Otherwise the thread observes the abort at a <dfn>cancellation checkpoint</dfn>, and the checkpoints are the points at which it is between things or waiting for one: when it takes a job from its microtask queue, at the resumption of an `await`, and in a wait &mdash; `Atomics.wait` and `Atomics.waitAsync` (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-atomics"></emu-xref>), and the Lock and Condition waits (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-synchronization"></emu-xref>). A wait that is already parked when the abort happens is woken by it. A wait that could only be observed on entry would leave a parked thread uncancellable, which is most of what cancellation is for.</p>

        <p>Observing it throws the signal's abort reason from whatever operation was pending. That is an ordinary abrupt completion and unwinds ordinarily: `finally` blocks run, `using` declarations dispose, a lock held through `hold` is released, and a reason that reaches the top of the thread's function rejects the handle.</p>

        <p>Those two sentences have an order, and it decides what a host may throw away. A thread parked on a wait is not idle: the abort is delivered <em>through</em> the wait, which completes abruptly with the reason. A thread that has been delivered an abort is not idle either: it is unwinding, and its remaining jobs are the `finally` blocks and `catch` clauses of that unwinding. So an abort abandons a thread's queued work only where the thread has nothing pending to deliver it through and has not already been delivered it &mdash; a thread that has not yet begun, or one between jobs. Abandoning the work of a thread that is unwinding would discard exactly the steps the paragraph above promises will run.</p>

        <p>There is no checkpoint at a loop back-edge. A computation with no suspension point is cancelled cooperatively, by reading the signal's aborted state, which crosses threads because the signal is an object of the shared heap and because an abort on one thread and the read that observes it on another are ordered by <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-memory-model"></emu-xref>.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Cancellation interrupts; it does not destroy. A thread torn down where it stands would leave broken exactly the invariants a lock was protecting, which is why `Thread.stop` and `Thread.Abort` were both removed from the languages that had them, and why the surviving design everywhere is an exception thrown at a point the thread can be caught at.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-threading-shared-modifier">
      <h1>The shared Modifier</h1>
      <p>`shared` before a type produces the Type Record { [[Kind]]: ~shared~, [[Target]]: the Type Record of its operand }. The grammar that admits it is `PrimaryType : `shared` PrimaryType`, given with the array and tuple types (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-and-tuple-types"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <p>It is a type error if the operand is not a value type (<emu-xref href="#sec-value-types"></emu-xref>): the sized numerics, the vector types, a value type class, a fixed-length array of a value type, and the `SoA.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` of the structure-of-arrays extension are what the modifier admits. It is a type error if the operand is itself ~shared~, and a type error if it is ~reference~, since `ref` denotes a location rather than a value and a location is already reached from wherever the thread that holds it can reach.</p>

      <p>An object needs no modifier and is not given one. There is one heap, so a thread that can reach a reference reaches the object it denotes; `shared` would claim nothing of an object type that is not already true of every object type, and would falsely suggest that a marked collection is a concurrent one when what the design offers is the ordinary collection under a Lock. A composite (<emu-xref href="#sec-composites"></emu-xref>) is refused for a stronger reason: it is frozen, so the question the modifier answers does not arise for it.</p>

      <p>A ~shared~ type is invariant in its [[Target]], as a ~reference~ is, and for the same reason: the storage it describes is read and written, so a mismatch in either direction is unsound. Between a ~shared~ type and a type that is not one, the modifier is instead transparent, because it is not observable in the value. IsSubtype (<emu-xref href="#sec-issubtype"></emu-xref>) reads, before its comparison of kinds: if _s_.[[Kind]] is ~shared~ and _t_.[[Kind]] is not ~shared~, return IsSubtype(_s_.[[Target]], _t_, _assumptions_); if _t_.[[Kind]] is ~shared~ and _s_.[[Kind]] is not ~shared~, return IsSubtype(_s_, _t_.[[Target]], _assumptions_). A value of type _T_ is therefore assignable to storage of type `shared `_T_, which is how a value is published, and a read of that storage yields a value of _T_.</p>

      <p>The unwrapping is one-sided on purpose. Two ~shared~ types meet under the invariant rule, and SameType holds of `shared `_T_ and _T_ for no _T_, which is what keeps the distinction load-bearing exactly where it has to be: a writable member is invariant (<emu-xref href="#sec-isobjectsubtype"></emu-xref>), so `{ x: shared uint32 }` is not viewable as `{ x: uint32 }`, and the regime a slot is under (<emu-xref href="#sec-shared-stability"></emu-xref>) cannot be laundered by aliasing the slot through an object type that drops the marker.</p>

      <p>RequireType (<emu-xref href="#sec-requiretype"></emu-xref>) enforces a ~shared~ type by enforcing its [[Target]]: publishing a value into shared storage applies the same check and the same conversion as storing it in unmarked storage of that type. The conversion is the part that matters. A boundary that tested membership without it would refuse `let a: shared uint32 = 5` while accepting `let a: uint32 = 5`, since a Number is not a member of `uint32` but converts to one, and no rule of this clause asks for those two to differ.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-shared-storage">
        <h1>Placement</h1>
        <p>Storage of a ~shared~ type is backed by a Shared Data Block, laid out at the offsets <emu-xref href="#table-layout-by-type"></emu-xref> gives its [[Target]]. The modifier decides placement and nothing about layout: `shared `_T_ and _T_ have the same size, alignment, and field offsets, so a value moves between marked and unmarked storage by copying and no representation changes.</p>

        <p>A `shared SoA.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` allocates each of its columns its own region. Two threads writing two different fields of one element therefore write two different regions, and no false sharing arises between them, which an interleaved layout cannot promise at any alignment.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Unmarked storage of a value type is not thereby unreachable from another thread, and this clause does not make it so. A binding a function closes over is reached by whatever thread runs that function. What the modifier buys is that the storage is shared-capable from the start: an implementation that discovers a second thread touching unmarked storage may place it in a Shared Data Block at that point, at a cost the marked case does not pay. That is an implementation's affair, and no operation of this specification consults the modifier to decide whether it is permitted (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-atomics"></emu-xref>).</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-shared-stability">
        <h1>Stability and Narrowing</h1>
        <p>Narrowing a binding or a field (<emu-xref href="#sec-narrowing"></emu-xref>) records at one program point a type narrower than the declared one and carries it to a later point. Carrying it assumes the storage did not change in between. The two regimes of this clause are the two answers to whether that assumption may be made.</p>

        <p>For storage that is not ~shared~, it may. A narrowing established before an operation that no thread of this agent can have changed the storage through survives that operation, and a check the narrowing makes redundant is elidable exactly as <emu-xref href="#sec-check-elision"></emu-xref> allows in a program with one thread. If another thread writes the storage anyway, the narrowing may be stale, and a stale narrowing is a branch taken on old information: the value read is still of the storage's declared type, because a write from any thread passes the same boundary check every write passes (<emu-xref href="#sec-typed-storage"></emu-xref>), so what the program loses is the refinement and never the declared type.</p>

        <p>For storage that is ~shared~, it may not. The slot carries no narrowing across any operation: each read of it yields its declared type, and a program that wants a narrowing reads once into a local binding, which is unmarked storage, and narrows that. A program with no reachable second thread is unaffected in either regime, since a narrowing is only lost where another thread could have written.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Declared types hold in both regimes, so every elision <emu-xref href="#sec-check-elision"></emu-xref> justifies at a declared type is justified here unchanged, and that clause needs no threading caveat. What differs is only how long a narrowing lives, which is a claim about refinement and not about type.</p>
          <p>This is the position a C or C++ compiler takes on non-atomic storage, transplanted into a language where the failure it permits is bounded. There, a compiler that assumes no concurrent mutation and is wrong may produce anything at all. Here the memory safety the core guarantees is unconditional, so the same assumption, when wrong, costs a value that is out of date and nothing else. That bound is what makes the assumption affordable, and it is why the modifier can be a claim the program makes rather than one the implementation must verify.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-threading-memory-model">
      <h1>Shared Typed Memory</h1>
      <p>This clause adds no memory model. The one the pinned edition defines already says what a racing program may observe, in terms of a candidate execution over a set of events and the agent-order, reads-from, and synchronizes-with relations among them. What was missing was only that its events were reachable exclusively through a TypedArray over a SharedArrayBuffer. Typed storage has byte addresses too (<emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>), so this clause names the events that typed accesses are, and everything that clause proves then applies to them unchanged.</p>

      <p>Let a <dfn>shared typed slot</dfn> be storage of a ~shared~ type, which <emu-xref href="#sec-shared-storage"></emu-xref> places in a Shared Data Block at a known offset for a known number of bytes. Then:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A read of a shared typed slot is a ReadSharedMemory event whose [[Order]] is ~unordered~, whose [[Block]] is that Shared Data Block, whose [[ByteIndex]] is the slot's offset, and whose [[ElementSize]] is the laid-out size of the slot's type.</li>
        <li>A write of a shared typed slot is a WriteSharedMemory event with the same fields and [[Order]] ~unordered~.</li>
        <li>An `Atomics` operation on a shared typed slot (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-atomics"></emu-xref>) is the corresponding event with [[Order]] ~seq-cst~, a read-modify-write being a ReadModifyWriteSharedMemory event as it is over a TypedArray.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>A slot whose type is one of the sized numerics or `boolean` has [[NoTear]] *true*: a read of it reads the bytes of a single write and never a mixture of two. A slot whose type is an aggregate &mdash; a value type class, a fixed-length array, a vector &mdash; has no such guarantee, and a read of it concurrent with a write may compose bytes of both. This is what <emu-xref href="#sec-types-across-agents"></emu-xref> states in prose and this clause states as events.</p>

      <emu-note>
        <p>Tearing is bounded in a way it is not in an unmanaged language. A torn read of an aggregate composes bytes that were each written by some thread through the typed-storage boundary (<emu-xref href="#sec-typed-storage"></emu-xref>), so every field of the result holds a value of that field's declared type. What the program loses is the relation between the fields &mdash; a point whose `x` is from one write and whose `y` is from another &mdash; and never the type of either. A program that needs the whole aggregate at once either takes a Lock (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-synchronization"></emu-xref>) or builds the value, freezes it, and publishes it by a single reference store, which is the composite idiom (<emu-xref href="#sec-composites"></emu-xref>) and is tear-free because a reference is one slot.</p>
      </emu-note>

      <p>Accesses to storage that is not ~shared~ are not events of this model, and a program with no `shared` storage and no `Atomics` has the memory model of the pinned edition unchanged. Where such storage is nonetheless reached by two threads, an implementation that has placed it in a Shared Data Block gives its accesses the same event vocabulary; where it has not, the accesses are not shared-memory accesses and the checker's assumption of thread stability (<emu-xref href="#sec-shared-stability"></emu-xref>) is what governs. Either way the outcome is bounded the same way: a stale value of the declared type, never an unsafe one.</p>

      <p>Two threads are ordered by the relations of the memory model and by nothing this extension adds. Settling a promise whose reaction belongs to another thread synchronizes-with the running of that reaction (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-scheduling"></emu-xref>), and the release of a Lock synchronizes-with the acquisition that follows it (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-synchronization"></emu-xref>); those are the edges an ordinary program is built from, and each is an ordinary seq-cst pairing rather than a new kind of ordering.</p>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-threading-atomics">
      <h1>Atomics on Typed Values</h1>
      <p>The `Atomics` operations of the pinned edition take a TypedArray and an index. This clause admits two further targets: a typed binding reached through a reference, and a typed own data property of an object. A program that has a `uint32` may operate on it atomically without first arranging for it to live inside a byte buffer.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-atomics-reference-arguments">
        <h1>Reference Arguments</h1>
        <p>An `Atomics` operation whose first argument position is written `ref` _binding_ receives a Reference Record rather than the value of the binding, as a call to any function declaring a `ref` parameter does (<emu-xref href="#sec-references-and-borrowing"></emu-xref>).</p>

        <p>A built-in function is therefore able to declare a parameter position as taking a reference, and each of the operations of this clause declares its first. This is a change to the references extension, which until now needed no such thing: a built-in had no `ref` parameter, so a reference argument decayed to its referent at every built-in boundary without exception. The exception is unavoidable here, since operating on a copy of the binding is not the operation the program asked for. Nothing else changes, and in particular the reflective call paths remain decay channels: `f.call(*null*, ref x)` decays at %Function.prototype.call%, which declares no such position, so what it forwards carries a value.</p>

        <p>The borrow is validated where the `ref` argument is evaluated, by RequireBorrowableReference (<emu-xref href="#sec-requireborrowablereference"></emu-xref>), which is how every `ref` argument reaches every callee. A reference that arrives at one of these operations has already passed it, so these operations do not apply it a second time.</p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-validateatomictarget" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          ValidateAtomicTarget (
            _args_: a List of ECMAScript language values,
            _operation_: ~integer-only~ or ~any-value-type~,
          ): either a normal completion containing an Atomic Target Record or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It resolves the shapes an `Atomics` operation admits to one description of a place in memory.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p>An <dfn>Atomic Target Record</dfn> has fields [[Block]] (a Data Block or Shared Data Block), [[ByteIndex]] (a non-negative integer), and [[Type]] (a Type Record).</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _args_[0] is a Reference Record, then
            1. Let _ref_ be ? RequireBorrowableReference(_args_[0]).
            1. Let _type_ be the declared type of the storage _ref_ denotes.
            1. Let _block_ and _byteIndex_ be the Data Block and offset at which that storage is placed.
          1. Else if _args_[0] is an Object that is not a TypedArray, then
            1. Let _key_ be ? ToPropertyKey(_args_[1]).
            1. If _args_[0] does not have a typed own data property whose key is _key_, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Let _type_ be the declared type of that property.
            1. Let _block_ and _byteIndex_ be the Data Block and offset at which that property is placed.
          1. Else,
            1. Return ? ValidateIntegerTypedArray(_args_[0], _args_[1]) as an Atomic Target Record whose [[Type]] is the element type of the TypedArray.
          1. If _type_ is not a value type, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If _operation_ is ~integer-only~ and _type_ is not an integer type, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If the laid-out size of _type_ (<emu-xref href="#sec-memory-layout"></emu-xref>) is not one the implementation performs atomically, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return the Atomic Target Record { [[Block]]: _block_, [[ByteIndex]]: _byteIndex_, [[Type]]: _type_ }.
        </emu-alg>

        <p>The `shared` modifier is not consulted. A binding carrying it and one not carrying it are equally valid targets, and the restrictions this operation enforces are restrictions of <em>type</em>: an integer type where the operation needs bit patterns, a value type of a size the implementation can operate on atomically, and a typed own data property rather than an ordinary one, an `any`-typed slot having no width for an operation to be atomic over.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Requiring the modifier would buy no invariant. Unmarked storage is not unreachable from another thread &mdash; a binding a function closes over is reached by whatever thread runs that function &mdash; so plain racing writes to it are possible whether or not the synchronized ones are permitted, and forbidding only the synchronized ones would leave a program the worse tool for the same storage. It would also fracture generic code: a function taking `ref uint32` would have to know whether its caller's storage carried a marker, and expressing that would mean a sharedness dimension through the reference types for no gain. What the modifier changes is cost, not legality (<emu-xref href="#sec-shared-storage"></emu-xref>).</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-atomics-typed-operations">
        <h1>The Operations</h1>
        <p>`Atomics.load`, `Atomics.store`, `Atomics.exchange`, and `Atomics.compareExchange` accept any target ValidateAtomicTarget (<emu-xref href="#sec-validateatomictarget"></emu-xref>) admits with _operation_ ~any-value-type~. `Atomics.add` and `Atomics.sub` accept the integer types and the floating-point types. `Atomics.and`, `Atomics.or`, `Atomics.xor`, `Atomics.wait`, `Atomics.waitAsync`, and `Atomics.notify` are ~integer-only~: a bitwise operation on a floating-point value has no meaning the program intended, and a wait compares bit patterns.</p>

        <p>A value stored through any of these operations passes the typed-storage boundary (<emu-xref href="#sec-typed-storage"></emu-xref>), so a store of a value not of the target's type throws as an ordinary assignment to that storage would, and the same conversion applies.</p>

        <emu-clause id="sec-atomics-float-arithmetic">
          <h1>Floating-Point Addition</h1>
          <p>`Atomics.add` and `Atomics.sub` on a target whose [[Type]] is a floating-point type perform the read-modify-write as a sequentially consistent compare-exchange loop: read the current value, compute the sum, and attempt to replace the value read with the sum, repeating until the replacement succeeds. Each attempt is one ReadModifyWriteSharedMemory event.</p>
          <p>The result is therefore atomic but not deterministic across interleavings, floating-point addition not being associative. A program that needs a reproducible sum accumulates per-slice partials and combines them in a fixed order, which is what <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-parallel-iteration"></emu-xref> does.</p>
        </emu-clause>

        <emu-clause id="sec-atomics-compare-exchange-predicate">
          <h1>The compareExchange Comparison</h1>
          <p>`Atomics.compareExchange` converts the expected value to the target's [[Type]] and then compares it with the value read using SameValueZero. The conversion is not optional: a comparison of an unconverted operand against a typed value read from the target never succeeds, so every compare-exchange would silently fail and a claim loop would spin rather than throw. The TypedArray form of the operation converts for the same reason. On an integer target this is indistinguishable from every other candidate. On a floating-point target it is the choice that makes a compare-exchange loop terminate, which <emu-xref href="#sec-types-across-agents"></emu-xref> already states normatively and this clause does not restate.</p>
          <emu-note>
            <p>Strict equality would not terminate: *NaN* is not strictly equal to itself, so a loop whose observed value is *NaN* would retry against the very value it read, forever. A comparison of representations would terminate but is not specifiable, since an implementation may write an implementation-chosen *NaN* encoding when it stores a floating-point value, which would leave the success of a compare-exchange varying by implementation. SameValue terminates and distinguishes *+0*<sub>𝔽</sub> from *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, which breaks the commonest use of the operation in the subtlest way: a computed *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub> fails to match a *+0*<sub>𝔽</sub> sentinel, so a claim loop intermittently refuses a slot that is arithmetically zero. SameValueZero is also the language's own storage equality, the predicate of Map, Set, and Array.prototype.includes, which is the role an expected-value comparison plays.</p>
          </emu-note>
        </emu-clause>

        <emu-clause id="sec-atomics-typed-wait">
          <h1>Waiting</h1>
          <p>`Atomics.wait`, `Atomics.waitAsync`, and `Atomics.notify` on a typed target use the WaiterList of the pinned edition, keyed by the target's [[Block]] and [[ByteIndex]] as they are keyed for a TypedArray. A wait on storage the implementation has not placed in a Shared Data Block places it in one, which is the promotion <emu-xref href="#sec-shared-storage"></emu-xref> describes and the one case in which it is forced rather than discovered.</p>
          <p>`Atomics.wait` throws a *TypeError* exception where the surrounding agent's [[CanBlock]] is *false*, as it does today. A wait is a cancellation checkpoint (<emu-xref href="#sec-thread-cancellation"></emu-xref>): a signal aborted while a wait is parked wakes it, and the wait then throws the signal's abort reason. The reactions of `Atomics.waitAsync` run on the agent that created them, as every reaction does (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-scheduling"></emu-xref>).</p>
        </emu-clause>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-threading-synchronization">
      <h1>Synchronization Objects</h1>
      <p>`Atomics` covers a single location. For anything larger &mdash; a multi-field update, a shared collection, a handoff &mdash; this clause specifies three ordinary objects: a Lock (<emu-xref href="#sec-lock-objects"></emu-xref>), a Condition (<emu-xref href="#sec-condition-objects"></emu-xref>), and a ThreadLocal (<emu-xref href="#sec-threadlocal-objects"></emu-xref>).</p>

      <p>Where the surrounding agent's [[CanBlock]] is *false*, `Lock.prototype.hold`, `Lock.prototype.acquire`, and `Condition.prototype.wait` throw a *TypeError* exception, as `Atomics.wait` does. They throw rather than quietly becoming their `async` counterparts: an operation returning _T_ on one thread and `Promise.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` on another would have a return type that depends on which thread is running it, which is not a thing a typed language can say.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-lock-objects">
        <h1>Lock Objects</h1>
        <p>A Lock has internal slots [[Owner]], an Agent Record or ~none~, and [[Waiters]], a List of waiting agents in the order they arrived.</p>

        <p>A Lock is not reentrant. Reentrant monitors buy tolerance of self-nesting at the price of owner-and-count bookkeeping on the fast path, a Condition that must release and restore a nesting depth, and the silent tolerance of a layering violation that a non-reentrant lock reports the moment it appears.</p>

        <p>It is therefore a *TypeError* exception for an agent to acquire a Lock it already owns through `hold` or `acquire`. Those forms block, so the acquisition cannot make progress: the agent that would have to release is the agent now parked, which is a certain deadlock, and the owner is known because `Condition.prototype.wait` must check it anyway. A specification cannot leave the outcome unstated, and the platform's answer for an operation that can never proceed is to throw &mdash; the same choice made when `Atomics.wait` on a host's main thread became an error rather than a frozen page.</p>

        <p>`asyncHold` while holding is not a deadlock and is permitted. The acquisition queues, and the holder may release from a later job before anything awaits the pending promise.</p>

        <emu-clause id="sec-lock.prototype.hold">
          <h1>Lock.prototype.hold ( _callback_ )</h1>
          <p>It acquires the Lock, calls _callback_ with no arguments, releases the Lock, and returns what _callback_ returned. If _callback_ returns an abrupt completion, the Lock is released and the completion propagates, so the release has the force of a `finally`.</p>
          <p>The closure does not escape the call, so an implementation may allocate nothing for it and may inline the critical section. It may not be marked `inline`, for the reason a `ref` callback may not (<emu-xref href="#sec-references-and-borrowing"></emu-xref>).</p>
        </emu-clause>

        <emu-clause id="sec-lock.prototype.acquire">
          <h1>Lock.prototype.acquire ( )</h1>
          <p>It acquires the Lock and returns a <dfn>lock guard</dfn>, an object with a `[Symbol.dispose]` method that releases it. `using guard = lock.acquire()` therefore holds the Lock for the enclosing block, which reads better than `hold` where the critical section wants an early `return` or spans a `try`.</p>
          <p>Disposing a guard twice is a *TypeError* exception, as calling a release function twice is, and for the same reason.</p>
          <emu-note>
            <p>A guard is where the type system can do what a callback cannot. A guard type the checker requires to be consumed by a `using` declaration, or disposed on every path, turns forgetting to release into a type error rather than a deadlock at run time. That refinement is not specified here, the guard being an ordinary disposable, but the shape is chosen so that it can be.</p>
          </emu-note>
        </emu-clause>

        <emu-clause id="sec-lock.prototype.asynchold">
          <h1>Lock.prototype.asyncHold ( )</h1>
          <p>It returns a promise for a release function, fulfilled when the Lock has been acquired for the calling agent. The promise's reactions run on the agent that created them, as every reaction does (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-scheduling"></emu-xref>), so the critical section that follows the `await` runs on the thread that asked for the Lock.</p>
          <p>The release function may be called once, by the agent that received it. A second call, or a call from another agent, is a *TypeError* exception. Tolerating a stale release would unlock a critical section that by then belongs to somebody else, which is the one failure a Lock exists to prevent.</p>
        </emu-clause>

        <emu-clause id="sec-lock-release-ordering">
          <h1>Ordering</h1>
          <p>Releasing a Lock synchronizes-with the acquisition that next obtains it, in the sense of <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-memory-model"></emu-xref>. Everything an agent did while holding the Lock therefore happens-before everything the next holder does, which is what makes a Lock a way to publish a multi-field update that <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-memory-model"></emu-xref> would otherwise permit to tear.</p>
          <p>Which waiter obtains a released Lock is implementation-defined. An implementation is not required to be fair, and a program that requires fairness builds it rather than assuming it.</p>
        </emu-clause>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-condition-objects">
        <h1>Condition Objects</h1>
        <p>A Condition has an internal slot [[Waiters]].</p>

        <emu-clause id="sec-condition.prototype.wait">
          <h1>Condition.prototype.wait ( _lock_ )</h1>
          <p>It is a *TypeError* exception if _lock_ is not a Lock owned by the surrounding agent. It releases _lock_ and parks the agent in one step, so no notification can be missed between the release and the park, and reacquires _lock_ before returning.</p>
          <p>An implementation may wake a waiter that was not notified. A program therefore tests its condition in a loop rather than assuming that returning from `wait` means the condition holds &mdash; which is the discipline every condition variable requires, and which this clause states rather than leaving to be discovered.</p>
        </emu-clause>

        <emu-clause id="sec-condition.prototype.asyncwait">
          <h1>Condition.prototype.asyncWait ( _lock_ )</h1>
          <p>It returns a promise fulfilled when the waiter has been notified and _lock_ reacquired, so the code after the `await` holds the Lock exactly as the code after `wait` does. Its reactions run on the agent that created them.</p>
        </emu-clause>

        <emu-clause id="sec-condition-notify-operations">
          <h1>Condition.prototype.notify and Condition.prototype.notifyAll</h1>
          <p>`notify` wakes one waiter, `notifyAll` wakes every waiter. Which one `notify` wakes is implementation-defined. Neither requires the calling agent to hold any Lock, since the Lock a waiter reacquires is the waiter's and not the notifier's.</p>
        </emu-clause>

        <emu-clause id="sec-condition-cancellation">
          <h1>Cancellation</h1>
          <p>A `wait` and an `asyncWait` are cancellation checkpoints (<emu-xref href="#sec-thread-cancellation"></emu-xref>). An abort delivered while a waiter is parked wakes it, and the wait then completes abruptly with the signal's abort reason &mdash; after reacquiring the Lock, so that the unwinding runs with the invariant the waiter was holding. A Lock acquisition is a checkpoint on the same terms.</p>
        </emu-clause>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-threadlocal-objects">
        <h1>ThreadLocal Objects</h1>
        <p>`ThreadLocal.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` is a generic intrinsic, in the manner of `SoA.&lt;`_T_`&gt;` and `Composite.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`. An instance holds storage per agent: reading its `value` reads the storage of the surrounding agent, and writing it writes that agent's, so no two threads see one another's.</p>
        <p>An agent that has not written the storage reads DefaultValueOf(_T_) (<emu-xref href="#sec-defaultvalueof"></emu-xref>), taken in the realm in which the ThreadLocal was created rather than in the reading agent's. Where _T_ is a parameterization its default is a crossing, and a crossing consults the declarations of a realm; taking the reading agent's would let two agents read one storage and receive different values, or one of them a *TypeError*, for a write neither performed. An agent's storage is unreachable once the agent is not, so a ThreadLocal does not keep the values of ended threads alive.</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>A ThreadLocal is coherent here because a reaction runs on the thread that created it (<emu-xref href="#sec-threading-scheduling"></emu-xref>). Were a continuation to resume on whichever thread settled the promise it awaited, a ThreadLocal read before an `await` and one after it could answer differently in the same function, and the primitive would be a trap rather than a tool. The two rules stand or fall together, which is why one specification carries both.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <p>None of these is compile-time evaluable (<emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>). Each is either shared mutable state or a question about which thread is asking, and neither survives being answered at compile time.</p>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-threading-parallel-iteration">
      <h1>Parallel Iteration</h1>
      <p>The `Thread` namespace object carries two operations over a range of integers. They exist because the alternative &mdash; spawning a thread per slice by hand and joining it &mdash; is both more code and worse, since a thread per call is a thread the pool would have reused.</p>

      <emu-clause id="sec-thread-partition">
        <h1>The Partition</h1>
        <p>Both operations cut _begin_ through _end_ into <dfn>slices</dfn>: disjoint contiguous sub-ranges covering the whole range, numbered from zero in ascending order of the elements they contain. How many, and where the cuts fall, is implementation-defined <em>as a function of _begin_ and _end_ alone</em>. Nothing else may influence it &mdash; not how many threads the host has, not how many are idle, not how long a body took on a previous call.</p>

        <p>Which agent executes which slice is not fixed and may vary between runs of one program. An implementation may move whole slices between agents freely, which is what lets it balance work; what it may not do is cut differently because of what its agents are doing.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>The constraint is what gives `parallelReduce` a reproducible answer over floating-point values, and it is chosen at the level where it costs least. Fixing the partition in this specification would deliver reproducibility across implementations too, but any constant this clause named would be arbitrary today and wrong on the hardware of a few years from now. Leaving the partition entirely to the implementation would deliver nothing: a result that changed with core count, or with whether a machine was busy, is not what a program means by deterministic. A partition that depends only on the range gives an implementation every tuning decision that matters and takes away only the one that would make the answer move.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-thread.parallelfor">
        <h1>Thread.parallelFor ( _begin_, _end_, _body_ )</h1>
        <p>It calls _body_ once with each integer in the range, in slices (<emu-xref href="#sec-thread-partition"></emu-xref>), and returns *undefined* when every slice has finished.</p>

        <p>The calling agent participates: it executes slices itself, and when none remain to claim it waits out the slices still in flight without parking on a WaiterList. A call therefore never blocks in the sense <emu-xref href="#sec-threading-synchronization"></emu-xref> uses, and is permitted on an agent whose [[CanBlock]] is *false* &mdash; a host's main thread included, which is where a frame loop lives and which is the reason the operation is shaped this way.</p>

        <p>Two properties follow, and both are normative. Executing every slice on the calling agent, in ascending slice order, is a conforming implementation: a host with no threads to give still runs the program, and gets the same answer. And a _body_ that itself calls `parallelFor` cannot deadlock by exhausting the pool, since the inner call participates on the same terms; the worst case is that the inner range runs on the agent that entered it.</p>

        <p>A reference into an element is valid for the slice that borrows it (<emu-xref href="#sec-reference-liveness"></emu-xref>), and it is a *TypeError* exception to change the length of an array a slice holds a reference into for the duration of the call. Because slices are disjoint, writes to distinct elements never contend; over a `shared SoA.&lt;`_T_`&gt;`, writes to distinct <em>fields</em> do not contend either, each field being its own region (<emu-xref href="#sec-shared-storage"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-thread.parallelreduce">
        <h1>Thread.parallelReduce ( _begin_, _end_, _initial_, _perElement_, _combine_ )</h1>
        <p>Each slice (<emu-xref href="#sec-thread-partition"></emu-xref>) folds _perElement_ over its own elements, starting from _initial_, producing one partial. The partials are then combined with _combine_ in ascending slice order, left to right. The calling agent participates as it does for `parallelFor`.</p>

        <p>The result is therefore a function of the range, the body, and the callbacks &mdash; and not of the scheduling. Given one implementation, the same _begin_ and _end_ and the same callbacks produce the same value on a busy machine and an idle one, on four cores and on sixty-four. This holds although _combine_ need not be associative, which matters because floating-point addition is not and is the case the operation exists for.</p>

        <p>The guarantee is only as strong as the callbacks. A _perElement_ or _combine_ that consults a ThreadLocal, asks which agent is running, or observes the scheduling in any other way reintroduces exactly the variation the partition rule removes, and this clause promises nothing about such a program.</p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-thread-parallel-errors">
        <h1>Errors</h1>
        <p>If a call to _body_ or _perElement_ completes abruptly in slice _k_, slices numbered above _k_ are cancelled, slices numbered below _k_ are allowed to finish, and the operation completes abruptly with the completion of the lowest-numbered slice that failed. A _combine_ that completes abruptly is attributed to the later of the two slices it was combining.</p>

        <p>That is the completion a sequential execution would have produced, since a sequential execution reaches the lowest failing index first and stops there. Failures are therefore as reproducible as results, and for the same reason.</p>

        <emu-note>
          <p>Reporting whichever failure happened first in time would be simpler and is what an unordered pool naturally does, but it would make the error a program sees depend on scheduling &mdash; so a program with two bad elements would report one of them on a fast machine and the other on a slow one. Collecting every failure would run work already known to be doomed, and the <em>set</em> collected would be scheduling-dependent anyway unless every slice ran to completion.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-thread-pool">
        <h1>The Pool</h1>
        <p>The agents these operations execute slices on are a resource of the agent cluster, sized and managed by the host, and reused across calls. Creating a thread per call would make the operations too expensive for the loop they are meant to sit inside.</p>
        <p>Neither operation is compile-time evaluable (<emu-xref href="#sec-compile-time-evaluability"></emu-xref>).</p>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-typed-json-parsing">
    <h1>Typed JSON Parsing</h1>
    <p>`JSON.parse` gains a generic overload, written `JSON.parse.&lt;_T_&gt;(_text_)`, that parses and validates in one pass. It parses _text_ as JSON, which is a *SyntaxError* on malformed input exactly as the untyped `JSON.parse` is, and then converts the resulting value against _T_ by <emu-xref href="#sec-coercejsonvalue"></emu-xref>. Well-formed JSON that does not satisfy _T_ is a *TypeError* naming the path at which the mismatch occurred, so a typed `catch (_e_: SyntaxError)` and `catch (_e_: TypeError)` separate a malformed document from a well-formed one of the wrong shape.</p>
    <p>A JSON array coerces against an ~array~ type, whose extent it must match where that is fixed, and against a ~tuple~ type, whose admitted lengths it must be one of and whose positions type its elements one by one, with a short array taking the trailing positions' declared defaults. Validation is otherwise layered as any other boundary in this specification: a leaf is checked against its type as it is reached, a parameterized type consults its metadata's validation judgment (<emu-xref href="#sec-isoftype"></emu-xref>), and a class or record `where` clause runs once the target is filled, as the construction boundary check. Unlike the general boundary of <emu-xref href="#sec-requiretype"></emu-xref>, which distinguishes a *RangeError* for a numeric value the target cannot represent, every failure here is a *TypeError*: in a parsed document the type is the schema, so a value outside a field's range violates the schema rather than overflowing an arithmetic. The overload takes no reviver, since a reviver observes the intermediate untyped graph this feature exists not to build.</p>
    <emu-clause id="sec-coercejsonvalue" type="abstract operation">
      <h1>
        CoerceJSONValue (
          _value_: an ECMAScript language value,
          _type_: a Type Record,
          _path_: a String,
        ): either a normal completion containing an ECMAScript language value or a throw completion
      </h1>
      <dl class="header">
        <dt>description</dt>
        <dd>It converts the JSON _value_ into _type_, or returns a throw completion whose value is a *TypeError* describing _path_. It may call user code, since converting a structural type reads and writes properties.</dd>
      </dl>
      <emu-alg>
        1. If _type_.[[Kind]] is ~any~, return _value_.
        1. If _type_.[[Kind]] is ~reference~ or _type_.[[Kind]] is ~shared~, return ? CoerceJSONValue(_value_, _type_.[[Target]], _path_).
        1. If _type_ is a composite type (<emu-xref href="#sec-composite-types"></emu-xref>), then
          1. If _type_ is the top composite type, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _shape_ be the shape of _type_.
          1. Let _data_ be ? CoerceJSONValue(_value_, _shape_, _path_).
          1. If _shape_.[[Kind]] is ~tuple~, then
            1. Let _elements_ be a new empty List.
            1. Let _len_ be ! LengthOfArrayLike(_data_).
            1. Let _i_ be 0.
            1. Repeat, while _i_ &lt; _len_,
              1. Append CanonicalizeCompositeValue(! Get(_data_, ! ToString(𝔽(_i_)))) to _elements_.
              1. Set _i_ to _i_ + 1.
            1. Return ? FindOrCreateTupleComposite(_elements_).
          1. Let _entries_ be a new empty List.
          1. For each own property key _k_ of _data_, in the order returned by ! _data_.[[OwnPropertyKeys]](), do
            1. Append the Composite Entry Record { [[Key]]: _k_, [[Value]]: CanonicalizeCompositeValue(! Get(_data_, _k_)) } to _entries_.
          1. Return FindOrCreateComposite(_entries_).
        1. If _type_.[[Kind]] is ~primitive~, then
          1. If _type_ is a numeric type, then
            1. If _value_ is not a Number, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. If _type_ is a sized integer type and the mathematical value of _value_ is not an integer in the range of _type_, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. If _type_ is the `number` type, return _value_.
            1. Return _value_ converted to _type_ by <emu-xref href="#table-numeric-conversions"></emu-xref>.
          1. If _type_ is the `string` type, then
            1. If _value_ is not a String, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Return _value_.
          1. If _type_ is the `boolean` type, then
            1. If _value_ is not a Boolean, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Return _value_.
          1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. If _type_.[[Kind]] is ~literal~, then
          1. If SameValue(_value_, _type_.[[Value]]) is *true*, return _value_.
          1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. If _type_.[[Kind]] is ~union~, then
          1. For each element _m_ of _type_.[[Members]], do
            1. Let _r_ be Completion(CoerceJSONValue(_value_, _m_, _path_)).
            1. If _r_ is a normal completion, return _r_.[[Value]].
          1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. If _type_.[[Kind]] is ~array~, then
          1. If _value_ is not an Array, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _len_ be ? LengthOfArrayLike(_value_).
          1. If _type_.[[Extent]] is not ~dynamic~ and _type_.[[Extent]] &ne; _len_, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _elements_ be a new empty List.
          1. Let _i_ be 0.
          1. Repeat, while _i_ &lt; _len_,
            1. Let _element_ be the element of _value_ at index _i_.
            1. Let _elementPath_ be the string-concatenation of _path_, the code unit 0x005B (LEFT SQUARE BRACKET), the decimal representation of _i_ as a String, and the code unit 0x005D (RIGHT SQUARE BRACKET).
            1. Let _converted_ be ? CoerceJSONValue(_element_, _type_.[[Element]], _elementPath_).
            1. Append _converted_ to _elements_.
            1. Set _i_ to _i_ + 1.
          1. Return CreateArrayFromList(_elements_).
        1. If _type_.[[Kind]] is ~tuple~, then
          1. If _value_ is not an Array, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _len_ be ? LengthOfArrayLike(_value_).
          1. If _len_ is not a length _type_ admits (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-membership"></emu-xref>), throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _elements_ be a new empty List.
          1. Let _i_ be 0.
          1. Repeat, while _i_ &lt; _len_,
            1. Let _element_ be the element of _value_ at index _i_.
            1. Let _elementPath_ be the string-concatenation of _path_, the code unit 0x005B (LEFT SQUARE BRACKET), the decimal representation of _i_ as a String, and the code unit 0x005D (RIGHT SQUARE BRACKET).
            1. Let _converted_ be ? CoerceJSONValue(_element_, the [[Type]] of the element of _type_.[[Elements]] that receives position _i_, _elementPath_).
            1. Append _converted_ to _elements_.
            1. Set _i_ to _i_ + 1.
          1. For each element _e_ of _type_.[[Elements]] whose position is not less than _len_, do
            1. If _e_.[[Rest]] is *false*, then
              1. Assert: _e_.[[Initial]] is not ~none~, since a tuple's defaults are trailing (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-and-tuple-types"></emu-xref>) and _len_ is a length _type_ admits.
              1. Append _e_.[[Initial]] to _elements_.
          1. Return CreateArrayFromList(_elements_).
        1. If _type_.[[Kind]] is ~object~, then
          1. If _value_ is not an Object or _value_ is an Array, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _result_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(%Object.prototype%).
          1. Let _keys_ be ? _value_.[[OwnPropertyKeys]]().
          1. For each element _k_ of _keys_ such that _k_ is a String and no Property Type Record of _type_.[[Properties]] has key _k_, do
            1. Let _accepted_ be *false*.
            1. For each index signature _s_ of _type_, do
              1. If _accepted_ is *false* and _s_ accepts _k_, then
                1. Let _kv_ be ? Get(_value_, _k_).
                1. Let _keyPath_ be the string-concatenation of _path_, the code unit 0x002E (FULL STOP), and _k_.
                1. Let _converted_ be ? CoerceJSONValue(_kv_, _s_'s value type, _keyPath_).
                1. Perform ! CreateDataPropertyOrThrow(_result_, _k_, _converted_).
                1. Set _accepted_ to *true*.
            1. If _accepted_ is *false*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. For each Property Type Record _p_ of _type_.[[Properties]], do
            1. Let _present_ be ? HasProperty(_value_, _p_'s key).
            1. If _present_ is *true*, then
              1. Let _pv_ be ? Get(_value_, _p_'s key).
              1. Let _fieldPath_ be the string-concatenation of _path_, the code unit 0x002E (FULL STOP), and _p_'s key.
              1. Let _converted_ be ? CoerceJSONValue(_pv_, _p_'s type, _fieldPath_).
              1. Perform ! CreateDataPropertyOrThrow(_result_, _p_'s key, _converted_).
            1. Else if _p_ is optional, then
              1. If _p_'s [[Initial]] is not ~none~, perform ! CreateDataPropertyOrThrow(_result_, _p_'s key, _p_'s [[Initial]]).
            1. Else,
              1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return _result_.
        1. If _type_.[[Kind]] is ~parameterized~, then
          1. Let _base_ be ? CoerceJSONValue(_value_, _type_.[[Base]], _path_).
          1. If ? IsOfType(_base_, _type_) is *false*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return _base_.
        1. If _type_.[[Kind]] is ~nominal~, then
          1. If _type_ is an enum type, then
            1. If _value_ equals the value of an enumerator of _type_, return that enumerator.
            1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If _type_ has a structural form, return ? CoerceJSONValue(_value_, that form, _path_).
        1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>
    <emu-note>
      <p>The physical form the extension describes, writing straight into contiguous typed storage with no intermediate graph, is an implementation strategy and not observable, so an implementation that parses to the untyped JSON value and then converts it conforms.</p>
      <p>The conversions this operation does not perform are the deferred part of the extension: a class target that fills a sealed layout without running its constructor, the exact wide numeric types (the 64-bit integers, `decimal128`, and `bigint`) whose digits must convert without first rounding through a Number, and the enforcement of the built-in refinement judgments, which parameterized validation consults but which are themselves specified with the primitive metadata they belong to. An ~intersection~ target is deferred with them, and deliberately: coercion must produce one value, so an intersection of object types needs a rule merging its members into the single shape the walk fills, including what a key both members declare converts to, and that rule belongs with the objects work rather than with serialization. A ~function~ target throws, since no JSON value denotes a function, and the remaining kinds cannot reach a specialized type.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

<emu-annex id="annex-evaluable-fragment" normative>
  <h1>The Compile-Time Evaluable Fragment</h1>
  <p>This annex fixes the compile-time-evaluable subset of the language as an implementation target in its own right, so that a tool that is not an ECMAScript engine can evaluate types and prove that it agrees with one. The determinism this specification already requires, one result or failure and never a different result (<emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref>), is what makes that agreement a checkable claim.</p>

  <emu-annex id="sec-fragment-grammar">
    <h1>The Grammar of the Fragment</h1>
    <p>The fragment is the ECMAScript grammar less the following, together with the reading discipline of <emu-xref href="#sec-iscompiletimeevaluable"></emu-xref>, which is what excludes input, output, and ambient state, since code in the fragment cannot name them.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>|AwaitExpression| and |YieldExpression|, and every async and generator form of function, method, and arrow.</li>
      <li>|ClassDeclaration| and |ClassExpression|: a builder transforms and constructs types but does not mint a nominal one (<emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref>).</li>
      <li>A direct or indirect `eval`, the Function constructor, the construction of a Proxy, and a dynamic `import()`, which the library rule below also excludes.</li>
      <li>The `debugger` statement.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Everything else is within the fragment: functions and closures over evaluable bindings, local bindings and their mutation, the ordinary operators and control forms including `try`, subject to <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref>'s carve-out, template literals, destructuring, spread, and regular expression literals.</p>
  </emu-annex>

  <emu-annex id="sec-fragment-library">
    <h1>The Library Surface</h1>
    <p>A built-in is within the fragment when its result is determined by its arguments, it performs no observable mutation outside values the call created, and it depends on no host or ambient state. The floor the fragment guarantees: the type operations of <emu-xref href="#sec-reflection"></emu-xref>, Type Object identity and `toString`, regular expression construction and matching, `Map` and `Set`, whose keys Type Objects serve as by interned identity, `Symbol` and its registry, and the pure methods and functions of String, Number, BigInt, Math, Array, Object, and JSON. `Symbol` is within the fragment although a fresh symbol is not determined by its argument: a fresh symbol is a value the call creates, as a fresh array or `Map` is, and the determinism the fragment requires is of the types a program computes and the order it observes them in, both of which the same source fixes by creating the same symbols in the same sequence. `Symbol.for` is within it by the ordinary rule, its result determined by its key. Excluded by the same rule: `Math.random`, Date, every locale-sensitive method, Atomics and shared memory, and weak references and finalization. The conformance suite fixes the exact enumeration.</p>
  </emu-annex>

  <emu-annex id="sec-fragment-semantics">
    <h1>Semantics and Conformance</h1>
    <p>The fragment's semantics are this specification's own, restricted to the fragment; no second evaluator is defined. A standalone implementation of the fragment conforms when, for every program of the fragment, it produces, position for position, the same interned types this specification produces, or fails where this specification fails, under the budget invariant of <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluation-budget"></emu-xref> and with the memoization of <emu-xref href="#sec-evaluatebuildercall"></emu-xref> or anything observably equivalent to it.</p>
    <emu-note>
      <p>The subset is deliberately small as a language: a conforming standalone evaluator is a small tree-walking interpreter, not an embedded engine, and because evaluation is memoized, an interactive tool evaluates each distinct application once. Regular expressions are the one sizeable obligation, and the pattern metadata of <emu-xref href="#sec-parameterized-types"></emu-xref> requires them anyway.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-annex>

  <emu-annex id="sec-fragment-conformance-suite">
    <h1>The Conformance Suite and the Acceptance Corpus</h1>
    <p>A conformance suite accompanies this annex, and conformance to the fragment is conformance to it together with this annex's text. Its seed is the design's acceptance corpus: one hundred and ninety programs, the whole of the type-challenges repository, the warm-up, thirteen easy, one hundred and four medium, fifty-five hard, and seventeen extreme, each pairing the erased-language solution with a builder solution whose assertions must hold under this specification's semantics. The assertions include type identity written `===`, which interning supplies and which the erased corpus can only imitate through unspecified checker behaviour.</p>
    <emu-note>
      <p>The corpus is maintained with the design, and the symbol literal types its eleventh challenge exercises are now those of <emu-xref href="#sec-literal-types"></emu-xref>, so no challenge is gated on an unadopted extension. The questions and divergences the corpus surfaced are recorded in its own notes and tracked with the design documents.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-annex>
</emu-annex>

<emu-annex id="annex-standard-kit">
  <h1>The Standard Builder Kit</h1>
  <p>The primitives are normative and live in the core: construction, relations, `never`, `keyof`, and the evaluation semantics. The kit is the roughly two hundred lines of ordinary evaluable code written over them, and it ships as source under one standard module, spelled `std:types` here and reconciled with the standard library extension by observing that the two do not overlap: that extension gives typed signatures to methods that already exist and introduces no module, where the kit is new code and needs one. The kit is therefore the single new specifier, and a signature listing is never one until its final home is reconciled with the standard library extension. Shipping it as source is the proof that no construct in it is engine magic, gives the ecosystem one interned vocabulary, so that `partial(User)` in two packages is one type, and makes the definitions themselves the reference documentation. A codebase that cannot assume the module can polyfill it verbatim.</p>

  <p>The surface, by family: property mapping and the object utilities (`mapProperties`, `mapPropertyTypes`, `partial`, `required`, `readonly`, `mutable`, `pick`, `omit`, `pickByValue`, `record`, `merge`, `renameProperties`, `propertyType`, `prop`, `objectOf`); keys and indexing (`keysOf`, `indexed`, `paths`); unions (`union`, `arms`, `exclude`, `extract`, `nonNullable`, `mapUnion`, `discriminants`, `byKind`, `handlers`, `removeKind`); functions (`fn`, `parameters`, `firstParameter`, `returnType`, `constructorParameters`, `thisParameterType`, `omitThisParameter`, `withThisType`, `instanceType`); tuples and arrays (`tupleOf`, `tupleElements`, `elementTypes`, `arrayOf`, `tail`, `reverse`, `zip`, `toArrayAll`, `toArrayEach`, `flatten`); literals and strings (`literal`, `literalValues`, `mapLiterals`, `uppercase`, `lowercase`, `capitalized`, `uncapitalized`, `suffixed`, `routeParams`); recursion and composition (`deepPartial`, `traverse`, `compose`, `awaited`); and the additions of the design's maximal set (`noInfer`, `brand`, `stringPattern`, `options`).</p>

  <p>The style is visible in the definitions. The four property mappers are one line each, the union filters two, and the brand three:</p>
  <pre><code class="javascript">export function partial(T: type): type { return mapProperties(T, p =&gt; ({ ...p, optional: true })); }
export function required(T: type): type { return mapProperties(T, p =&gt; ({ ...p, optional: false })); }
export function readonly(T: type): type { return mapProperties(T, p =&gt; ({ ...p, readonly: true })); }
export function mutable(T: type): type { return mapProperties(T, p =&gt; ({ ...p, readonly: false })); }

export function exclude(T: type, U: type): type {
  return union(arms(T).filter(arm =&gt; !Reflect.isAssignable(arm, U)));
}
export function extract(T: type, U: type): type {
  return union(arms(T).filter(arm =&gt; Reflect.isAssignable(arm, U)));
}

export function brand(T: type, tag: string | symbol): type {
  return Reflect.makeType({ kind: 'parameterized', base: T, metadata: { brand: tag } });
}</code></pre>

  <emu-note>
    <p>Where the kit and the core describe one operation, they must agree, and the corpus checks that they do: `keysOf` computes the function <emu-xref href="#sec-keytypesof"></emu-xref> specifies for `keyof`, and the corpus's library blocks assert each shipped helper against the exercise it solves. The kit's normative status is this annex's status: informative, with the primitives it is written over carrying the normativity.</p>
  </emu-note>
</emu-annex>
